{"format":"HARZSEARCH-INDEX","version":"0.1","crawl":{},"documents":[{"content_hash":"84c8656bbb8522aba980b516357115197d38f6d26812a25414132db833f3542c","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:12.916122+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/models/indexes/","language":"","text":"Model index reference¶ Index classes ease creating database indexes. They can be added using the Meta.indexes option. This document explains the API references of Index which includes the index options. Referencing built-in indexes Indexes are defined in django.db.models.indexes, but for convenience they’re imported into django.db.models. The standard convention is to use from django.db import models and refer to the indexes as models.<IndexClass>. Index options¶ class Index(*expressions, fields=(), name=None, db_tablespace=None, opclasses=(), condition=None, include=None)[source]¶ Creates an index (B-Tree) in the database. expressions¶ Index.expressions¶ Positional argument *expressions allows creating functional indexes on expressions and database functions. For example: Index(Lower(\"title\").desc(), \"pub_date\", name=\"lower_title_date_idx\") creates an index on the lowercased value of the title field in descending order and the pub_date field in the default ascending order. Another example: Index(F(\"height\") * F(\"weight\"), Round(\"weight\"), name=\"calc_idx\") creates an index on the result of multiplying fields height and weight and the weight rounded to the nearest integer. Index.name is required when using *expressions. Restrictions on Oracle Oracle requires functions referenced in an index to be marked as DETERMINISTIC. Django doesn’t validate this but Oracle will error. This means that functions such as Random() aren’t accepted. Restrictions on PostgreSQL PostgreSQL requires functions and operators referenced in an index to be marked as IMMUTABLE. Django doesn’t validate this but PostgreSQL will error. This means that functions such as Concat() aren’t accepted. MariaDB Functional indexes are unsupported and ignored with MariaDB. fields¶ Index.fields¶ A list or tuple of the name of the fields on which the index is desired. By default, indexes are created with an ascending order for each column. To define an index with a descending order for a column, add a hyphen before the field’s name. For example Index(fields=['headline', '-pub_date']) would create SQL with (headline, pub_date DESC). name¶ Index.name¶ The name of the index. If name isn’t provided Django will auto-generate a name. For compatibility with different databases, index names cannot be longer than 30 characters and shouldn’t start with a number (0-9) or underscore (_). Partial indexes in abstract base classes You must always specify a unique name for an index. As such, you cannot normally specify a partial index on an abstract base class, since the Meta.indexes option is inherited by subclasses, with exactly the same values for the attributes (including name) each time. To work around name collisions, part of the name may contain '%(app_label)s' and '%(class)s', which are replaced, respectively, by the lowercased app label and class name of the concrete model. For example Index(fields=['title'], name='%(app_label)s_%(class)s_title_index'). db_tablespace¶ Index.db_tablespace¶ The name of the database tablespace to use for this index. For single field indexes, if db_tablespace isn’t provided, the index is created in the db_tablespace of the field. If Field.db_tablespace isn’t specified (or if the index uses multiple fields), the index is created in tablespace specified in the db_tablespace option inside the model’s class Meta. If neither of those tablespaces are set, the index is created in the same tablespace as the table. See also For a list of PostgreSQL-specific indexes, see django.contrib.postgres.indexes. opclasses¶ Index.opclasses¶ The names of the PostgreSQL operator classes to use for this index. If you require a custom operator class, you must provide one for each field in the index. For example, GinIndex(name='json_index', fields=['jsonfield'], opclasses=['jsonb_path_ops']) creates a gin index on jsonfield using jsonb_path_ops. opclasses are ignored for databases besides PostgreSQL. Index.name is required when using opclasses. condition¶ Index.condition¶ If the table is very large and your queries mostly target a subset of rows, it may be useful to restrict an index to that subset. Specify a condition as a Q. For example, condition=Q(pages__gt=400) indexes records with more than 400 pages. Index.name is required when using condition. Restrictions on PostgreSQL PostgreSQL requires functions referenced in the condition to be marked as IMMUTABLE. Django doesn’t validate this but PostgreSQL will error. This means that functions such as Date functions and Concat aren’t accepted. If you store dates in DateTimeField, comparison to datetime objects may require the tzinfo argument to be provided because otherwise the comparison could result in a mutable function due to the casting Django does for lookups. Restrictions on SQLite SQLite imposes restrictions on how a partial index can be constructed. Oracle Oracle does not support partial indexes. Instead, partial indexes can be emulated by using functional indexes together with Case expressions. MySQL and MariaDB The condition argument is ignored with MySQL and MariaDB as neither supports conditional indexes. include¶ Index.include¶ A list or tuple of the names of the fields to be included in the covering index as non-key columns. This allows index-only scans to be used for queries that select only included fields (include) and filter only by indexed fields (fields). For example: Index(name=\"covering_index\", fields=[\"headline\"], include=[\"pub_date\"]) will allow filtering on headline, also selecting pub_date, while fetching data only from the index. Using include will produce a smaller index than using a multiple column index but with the drawback that non-key columns can not be used for sorting or filtering. include is ignored for databases besides PostgreSQL. Index.name is required when using include. See the PostgreSQL documentation for more details about covering indexes. PostgreSQL-specific indexes In addition to B-Tree indexes (via Index), PostgreSQL also supports GistIndex and SpGistIndex indexes.","title":"Model index reference¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/models/indexes/","id":0},{"content_hash":"0346b0ab212c91d87e334de4ccbafaa5c17ec496c9f8159705d4c0f58a56ff0f","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:12.775096+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-business-verification","language":"","text":"What is Business Verification?ByEmmanuel AgwuApril 27, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Business VerificationWhat is the Importance Of Business Verification?What Organizations Require Business Verification?How To Conduct Effective Business VerificationAchieving Automate Business Verification with YouverifyBottom LineShare this postBusiness Verification is a crucial part of KYB regulations and processes. These processes are intended to mitigate or kick against fraudulent practices in commercial B2B environments. KYB regulations were introduced in 2016 by the Financial Crimes Network. When dealing with new businesses it is important to follow these protocols to avoid misrepresentation, money laundering and fraud. The global fraud detection and prevention market — which includes business verification technologies — is expected to grow from about $64 billion in 2025 to $246 billion by 2032, emphasizing the rising investment to combat fraud through verification and other methods. This article is an exploration of just what Business Verification is. Understanding Business Verification Business verification is the process of confirming the legitimacy and accuracy of information provided by a business or organisation. This process is typically carried out by financial institutions, government agencies, and other entities that need to verify the identity and credibility of a business for compliance or risk management purposes. Business verification usually involves gathering and reviewing documentation such as business registration and incorporation documents, tax filings, financial statements, and other relevant information. In some cases, third-party verification services may be used to verify information such as the existence of a business, its ownership structure, and its financial performance. The goal of business verification is to ensure that the information provided by a business is accurate and reliable and to prevent fraud and other forms of financial crime. What is the Importance Of Business Verification? Business verification is an important part of compliance and risk management efforts, particularly in industries such as banking, insurance, and investment management where the consequences of fraudulent or non-compliant activity can be significant. i. Preventing fraud: Business verification helps to prevent fraudulent activity by confirming the legitimacy of a business and its ownership structure. This is particularly important in industries such as banking, insurance, and investment management where the consequences of fraudulent activity can be significant. ii. Compliance: Business verification is often required by regulatory bodies to ensure compliance with laws and regulations. By verifying the legitimacy and accuracy of business information, regulatory compliance can be assured. iii. Risk management: Business verification is an important part of risk management, particularly in industries where the consequences of fraudulent or non-compliant activity can be significant. By verifying the legitimacy and accuracy of business information, the risk of financial crime and reputational damage can be reduced. iv. Reputation: Verifying the legitimacy and accuracy of business information helps to maintain the reputation of businesses and organisations. Inaccurate or fraudulent information can damage a business's reputation and lead to a loss of customer trust. v. Investment decisions: Business verification is important for investors who need to make informed investment decisions. Accurate and reliable information about a business's financial performance and ownership structure is essential for making sound investment decisions. Business verification is important for preventing fraud, ensuring compliance, managing risk, maintaining reputation, and making informed investment decisions. What Organizations Require Business Verification? Business verification is needed by a variety of entities that require assurance about the legitimacy and accuracy of business information. Some examples of major institutions that require business verification include: a. Financial Institutions Before opening accounts or issuing loans, banks, credit unions, and other financial institutions must confirm the identification and authenticity of firms. This saves them from a lot of errors and losses that arise from these errors and fraudulent or illicit activities that are perpetrated because of these errors or misrepresentation and forgery. b. Government entities: As part of their compliance and risk management activities, government entities like tax authorities, trade commissions, and regulatory bodies may demand that enterprises submit to verification. c. Insurance firms:In order to determine the level of risk involved in insuring a certain business, insurance companies may request business verification as part of their underwriting process. d. Suppliers and vendors: To maintain supply continuity and prevent fraud, companies that engage in supply chain operations may need to verify the legality and dependability of their suppliers and vendors. e. Investors: It is common for investors to conduct due diligence on a business before making investment decisions. This involves a thorough examination of the company's financial performance, ownership structure, management team, competitive landscape, and other key factors that may impact the investment's potential for success. Any entity that requires assurance about the legitimacy and accuracy of business information may need business verification. This includes financial institutions, government agencies, insurance companies, suppliers and vendors, and investors, among others. How To Conduct Effective Business Verification Follow the steps below to conduct effective business verification: 1. Collect Business Information Businesses will need to collect all the necessary business information such as the company's name, registration number, physical address, and phone number. 2. Verify Business Registration The business registration documents need to be checked to confirm that the company is legally registered with the relevant authorities. This step can be done by checking with the government registry or using a third-party verification service. 3. Check the Business License Verify the business licence if applicable to ensure the company is authorised to operate in its specific industry. 4. Confirm Ownership Confirm the ownership structure of the company and verify the identity of the owners, directors, and key personnel. 5. Verify Financial Information Verify the financial information of the company to assess its financial stability and any potential risks. This includes checking financial statements, bank statements, and credit reports. 6. Conduct Sanctions and Watchlist Checks Screen the company against global sanctions lists and watchlists to ensure that it is not involved in any illegal or suspicious activities. 7. Verify Business Operations Verify the company's operational details, including the products or services offered, the target market, and any business partnerships or affiliations. 8. Conduct a Site Visit Conduct site visits to confirm the physical location of the company and assess the business operations on the ground. 9. Document the Verification Process Document all the steps taken during the verification process and maintain a record of the information obtained. 10. Regularly Update Information Regularly update the business information and conduct ongoing monitoring to ensure continued compliance with regulatory requirements and mitigate risks associated with fraudulent activities. Conducting business verification in KYB involves collecting and verifying the company's identity and business details to comply with regulatory requirements and mitigate risks associated with fraudulent activities. Achieving Automate Business Verification with Youverify Business verification is now easier than ever due to AI-powered solutions that are able to connect with government-backed databases and automatically verify business information. Youverify for example, provides a Know Your Business (KYB) solution that allows organisations to verify details of a business in a single click and do not need to manually visit directories or the physical location of the business. With the Youverify global KYB solution, organisations can conduct advanced searches, receiving extensive information on the business which includes: Company InformationThe total shares value of the company A breakdown of the value of the shares for all individual shareholders, while also highlighting the person(s) of significant controlAll the Key personnel associated with the business and their designationOther associated companies of key personnelAffiliated companiesCompany history and Filings See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for business verification for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Bottom Line Business verification is an important part of KYB, and it is essential for all businesses to follow the protocols involved. Business verification shields businesses from unnecessary risks that can be avoided as well as sanctions since business verification makes part of KYB regulations introduced by financial crimes networks and imposed by central business and financial bodies or entities today. Related ArticlesKnow Your Business (KYB)Emmanuel Agwu•November 28, 2022Introducing The Youverify Global Know Your Business (KYB) SolutionRead MoreKnow Your Business (KYB)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•February 9, 20237 Steps to Establishing an Effective KYB processRead MoreKnow Your Business (KYB)Temitope Lawal•June 18, 2024How to Protect Your Business from Identity Fraud in the USRead More","title":"What is Business Verification?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-business-verification","id":1},{"content_hash":"6bcd6359b227bbc46317da3904202c2261ce238aa9d4b3f9dff7f65febcf345a","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:12.571945+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/06/france-italie-se-termine-par-un-match-nul_2073100_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives France-Italie se termine par un match nul Le Monde Publié le 06 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 06 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"France-Italie se termine par un match nul","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/06/france-italie-se-termine-par-un-match-nul_2073100_1819218.html","id":2},{"content_hash":"fabc9a2686345f6e3c57b804aa16aa32537db23205387309f7a27766ee853564","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:12.365198+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-a-hot-toddy-for-the-winter-chill-26189","language":"","text":"Health Check: a hot toddy for the winter chill Published: June 30, 2014 12:20am EDT They may not cure your cold but they’ll will help you forget your miseries. Drew Leavy/Flickr, CC BY-NC-ND https://theconversation.com/health-check-a-hot-toddy-for-the-winter-chill-26189 https://theconversation.com/health-check-a-hot-toddy-for-the-winter-chill-26189 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Feeling miserable? Is your nose blocked and your throat sore? If not, it will be sometime this winter, probably at least a couple of times. And then someone will probably say at your low ebb that “you need a hot toddy (or tottie)”. A hot toddy is the generic name for a hot drink that contains spirits, water, citrus, something sweet and something spicy. Most people use whiskey or brandy. The sweet component is made up of liberal amounts of sugar or honey. The preferred spices usually include the Christmassy ones like cinnamon, cloves, ginger, cardamom and/or nutmeg. Toddies have much in common with mulled wine (also variously known as hot wine, glögg, Glühwein, vin chaud), in which red wine or port plays essentially the same role as spirits, is mixed with sugar and spices, and is similarly used on chilly winter nights for the same revitalising purposes. Yet it is the humble toddy that has become colloquial for “tonic” or “pick-me up”. Whisky was historically known as the water of life. Becky Stern/Flickr, CC BY-SA The tradition of mulled wine and its efficacy dates back to ancient Rome. But toddies only joined the drink cart in the 16th and 17th centuries. It may be that if you had no access to wine (which was reserved for the sacrament, imported and/or prohibitively expensive), you had to improvise. Indeed, frugal innovation was a key attribute of “make-do” Calvinist ethic. In Latin, totie (totus) meant something that was “brought together or amalgamated”. The idiomatic “tottie” perhaps refers to the way its otherwise insoluble spices are slowly infused together into the alcohol over a low heat. The hot toddy is very different from the beverage made from the sap of palm trees across Asia (which is also known as toddy, kallu or palm wine). It is sometimes suggested that this is where the hot toddy may have appropriated its name from, as nostalgic travellers returning to a sunless world devoid of palm trees yearned for a hot substitute to remind them of warmer climes. Another explanation says the name “toddy” refers to the spring called “Tod’s Well” which was the main water supply to Edinburgh. The murky peat-stained water on which the Scots subsisted probably looked much like the murky spice-laden water-added whiskey concoction that they called a “toddy”. Whiskey was historically known as “aqua vitae” (the water of life) and its medicinal properties widely recommended as a cure-all. But in those days, whiskey was almost unpalatable (and still is to many). Another origins story suggests the “toddy” was invented by doctors simply as a means to make medicinal whiskey agreeable to women and children. Interestingly, the word “toddy” seems to come into use about the same time as (or a few glasses before) the word “toddle” meaning an unsteady gait. This also gives rise to the colloquial term for young children as “toddlers”. However it is concocted or wherever it originally came from, its therapeutic properties are now legendary. Allopathic traditions believe that preventing or treating an illness with its opposite is the best route. So the opposite of cold is appropriately hot, with added spices and alcohol to bring even more redness to the cheeks. This year, most of us will catch two or three colds. Micah Taylor/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA Certainly, the steam from any hot drink is able to dissolve nasal mucus to open blocked nose and sinuses, for a few minutes at least. This may be the sole basis of the legendary tonic effects of chicken soup. The citrus is a source of vitamin C. But even if you took vitamin C supplements every day for prevention, you still won’t avoid catching a cold. Nutmeg, cloves and cinnamon have long been used to treat colds and really do contain chemicals with antihistamine, anaesthetic and/or anti-inflammatory properties. But not in the small doses obtained in a palatable hot toddy. Probably the only proof is in fact the small amount of alcohol in a toddy, which has sedative and relaxant effects. As ever, high doses may make things worse for your cold. But with the spice and preparation time, you really only ever get to drink one hot toddy. Ultimately, the cold will get better by itself. But while you’re feeling down, a toddy can help you forget your miseries. Which is really what it’s for. Further reading – Health Check: does alcohol have medicinal properties? Alcohol Colds Winter Health Check Winter health Merlin Thomas, Baker Heart and Diabetes Institute Author Merlin Thomas Adjunct Professor of Preventive Medicine, Baker Heart and Diabetes Institute Disclosure statement Merlin Thomas does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.kt6dprws3","title":"Health Check: a hot toddy for the winter chill","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-a-hot-toddy-for-the-winter-chill-26189","id":3},{"content_hash":"9187ab4ef53a21a9b95b7b6611a27c9c77dab100359dddb4bcb45a53260bf68e","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:12.186452+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/business","language":"","text":"Business Oil prices jump 5% on Middle East tensions and US hurricane threatGlobal bond market sell-off continues and stocks fall amid reports White House has asked to see options for more strikes against IranTrump made ‘clear’ that media ban was due to critical coverage, court told11h agoStarbucks reportedly exploring Chipotle takeover12h agoTop bosses at Goldman Sachs to share bonus pot worth up to $500m11h agoMicrosoft suspended from applying for green cards for H-1B workers, JD Vance says11h agoSkepticism mires Trump’s Iowa steel plant project: ‘Promises are not the same as jobs’McDonald’s sued for allegedly using AI tool to determine pricing for franchisesDisney accuses Trump officials of ‘blatant violations’ of constitution amid FCC battleS&P 500 and Nasdaq surge to record highs after AI chipmaker rallyAll storiesSign up to the Business Today emailIn depthHideMillionaire tax no more? Rightwing groups lead effort to repeal Washington state lawAn initiative on November’s ballot aims to overturn the 9.9% tax on earnings above $1m – who’s winning the fight?The American dream remains elusive in the valley that inspired East of EdenRepublicans say they hate communists. So why do they like Donald Trump?Around the worldHideCanada’s Weston family buys Boots for $8.9bnEuro falls to 17-month low against dollar amid French debt fears‘The violence is off the scale’: staff and passengers at risk as assaults on trains in Great Britain soar to record highTech in cars can be used to snoop on you, Dutch spy chiefs warnFeatured topicsHideThe New Face of Small BusinessWhen her ‘soul cat’ died, she was bereft. Now she designs memorial jewelry to help others with pet lossMillennial financeFinancial bullying can ruin a marriage: first-person stories…… commentsIncome inequalityThe American dream remains elusive in the valley that inspired East of EdenRace and money‘I ain’t goin nowhere’: Gullah Geechee people fight off developers with a historic referendumMost viewedHideMost viewed in businessMost viewed Across the guardianMost viewed in businessTop bosses at Goldman Sachs to share bonus pot worth up to $500mStarbucks reportedly exploring Chipotle takeoverSkepticism mires Trump’s Iowa steel plant project: ‘Promises are not the same as jobs’Shell expects refineries to almost double the profit from every barrel of fuel madeMcDonald’s sued for allegedly using AI tool to determine pricing for franchisesJaguar targets young Americans with Type 01 EV – but it’s too long for UK parking spacesOil prices jump 5% on Middle East tensions and US hurricane threatLondon to Chicago BA flight declares emergency after dropping 28,000 feetOil and gas prices jump on Middle East shipping attacks, sending bond yields higher and stocks lower – as it happened Asos says customers’ names, contact details and search histories hackedMost viewed Across the guardianThree sisters who drowned in Brighton took their own lives, inquest findsThe dreadful decline of hosting at home: ‘I feel shame any time a friend comes over’Astronomers detect mysterious burst of energy from a galaxy far, far away Pentagon says execution of Fort Hood shooter will be livestreamedDoJ seeks to charge ex-Trump aide who gave key testimony on January 6 attackICE agent shoots man in New York City with five-year-old reportedly in carScientists discover 180,000 giant tortoises on one Seychelles island ‘Undistilled cheating’: Missouri voters furious in aftermath of Trump bid to rig electoral mapsCurtis Flowers, US man wrongfully imprisoned for 22 years, dies aged 56Anthropic bans users from ‘needless abusive or cruel behavior’ towards Claude","title":"Business and finance news from Guardian US | The Guardian","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/business","id":4},{"content_hash":"2f4aa0aa1259a1c6f846de034b2264f32a0afb8de5233f3e190e1d1ebd391f07","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:30:11.757274+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/faqs/banking-faqs/list-of-fixed-deposit-rates-of-various-banks/articleshow/63123024.cms","language":"","text":"List of fixed deposit rates of various banks - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSSign InTOIBusinessFAQsFinancial LiteracyIndia BusinessFinancial CalculatorsInternational BusinessMarketsVideosCryptocurrencyTOI DialoguesCredit ScoreWealthSensexPhotosStartupsBudget 2025Web StoriesFinancial Literacy SummitToday's ePaperGold FAQsPension Funds FAQsAadhaar FAQsIncome Tax FAQsMarket FAQsNewsBusiness NewsFAQs NewsList of fixed deposit rates of various banksTrendingHindenburg ResearchAdani GroupInfosys Share PriceJanuary Bank HolidaysBank Holidays8th Pay CommissionBudget 2025Hindenburg ResearchAdani GroupInfosys Share PriceJanuary Bank HolidaysBank Holidays8th Pay CommissionBudget 2025Hindenburg ResearchAdani GroupInfosys Share PriceJanuary Bank HolidaysBank Holidays8th Pay CommissionBudget 2025This story is from March 1, 2018List of fixed deposit rates of various banksTOI-Online / Mar 1, 2018, 12:46 ISTShareAA+Text SizeSmallMediumLargeA fixed deposit is a form of investment method offered by banks and several non-banking financial companies (NBFCs). In a fixed deposit, you deposit a certain amount of money for a fixed time period during which this money earns interest. Fixed deposit is a tried and tested way to increase your savings, as it gives a higher rate of interest than savings accounts A fixed deposit is a form of investment method offered by banks and several non-banking financial companies (NBFCs). In a fixed deposit, you deposit a certain amount of money for a fixed time period during which this money earns interest. Fixed deposit is a tried and tested way to increase your savings, as it gives a higher rate of interest than savings accounts. In addition to this, FDs are considered to be one of the safest investment options, as they are independent of market fluctuations, and returns on them are assured. Below-mentioned is the list of fixed deposit rates of various banks: Bank FD Rate of Interest Senior Citizen FD Rates SBI Fixed Deposit 5.25% - 6.25% 5.75% - 6.75% HDFC Bank Fixed Deposit 3.50% - 6.75% 4.00% - 7.25% ICICI Bank Fixed Deposit 4.00% - 6.75% 4.50% - 7.25% Axis Bank 3.50% - 6.85% 3.50% - 7.35% Kotak Bank 3.50% - 6.85% 4.00% - 7.35% IDFC Bank 4.00% - 7.50% 4.50% - 8.00% Bank of Baroda 4.25% - 6.55% 4.75% - 7.05% Citibank 3.00% - 5.25% 3.50% - 5.75% IDBI Bank 4.25% - 6.75% 4.25% - 7.25% Indian Bank 4.50% - 6.50% 5.00% - 7.00% Indian Overseas Bank 4.00% - 6.75% 4.50% - 7.25% OBC 4.00% - 6.60% - PNB 5.25% - 6.60% 5.75% - 7.10% HDFC 7.35% - 7.40% 7.60% - 7.65% PNB Housing Finance 7.45% - 7.45% 7.70% - 7.70% Allahabad Bank 4.00% - 6.50% - Andhra Bank 4.00% - 6.50% 4.50% - 7.00% Bank of India 5.25% - 6.60% 5.25% - 7.10% Bank of Maharashtra 4.25% - 6.50% 4.25% - 7.00% Canara Bank 4.20% - 6.50% 4.70% - 7.00% Central Bank of India 4.75% - 6.60% 5.25% - 7.00% Dena Bank 4.00% - 6.50% 4.50% - 7.00% Punjab and Sind Bank 4.00% - 6.60% 4.00% - 7.10% UCO Bank 4.50% - 6.50% - Union Bank of India 5.00% - 6.75% 5.50% - 7.25% Vijaya Bank 4.00% - 6.60% 4.50% - 7.10% United Bank of India 4.00% - 6.10% 4.00% - 6.50% Federal Bank 3.50% - 6.75% 4.00% - 7.25% Bandhan Bank 3.50% - 7.00% 4.00% - 7.50% HSBC Bank 3.00% - 6.25% 3.50% - 6.75% South Indian Bank 4.00% - 7.10% 4.50% - 7.60% IndusInd Bank 4.00% - 6.85% 4.50% - 7.35% DCB Bank 4.00% - 7.55% 4.50% - 8.05% Karur Vysya Bank 5.00% - 7.00% 5.00% - 7.50% DHFL 7.70% - 8.00% 7.95% - 8.25% LIC Housing Finance 7.30% - 7.45% 7.55% - 7.70% Syndicate Bank 4.75% - 6.50% 5.25% - 7.00% DBS Bank 4.00% - 7.20% 4.00% - 7.20% Karnataka Bank 3.50% - 7.25% 4.00% - 7.75% Dhan Laxmi Bank 4.00% - 6.60% 4.00% - 7.10% Lakshmi Vilas Bank 4.50% - 7.25% 4.50% - 7.75% RBL Bank 5.00% - 7.20% 5.50% - 7.70% Standard Chartered Bank 4.25% - 6.85% 5.00% - 7.35% Corporation Bank 4.50% - 6.70% 5.00% - 7.20% Jammu And Kashmir Bank 5.00% - 6.75% 5.50% - 7.25% Yes Bank 5.00% - 6.75% 5.50% - 7.25% Stay informed with the latest Business News on Times of India. Explore the list of Bank Holidays, stay informed about Budget 2025, discover the new Income Tax Slabs, and use the Income Tax Calculator for hassle-free tax planning.Unlock Investment Potential: Enroll in ET's Stock Valuation Workshop - Batch 3. Secure Your Spot Now!End of ArticleFOLLOW US ON SOCIAL MEDIA Visual Stories Previous9 Simple Ways to Boost Brain Power While StudyingeducationNine minute daily habits that will change your life: Student editioneducation9 Good Study Habits to Developeducation9 Supercharged Study Habits for Exam Successeducation9 Common Myths About Board Exam Preparation Debunkededucation8 life-changing benefits of learning one new thing every dayeducation8 essential memory techniques every student needs to mastereducation8 Clear Indicators That Suggest You Could Excel as an Engineereducation9 Myths About Online Learning Debunked: What Every Student Should Knoweducation8 Online Resources for Free Books to Help You Study at Homeeducation Next123 Photostories Archana Parmeet recall their humble beginnings in Industry​5 lesser-known benefits of keeping your life private, as per psychology​The 6 dates of 'Shahi snan' and the importance each of them holds9 tiny habits that can boost memory and increase retention powerFrom Himanshi Khurana getting hospitalised to Chum Darang- Karan Veer Mehra confessing their feelings to each other; Top TV newsWeekly Horoscope: January 20th to 26th, 20255 things people notice about you at first meeting, as per psychologyBigg Boss 18: Confirmed list of the contestants of Salman Khan hosted controversial show​Bigg Boss Tamil 8: From strong contender to entertainer of the house; Vishal’s remarkable journey​Bigg Boss 18: From Shilpa Shirodkar making Vivian Dsena the first 'Time God' to Karan Veer Mehra shaving Rajat Dalal's beard; A look at defining moments before the finaleHow tall are Trump family members​Bigg Boss Tamil: Here's what’s keeping the winners of previous seasons busy​123Hot PicksArvind KejriwalColdplay Mumbai concertSaif Ali Khan AttackerColton Moore8th Pay CommissionStock Market TodayRailway Budget 2025TOP TRENDINGDwyane WadeGood Study Habits to DevelopNBA Trade NewsPatrick MahomesLa Lakers Trade RumorsNBA Trade RumorMaha Kumbh 2025Spirit Airlines hoodieCBSEVirat Kohli IPL CareerTired of too many a