J+4\u0002Û\u0013Y\u0007f©0È$^µOê;BÕOTo`nb\u0011øWþ¦J\u001aØ\u0011|üSÌ*ëâxH-ëÜv®t\u000fU\u0002k ì\u0014C`úá\u0016\u0013øA\u0001hÛÌ�üL\u0002�ZêvN±©gIõ:\u0005b0\u00155 ö\u0004¡dÂ®×ad·�ÇL[ �À<Ô@Áv0r\u0011%\u0016r\u0010H \u001a\u0011 SÍ\u0006 ½mdÌ ÁkÊÈ¸·ò§ùÔ}\u0006¥3\u0018_\u0002\u0004³Ó(ÿ�´À ÷\u0011 \u0002#Róî\u0014¿çÿ�\b²ç( ½A\u0005Ù°ÿ�¹cGt\u0003)]cÈ`\u0007\u0005m%ù(I}e\u0003X©DåzI´*±,\u001bD®ásÅ£t*5\u0003HÐ\u0004-(v`Mv ,4õ0\u000fïS?\bqyDK?ÿÙ","title":"","url":"https://i0.wp.com/prnigeria.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Village-fleeing-Bandits.jpg?fit=528%2C456&ssl=1","id":60},{"content_hash":"79bdfbaca1ac1f0296a6fce3cf986dee91b8795224f5f8d843c9000df3abe720","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:57.714303+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-04-09/s-shakthidharan-shakthi-windham-campbell-prize/106545452","language":"","text":"Australian playwright S Shakthidharan wins $250,000 Windham-Campbell PrizeBy Hannah StoryABC ArtsTopic:Awards and PrizesPosted Thu 9 Apr 2026 at 11:33amThu 9 Apr 2026 at 11:33amThu 9 Apr 2026 at 11:33am, updated Thu 9 Apr 2026 at 12:24pmThu 9 Apr 2026 at 12:24pmThu 9 Apr 2026 at 12:24pmS Shakthidharan has won a major international prize for drama, which awards $250,000 so a writer can focus on their work without financial pressures. (Supplied: Belvoir)abc.net.au/news/s-shakthidharan-shakthi-windham-campbell-prize/106545452Link copiedShareShare articleSri Lankan Australian playwright S Shakthidharan, widely known as Shakthi, has won the $US175,000 ($250,000) Windham-Campbell Prize for drama.The international prize is awarded each year to writers of fiction, non-fiction, poetry and drama for their body of work. Writers are nominated secretly and cannot apply.Shakthi won the award for his ambitious, multigenerational plays exploring Sri Lankan Tamil migrant experiences, including his debut Counting and Cracking. The play, co-written with Belvoir artistic director Eamon Flack, also won the $100,000 Victorian Prize for Literature in 2020.Counting and Cracking spans nearly five decades and runs at three-and-a-half hours. (Supplied: Belvoir/Brett Boardman)Shakthi told ABC Arts he \"felt proud our stories can be on that global stage\". He found out he won via email a few weeks ago while shooting his debut movie The Laugh of Lakshmi, about a mother and son separated by civil war, in Sri Lanka.\"The stories I tell are not the usual stories this country tells,\" he said.\"To get that global recognition hopefully puts forward a version of Australia which is a bit more progressive than our current reality here.\"Shakthi added the money also makes it possible for him to continue to pursue a career as a writer.\"I still have to fight hard to do anything in this country,\" he said.\"It's so incredible to be able to write from a place of focusing on the art.\"Rajan Velu, Kalieaswari Srinivasan and Anandavalli in Shakthi's second play, The Jungle and the Sea, at Belvoir in 2022. (Supplied: Belvoir/Sriram Jeyaraman)Writing on Instagram, Shakthi said he was \"still in shock\" about his win.\"The prize is for an artist's body of work, he wrote. The judges decide the winners by reading their work. This means a group of strangers overseas — who had never heard of me — were taken in by these stories of Asia and Australia and chose to embrace them.\"I like that. It's what writing can do: pull you in to the specific, vulnerable, emotional truths of a place and a people you have never encountered before.\"Inspired by Shakthi's family history, Counting and Cracking went on to tour the UK and New York. His family was forced to leave Sri Lanka following the 1983 Black July pogrom in Colombo, which killed an estimated 5,600 Tamils.Speaking to ABC Arts in 2024, when the play opened in Melbourne, Shakthi said making Counting and Cracking helped him and his mother come to terms with their migrant identities.\"To tell the gloriously complex story of your community in full public view, and to have other people embrace that, has been a radical act of belonging.\"In 2022, Shakthi reunited with Flack for The Jungle and the Sea, which explicitly uncovered the toll of the Sri Lankan civil war (1983-2009).Sri Lankan civil war playShakthidharan’s acclaimed debut play broke silences in his home and in his community and his latest contends with more recent, painful history.His latest play, The Wrong Gods, about the tension between progress and tradition, and ensuing environmental degradation, opened in Sydney in 2025.Last year, Shakthi also published his debut memoir, Gather Up Your World in One Long Breath.The Windham-Campbell judges described Shakthi as a \"rare storyteller whose work traverses time and space while remaining anchored in core emotional truths\".Shakthi is also director and co-founder of Western Sydney theatre company Kurinji. (ABC Arts: Anna Kucera)\"Defying local expectations, Shakthidharan brings his whole self — both the Eastern and Western aspects — to his work, opening space for a richer definition of what 'Australian theatre' can be.\"Other winners of the Windham-Campbell Prizes this year include British novelist Gwendoline Riley and Belgian American writer Lucy Sante. Past Australian winners of the prize include author Helen Garner, playwright Patricia Cornelius and poet Ali Cobby Eckermann.The Jungle and the Sea is at Belvoir St Theatre from July 11-Aug 2, and Melbourne Theatre Company from August 14-September 12. Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated stories'You have to laugh': Why this critique of corporate work culture is a comedyTopic:TheatreMeet the 25yo director at the helm of Miranda Otto's return to the stageTopic:TheatreHow a 5,000-year-old garment went from being called 'traditional' to the Met GalaTopic:FashionRelated topicsArtArts, Culture and EntertainmentAustraliaAwards and PrizesBiographyBooksPerforming ArtsSri LankaSydneyTheatreUnited StatesTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Australian playwright S Shakthidharan wins $250,000 Windham-Campbell Prize","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-04-09/s-shakthidharan-shakthi-windham-campbell-prize/106545452","id":61},{"content_hash":"b5c67051cb37b0f9ac5e70d6f643bacf7e0c16b4cd19343a9a227302f688afa6","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:57.570380+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/keep-the-joy-of-giving-alive-year-round-with-a-donor-advised-fund.html","language":"","text":"Keep charitable giving alive year-round with a donor-advised fund Donor-advised funds are a “one-stop shop” to manage your charitable giving, offering convenience, flexibility and tax advantages GIVING IS LIKELY TOP OF MIND FOR YOU AT THE END OF THE YEAR as nonprofits tend to be more insistent in their appeals. Have you wondered if there were a more organized and thoughtful way to make a difference year-round? A way to plan for a more powerful and long-lasting impact? If so, consider a donor-advised fund (DAF), a more structured way of giving that has been steadily gaining popularity with donors. 1 of 4 A donor-advised fund account (DAF) is a personal charitable giving account that can be funded with gifts of appreciated publicly traded stock, mutual funds or cash. You also can make contributions of complex assets, including illiquid and nonfinancial assets ― such as closely held business interests, commercial or residential real estate, timberland, farms and ranches, as well as gas, oil and mineral rights ― without incurring capital gains taxes. DAFs compare favorably with other structured ways to organize your charitable giving. They allow you to avoid the higher costs, administrative hurdles and annual distribution minimums of a private foundation. In addition, a DAF is easy to set up and maintain, and you can manage all your giving activities online. For clients who value privacy, DAFs, including the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund (CGF) ― provide two layers of confidentiality. You can name your fund after your family or choose a name unrelated to you. And with each grant, CGF clients may elect to have their name and the name of their fund accompany each grant or remain anonymous. There are many ways a DAF could potentially help you simplify your giving year-round, and for years to come. Donald Greene, National Donor-Advised Fund Executive at Bank of America Private Bank​ notes five of the primary benefits. “Donor-advised funds can meet the needs of a wide range of donors from experienced philanthropists to those just getting started.” ― Donald Greene, National Donor-Advised Fund Executive at Bank of America Private Bank​ DAFs offer immediate, market-rate tax benefits You can put a lump sum into a DAF and then have the fund distribute cash to organizations of your choice at your own pace. “Doing so gives you immediate, market-rate, maximum charitable deductions, while grants can be made to your favorite charities over an extended period,” says Greene. You can also forward any unused charitable income tax deduction for up to five years while assets in the fund can grow tax-free, maximizing your impact. DAFs offer flexibility Donor-advised funds are designed to meet the needs of a wide variety of donors. The CGF, for example, offers clients access to more than 1.9 million IRS-approved charities in the CGF directory of nonprofits. Some donors are just starting out as philanthropists and are looking for help in determining how best to make a charitable impact, while other donors know exactly the charities they want to give to, how much they want to give and when. For these donors, the database can serve as a place to find appropriate addresses or contact information. DAFs allow your contributions the potential for growth A DAF keeps assets invested in your charitable giving account while you determine which charities will receive grants, what the amounts will be and when they will be given, in line with your charitable goals. The CGF offers donors eight different investment objectives from which to choose, each with a distinct strategic asset allocation. Portfolios are customized to your goals and time frames, and they provide you with a choice of sustainable, actively-managed and passive (all ETF) portfolios. This customization, along with professional management of your assets, may allow your contributions to continue to grow free from capital gains obligations and potentially enhance the overall charitable impact of your gift. DAFs organize your giving in one place, providing convenience Rather than practicing “checkbook philanthropy” by making donations to individual charities and requesting receipts from each, you can streamline the administrative process through a DAF. The CGF has exceptional digital capabilities. Your account can be managed online with ease and security. You have the ability to make grant recommendations and update investment objectives online on the MyMerrill or Bank of America Private Bank app or online portal. You also are able to set up single or recurring grants anytime using the bank’s secure website. The CGF performs all administrative tasks, including regulatory reporting, helping to free up your time. DAFs enable you to give with purpose and receive recognition DAFs sit squarely at the intersection of passion and purpose, says Greene. “Giving through the CGF, a donor-advised fund is an opportunity for clients who want to make charitable contributions now, but also determine how and where to distribute these funds over time,” he notes. “You can be very purposeful, strategic and impactful.” In return you can choose to continue to receive acknowledgments of your gifts and you have the personal satisfaction of knowing that your gifts are making a difference in the world. Depending upon how important privacy is to you, a DAF allows you the option of giving your chosen non-profit your name or allowing you to remain anonymous. Ask your advisor for more information on how a donor-advised fund works, and whether the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund might help you meet your giving goals. Choose your advisor in a more personalized way All our advisors are committed to putting your needs and priorities first. Find some who match your personal preferences too. Loading... Try Advisor Match Answer a few questions Want us to contact you? Submit a request Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor Donor-advised fund and private foundation management are provided by Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America N.A., Member FDIC and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation. Donor-advised fund management is provided by Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., Member FDIC and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). Trust, fiduciary, and investment management services are provided by wholly owned banking affiliates of BofA Corp., including Bank of America, N.A. and its agents. Brokerage services may be performed by wholly owned brokerage affiliates of BofA Corp., including Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated (also referred to as “MLPF&S” or “Merrill”). Merrill is a registered broker-dealer, a registered investment adviser and Member SIPC. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you How to integrate complex assets into your giving strategy Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles The smarter way to give – the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund (CGF) Read more Charitable vehicles comparison Read more","title":"Keep charitable giving alive year-round with a donor-advised fund","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/keep-the-joy-of-giving-alive-year-round-with-a-donor-advised-fund.html","id":62},{"content_hash":"37cb647d325f93a974450f34329c80f2f642e44dfa55f59e3d593f894e845aec","domain":"www.dw.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:57.393587+00:00","final_url":"https://www.dw.com/en/russias-war-in-ukraine/t-60931789","language":"","text":"Russia's war in Ukraine Skip to contentSkip to main menuSkip to more DW sitesLatest videosLatest audioRegionsAfricaAsiaEuropeLatin AmericaMiddle EastNorth AmericaGermanyTopicsClimateEqualityHealthHuman RightsMigrationTechnologyCategoriesBusinessScienceEnvironmentCultureSportsIn focusIsraelNobel PrizeUkraineLatest audioLatest videosLive TVAdvertisementImage: Zaporizhzhia Regional Military Administration/REUTERSRussia's war in UkraineRussia launched a full-scale war on Ukraine in February 2022, an escalation of a conflict that began with Russia's annexation of Crimea in 2014.Skip next section NewsNews10/09/2026October 9, 2026Ukraine, Europe, US officials to meet for talks to end war10/08/2026October 8, 2026South Korea recalls Ukraine ambassador amid POW dispute10/08/2026October 8, 2026Russian strike on buses in Ukraine's Kramatorsk kills 3010/04/2026October 4, 2026Germany's Merz visits Kyiv amid Russian strikes10/03/2026October 3, 2026Ukraine to step up oil attacks as Russia hits Kyiv bridges10/02/2026October 2, 2026Ukraine: Kyiv bridges shut after fatal Russian drone assaultShow moreSkip next section Reports & AnalysisReports & AnalysisBlack Sea security: Is Bulgaria the weak link?Black Sea security: Is Bulgaria the weak link?Two ships have sunk in the Black Sea after recent drone attacks. What does that mean for security in the region?Politics10/08/2026October 8, 2026Ukraine is not likely to go offline despite attacksUkraine is not likely to go offline despite attacksAs Russia targets Ukraine's data centers, experts say the country's internet system is too robust to shut down entirely.Technology10/08/2026October 8, 2026NATO members in Kyiv: 'We stand unwavering with Ukraine'NATO members in Kyiv: 'We stand unwavering with Ukraine'NATO's top military leaders have made a rare joint visit to Ukraine, signaling continued allied support for the country.Conflicts10/08/2026October 8, 202614:40 minCan Bulgaria be a safe gateway for Asian imports to the EU?Can Bulgaria be a safe gateway for Asian imports to the EU?Bulgaria could benefit from the EU's push for more trade via the \"Middle Corridor,\" but security concerns remain. Trade10/08/2026October 8, 2026Merz pledges military and financial aid on visit to UkraineMerz pledges military and financial aid on visit to UkraineThe German chancellor's visit comes as Ukraine faces a fifth — and possibly toughest — winter of war with Russia. Conflicts10/04/2026October 4, 202602:03 minWhat can German Chancellor Friedrich Merz offer Ukraine?What can German Chancellor Friedrich Merz offer Ukraine?Merz arrived in Kyiv for an unannounced visit. Germany is among Ukraine's largest supporters. Conflicts10/04/2026October 4, 202610:44 minShow moreAdvertisementSkip next section In the spotlightIn the spotlightLatvians head to the polls with Russia on their minds Security fears dominate the agenda ahread of the vote, but so does a debate over the economic ties with Moscow.Politics10/01/2026October 1, 2026Why is Russia seizing control of European companies?Why is Russia tightening its grip on European-owned companies but taking a different approach toward American firms?Conflicts10/01/2026October 1, 202611:25 minGlobal trade weathers Iran, Ukraine and Trump's tariffsSeveral major shocks in six years have stalled globalization. Is this just a setback or the start of a great reversal?Trade09/29/2026September 29, 2026Why diesel prices are soaring and what it means for EuropeDiesel prices are soaring and the US is considering an export ban. But why is diesel becoming so expensive so fast?Business09/28/2026September 28, 2026Lithuanian prime minister urges stronger NATO air defensePrime Minister Mindaugas Sinkevicius outlined threats from Russia and Belarus, and NATO's need for stronger air defense.Politics09/25/2026September 25, 2026Warnings of famine grow in Ukrainian town of Oleshky Food supplies to the town of Oleshky, in the occupied part of the southern Kherson region, have been cut off since May.Conflicts09/23/2026September 23, 2026All content (3467) on this topicFooterAbout DWWho we arePressDW Global Media ForumDW offersLearn GermanDW AkademieServiceNewslettersReceptionFAQContactTopics from A to ZHeadlinesB2BSales & DistributionTravelAdvertisingGerman News ServiceFollow us onAdd DW as preferred source on Google© 2026 Deutsche WellePrivacy PolicyLegal noticeDigital accessibilityData privacy settings","title":"Russia's war in Ukraine","url":"https://www.dw.com/en/russias-war-in-ukraine/t-60931789","id":63},{"content_hash":"f3eebc37eaaad8c6c83f6a50ba21570ba64d14119702cdecffa53190cb9be649","domain":"tutorialspoint.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:57.044553+00:00","final_url":"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/article/how-can-we-convert-character-array-to-a-reader-in-java","language":"","text":"How can we convert character array to a Reader in Java? Java Object Oriented Programming Programming Converting a character array to a reader in Java means treating the array as a stream of characters to be read sequentially. Let's quickly learn about character array and its creation in Java: In Java, a character array is an array that holds elements of only char type, and it is also used to store a sequence of characters. Following is the syntax to define a character array in Java: char char_array_name[] = {'char1', 'char2', 'char3'....} or char char_array_name[] = new char[size]; Here, char_array_name: The name of the character array. char_values: These are the char type values that the array holds. size: The number of elements that the character array can hold. Converting Character Array to Reader using CharArrayReader To convert a character array to a reader in Java we have a class named Reader. In Java, the Reader class is an abstract class that works as the basis for reading a character stream. We can use its subclass CharArrayReader while converting the character array to a reader process. The CharArrayReader can implement a character buffer that can be used as a character input stream. This class reads characters from a character array either completely or partially starting from an offset. Syntax Following is the syntax for creating a CharArrayReader class: public class CharArrayReader extends Reader Here, the CharArrayReader class extends on Reader, because the Reader is the parent class of it. Example In the following program, we use the CharArrayReader class to treat a character array as a stream and use the BufferedReader class to read and print its content line by line: import java.io.BufferedReader; import java.io.CharArrayReader; public class CharToReader { public static void main(String args[]) throws Exception { //creating a character array with initial values char array[] = { 'T', 'u', 't', 'o', 'r', 'i', 'a', 'l', 's', ' ', 'P', 'o', 'i', 'n', 't'}; System.out.println(\"The character array elements are: \"); for(int i = 0; i<array.length; i++){ System.out.print(array[i] + \" \"); } CharArrayReader car = new CharArrayReader(array); BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(car); String line; System.out.println(\"\\nAfter converting character array to a reader: \"); while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) { System.out.println(line); } } } The above program displays the following output: The character array elements are: T u t o r i a l s P o i n t After converting character array to a reader: Tutorials Point Example 2 This is another similar example of converting a character array to a reader using the CharArrayReader class. We haven't used the BufferedReader class; instead, we directly read characters from the CharArrayReader using its read() method in a loop: import java.io.CharArrayReader; import java.io.IOException; public class CharToReader { public static void main(String args[]) throws Exception { //creating a character array char[] charArray = {'T', 'u', 't', 'o', 'r', 'i', 'x'}; System.out.println(\"The character array is: \"); for(int i = 0; i<charArray.length; i++){ System.out.print(charArray[i] + \" \"); } //using CharArrayReader class try (CharArrayReader reader = new CharArrayReader(charArray)) { int value; System.out.println(\"\\nAfter converting to a reader: \"); while ((value = reader.read()) != -1) { char character = (char) value; System.out.print(character); } } catch (IOException e) { e.printStackTrace(); } } } Following is the output of the above program: The character array is: T u t o r i x After converting to a reader: Tutorix Vivek Verma Updated on: 2025-08-28T16:19:10+05:30 588 Views Learn More in Our Tutorials Unicode Conversion from/to Reader/Writer Java I18n Java - ByteArrayOutputStream toString(String charsetName) method Java Java - ObjectInputStream readChar() method Java Java - FileReader read(char[] cbuf, int off, int len) method Java Java.lang.String.valueOf(char[] data) Method Java Print Page Previous Next Advertisements","title":"How can we convert character array to a Reader in Java?","url":"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/article/how-can-we-convert-character-array-to-a-reader-in-java","id":64},{"content_hash":"31a474383b0975195743ae0c1316159e1b6f580b0153b2e7ae34c9379efc0cd5","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.788308+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/la-syrie-n-a-pas-encore-pris-de-decision-a-l-egard-de-la-defense-du-proche-orient_2071907_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives LA SYRIE N'A PAS ENCORE PRIS DE DÉCISION à l'égard de la défense du Proche-Orient Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"LA SYRIE N'A PAS ENCORE PRIS DE DÉCISION à l'égard de la défense du Proche-Orient","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/la-syrie-n-a-pas-encore-pris-de-decision-a-l-egard-de-la-defense-du-proche-orient_2071907_1819218.html","id":65},{"content_hash":"76e4a5550001a00119066a4b47c8c6810a291cdabb963cbd6b20edd525549441","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.766983+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/article-failing-to-fulfill-icc-warrants-is-also-a-failure-to-defend-canadas/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:The International Criminal Court (ICC) in The Hague, Netherlands. In 2024, the ICC issued an arrest warrant for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, charging him with war crimes and crimes against humanity over the war in Gaza.Piroschka Van De Wouw/Reuters CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountMark Kersten is an assistant professor in criminal justice at the University of the Fraser Valley and a senior consultant at the Wayamo Foundation.With Canada’s sovereignty coming under consistent threat from the United States, Ottawa should take every opportunity it gets to articulate – to Canadians and others – that we are a proud and sovereign state. That includes the way we engage with international organizations, such as the International Criminal Court (ICC). On that point, however, the Canadian government is signalling that our sovereignty is up for negotiation. In November, 2024, the ICC issued an arrest warrant for Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, charging him (as well as then-defence minister Yoav Gallant and three since-deceased Hamas leaders) with war crimes and crimes against humanity over the war in Gaza. The warrant was based on a careful investigation, a review of the evidence against Mr. Netanyahu (including by world-renowned judge and Holocaust survivor Theodor Meron), and the approval of ICC judges. Open this photo in gallery:Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu holds a news conference, following a U.S.-Iran deal, in Jerusalem, in June.Ronen Zvulun/ReutersThen-prime minister Justin Trudeau said Canada, a member-state of the Court, would “abide” by the decision and enforce the warrant. A year later, Prime Minister Mark Carney doubled down, telling Bloomberg twice that Mr. Netanyahu would be arrested if he came to Canada.That September, the Israeli leader travelled to the United Nations General Assembly in New York. The U.S. is not a member-state of the ICC and so had no legal obligation to arrest or surrender Mr. Netanyahu to the Court. But something curious happened along the way: The aircraft carrying Mr. Netanyahu did not take the most direct route from Tel Aviv to New York. Instead, the plane weaved to evade numerous European states as well as Canada – presumably out of fear that the aircraft might be forced to land and Mr. Netanyahu would be sent to The Hague.The diverted travel path was a small but important victory for justice and accountability. Mr. Netanyahu made it to New York, but his world had changed. He could not do whatever he wanted. If nothing else, the ICC warrant against him meant that his world had shrunk.Canadian judge among four ICC officials sanctioned by U.S.Since then, Mr. Netanyahu has travelled to the U.S. on three separate occasions. On each, he entered Canadian airspace, travelling over our Atlantic provinces. Ottawa has been mum on whether it allowed this to happen, whether the Israeli government was expressly permitted to travel through Canadian airspace, or how it believes that letting the Israeli leader travel over Canada aligns with our obligations to detain and surrender ICC suspects to the Court.This, ultimately, is a matter of our sovereignty. Under international law, states exercise sovereignty over their territory, which extends to their land mass, coastal waters and the airspace above them (up to the edge of outer space). Canada has previously – and rightfully – excluded foreign actors from entering the skies above the country. After Russia’s full-scale invasion and war of aggression on Ukraine in February, 2022, Canada immediately closed its airspace to any Russian planes – a defiant act of sovereign prerogative in solidarity with the people of Ukraine.Late last month, a Global Affairs spokesperson suggested to the CBC that it was up to Israel to decide whether to fly through Canadian airspace, adding that “Global Affairs Canada does not comment on which route a foreign state chooses to take.”U.S. campaign against international court threatens to jeopardize Darfur probeThis is nonsense. It is up to Canada, and Canada alone, to decide who enters our territory. All of this comes at a time when the Trump administration has been actively seeking to undermine Canada’s sovereignty, relentlessly saying that the U.S. should make us a “51st state”. It also comes at a time when Washington has launched an unprecedented assault on the ICC, a key prong of which is sanctioning the Court’s judges, including Canada’s Kimberly Prost.This should lead Ottawa to a simple conclusion: defending the ICC today is about defending Canadian sovereignty. Ensuring that our airspace is not breached by suspected atrocity perpetrators – regardless of where they are from – is about protecting our territorial integrity as much as international justice. Standing firm in our conviction that a Court that Canada helped build, as well as its judges, should be protected against unwarranted attacks is not just about international law: it speaks to who we are, what we value, and whether we take our sovereignty seriously. Doing so might not stop bullies, but pulling our punches will only invite more threats to our integrity as a country.In failing to stand up for the ICC, Canada is failing to stand up for itself. It also risks continuing to, as Mr. Carney denounced in his Davos speech, engage in the “performance of sovereignty while accepting subordination.”","title":"Failing to fulfill ICC warrants is also a failure to defend Canada’s sovereignty","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/article-failing-to-fulfill-icc-warrants-is-also-a-failure-to-defend-canadas/","id":66},{"content_hash":"f75f48651d8f81a2660b16aaabb03bf0c49c3a2f5664d7b72833867c364110fd","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.763159+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-carbon-tax-repeal-29389","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the carbon tax repeal Published: July 17, 2014 9:12pm EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-carbon-tax-repeal-29389 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-carbon-tax-repeal-29389 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics including the carbon tax repeal, opinion polls, Bill Shorten’s strong stance on an emissions trading scheme, the mining tax repeal and the rank and file Greens who would like Christine Milne to support the restoration of the fuel excise. Video The Week in Politics Stephen Parker, Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Authors Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.tkram436t","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the carbon tax repeal","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-carbon-tax-repeal-29389","id":67},{"content_hash":"813b7ce4b944c3dbad70b3b55bb9335a25b1fab693b1bc2f6ccfd7fc3286e8fe","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.736240+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-address-verification-service-avs","language":"","text":"What is Address Verification Service (AVS)?ByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMay 3, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Address Verification Service (AVS)How Does Address Verification Service Work?Achieving Address Verification with YouverifyBottom LineShare this postAddress verification is an important aspect of KYC and KYB processes. Address verification services are processes companies and organisations use to verify the accuracy of an individual's address information. This service is commonly used by businesses that ship products or provide services to customers, as well as financial institutions that need to verify the identity of their customers. Understanding Address Verification Service (AVS) Address verification services work by comparing the address information provided by the customer to a database of valid addresses. The database is typically maintained by a third-party provider specialising in address verification. The service can verify whether an address exists, is valid, and matches the information on file with the post office. Address verification services can also check for spelling errors and formatting issues that may cause problems with delivery/ billing, or worse, non-compliance. By verifying the accuracy of an address, businesses can reduce the risk of shipping products to the wrong address, sending invoices to the wrong person, or encountering other logistical problems. In addition to reducing errors and improving efficacy, address verification services can also help businesses comply with regulatory requirements related to anti-money laundering and know-your-customer regulations. These regulations require financial institutions to verify the identity of their customers, which can include verifying their address information. Address verification services are an important tool for businesses and organisations that need to verify the accuracy of their customers' address information. Businesses can reduce errors, improve efficiency, and comply with regulatory requirements by using these services. Address Verification Services (AVS) is a useful tool provided by credit card processors and issuing banks to merchants to prevent fraudulent credit card transactions. It works by checking the billing address provided by the card user against the billing address on record at the issuing bank. This helps detect suspicious transactions and reduce the risk of credit card fraud. Essentially, AVS safeguards merchants and cardholders by verifying that the address provided is valid and matches the information on file with the bank. By doing so, AVS can help protect against unauthorised purchases and fraudulent activities. Related Article - How To Get A Proof of Address How Does Address Verification Service Work? Address Verification Systems (AVS) are essential to online transactions and credit card processing. During checkout, a customer provides their address, which is then compared to the billing address on file with the issuing bank. The issuing bank then returns an AVS code to the merchant, indicating whether the addresses match. AVS codes are single-letter codes that are used as a guide by merchants to determine how to proceed with the transaction. These codes can be used to approve or decline the transaction based on the level of verification required. AVS response codes are critical in preventing fraud and protecting merchants and customers from unauthorised transactions. By clearly indicating whether the billing address matches the address on file with the issuing bank, merchants can make informed decisions about how to proceed with the transaction. In simple terms, AVS authentication is utilised as a part of a multilayered fraud protection system that ensures that valid transactions are approved, and suspicious ones are declined. With the increasing prevalence of online transactions, AVS has become an essential tool for ensuring the security and integrity of these transactions. Achieving Address Verification with Youverify Youverify offers simple compliance automation services for financial institutions and small, medium and large businesses. Among its flagship products include an award-winning address verification service that provides businesses with a quick and efficient way to verify the accuracy of customer addresses physically. Youverify's Address Verification service is designed to help businesses verify the accuracy of customer addresses in real-time, using multiple data sources, including government databases and commercial data sources. This service helps businesses reduce the risk of fraud, increase customer data accuracy, and enhance overall compliance. Bottom Line Address Verification Services are handy because they help businesses ensure customer data's accuracy, reduce the risk of fraud, and improve customer communication. By using these tools, businesses can quickly and easily verify their customers' addresses, identities, and phone numbers, which can help reduce errors in shipping and billing information and minimise the risk of chargebacks and other forms of fraud. Address verification tools can also help businesses improve their customer communication by ensuring they have accurate contact information for their customers. This can help to improve customer satisfaction and loyalty. Evidently, address verification tools are a valuable resource for businesses that want to improve the accuracy and integrity of their customer data, reduce the risk of fraud, and enhance customer communication and satisfaction. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesView All PostsAddress VerificationEmmanuel Agwu•June 15, 2023Digital Address VerificationRead MoreAddress VerificationEmmanuel Agwu•August 1, 2022REVEALED: Financial Institutions Need To Conduct Physical Address Verification Or Face Up To N20 Million Fine - Here's WhyRead MoreAddress VerificationHakeem Akiode•January 3, 2024Address Verification in GhanaRead More","title":"What is Address Verification Service (AVS)?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-address-verification-service-avs","id":68},{"content_hash":"655d392b65229089f79956d549dae398ff06488ed3992fb3392c5da097858c97","domain":"supabase.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.727732+00:00","final_url":"https://supabase.com/features/regional-invocations","language":"","text":"Regional invocationsExecute an Edge Function in a region close to your database.Stage:General AvailabilityAvailable on self-hosted:N/ASupabase's Regional Invocations feature allows you to execute Edge Functions in a region close to your database, optimizing performance for database-intensive operations. This capability ensures low-latency communication between your functions and database, enhancing overall application responsiveness. Key benefits Reduced latency: Minimize round-trip time between edge functions and database for faster operations. Improved performance: Enhance the speed of database queries and updates from your edge functions. Data locality: Keep data processing close to where the data is stored for efficiency. Consistency: Ensure that edge functions operate on the most up-to-date data with minimal delay. Cost optimization: Reduce data transfer costs between regions. Simplified architecture: Streamline your application's geographic setup for database operations. Compliance support: Help meet data residency requirements by processing data in specific regions. Regional invocations are particularly valuable for: Applications with data-intensive serverless functions Real-time data processing workflows requiring low latency Financial applications needing quick database reads and writes Analytics services processing large volumes of data Compliance-sensitive applications with data residency requirements Any scenario where minimizing function-to-database latency is crucial By leveraging Supabase's Regional Invocations, you can significantly enhance the performance of your database-centric serverless functions. This feature allows you to build more responsive applications, especially those requiring frequent or complex database interactions, by ensuring that your Edge Functions operate as close as possible to your Supabase database.Read DocumentationShareRealtime - Presence AuthorizationReports & MetricsBuild in a weekend, scale to millionsStart your projectRequest a demo","title":"Regional invocations","url":"https://supabase.com/features/regional-invocations","id":69},{"content_hash":"83905c5103d66104d576e46fb7c2d06f5fe605332eb4c46a5c70b84bd1144881","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.694646+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-02/a-room-with-a-view-play-grace-chapple-never-closer-the-landlord/107188724","language":"","text":"Grace Chapple exposes political heart of A Room with a View in new playHBy Hannah StoryABC ArtsTopic:TheatrePosted Fri 2 Oct 2026 at 12:00pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 12:00pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 12:00pmGrace Chapple is an emerging Australian playwright, now based in London. (Supplied: Belvoir/Brett Boardman)abc.net.au/news/a-room-with-a-view-play-grace-chapple-never-closer-the-landlord/107188724Link copiedShareShare articleWhen Grace Chapple visited Florence, Italy, in 2022, she was listening to the audiobook of A Room with a View.Travelling solo for the first time, aged 27, she found herself in the Uffizi Gallery — where Botticelli's The Birth of Venus hangs — feeling unsure what to do with her life.\"I was thinking, 'Do I even know who I am?'\" Chapple says.\"You know that first time you're forced to confront yourself alone and how you interact with the world?\"As she wandered the city, a little hungover, so too did the protagonist of the novel: Lucy Honeychurch.In English author EM Forster's 1908 novel A Room with a View, Lucy visits Italy, chaperoned by her spinster cousin Charlotte Bartlett.There, she meets the charismatic but strange George Emerson and is wooed by another suitor, the wealthy but condescending Cecil Vyse.It's the set-up for a love triangle, but Forster's novel was more than a romance; it's Lucy's coming-of-age story, which also pokes fun at turn-of-the-century English society.That complexity spurred Chapple to adapt it for Sydney's Belvoir St Theatre, where it opens this month before travelling to Melbourne Theatre Company.\"The novel hit me hard,\" she says. \"It found me at the moment in my life where I really needed it.\"Listening to A Room with a View in Florence did more than inspire Chapple creatively. It encouraged her to \"blow up\" her life.Returning to Sydney, where she had moved from Perth to study playwriting at the National Institute of Dramatic Art (NIDA), Chapple broke up with her long-term boyfriend.\"When you're stuck on train tracks in life, you need something huge to shake you out of it,\" Chapple says.Finding inspiration at homeThat year, Chapple's debut play, Never Closer, set during the Troubles, premiered as part of Belvoir's independent 25A program. Two years later, it opened on the theatre's main stage.Grace Chapple was working front-of-house at Belvoir when Never Closer premiered in 2022. (Supplied: Belvoir/Brett Boardman)A new production from indie company Patalog Theatre will return to Melbourne this month, after earning rave reviews in May.Never Closer is the story of five schoolfriends reuniting for Christmas Eve at Deidre's house in Northern Ireland in 1987.Seeing each other a decade after they finished school, with many of them leaving Northern Ireland to work and study, unresolved tensions rise to the surface, especially after Niamh brings home her English fiancé Harry.It was the play Chapple wrote while studying at NIDA.She recalls being asked when she applied: \"If you had a year left to live, what would you write about?\"She doesn't know why she burst into tears: \"I just said, 'My mum'.\"Chapple's mum grew up in Northern Ireland and moved to London at 17 to study medicine, where she met Chapple's dad, an Englishman.\"That became the base of the play,\" Chapple explains.Chapple's mum loved Never Closer and was inspired to start writing too. (Supplied: Theatre company/Photographer)\"But it was mixed in with the fact I had just left home for the first time and was dealing with all these weird feelings about leaving behind my friends and my home and not knowing if there would be a home to go back to.\"It made me think about my mum and how she never went back.\"Beneath the period romanceAfter members of the Belvoir team saw Never Closer during its 25A season, they wanted to know what Chapple was going to do next.\"They were keen to invest in me as an artist,\" she says.\"They said, 'What else do you have? Is there anything you want to adapt you might be able to turn around quicker than an original?'\"When she listened to A Room with a View earlier that year, Chapple had been struck by its theatricality.\"[Forster] writes in scenes and it's all about relationships,\" she explains. \"The action hinges around conversations.\"With buy-in from Never Closer director Hannah Goodwin, Chapple pitched the adaptation to Belvoir artistic director Eamon Flack.\"At first glance it feels like a quite conventional period romance … but the romance is really a vehicle for talking about more exciting political and transgressive topics than we're used to getting out of your Jane Austens,\" she explains.Nathalie Morris (Bump) stars as Lucy in Grace Chapple's adaptation of A Room with a View. (Supplied: Belvoir/Brett Boardman)Chapple says A Room with a View resonates with a modern audience because Lucy is, like all young people, \"learning to think for yourself in a world that is really trying to tell you how to think\".\"It's about how dangerous it can be to a dominant culture when people start to break away and carve their own paths.\"It's a journey everyone has to go on of figuring out how to live honestly in this world and be true to the person you are and how much that can cost.\"A show about the housing crisisThat question of cost is something Chapple is thinking about with her next project, her second original play, The Landlord, which will feature in Belvoir's 2027 season.It reunites the writer with Goodwin and Never Closer star Ariadne Sgouros (High Country) and breaks down intergenerational inequality in a story set across two Surry Hill terraces. Both are owned by 65-year-old John — but only one is falling apart.Chapple started writing The Landlord in 2024, when she was on a research trip to Cambridge University to pore over its archive of Forster's manuscripts and diaries for her A Room with a View.Eamon Flack describes Grace Chapple's The Landlord, starring Ariadne Sgouros, as \"cut-glass sharp\". (Supplied: Belvoir/Daniel Boud)\"[The Landlord] is born out of just all the dehumanising experiences that come from being a renter,\" she says.It's a subject many millennial artists are grappling with in their work.\"Every conversation I've had since I moved to Sydney works its way back to the housing crisis eventually,\" Chapple says.\"Like, someone's got a shitty rental situation or they're getting kicked out or they're paying too much or they're trying to buy and can't.\"While it's the subject on everyone's lips, not everyone has Chapple's expertise.She's approaching the subject armed with a degree in economics.\"I was taught the narratives that are constructed around capitalism that protect it and make it seem like this perfect, inevitable thing,\" she says.\"I liked the idea of unpicking the narratives I was taught, but for people who didn't study it.\"A Room with a View is at Belvoir St Theatre, Sydney, from October 3 - November 1 and Melbourne Theatre Company from November 14 - December 19.Never Closer is at fortyfivedownstairs, Melbourne, from October 15 - November 1.The Landlord is at Belvoir St Theatre, Sydney, from September 25 - October 24, 2027.Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated stories'It's coming for everything': Eddie Perfect on AI and his new satireTopic:TheatreSunrise at an Australian beach is the star of this immersive showTopic:TheatreArtist overlooked for decades entices Rachel Griffiths back to stageTopic:TheatreRelated topicsArts, Culture and EntertainmentAustraliaBooksFictionNSWNovelPerforming ArtsRomance BooksSouthbankSurry HillsTheatreVICTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Grace Chapple exposes political heart of A Room with a View in new play","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-02/a-room-with-a-view-play-grace-chapple-never-closer-the-landlord/107188724","id":70},{"content_hash":"b4818c966fb4f7faaf45b2ae70327141073f212dff232b01cf844f7ec9677ca4","domain":"son.gov.ng","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.477728+00:00","final_url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/05/14/son-engages-stakeholders-in-asaba-to-champion-quality-standards/","language":"","text":"SON ENGAGES STAKEHOLDERS IN ASABA TO CHAMPION QUALITY STANDARDS In a determined move to combat the influx of substandard products nationwide, the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) convened a crucial sensitization workshop in Asaba on Friday, May 9th, 2025. The forum aimed to galvanize stakeholders from diverse sectors on the critical importance of upholding quality standards through self-regulation and ethical practices. Mrs. Talatu Ethan Director, Corporate Affairs The Director General/Chief Executive, Standards Organisation of Nigeria, Dr. Ifeanyi Chukwunonso Okeke, emphasized that standardization and quality assurance are collective responsibilities demanding strong collaboration between public and private sector players. Represented by the Director, SON Corporate Affairs Directorate, Mrs. Talatu Ethan, the DG implored participants to: Actively embrace self-regulatory conduct within their operations. Support market associations as effective platforms for peer enforcement of standards. Champion ethical behavior across all industry sectors. Ensure that only certified, high-quality products are traded and utilized. Mrs. Talatu reiterated the DG’s powerful call to action, urging everyone – manufacturers, traders, importers, exporters, and all stakeholders – to “do the right thing” in their respective roles to safeguard product quality and consumer safety. Mrs. P.O Kussy-Wanza, Head South-South region The Head, SON South-South Region, Mrs. P. O. Kussy-Wanza, in her remarks warmly welcomed participants and clearly articulated the workshop’s objective, which is to significantly reduce the prevalence of substandard goods in the Nigerian market. The Group Head, Mechanical Engineering at SON, Engr. I. P. Ega enlightened attendees on the profound impact of SON’s activities on the nation’s economic advancement. Dr. T. Oni, the Head of the Science Laboratory Technology (SLT) Department at Delta State Polytechnic, delivered a compelling presentation on fostering a culture of quality and standards through proactive self-regulatory measures and robust ethical codes. Group Photograph of SON and the Stakeholders The interactive session saw participants raise pertinent questions, which were thoroughly addressed by SON representatives. A recurring sentiment among stakeholders was a strong demand for increased sensitization and awareness campaigns at the grassroots level to effectively tackle the issue of substandard products. This workshop underscores SON’s proactive approach to partnering with stakeholders in building robust, quality infrastructure that protects consumers and fosters sustainable economic growth in Nigeria.","title":"SON Engages Stakeholders in Asaba to Champion Quality Standards","url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/05/14/son-engages-stakeholders-in-asaba-to-champion-quality-standards/","id":71},{"content_hash":"138414224d5143158d0db854ded550778c7978a8f349fd9248e7578250d586ab","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.387187+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/models/constraints/","language":"","text":"Constraints reference¶ The classes defined in this module create database constraints. They are added in the model Meta.constraints option. Referencing built-in constraints Constraints are defined in django.db.models.constraints, but for convenience they’re imported into django.db.models. The standard convention is to use from django.db import models and refer to the constraints as models.<Foo>Constraint. Constraints in abstract base classes You must always specify a unique name for the constraint. As such, you cannot normally specify a constraint on an abstract base class, since the Meta.constraints option is inherited by subclasses, with exactly the same values for the attributes (including name) each time. To work around name collisions, part of the name may contain '%(app_label)s' and '%(class)s', which are replaced, respectively, by the lowercased app label and class name of the concrete model. For example: CheckConstraint(condition=Q(age__gte=18), name=\"%(app_label)s_%(class)s_is_adult\") Validation of Constraints Constraints are checked during the model validation. BaseConstraint¶ class BaseConstraint(*name, violation_error_code=None, violation_error_message=None)[source]¶ Base class for all constraints. Subclasses must implement constraint_sql(), create_sql(), remove_sql() and validate() methods. All constraints have the following parameters in common: name¶ BaseConstraint.name¶ The name of the constraint. You must always specify a unique name for the constraint. violation_error_code¶ BaseConstraint.violation_error_code¶ The error code used when ValidationError is raised during model validation. Defaults to None. violation_error_message¶ BaseConstraint.violation_error_message¶ The error message used when ValidationError is raised during model validation. Defaults to \"Constraint “%(name)s” is violated.\". validate()¶ BaseConstraint.validate(model, instance, exclude=None, using=DEFAULT_DB_ALIAS)[source]¶ Validates that the constraint, defined on model, is respected on the instance. This will do a query on the database to ensure that the constraint is respected. If fields in the exclude list are needed to validate the constraint, the constraint is ignored. Raise a ValidationError if the constraint is violated. This method must be implemented by a subclass. CheckConstraint¶ class CheckConstraint(*, condition, name, violation_error_code=None, violation_error_message=None)[source]¶ Creates a check constraint in the database. condition¶ CheckConstraint.condition¶ A Q object or boolean Expression that specifies the conditional check you want the constraint to enforce. For example: CheckConstraint(condition=Q(age__gte=18), name=\"age_gte_18\") ensures the age field is never less than 18. Expression order Q argument order is not necessarily preserved, however the order of Q expressions themselves are preserved. This may be important for databases that preserve check constraint expression order for performance reasons. For example, use the following format if order matters: CheckConstraint( condition=Q(age__gte=18) & Q(expensive_check=condition), name=\"age_gte_18_and_others\", ) Oracle < 23c Checks with nullable fields on Oracle < 23c must include a condition allowing for NULL values in order for validate() to behave the same as check constraints validation. For example, if age is a nullable field: CheckConstraint(condition=Q(age__gte=18) | Q(age__isnull=True), name=\"age_gte_18\") UniqueConstraint¶ class UniqueConstraint(*expressions, fields=(), name, condition=None, deferrable=None, include=None, opclasses=(), nulls_distinct=None, violation_error_code=None, violation_error_message=None)[source]¶ Creates a uniqueness guarantee in the database, enforced by either a unique constraint or a unique index depending on the options used. Constraint vs. index implementation Setting only UniqueConstraint.fields creates a true database constraint (ADD CONSTRAINT ... UNIQUE). Specifying any of UniqueConstraint.expressions, UniqueConstraint.opclasses, UniqueConstraint.condition, or UniqueConstraint.include creates a unique index (CREATE UNIQUE INDEX) instead. In this documentation, the term “unique constraint” is used for both cases to mean a uniqueness guarantee enforced by the database. expressions¶ UniqueConstraint.expressions¶ Positional argument *expressions allows creating functional unique constraints on expressions and database functions. For example: UniqueConstraint(Lower(\"name\").desc(), \"category\", name=\"unique_lower_name_category\") creates a unique constraint on the lowercased value of the name field in descending order and the category field in the default ascending order. Functional unique constraints have the same database restrictions as Index.expressions. fields¶ UniqueConstraint.fields¶ A list of field names that specifies the unique set of columns you want the constraint to enforce. For example: UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"room\", \"date\"], name=\"unique_booking\") ensures each room can only be booked once for each date. condition¶ UniqueConstraint.condition¶ A Q object that specifies the condition you want the constraint to enforce. For example: UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"user\"], condition=Q(status=\"DRAFT\"), name=\"unique_draft_user\") ensures that each user only has one draft. These conditions have the same database restrictions as Index.condition. deferrable¶ UniqueConstraint.deferrable¶ Set this parameter to create a deferrable unique constraint. Accepted values are Deferrable.DEFERRED or Deferrable.IMMEDIATE. For example: from django.db.models import Deferrable, UniqueConstraint UniqueConstraint( name=\"unique_order\", fields=[\"order\"], deferrable=Deferrable.DEFERRED, ) By default constraints are not deferred. A deferred constraint will not be enforced until the end of the transaction. An immediate constraint will be enforced immediately after every command. Unique constraints with condition, include, opclasses, or expressions may be implemented as unique indexes rather than unique constraints. In that case, deferrable cannot be set. MySQL, MariaDB, and SQLite. Deferrable unique constraints are ignored on MySQL, MariaDB, and SQLite as they do not support them. Warning Deferred unique constraints may lead to a performance penalty. include¶ UniqueConstraint.include¶ A list or tuple of the names of the fields to be included in the covering unique index as non-key columns. This allows index-only scans to be used for queries that select only included fields (include) and filter only by unique fields (fields). For example: UniqueConstraint(name=\"unique_booking\", fields=[\"room\", \"date\"], include=[\"full_name\"]) will allow filtering on room and date, also selecting full_name, while fetching data only from the index. Unique constraints with non-key columns are ignored for databases besides PostgreSQL. Non-key columns have the same database restrictions as Index.include. opclasses¶ UniqueConstraint.opclasses¶ The names of the PostgreSQL operator classes to use for this unique index. If you require a custom operator class, you must provide one for each field in the index. For example: UniqueConstraint( name=\"unique_username\", fields=[\"username\"], opclasses=[\"varchar_pattern_ops\"] ) creates a unique index on username using varchar_pattern_ops. opclasses are ignored for databases besides PostgreSQL. nulls_distinct¶ UniqueConstraint.nulls_distinct¶ Whether rows containing NULL values covered by the unique constraint should be considered distinct from each other. The default value is None which uses the database default which is True on most backends. For example: UniqueConstraint(name=\"ordering\", fields=[\"ordering\"], nulls_distinct=False) creates a unique constraint that only allows one row to store a NULL value in the ordering column. Unique constraints with nulls_distinct are ignored for databases besides PostgreSQL. violation_error_code¶ UniqueConstraint.violation_error_code¶ The error code used when a ValidationError is raised during model validation. Defaults to BaseConstraint.violation_error_code, when either UniqueConstraint.condition is set or UniqueConstraint.fields is not set. If UniqueConstraint.fields is set without a UniqueConstraint.condition, defaults to the Meta.unique_together error code when there are multiple fields, and to the Field.unique error code when there is a single field. violation_error_message¶ UniqueConstraint.violation_error_message¶ The error message used when a ValidationError is raised during model validation. Defaults to BaseConstraint.violation_error_message, when either UniqueConstraint.condition is set or UniqueConstraint.fields is not set. If UniqueConstraint.fields is set without a UniqueConstraint.condition, defaults to the Meta.unique_together error message when there are multiple fields, and to the Field.unique error message when there is a single field.","title":"Constraints reference¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/models/constraints/","id":72},{"content_hash":"7e27db2247623fe42f9aa615d0ff325fc03e6ac9141a04f52b8bd9eb5a8c54f6","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:56.241291+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/stories-for-you","language":"","text":"Stories For You | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Stories For You | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/stories-for-you","id":73},{"content_hash":"91c912e20136ed631d8192d9f3edbad44da1842274feae23a91a8111354540a0","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:55.973159+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/gst-council-scraps-tax-officers-arrest-powers-eases-refund-rules/articleshow/134800724.cms","language":"","text":"GST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers, eases refund rules - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIBusinessIndia BusinessBudget 2026Mutual FundsInfrastructureGold Rates TodaySilver Rates Today Platinum Rates TodayPetrol PriceDiesel PriceCNG PriceLPG PriceFinancial LiteracyDon't Get ScammedFinancial CalculatorsInternational BusinessCybersecurityIFSC CodeVideosFinancial Literacy SummitEconomyToday's ePaperLiveBudgetIFSCPAN CardAadhaar CardIPOIncome TaxSavings Growth CalculatorNewsBusiness NewsIndia Business NewsGST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers, eases refund rulesTrendingUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayGST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers, eases refund rulesTNN / Oct 09, 2026, 03:21 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint A view of the 57th Goods and Services Tax (GST) Council Meeting, chaired by Union Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman, in New Delhi on Thursday NEW DELHI: Nine years after the biggest indirect reform in the country, GST Council on Friday decided on a massive procedural overhaul that will rework the way businesses operate, enable them to get refunds and credit for taxes paid, allow for movement of trucks with fewer stoppages and do away with officers' arrest powers.From registration and returns to refunds decriminalisation the second part of GST2.0 was completed with the elements to be rolled out from April, allowing for amendments to the law and for the systems to be revamped.Amid the unanimity at the all-powerful body led by finance minister Nirmala Sitharaman, there were two subjects on which decisions were deferred.First, the proposal to only stop input tax credit (ITC) for entities that have collected tax, but not deposited it, while allowing it for others in the chain to claim credit was referred to a panel of officers.The committee has been tasked with finalising its recommendations and coming back to the Council within three months so that it can be ready by April, Sitharaman told reporters after the six-hour meeting.Focus on faster decisions, lower compliance costs: PMPM Narendra Modi, in a social media post, said, “The Council’s recommendations on removal of arrest provisions, greater decriminalisation and simpler procedures will have a very positive impact. The focus is clear: Faster decisions. Lower compliance costs. Automated refunds. Trust-based administration. These are in line with our constant efforts to make GST more efficient, transparent and citizen-friendly.”After the meeting, Sitharaman said states and the Centre have worked together to move to a trust-based regime for taxpayers.One of the most significant changes is to do away with arrest powers for officers under section 69 of Central GST (CGST) Act. While the powers were not available under the VAT regime, the threat of arrest has often been dangled by officials. Separately, the monetary threshold for prosecution has also been raised five-fold to Rs 5 crore, while also rationalising punishment under section 132 of CGST Act.Even dispute resolution has been reformed with the Council deciding against issuing notices in cases where the tax involved is under Rs 10,000. What’s more, even in the ongoing 12 lakh-odd cases, notices will be withdrawn.Further, while penalties will be deemed to be a “charge”, where tax is paid voluntarily, in non-fraud cases a reduced penalty of 5% will be imposed if the interest is paid within 30 days or 60 days in case of adjudication. The maximum general penalty is also sought to be slashed from Rs 25,000 to Rs 10,000 and the upper-limit for pre-deposit in case of filing of appeals will be capped at Rs 40 crore.In a bid to reduce truck stoppages, officials in states other than the origin and destination will no longer be able to intercept vehicles. And, even in cases where it is allowed by an officer of the rank of a joint commissioner, it can only be done when there is specific information, FM told reporters, while asserting that the decisions have been taken to ensure that life becomes easier for businesses and is part of the “reforms express”.The other major change is on the refunds front, where the idea is to process 90% of the claims within three days and reduce the period of acknowledgement from 15 days to 10, with a system of deemed acknowledgement introduced.A host of input tax credit related simplifications have been introduced allowing businesses to claim refunds of the accumulated input tax credit related to capital goods. Starting Nov, the facility can be spread over 60 months and will come as a boost to long-gestation projects. Similarly, restrictions on ITC use have been removed for supplies such as health and life insurance, outdoor catering, free samples, telecom towers and pipelines laid in factories.Host of simplificationsA host of simplifications have also been offered for the services sector, which will benefit exports. From small businesses with up to Rs 5 crore turnover and selling only to consumers (B2C), GST Council approved in-principle a plan to allow filing of annual returns.Besides, those selling on e-commerce platforms will not be required to register in multiple states, helping 90% of such sellers.There was a host of registration related rationalisation, meant to ensure that most businesses can register in three days, while allowing changes automatically, apart from altering the principal place of business. Even returns were sought to be simplified to automate details and work towards an invoice management system.You use AI every day. Now get your AI Quotient. Take the AIQ test.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaPhotostoriesKangana Ranaut goes full Himachali royalty in a statement topi, Kullu shawl, and a cultural nod to President Droupadi Murmu5 habits that matter more than supplements: Doctor shares simple daily habits for better sleep, fitness, nutrition, stress management and preventive health that can support a longer, healthier life5 lipstick shades to pair with your chaniya choli this NavratriOptical illusion personality test: What you see first claims to reveal whether you are a practical thinker or a gut-feeling personKareena Kapoor Khan just gave motorsport a Bebo makeover in sleek black tailoring10 baby names inspired by astronauts, space pioneers and cosmic explorersGen Z is making Indian textiles cool again: 5 heritage weaves that are having a fashion comeback7 things you should always have in your office bag: These everyday essentials can come in handy if you