ght nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Science nonprofit fundraising email | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/science-nonprofit-fundraising-email","id":155},{"content_hash":"c36df483691af8b3ae23c1fd6f3dfc48b1669e68407c751ffd7bc15f3f2730c5","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:38.802423+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-general-eisenhower-candidat-democrate-a-la-presidence-des-etats-unis_2073287_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Washington, 6 novembre. - Malgré les démentis répétés du principal intéressé, personne ne veut croire ici qu'au cours de leur déjeuner en tête à tête le président et le général n'ont pas abordé les questions politiques. La possibilité de voir en fin de compte le général Eisenhower se présenter sous l'étiquette démocrate est de nouveau sérieusement considérée par les observateurs autorisés. Ceux-ci font remarquer en effet que son éventuelle nomination par une convention républicaine posera quelques difficultés. Sans parler de l'opposition quasi ouverte d'hommes comme le sénateur Taft ou du général Mac Arthur, \" Ike \" n'est pas l'homme qui pourrait attaquer brutalement, comme se devrait un bon candidat de l'opposition, la politique du président Truman, auquel sa fortune militaire et politique est associée et avec lequel il a d'excellentes relations personnelles. En outre il est vraiment l'homme du département d'Etat et s'expose à ce titre à de vives critiques de certains milieux républicains. Mais si sa candidature risque de provoquer de gros remous au sein du \" great old party \", en revanche le parti démocrate et le président Truman verraient avec plaisir le général Eisenhower entrer dans la lutte pour leur cause commune. En dépit des réserves attendues de la part des syndicats, qui connaissent le passé républicain du général et son opposition au Fair Deal, la candidature Eisenhower rallierait assez d'électeurs indépendants et surtout les démocrates du Sud, dont la traditionnelle rébellion prend cette année une ampleur nouvelle sous la conduite du gouverneur Byrnes. Reste à savoir si \" Ike \" accepterait d'être enrôlé sous la bannière démocrate. Dans l'affirmative on envisagerait pour son remplacement les généraux Collins, Ridgway ou Gruenther... Mais il ne s'agit là que de spéculations. Les problèmes atlantiques Il est vrai que les problèmes du N.A.T.O. étaient assez graves pour occuper exclusivement les deux interlocuteurs, auxquels se sont joints dans l'après-midi les principaux chefs militaires et le grand patron de la mobilisation économique, M. Charles Wilson. La présence de M. Wilson signifia que l'on a examiné de plus près le problème des livraisons américaines et des possibilités des usines américaines. Certes pour le moment les Américains ne semblent vouloir considérer que les responsabilités européennes dans ce que certains journaux appellent déjà l'échec du programme atlantique. Mais on reconnaît toutefois dans les milieux officiels que les livraisons américaines à l'Europe ont subi des retards et ont porté trop souvent sur du matériel démode. Un nouvel effort devra donc être demandé aux usines américaines qui n'ont pas exécuté le programme prévu par suite de la pénurie de machines-outils. Il vous reste 25.02% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"LE GENERAL EISENHOWER CANDIDAT DÉMOCRATE A LA PRÉSIDENCE DES ÉTATS-UNIS ?","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-general-eisenhower-candidat-democrate-a-la-presidence-des-etats-unis_2073287_1819218.html","id":156},{"content_hash":"92a108cc22c243468d9f030e8c8b9aee23d530b365cd1583bae91ee6c32de11d","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:38.749394+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-tips-on-eating-to-recover-after-exercise-19525","language":"","text":"Health Check: tips on eating to recover after exercise Published: August 11, 2014 1:59am EDT Re-fuelling after exercise is important for maintaining adequate energy stores for beneficial changes in your muscles (training adaption). Szabó Varga Katalin/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA https://theconversation.com/health-check-tips-on-eating-to-recover-after-exercise-19525 https://theconversation.com/health-check-tips-on-eating-to-recover-after-exercise-19525 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Sports performance was once thought to be enhanced by practices such as drinking cognac before an Olympic marathon run. Thankfully, today’s nutrition strategies are more scientific and properly researched. We now know quite a lot about when and what to eat before, during, and after exercise for better performance. Of course, what you eat at all these points plays a role but what you eat to recover after exercise (recovery nutrition) supports the body’s physiological adaptation to the demands of training and competition. And it prepares the body for the next event. It replenishes fuel stores in muscles and the liver, minimises muscle loss and encourages muscle growth and repair, minimises fatigue and injury, and supports immune function. Replenishing fluid is also vital but outside the scope of this article. Re-fuelling after exercise is important for maintaining adequate energy stores for beneficial changes in your muscles (training adaption). But it shouldn’t be in excess of the metabolic demands of what you’ve done if you want to maintain appropriate weight, body composition and support performance goals. Getting carbs right Training and competition require energy, and the energy muscles use is supplied by a mix of fuels in different proportions depending on the intensity and duration of exercise. In general terms, carbohydrates are the primary fuel source as exercise intensity increases. The type of carbohydrate used by the body as fuel for exercise is glucose. Consuming carbohydrates before and during prolonged exercise (such as a marathon run) will replenish glucose, delay the onset of fatigue, and allow you to compete for a longer period of time. Glucose is stored in the muscles and liver as glycogen, and research shows low muscle glycogen can impair performance. Depending on when the last carbohydrate meal was consumed, glycogen reserves can be depleted after about 60 to 90 minutes of intense exercise. Planning for glycogen replacement requires adjusting for variations in exercise intensity and duration, the amount of muscle you have, your training goals, and dietary intake at other times, including before and during events. So total carbohydrate targets can vary considerably. Consuming carbohydrates before and during prolonged exercise (such as a marathon run) will replenish glucose, delay the onset of fatigue, and allow you to compete for a longer period of time. ed_needs_a_bicycle/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA Post-exercise carbohydrate recommendations for athletes generally suggest consuming about 1.2 grams of carbohydrate for each kilogram of body weight within an hour of completion, and each hour for four hours after activity. This should be followed by regular carbohydrate intake appropriate to meet daily requirements. Replacing muscle protein But there are also other things you’re going to need to recover properly. What you generally eat and the type of exercise you do can both affect the synthesis of protein in your muscles. Physical training is a stimulus for muscle growth and adaptation. Hard-working muscles come under stress during physical activity, and the body’s limited reserves of amino acids (what proteins are made from) become rapidly depleted. Consuming protein or amino acids after exercise can reduce muscle breakdown and encourage new tissue growth. But here again, individual responses and requirements can vary quite a lot. The type and amount of your protein intake both play a role in muscle reconditioning after exercise, with some evidence suggesting that dairy proteins, such as whey protein, may offer advantages over other sources. But studies are not consistent about ideal timing. Some suggest consumption before exercise while others recommend consumption within 24 hours of completion offers most benefit. Generally speaking, consuming 20 to 25 grams of quality protein as soon as possible after exercise is recommended for building muscle and help reconditioning. Another recommendation is around 0.25 to 0.3 grams protein per kilogram of body mass. Best foods It follows that foods containing both carbohydrates and protein are generally recommended as being best for recovery. And there are other advantages – consuming protein with carbohydrates may improve glycogen replenishment and reduce muscle breakdown. The optimal carbohydrate to protein ratio for glycogen replenishment is three to one. Consuming protein after exercise can reduce muscle breakdown and encourage new tissue growth. Isaac Hsieh/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA In general, moderate to high glycaemic index, low-fibre carbohydrate sources are preferable because they’re digested and absorbed rapidly. And good quality plant- or animal-derived protein foods are better because they contain essential amino acids. The average person exercising at low to moderate intensities for around 30 minutes a day on most days of the week, should follow general dietary guidelines to meet their energy and nutritional needs. But it’s worthwhile trying to adjust mealtimes to coincide with recommended post-workout time (30 to 90 minutes) for eating. Adding an extra recovery meal, or a high-sugar sports drink, will merely increase your risk of consuming more than you need. Specialised nutrition plans are particularly useful for highly active and trained people who engage in moderate to high intensity activity for between two and six hours every day most days of the week. Energy requirements increase at this level and carefully planned nutrient intake and timing will support performance while minimising fatigue and injury. Here are a few examples of foods that may support an athlete’s recovery eating plan, containing protein, as well as approximately 50 grams of carbohydrate: two cups of breakfast cereal with milk, two slices of toast with 220 grams baked beans, a large baked potato with cottage cheese plus glass of milk, 200 grams of fruit yoghurt with 300 grams of fruit, or a bread roll with lean meat or cheese and a large piece of fruit The most important thing to remember is that there is no one size that fits all. Individual nutrition plans are best developed with the help of qualified sports nutrition experts and may involve some trial and error for the best outcome. Sport and exercise Nutrition Health Check Kristina Vingrys (Nelson), Lily Stojanovska, Michael Mathai, Nigel Stepto, Victoria University Authors Kristina Vingrys (Nelson) PhD Candidate and Nutritionist, Victoria University Lily Stojanovska Professor and Acting Director Centre for Chronic Disease Prevention and Management, Victoria University Michael Mathai Associate Professor, Victoria University Nigel Stepto Associate Professor in Exercise Physiology and Clinical Exercise Science Research Program Leader in Institute of Sport Exercise and Active Living (ISEAL), Victoria University Disclosure statement Nigel Stepto has received funding from National Health and Medical Research Council and Australian Institute of Sport (Australian Sports Commission). He is affiliated with Exercise and Sports Science Australia (ESSA). Kristina Vingrys (Nelson), Lily Stojanovska, and Michael Mathai do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners Victoria University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.wq64npgke","title":"Health Check: tips on eating to recover after exercise","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-tips-on-eating-to-recover-after-exercise-19525","id":157},{"content_hash":"474645bc08293da8486a2bd5748e161af5cc4d455f15d0ad9886b62dc624f7f2","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:38.361425+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/kellie-scott/6830306","language":"","text":"Kellie Scott - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenKellie ScottBrisbane, QLDKellie Scott is a reporter for ABC Lifestyle. Kellie is passionate about creating content that helps people stay informed and improve their lives, covering topics including parenting, sex and relationships, domestic violence, career and money, and women's health.Contact Kellie ScottLatest by Kellie ScottThree experts on the future of AI avoidersWhether it was from the mouth of a tech leader or celebrity, you've probably heard that \"if you don't use AI, you'll be left behind\". I went to three AI academics to ask if that's true.Topic:AIThu 8 Oct 2026 at 5:30amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 5:30amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 5:30amOur wedding was a political statement — and that was the whole pointFreddy Grant and Burton Reynolds were married in 2016 using a legal loophole for same-sex marriage. Now celebrating their 10th anniversary, Freddy reflects on their love and the political statement their wedding created.KPerspective by Kellie ScottSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:00amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:00amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:00amThe best way to clean your toilet and how oftenMost stains present in toilet bowls are due to scale build-up from the water supply, and removing them requires more than your average toilet cleaning, experts explain.Topic:HomeFri 25 Sep 2026 at 3:02pmFri 25 Sep 2026 at 3:02pmFri 25 Sep 2026 at 3:02pmInfertility was incredibly hard. Loving one another got us throughWhen IVF didn't work, Susanne and Mike Armour had to face the difficult decision to stop trying for a baby. The couple say their love for one another got them through.KPerspective by Kellie ScottSun 20 Sep 2026 at 9:00amSun 20 Sep 2026 at 9:00amSun 20 Sep 2026 at 9:00amParents should 'urgently' discuss sexual choking with their childrenThere is an urgent need for parents to discuss the risks of sexual strangulation with their children following the recent death of a 16-year-old girl from Sydney, experts warn.Topic:ParentingWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:41amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:41amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:41amI'm childless not by choice, but I do have biological childrenNot being able to have a baby of my own was painful. Sperm donation helped me move through that grief.KPerspective by Kellie ScottTue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:01pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:01pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:01pmThe risks of borrowing from the bank of mum and dadThe bank of mum and dad is estimated to be worth $35 billion, but the taboo of talking about money is putting families at risk when using it.Topic:RelationshipsTue 8 Sep 2026 at 4:30amTue 8 Sep 2026 at 4:30amTue 8 Sep 2026 at 4:30amFive recipes that celebrate spring, including an easy dessertStart spring off right with five of our audience's favourite recipes from the season, including a satisfying dessert. Topic:RecipesMon 7 Sep 2026 at 2:59pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 2:59pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 2:59pmThe women 'quietly quitting' their relationships\"Quiet quitting\" began as a social media trend where workers were encouraged to avoid burnout in their jobs by doing only the bare minimum. Some women are applying that to their romantic relationships. Topic:RelationshipsThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:30amThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:30amThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:30amShould you give your child's school permission to post their images?If you don't like the idea of your child's face appearing in the school newsletter or Facebook page, you can refuse consent. But experts warn there can be social consequences for children. Topic:ParentingTue 1 Sep 2026 at 4:30amTue 1 Sep 2026 at 4:30amTue 1 Sep 2026 at 4:30amParalympian fell in love with his wife in a traffic jamBig wave surfer and Paralympic cyclist Matt Formston has a long list of achievements, but says the relationship with wife Bex is by far his greatest. KPerspective by Kellie ScottSun 30 Aug 2026 at 9:00amSun 30 Aug 2026 at 9:00amSun 30 Aug 2026 at 9:00amMobile phones are 'Trojan horses' for germs. Here's how to clean themMobile phones behave like \"biological magnets\", with thousands of micro-organisms living on their surface, experts say. Here's how to keep them clean. Topic:HealthFri 21 Aug 2026 at 3:06pmFri 21 Aug 2026 at 3:06pmFri 21 Aug 2026 at 3:06pmThe taboo experience of motherhood regretMany parents have moments where they miss their life pre-kids. But what's it like when that feeling isn't fleeting? And how does it feel to carry something, that if said out loud, comes at a significant social cost?Topic:Family and RelationshipsTue 18 Aug 2026 at 12:04pmTue 18 Aug 2026 at 12:04pmTue 18 Aug 2026 at 12:04pmThe pros and cons of blind boxes for kidsWhile blind boxes such as Labubus and Pokémon cards can be fun, experts warn parents should be aware of their similarities with gambling and help children create healthy habits around them.Topic:ParentingThu 13 Aug 2026 at 12:01pmThu 13 Aug 2026 at 12:01pmThu 13 Aug 2026 at 12:01pmIs having a romantic AI companion cheating? Some people think soPeople are increasingly finding emotional support and romantic connection in AI companions, and turning to them even when in a committed — human — relationship.Topic:AIThu 13 Aug 2026 at 9:00amThu 13 Aug 2026 at 9:00amThu 13 Aug 2026 at 9:00amConsidering pet insurance? Read this firstPet insurance can give you peace of mind but as a \"complex financial product\", it can be difficult to calculate if it's a worthwhile investment. Topic:PetsTue 11 Aug 2026 at 4:30amTue 11 Aug 2026 at 4:30amTue 11 Aug 2026 at 4:30amConstantly being asked to perform duties outside of your job description? Read thisIndividual employment contracts often include a clause that says an employee may be required to undertake other duties as required, but what is fair and reasonable depends on a few things. Topic:WorkMon 3 Aug 2026 at 4:30amMon 3 Aug 2026 at 4:30amMon 3 Aug 2026 at 4:30am'Vasectomy at 21 and no regrets': Men on choosing to be childfreeWhat readers have to say about being childfree, after our story on why more men are turning away from fatherhood. Topic:FamilyWed 29 Jul 2026 at 3:13pmWed 29 Jul 2026 at 3:13pmWed 29 Jul 2026 at 3:13pmStruggling to save? Here are five ways to curb your impulse spendingIf you'd rather be saving than shopping, but are struggling to switch gears, there are ways to change your habits. Topic:Personal FinanceTue 28 Jul 2026 at 7:22amTue 28 Jul 2026 at 7:22amTue 28 Jul 2026 at 7:22amThe risks to be aware of when using AI chatbots for relationship adviceWhile the accessibility and \"judgement-free zone\" of AI chatbots can be attractive when seeking relationship advice, experts warn there are risks you need to be aware of. Topic:RelationshipsMon 27 Jul 2026 at 4:30amMon 27 Jul 2026 at 4:30amMon 27 Jul 2026 at 4:30am'I just laid in his cot and cried': Mothers on child lossWomen who have lost children talk about continuing in the wake of such grief, and how people stepped up, or stepped away.Topic:Death and DyingWed 22 Jul 2026 at 2:36pmWed 22 Jul 2026 at 2:36pmWed 22 Jul 2026 at 2:36pmIan doesn't want children. He's part of a growing trend among menAbout 15 per cent of Australian men say they don't want children, an increase from 11 per cent in 2005. Ian says his own childhood played a large part in his decision. Topic:FamilyTue 21 Jul 2026 at 12:05pmTue 21 Jul 2026 at 12:05pmTue 21 Jul 2026 at 12:05pmLove story: Inseparable since meeting, these besties are planning retirement togetherTania Differding and Ella Crofts met as teenagers and have been inseparable since. The best friends, now aged 43, are planning the rest of their lives around one another.KPerspective by Kellie ScottSun 19 Jul 2026 at 1:10pmSun 19 Jul 2026 at 1:10pmSun 19 Jul 2026 at 1:10pmToilet germs and other things living in your make-up brushesVarious studies have shown that make-up tools, including brushes, can harbour a range of bacteria, including opportunistic pathogens. Topic:BeautyFri 17 Jul 2026 at 3:04pmFri 17 Jul 2026 at 3:04pmFri 17 Jul 2026 at 3:04pmHow setting small, specific goals can help reduce your drinkingIf you've fallen off the Dry July wagon or you just aren't interested in teetotalling, trying smaller steps to reducing your alcohol intake can still make a difference to your health.Topic:AlcoholMon 13 Jul 2026 at 7:54amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 7:54amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 7:54amLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Kellie Scott","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/kellie-scott/6830306","id":158},{"content_hash":"f4bf119d2135eb6906feee1f43218568640840bfff5f46e8800f6cecf66225c8","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:37.612355+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-prix-du-chauffage-central-sont-en-augmentation-cet-hiver-de-60-a-70_2072169_1819218.html","language":"","text":"La hausse importante enregistrée par les prix des charbons depuis l'hiver dernier va inévitablement se répercuter sur tes tarifs du chauffage central dans les immeubles à chauffage collectif. Il n'est cependant pas possible de mesurer par un pourcentage unique cette augmentation, qui varie en réalité suivant les nombreux cas particuliers. Déjà lorsqu'il était loisible de s'approvisionner en combustible de son choix, les différences pouvaient être sensibles entre les prix de retient d'une installation vieillie et d'un chauffage moderne ; entre systèmes à air chaud, vapeur ou mazout ; entre un immeuble en dur, avec une bonne isolation thermique et une maison en carcasse de béton et corps de briques creuses, etc.. Ainsi, l'an dernier, tel gros bloc d'immeubles équipé d'une seule chaufferie quasi industrielle voyait le coût journalier d'un radiateur type descendre à 20 francs environ, alors que la moyenne générale pour Paris s'établissait autour de 30 francs. A l'inverse, un pavillon isolé, exposé au froid de toutes parts, peut aisément coûter en chauffage 30 ou 40 % plus cher qu'un appartement situé dans un immeuble enserré entre d'autres maisons. A ces raisons permanentes de disparité dans les prix s'ajoute cette année celle qui provient des difficultés d'approvisionnement en certaines qualités. Sombre d'immeubles par exemple ont dû, faute de coke, se fournir en anthracites américains ; bons combustibles pour cet usage, ces charbons ont un grave défaut : leur prix élevé (et aussi leur calibre, qui exige souvent un concassage préalable dont les frais s'ajoutent au prix). Une chaudière qu'on alimentait l'an dernier avec du coke à 8.500 francs, et qui le sera exclusivement pour 1951-1952 en anthracite américain d'un prix moyen se 18.500 francs, verra donc son prix de revient majoré d'environ 100 %. C'est le cas extrême. A l'opposé, si l'approvisionnement a pu se faire encore en combustibles français, la majoration (le coke coûtant à peu près cette année 12.500 francs) ne devrait pas, pour une durée égale de chauffage, dépasser de plus de 50 % le prix payé l'hiver dernier. Il vous reste 30.74% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"LES PRIX DU CHAUFFAGE CENTRAL sont en augmentation cet hiver de 60 à 70 %","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-prix-du-chauffage-central-sont-en-augmentation-cet-hiver-de-60-a-70_2072169_1819218.html","id":159},{"content_hash":"c4d9d7c73d100ab6209827fc3d5cb2a389370e48ae32c80c92d0dbb5de1bf812","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:37.585141+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/editorials/article-airport-reform-moves-to-the-runway-at-last/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:International passengers arrive at Pearson Airport in Toronto in February.Jon Blacker/The Canadian Press CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountPrime Minister Mark Carney’s announcement on Sept. 15 that Ottawa would “seek private investment through long-term concessions to operate Canada’s four largest airports” is a policy whose arrival is a long overdue reform. Of course, unions have concerns about job protection. But the idea of selling operating concessions for Toronto-Pearson, Montreal, Vancouver and Calgary to pension funds and other investors is a much needed break from a status quo that has inflated costs for travellers. But it doesn’t go far enough. The concession model Mr. Carney is proposing risks doing little more than raising tens of billions of dollars for the Liberal government to spend as it sees fit while handing a steady source of income to concession-holders who will be under only nominally more pressure than then current airport operators to see to the needs of travellers.Carney details plans to open up Canada’s four largest airports to private investmentWhat Mr. Carney – he who once said, “We used to take big, bold risks in Canada. It’s time to swing for the fences” – should have announced is that Ottawa will sell its major airports outright: land, assets and everything else.He should also have announced that Ottawa will allow foreign ownership and control of Canadian airlines and that foreign carriers can operate flights between Canadian destinations, known as cabotage. (American airlines could be eventually included as part of a wider trade arrangement.) This would have been an upper-deck home run for Canadians. It would have paved the way for more choice, lower travel costs and better airport experiences. It would have also ended a system that has been locked in place by a lack of political courage in Ottawa.Since the 1990s, Canada’s major airports have been run by non-profit authorities that pay an annual ground rent to the federal government. In 2025, that tax came to $525-million – money that was sent into the abyss of general revenues.Ottawa has effectively been milking airports for cash for more than 30 years without putting any money back into them – the kind of behaviour associated with rapacious private investors. It is up to the airport authorities to raise money for improvements. It should come as no surprise, then, that airport improvement fees in Canada are over six times higher than in the United States, where federal law requires any revenues raised in an airport to stay in that airport.As well, the American federal government continues to contribute to airport improvement, unlike its Canadian counterpart.Analysis: What to watch as negotiations ramp up for Carney’s airport privatization plansIn terms of aeronautical charges paid by airlines, such as landing and takeoff fees, Toronto-Pearson is the most expensive in North America and the fifth most expensive in the world, according to the Jacobs Review of Airport Charges. Vancouver is the 15th most expensive, ahead of major hubs like Amsterdam, Singapore and Paris-CDG.These high fees and improvement costs get passed on to travellers in the form of higher ticket prices and $6 bottles of water once you get past security.No one can argue convincingly that Canada’s big airports have been run efficiently and effectively under the current regime, or that Ottawa has created a regulatory environment in the airline industry that is conducive to innovation, lower costs and the other benefits of competition.That should make any change, even the one now proposed by Mr. Carney, welcome. But his plan seems built more around his need to raise gobs of cash for his government’s agenda than around the notion of improving the lot of Canadian airline passengers. Mr. Carney’s Transport Minister, Steve MacKinnon, said last month that “fares and fees will be partially regulated” under the proposal but would not guarantee that the price of airline tickets wouldn’t rise.Smart regulations are key, with a recognition that airports don’t exist in a theoretically pure competitive market. A requirement to invest a portion of profits to airport improvement would be a good starting point. But if Mr. Carney wants to raise money and truly improve airline travel in Canada, he should lift existing ownership and cabotage rules and sell airport lands and assets to the highest bidder.He wants Canada to swing for the fences. So why did he just lay down a bunt?","title":"Airport reform moves to the runway, at last","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/opinion/editorials/article-airport-reform-moves-to-the-runway-at-last/","id":160},{"content_hash":"5ecdb45815c78083a96e4fa2e61cf79374f4599e50a12285ded0a22e215b99f7","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:37.551467+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-joe-hockeys-gaffes-30567","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Joe Hockey’s gaffes Published: August 15, 2014 1:13am EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-joe-hockeys-gaffes-30567 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-joe-hockeys-gaffes-30567 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics including Joe Hockey’s bungles, ASIO Director-General of Security David Irvine, metadata and the Twitter craze #otherthingsthepoordontdo. Video Joe Hockey The Week in Politics David Irvine Stephen Parker, Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Authors Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.73yh4kmu7","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Joe Hockey’s gaffes","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-joe-hockeys-gaffes-30567","id":161},{"content_hash":"92252194cd56ee9b6eb1e1199e82167a4f2a36268805fd171e419a5b5a76379e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:36.631957+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-futur-musee-de-l-uvre-notre-dame_2072333_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Le futur musée de l'œuvre Notre-Dame Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Le futur musée de l'œuvre Notre-Dame","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-futur-musee-de-l-uvre-notre-dame_2072333_1819218.html","id":162},{"content_hash":"bc684f11aa52791e01ba51d673cab3ccc0ffb659aeeab35838186492f3033d40","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:36.350261+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-101-why-pyne-has-failed-to-sell-his-education-reforms-30242","language":"","text":"Politics 101: why Pyne has failed to sell his education ‘reforms’ Published: August 17, 2014 4:23pm EDT Christopher Pyne’s education “reforms” have been wildly unpopular, and he hasn’t been very successful in winning people over. Why? AAP https://theconversation.com/politics-101-why-pyne-has-failed-to-sell-his-education-reforms-30242 https://theconversation.com/politics-101-why-pyne-has-failed-to-sell-his-education-reforms-30242 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article No. And with that, Clive Palmer indicated that the populist Palmer United Party (PUP) would not support the federal government’s proposed deregulation of the tertiary education system. At least in its short life-span, the PUP has shown itself to be thoroughly consistent in its inconsistency. A backflip isn’t impossible. Nevertheless, while I’ve written elsewhere about the merits of education minister Christopher Pyne’s changes to higher education, we need to talk about the politics too. For the long, sorry saga serves as an ideal case study of how not to go about building support for a controversial reform program. The first problem was that of consultation. When the reforms were announced in the May budget, the government caught all of the key stakeholders off-guard. The lack of prior consultation meant the government struggled from the outset to avoid the perception that its new policy simply reflected a pre-ordained template to turn the sector into a market. A little research is highly illuminating in this regard. In November 2013, Pyne appointed a review panel to develop policy recommendations. “Demand”, not “value” or “access” or anything else, was positioned as the dominant metric; the report was entitled the Review of the Demand-Driven System. The review panel comprised David Kemp, former education minister in the Howard government, and Andrew Norton, his long-time adviser. And no-one else. Submissions were invited, with a deadline of December 16. In the month or so available for input, only two of the more than 80 submissions received were from organisations representing students and only four from concerned faculty members. A total of just six came from the people actually inside the classrooms. As soon as fee deregulation was announced, therefore, the government was struggling to argue that its policy was anything but ideological. Pyne’s plan soon met with stiff public resistance. Many were concerned about long-term student debt and a narrowing of access. HECS architect Bruce Chapman pointed out that deregulation would mean a doubling or even a tripling of fees for some courses. In this context, the government found, to its evident astonishment, that it could not even rely on support from the one group of people it should have been able to count on: university vice-chancellors. Ian Young, vice-chancellor of the Australian National University (ANU) and chairman of the Group of Eight (Go8), recently described fee deregulation as the “holy grail” (evidently, Professor Young needs to get out more). But other vice-chancellors were not as enthusiastic. Vice-chancellors from smaller and regional universities worried that increased competition would result in a shift of students, prestige and funds to the Go8, leaving them out in the cold. As Australian Catholic University vice-chancellor Greg Craven colourfully pointed out: The most important thing is not that you have the best university in the world but the best university system in the world. You don’t want to have one Rolls-Royce and 12 clapped-out Commodores. By any logic, Pyne’s winter 2014 “listening tour” of regional universities should have come before the policy announcement, not afterwards. The second flaw in the politics was the government’s inability to show how deregulation would produce better tangible outcomes. Research is notoriously hard to quantify, so the government had its work cut out for it on that front. But it proceeded to play a poor hand terribly. Many scholars (including myself) pointed out that the government’s desire to pursue a competition-driven model based on the American system drew all the wrong lessons about why US universities regularly dominate global research rankings. Nevertheless, the government pressed ahead. Confusing obduracy for resolve, it produced a shamefully misleading table on the 2014 Times Higher Education Rankings: When compared with countries, not entire continents, Australia has the fourth-most universities on that readily Googlable list: more than France or China or Japan or Russia. It punches way above its weight. The US would have to have 57 universities on the list to match Australia’s GDP to Top University ratio. Meanwhile, on the teaching front, the government was unable to avoid the lingering perception that students would pay more but not receive value for money. Why was it unable to rectify this? Largely because the government wanted to avoid having a conversation about the extent to which the reforms were driven by business rather than educational considerations. In 2012, international education was Australia’s fourth-largest export, generating about A$14.5 billion in revenue. There are currently 233,099 international students in Australia, or 22.3% of the total, the highest proportion of all OECD countries. The government has strenuously avoided acknowledging in public that the “demand” it was really interested in was foreign rather than local, but the budget papers themselves are explicit: We are vying for students in a fiercely competitive international market … Currently, our universities have limited prospects of competing with the best in Europe and North America and the fast-developing universities of Asia. The government knew that if the public grasped that under the new system Australian students would be paying for what international students demanded, instead of international students effectively subsidising locals (as currently occurs), the policy was dead in the water. The final issue with the Pyne reforms was that the government signally failed to provide any concrete substance to its vision of the future. The policy is heavy on process; the Norton-Kemp “review” is very heavy going for even the most dedicated insomniacs like myself. But the government has been unable to say what higher education would look like ten, 20, or 40 years down the track, precisely because under its plan the government would abdicate the shaping of that future in favour of market forces. Again, this made the policy hard to sell. The government put itself in a position where it could not tell voters what they would be paying for their children’s education down the line nor what they would be getting for their money. Instead, it was forced to rely on a campaign of negativity, denigrating the current university system – whose support it badly needed. The irony of this political calamity is that it should have been anything but unanticipated. If Pyne had done his homework, he would know that deregulation of higher education was a central plank in John Hewson’s neoliberal Fightback! program, resoundingly rejected by the public in the “unlosable election” way back in 1993. Pyne would also know that, sobered by this experience, the Howard government repeatedly restricted itself to piecemeal incrementalism, resisting substantial pressure from the right wing to go further, faster. Kemp, Norton and prime minister Tony Abbott were key figures in these earlier crusades. Pyne, with his long history as a moderate, should have known better than to help them pursue their holy grail once more. Higher education policy Fee deregulation Christopher Pyne Higher education funding Jamie Miller, Cornell University Author Jamie Miller Postdoctoral Fellow in History, Mario Einaudi Center for International Studies, Cornell University Disclosure statement Jamie Miller does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.fgtr69a54","title":"Politics 101: why Pyne has failed to sell his education ‘reforms’","url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-101-why-pyne-has-failed-to-sell-his-education-reforms-30242","id":163},{"content_hash":"88eaae8c7042dc3b67831e5eeb6fa09d6683d49a0d265de0d52ccbc21d6efd69","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:35.230533+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/investing/personal-finance/article-what-will-airport-privatization-mean-for-travellers-in-canada/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Travellers at Toronto's Pearson International Airport. Proponents of Ottawa's proposed privatization model point to fewer cancellations and more international routes as potential benefits.Sammy Kogan/The Canadian Press CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountTravellers could see fewer cancelled flights, more international routes offered by low-cost carriers and sleeker amenities if Ottawa’s plan to privatize airports follows in the footsteps of major regional hubs in other countries, proponents say. But critics warn that success elsewhere won’t be easily replicated in Canada, where a lack of competition adds different considerations.The model proposed by Prime Minister Mark Carney at the inaugural Canada Investment Summit would involve opening up the doors to private investment in Canada’s major airports, while the federal government would retain ownership of the underlying land and assets. Proponents argue that this model has revitalized airports around the world.The bottom line is “the airports seem to improve,” Michael Weisbach, co-author of a seminal study on airport ownership models by the U.S. National Bureau of Economic Research, said in an interview. “They start to win awards, the cancellation rates went down, the number of routes that fly into cities goes up.”At Canada’s first-ever investment summit in Toronto, Prime Minister Mark Carney says he wants to see private investors operate Canada's four largest airports.The Canadian PressIn fact, the study, most recently updated this year, found that flight cancellation rates dipped as much as 39 per cent under private equity ownership. (It found no similar benefits after private acquisitions that didn’t involve private equity.)When JPMorgan Asset Management first bought a stake in Cairns airport in Queensland, Australia, as part of a 99-year lease with the regional government, the effects on service and infrastructure were immediate, Mr. Weisbach said. Some improvements were small and aesthetic. “Where the other airports look a little bit rundown,” Mr. Weisbach said, “they had nice coffee shops, nice gift shops – the stores are all much nicer.”But the biggest changes involved the number of international routes available, including from lower-cost carriers. “They negotiated with governments all around Asia to put in non-stop flights from Tokyo and Seoul and Singapore to Cairns,” said Mr. Weisbach, who is also a professor of finance at Ohio State University. “The whole community benefited.”While Cairns has a uniquely favourable location near some of Australia’s most famous natural landmarks, the advantages it experienced were not outliers. A 2023 University of Alberta study of 2,400 airports found that those privately operated routinely functioned more efficiently, with airports purchased by private equity funds in particular seeing flight cancellations decline by 50 per cent on average.But a direct comparison with other jurisdictions may fail to consider Canada’s unique competitive environment and regulations, which would affect how privatization plays out, according to researchers who have studied international models. A privatized airport would need to examine both airport fees and flight-related costs to maximize revenue for shareholders, said David Macdonald, a senior economist for the Canadian Centre for Policy Alternatives.Airport fees include “everything that you’re going to pay as an individual going to the airport,” such as parking or baggage carts, Mr. Macdonald said.In Britain and Europe, many privatized airports charge the equivalent of up to $3.50 for baggage carts, for example.“You’re also charging the airlines more to deplane passengers, to take off, to land,” Mr. Macdonald said. At Scotland’s busiest airport, Edinburgh International, drivers are charged the equivalent of roughly $17 to enter the drop-off zone, with similar fees across a handful of privately operated airports in Britain. Besides being unpopular with locals doing pickups, the fees also increase the cost of ride-hailing services. While in Edinburgh’s case, the charges were introduced decades after the British Airports Authority was privatized, fees have been raised aggressively in the past 10 years. The Scottish Conservatives last year accused the airport of making travellers a “cash cow.” Airport bosses, for their part, said the move was meant to reduce emissions from automobiles, which they called “the most polluting mode” of travel.While some airports privatized internationally may draw in more routes and still keep airfares from rising substantially, a competitive environment could be an important factor, Mr. Macdonald said. “If you have a situation where there’s actual airport competition, then you can steal flights from other airports,” he said. “Instead of increasing the cost to passengers, you increase the volume of flights, you move more people through your airport, have better use of space.”Such a move is possible in Europe, with many airports in close proximity, but not in Canada. “People in Montreal aren’t going to drive to Ottawa,” Mr. Macdonald said. Meanwhile, a report from the Canadian Labour Congress released this week said that privatization of airports in Australia both squeezed more money from passengers and put airport jobs at risk.Revenue collected from airlines per passenger at Perth Airport, for example, rose by more than 60 per cent over 10 years, the trade union organization found, while Sydney Airport trimmed 40 per cent of its work force after postsale job protections expired. Matthew da Mota, research director at the Canadian Shield Institute, a public-policy think tank, has researched Ottawa’s proposal. He emphasized that while much remains unknown, Mr. Carney’s model could generate consumer benefits beyond travel at Canada’s major airports. A shift toward private investment could unlock new funding for more airports in remote communities, better roads and digital infrastructure, without requiring the government to borrow as much to finance them, he said. One example cited by Ottawa is new broadband internet infrastructure.But he still feels that the the immediate gains for travellers remain dubious. “I’ve had people ask me in the last day, ‘Do we have a major problem with airports not functioning super well?’” Mr. da Mota said. “I think for most people, it’s not always the best experience, but it’s fine.”","title":"What could airport privatization mean for travellers in Canada?","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/investing/personal-finance/article-what-will-airport-privatization-mean-for-travellers-in-canada/","id":164},{"content_hash":"94febbef8eb1e2d2c7749f67832978b166739d28d5dc4edb7dea19c8fe6fc250","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:35.187455+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/bull-renforcent-du-commonwealth-bull-denationalisation-de-l-acier_3140710_1819218.html","language":"","text":"• Renforcent du Commonwealth • Dénationalisation de l'acier Londres, 6 novembre. - M. Winston Churchill présente aujourd'hui mardi son gouvernement aux Communes. Bien que la santé du roi s'améliore constamment, il n'était pas possible au souverain d'ouvrir lui-même la session du nouveau Parlement. La cérémonie est donc dépourvue de sa splendeur habituelle, et les lords n'ont pas eu besoin de revêtir leurs impressionnantes robes de soie et d'hermine. Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"• Renforcent du Commonwealth • Dénationalisation de l'acier","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/bull-renforcent-du-commonwealth-bull-denationalisation-de-l-acier_3140710_1819218.html","id":165},{"content_hash":"f699180e4ea47ad01291d51e65b1bcf8157ede6e00c4d753eb27f48945a941c7","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:35.152645+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-sitting-versus-standing-30145","language":"","text":"Health Check: sitting versus standing Published: August 17, 2014 11:51pm EDT Alternating between sitting and standing is best. ramsey beyer/Flickr, CC BY https://theconversation.com/health-check-sitting-versus-standing-30145 https://theconversation.com/health-check-sitting-versus-standing-30145 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article It seems the world is finally coming to terms with the fact that humans evolved to stand, not to sit – well, health researchers, savvy office workers and many commuters, at least. The evidence is mounting to show that spending too much sitting at work, during your commute and for leisure increases your risk of diabetes, certain cancers, heart disease and early death. This isn’t a new revelation. Bernardino Ramazzini first described the ill effects of too much sitting at work in the 1700s and advised people to break up sitting and stimulate blood flow. But technological advances and ergonomic experts have made sitting more comfortable and more enticing. Australian adults now sit for an average of nearly nine hours a day. This is longer than the time that most people spend sleeping. So, is it time to buy a standing desk? Let’s examine the evidence. Many people know when they’ve been sitting too long because their back or neck gets sore. These are effects many can relate to because we can actually feel them. But it’s what you can’t feel or see that you may need to be concerned about. Canadian researcher Dr Peter Katzmarzyk, for instance, found that those who sat almost all of the time had nearly a one-third higher risk of early death than those who stood almost all of the time. University College London researcher Dr Emmanuel Stamatakis found similar results among women in the United Kingdom: those whose work involved mostly standing/walking about had a 32% lower risk of early death than those who worked in sitting jobs. For the average adult, standing burns more calories and involves more muscular contraction than sitting. One study reported 2.5 times higher average muscular activity of the thigh when standing compared to sitting. This is important for improving blood sugar profiles and vascular health, reducing the risk of early death. But it’s important to note prolonged standing can also have adverse health effects. Compared to sitting, when we stand, our hearts and circulatory systems work harder to maintain blood flow to the brain, because they are countering the effects of gravity. Standing still for long periods of time can lead to swelling, heaviness or cramping of the legs. Enforced standing has actually been used as an interrogation technique (though former US secretary of defense Donald Rumsfeld couldn’t understand why it was only for four hours – he stood for eight to ten hours a day). If standing still for too long is potentially risky, what should you do? To obtain the health benefits of standing and reduce the potential adverse effects, the best option is to alternate between sitting and standing. Our message is to stand up, sit less and move more. Alternating between sitting and standing will increase muscular contractions, stimulating blood flow and resulting in more calories burnt and healthier blood sugar levels. Recent findings from our lab show that alternating between 30 minutes of sitting and standing can improve blood sugar levels after a meal. Now, if you’re leaning towards getting a standing desk but are concerned about your concentration and productivity, there’s some good news. Research shows task performance such as typing, reading and performing cognitive tests is largely unaffected by standing desks. Thomas Jefferson, Winston Churchill, Virginia Woolf, and Ernest Hemingway fought off the urge to sit with the aid of standing desks. It might be time for you to do the same, and alternate between sitting and standing. If you’re still not ready for a stand-up desk, these tips might help get you moving: take regular breaks during long drives in the car stand up on public transport choose more active ways to hang out with friends (swap the cafe for a walk) stand at the bar instead of sitting on the comfy couches have standing meetings (they usually end faster) stand up while on the phone. Physical activity Health Check Bethany Howard, Neville Owen, Baker Heart and Diabetes Institute Author Bethany Howard PhD Candidate and Research Assistant, Physical Activity Laboratory, Baker Heart and Diabetes Institute Contributor Neville Owen Program Head: Behavioural and Generational Change; Head, Behavioural Epidemiology Laboratory, Baker Heart and Diabetes Institute Disclosure statement Bethany Howard receives funding from the NHMRC and the Heart Foundation. Neville Owen receives funding from the NHMRC. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.kkfq9nk9r","title":"Health Check: sitting versus standing","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-sitting-versus-standing-30145","id":166},{"content_hash":"bbb0316226f5d4e650c61b82262c6f82bd2002b6fdd4d1200c86da95fbad1865","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:34.926287+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/types-of-adverse-media-categorisation","language":"","text":"What are the Types of Adverse Media Categorisation?ByEmmanuel AgwuApril 25, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Is Adverse Media?What are the Categories Of Adverse Media?Why Different Adverse Media Types Are ImportantBottom LineShare this postAdverse media, leaning on the two words that contain the term, implies adverse and media. Adverse refers to a factor(s) and items that are harmful or unfavourable for a cause or intent. The media is associated with publishing, the internet, and broadcasting. It involves disseminating information to the public or the masses. This article addresses Adverse Media, how it affects businesses and how it can be categorised. It begins with addressing just what Adverse media is. What Is Adverse Media? Adverse Media can be described as negative or damaging information about individuals, organisations or formal entities that have the tendency to be a risk to businesses or formal institutions. This information can be disseminated through the news, articles, social media posts, legal filings, and other public records that can serve as evidence of possible involvement in financial crimes, corruption, and illegal activities. Since adverse media is so damaging for companies, companies must be proactive about effectively identifying, collecting, and analysing adverse media data is essential for managing reputational, legal and regulatory risks. Adverse media is crucial; this is because it can help identify potential risks and threats that are associated with individuals or entities. For example, financial institutions can use adverse media solutions to screen clients and partners for potential money laundering or terrorist financing risks. Adverse media can also aid companies in gaining access to the reputation of their business partners or vendors and ensuring that they have not been associated with unethical or illegal activities. What are the Categories Of Adverse Media? There are several types of adverse media to take note of in order to ensure that companies are safe from risks, scrupulous customers or users; 1. Violence Violence can involve clients directly or may be carried out on their behalf; it may generate crucial and several types of averse media, especially when it is involved in a criminal organised venture or intent. Violent crimes can also be associated with political and workforce conflicts and disputes or exacerbated patterns of human abuse. 2. Financial Crimes Financial crime entails a large or wide range of activities which includes; money laundering, bribery, forgery, fraud, insider training etc. Financial crime is a very broad and complex criminal field and can be covered in both traditional as well as emerging media, e.g. social media. 3. Terrorism Financing terrorism and other terror-related activities tends to generate significant adverse media across different media outlets. These types of terror-related media can include subtle to dominant encouragement, radicalisation, as well as the direct perpetration of terrorist acts. Related Article - Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important? 4. Fraud Fraud can be committed using a variety of mechanisms. Fraudulent letters are one of the ways it can also include; telephone calls, emails (spamming and phishing) and illegal or unprotected websites laced with malicious codes, bots and viruses. Fraud can either be a civil or criminal offence. Fraud has a lot of different end goals; it can involve illegal monetary gain or a gain of property, citizenship benefits and passing immigration protocols. 5. Cybercrime Any criminal activity that involves the use of a computer or a networked device like a phone or a tablet is considered a cybercrime and can be filed as such with a law enforcement agency or reported as one. Cybercrime covers a cluster of illicit activities that are used to facilitate offences like; money laundering, identity fraud, etcetera. 6. Sexual Crimes Sexual crime entails a wide range of activities, from violence, abuse and rape to distributing or possessing illegal imagery and videography. Sexual crimes also facilitate other illicit activities like money laundering and cybercrime. 7. NarcoticsThis involves the use or sale of hard drugs as well as their production and trafficking. Narcotics offences and associated news are usually related to financial crimes, which include money laundering and terrorism financing. 8. Regulatory Misconduct Regulatory misconduct can be a form of crime itself; it can also be known as non-compliance. It is an indication that clients are engaged in other types of criminal activity. The types of adverse media stories that are related to regulatory offences regularly cross over with financial crime. 9. Property Adverse media stories involving properties can entail activities which include theft, arson, larceny, and burglary. Etc. It can constitute civil or criminal misconduct and vary largely in scope. Property crimes are usually reported across media outlets. Why Different Adverse Media Types Are Important Adverse media categorisation is a very beneficial KYC tool; the FATF recommendations require financial institutions to understand their client's reputations, including previous criminal liabilities such as involvement in money laundering investigations. Compliance with that directive necessitates screening for all forms of negative media, which generally entails time-consuming manual inspections of enormous, unsorted volumes of news articles, blog posts, and social media posts. Adverse media is any negative or potentially damaging information that can be found in public sources such as news articles, regulatory reports, legal filings, or social media. Different adverse media types are important because they can provide valuable information about potential risks or concerns related to individuals, businesses, or other entities. Here are some reasons why different adverse media types are important: 1. Adverse Media Categorisation Can Reveal Potential Financial Crimes: Adverse media can uncover information about individuals or businesses engaging in financial crimes such as fraud, money laundering, or corruption. That way, it can warn a company about dealing with a firm that can pose potential risks. 2. Adverse Media Categorisation Can Highlight Reputational Risks: Adverse media can reveal information that could harm a company's or individual's reputation, such as unethical behaviour, legal violations, or negative public opinion. 3. Adverse Media Categorisation Can Help Identify Potential Compliance Risks: Adverse media can provide information about companies or individuals that may be in violation of laws or regulations, which can help identify potential compliance risks. 4. They Can Assist With Due Diligence Adverse media can be an important source of information for conducting due diligence on individuals or businesses prior to entering into a business relationship or investment. 5. They Can Support Risk Management Efforts Adverse media can help organisations identify and assess potential risks related to their operations, customers, or partners, which can inform risk management strategies. Different types of adverse media are important because they can provide valuable information about potential risks, compliance issues, and reputational concerns, which can inform decision-making and risk management efforts. Read Also - Adverse Media Screening Effective Practices Bottom Line Adverse media screening is a process that involves the use of technology and other tools to search for and analyse adverse media related to specific individuals or entities. Adverse media can be an important source of information for companies and organisations to identify and assess potential risks related to their operations, customers, or partners. Adverse media screening is often used as part of due diligence efforts for compliance and risk management purposes. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for Adverse Media screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a free demo today.Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"What are the Types of Adverse Media Categorisation?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/types-of-adverse-media-categorisation","id":167},{"content_hash":"524a72e066c81d1397b2f86bd53b0603e82e2f8816c70caa4cb6831f8d0f8f17","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:34.677356+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/logging/","language":"","text":"Logging¶ See also How to configure and use logging Django logging overview Django’s logging module extends Python’s builtin logging. Logging is configured as part of the general Django django.setup() function, so it’s always available unless explicitly disabled. Django’s default logging configuration¶ By default, Django uses Python’s logging.config.dictConfig format. Default logging conditions¶ The full set of default logging conditions are: When DEBUG is True: The django logger sends messages in the django hierarchy (except django.server) at the INFO level or higher to the console. When DEBUG is False: The django logger sends messages in the django hierarchy (except django.server) with ERROR or CRITICAL level to AdminEmailHandler. Independently of the value of DEBUG: The django.server logger sends messages at the INFO level or higher to the console. All loggers except django.server propagate logging to their parents, up to the root django logger. The console and mail_admins handlers are attached to the root logger to provide the behavior described above. Python’s own defaults send records of level WARNING and higher to the console. Default logging definition¶ Django’s default logging configuration inherits Python’s defaults. It’s available as django.utils.log.DEFAULT_LOGGING and defined in django/utils/log.py: { \"version\": 1, \"disable_existing_loggers\": False, \"filters\": { \"require_debug_false\": { \"()\": \"django.utils.log.RequireDebugFalse\", }, \"require_debug_true\": { \"()\": \"django.utils.log.RequireDebugTrue\", }, }, \"formatters\": { \"django.server\": { \"()\": \"django.utils.log.ServerFormatter\", \"format\": \"[{server_time}] {message}\", \"style\": \"{\", } }, \"handlers\": { \"console\": { \"level\": \"INFO\", \"filters\": [\"require_debug_true\"], \"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\", }, \"django.server\": { \"level\": \"INFO\", \"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\", \"formatter\": \"django.server\", }, \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"filters\": [\"require_debug_false\"], \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", }, }, \"loggers\": { \"django\": { \"handlers\": [\"console\", \"mail_admins\"], \"level\": \"INFO\", }, \"django.server\": { \"handlers\": [\"django.server\"], \"level\": \"INFO\", \"propagate\": False, }, }, } See Configuring logging on how to complement or replace this default logging configuration. Django logging extensions¶ Django provides a number of utilities to handle the particular requirements of logging in a web server environment. Loggers¶ Django provides several built-in loggers. django¶ The parent logger for messages in the django named logger hierarchy. Django does not post messages using this name. Instead, it uses one of the loggers below. django.request¶ Log messages related to the handling of requests. 5XX responses are raised as ERROR messages; 4XX responses are raised as WARNING messages. Requests that are logged to the django.security logger aren’t logged to django.request. Messages to this logger have the following extra context: status_code: The HTTP response code associated with the request. request: The request object that generated the logging message. django.server¶ Log messages related to the handling of requests received by the server invoked by the runserver command. HTTP 5XX responses are logged as ERROR messages, 4XX responses are logged as WARNING messages, and everything else is logged as INFO. Messages to this logger have the following extra context: status_code: The HTTP response code associated with the request. request: The request object (a socket.socket) that generated the logging message. django.template¶ Log messages related to the rendering of templates. Missing context variables are logged as DEBUG messages. django.db.backends¶ Messages relating to the interaction of code with the database. For example, every application-level SQL statement executed by a request is logged at the DEBUG level to this logger. Messages to this logger have the following extra context: duration: The time taken to execute the SQL statement. sql: The SQL statement that was executed. params: The parameters that were used in the SQL call. alias: The alias of the database used in the SQL call. For performance reasons, SQL logging is only enabled when settings.DEBUG is set to True, regardless of the logging level or handlers that are installed. This logging does not include framework-level initialization (e.g. SET TIMEZONE). Turn on query logging in your database if you wish to view all database queries. django.utils.autoreload¶ Log messages related to automatic code reloading during the execution of the Django development server. This logger generates an INFO message upon detecting a modification in a source code file and may produce WARNING messages during filesystem inspection and event subscription processes. django.contrib.auth¶ Log messages related to django.contrib.auth, particularly ERROR messages are generated when a PasswordResetForm is successfully submitted but the password reset email cannot be delivered due to a mail sending exception. django.contrib.gis¶ Log messages related to GeoDjango at various points: during the loading of external GeoSpatial libraries (GEOS, GDAL, etc.) and when reporting errors. Each ERROR log record includes the caught exception and relevant contextual data. django.dispatch¶ This logger is used in Signals, specifically within the Signal class, to report issues when dispatching a signal to a connected receiver. The ERROR log record includes the caught exception as exc_info and adds the following extra context: receiver: The name of the receiver. err: The exception that occurred when calling the receiver. django.security.*¶ The security loggers will receive messages on any occurrence of SuspiciousOperation and other security-related errors. There is a sub-logger for each subtype of security error, including all SuspiciousOperations. The level of the log event depends on where the exception is handled. Most occurrences are logged as a warning, while any SuspiciousOperation that reaches the WSGI handler will be logged as an error. For example, when an HTTP Host header is included in a request from a client that does not match ALLOWED_HOSTS, Django will return a 400 response, and an error message will be logged to the django.security.DisallowedHost logger. These log events will reach the django logger by default, which mails error events to admins when DEBUG=False. Requests resulting in a 400 response due to a SuspiciousOperation will not be logged to the django.request logger, but only to the django.security logger. To silence a particular type of SuspiciousOperation, you can override that specific logger following this example: LOGGING = { # ... \"handlers\": { \"null\": { \"class\": \"logging.NullHandler\", }, }, \"loggers\": { \"django.security.DisallowedHost\": { \"handlers\": [\"null\"], \"propagate\": False, }, }, # ... } Other django.security loggers not based on SuspiciousOperation are: django.security.csrf: For CSRF failures. django.db.backends.schema¶ Logs the SQL queries that are executed during schema changes to the database by the migrations framework. Note that it won’t log the queries executed by RunPython. Messages to this logger have params and sql in their extra context (but unlike django.db.backends, not duration). The values have the same meaning as explained in django.db.backends. django.contrib.sessions¶ Log messages related to the session framework. Non-fatal errors occurring when using the django.contrib.sessions.backends.cached_db.SessionStore engine are logged as ERROR messages with the corresponding traceback. Handlers¶ Django provides one log handler in addition to those provided by the Python logging module. class AdminEmailHandler(include_html=False, email_backend=None, reporter_class=None, using=None)[source]¶ This handler sends an email to the site ADMINS for each log message it receives. If the log record contains a request attribute, the full details of the request will be included in the email. The email subject will include the phrase “internal IP” if the client’s IP address is in the INTERNAL_IPS setting; if not, it will include “EXTERNAL IP”. If the log record contains stack trace information, that stack trace will be included in the email. The include_html argument of AdminEmailHandler is used to control whether the traceback email includes an HTML attachment containing the full content of the debug web page that would have been produced if DEBUG were True. To set this value in your configuration, include it in the handler definition for django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler, like this: \"handlers\": { \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", \"include_html\": True, }, } Be aware of the security implications of logging when using the AdminEmailHandler. Email is sent using the default mailer. This can be overridden by setting the using argument of AdminEmailHandler, like this: \"handlers\": { \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", \"using\": \"internal\", }, } If the specified mailer is not configured in the MAILERS setting, no email will be sent and no additional error will be raised. By setting the deprecated email_backend argument of AdminEmailHandler, the email backend that is being used by the handler can be overridden. email_backend is not supported when MAILERS is defined or when the using argument is provided. The reporter_class argument of AdminEmailHandler allows providing an django.views.debug.ExceptionReporter subclass to customize the traceback text sent in the email body. You provide a string import path to the class you wish to use, like this: \"handlers\": { \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", \"include_html\": True, \"reporter_class\": \"somepackage.error_reporter.CustomErrorReporter\", }, } Changed in version 6.1: The using argument was added. Deprecated since version 6.1: The email_backend argument is deprecated. Use using instead. send_mail(subject, message, *args, **kwargs)[source]¶ Sends emails to admin users. To customize this behavior, you can subclass the AdminEmailHandler class and override this method. Filters¶ Django provides some log filters in addition to those provided by the Python logging module. class CallbackFilter(callback)[source]¶ This filter accepts a callback function (which should accept a single argument, the record to be logged), and calls it for each record that passes through the filter. Handling of that record will not proceed if the callback returns False. For instance, to filter out UnreadablePostError (raised when a user cancels an upload) from the admin emails, you would create a filter function: from django.http import UnreadablePostError def skip_unreadable_post(record): if record.exc_info: exc_type, exc_value = record.exc_info[:2] if isinstance(exc_value, UnreadablePostError): return False return True and then add it to your logging config: LOGGING = { # ... \"filters\": { \"skip_unreadable_posts\": { \"()\": \"django.utils.log.CallbackFilter\", \"callback\": skip_unreadable_post, }, }, \"handlers\": { \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"filters\": [\"skip_unreadable_posts\"], \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", }, }, # ... } class RequireDebugFalse[source]¶ This filter will only pass on records when settings.DEBUG is False. This filter is used as follows in the default LOGGING configuration to ensure that the AdminEmailHandler only sends error emails to admins when DEBUG is False: LOGGING = { # ... \"filters\": { \"require_debug_false\": { \"()\": \"django.utils.log.RequireDebugFalse\", }, }, \"handlers\": { \"mail_admins\": { \"level\": \"ERROR\", \"filters\": [\"require_debug_false\"], \"class\": \"django.utils.log.AdminEmailHandler\", }, }, # ... } class RequireDebugTrue[source]¶ This filter is similar to RequireDebugFalse, except that records are passed only when DEBUG is True.","title":"Logging¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/logging/","id":168},{"content_hash":"4e8d20532f3e09c658fb72b010ac34b9a2f30aa58ddd00a72f3554a07333ec9e","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:34.614502+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-04/max-verstappen-wins-f1-bahrain-grand-prix-in-malaysia/107227400","language":"","text":"Max Verstappen wins F1 Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia, George Russell retires lateTopic:Formula 1Posted Sun 4 Oct 2026 at 11:19pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 11:19pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 11:19pmMax Verstappen secured his first win of the season in Malaysia. (AP: Aijaz Rahi)In short:Max Verstappen has secured his first win of the Formula 1 season, as Kimi Antonelli extended his drivers' championship advantage.Antonelli finished second, while teammate and title rival George Russell suffered a suspected power unit failure with five laps to go.What's next?Formula 1 heads to Singapore for the next race of the season on Sunday, October 11.abc.net.au/news/max-verstappen-wins-f1-bahrain-grand-prix-in-malaysia/107227400Link copiedShareShare articleMax Verstappen's long wait for another Formula 1 race win is finally over, and the drivers' championship race might be over, too.Verstappen took his first victory of an often-frustrating year for the four-time champion at Formula 1's Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia on Sunday. A retirement for George Russell handed a huge title boost to Kimi Antonelli.Verstappen was leading from Antonelli and Russell behind the safety car with five laps to go when Russell suddenly pulled over in his Mercedes.Loading Twitter contentAntonelli further stretched his lead over teammate Russell from 66 points to 84, with seven races remaining on the schedule, including those in Qatar and Abu Dhabi.Mercedes told Antonelli over the radio it appeared Russell had suffered a power unit problem.Antonelli finished second and Ferrari's Lewis Hamilton passed Verstappen's Red Bull teammate Isack Hadjar for third.Four-time champion Verstappen had not won since the final race of last season, but upgrades to his Red Bull car made him competitive again in Malaysia this week.\"Good job guys, wow,\" he told the team over the radio, thanking them for a \"fantastic weekend\".Delays for rain and software issuesThe first wet-weather race for F1 in 2026 started more than 90 minutes later than planned. Loading Twitter contentThe start was first delayed 40 minutes for heavy rain, then suspended again when cars had apparent software problems on the formation lap.When it finally did get underway, Verstappen was slow off the line and lost places to Antonelli and Russell. He chased down Russell and then got past Antonelli in a side-by-side fight after a safety-car restart.After Verstappen took the win, he had another small delay before hearing his anthem. Crowd chants filled an awkward silence as Verstappen waited on the top step of the podium before the Dutch anthem finally began.The race was relocated because of the Iran war, becoming F1's first event at the Sepang circuit in Malaysia since 2017.Ferrari recovers from early blundersHamilton's third place capped a remarkable recovery for the seven-time champion and ends a run of five races without a podium finish for the Italian team.It seemed almost impossible early in the race as Hamilton, and his teammate Charles Leclerc, were near the back of the pack. Ferrari had gambled on sending them to the start on dry-weather tyres as the track began to dry after rain, causing Hamilton to drop from second to near the back.Leclerc also overtook Hadjar late on to follow Hamilton into fourth place, as Hadjar ended up fifth. Oscar Piastri was sixth for McLaren after a punchy late battle with Liam Lawson of Racing Bulls, who was seventh after racing near the front at the start.Fernando Alonso was eighth in Aston Martin's best result of the season, Lando Norris ninth after going off-track and losing places, with Lawson's teammate Arvid Lindblad 10th.APThe ABC of SPORTSports content to make you think... or allow you not to. A newsletter delivered each Saturday.Email addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleSportButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionLIVEWallabies must 'walk the walk' if 40-year-old curse is to endAnalysis by Luke PentonyKiwi driver endures 42G impact from high-speed crash at BathurstTopic:Motor SportsThe mixed fortunes of Australia's last South African touring partyTopic:CricketPiastri hopes for better in Singapore as fading challenger promises 'all-out' attackTopic:Formula 1Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsBahrainFormula 1MalaysiaMotor SportsSportTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Max Verstappen wins F1 Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia, George Russell retires late","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-04/max-verstappen-wins-f1-bahrain-grand-prix-in-malaysia/107227400","id":169},{"content_hash":"59708a51c802312ad83f74be0d833d1a31e834bb0dc04175c0b5725076d7eecf","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:34.161687+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-bill-shorten-30834","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Bill Shorten Published: August 22, 2014 2:36am EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-bill-shorten-30834 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-bill-shorten-30834 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics including Bill Shorten’s identification of himself in a rape allegation, what the public’s reaction will be, how social media and the 24 hour news cycle has changed how long a subject is in the public and the public’s respect for politicians. Video Bill Shorten The Week in Politics Stephen Parker, Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Authors Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.pqar3v6a4","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Bill Shorten","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-bill-shorten-30834","id":170},{"content_hash":"3c7429e2160301a6c85f14ae258a75a1f4684c59f98f8bf02392b94881c35317","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:33.984511+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-cours-par-correspondance-de-renseignement-public-du-premier-degre-commenceront-le-15-novembre_2073118_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Les cours par correspondance de renseignement public du premier degré commenceront le 15 novembre Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Les cours par correspondance de renseignement public du premier degré commenceront le 15 novembre","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-cours-par-correspondance-de-renseignement-public-du-premier-degre-commenceront-le-15-novembre_2073118_1819218.html","id":171},{"content_hash":"c5d05d657f1f1f9ce320bc0d2f43da4113784831f9f5b41a6beddbdd7392e9c7","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:33.573937+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/faceless-system-in-place-for-central-gst-payers/articleshow/134803787.cms","language":"","text":"Faceless system in place for central GST payers - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIBusinessIndia BusinessBudget 2026Mutual FundsInfrastructureGold Rates TodaySilver Rates Today Platinum Rates TodayPetrol PriceDiesel PriceCNG PriceLPG PriceFinancial LiteracyDon't Get ScammedFinancial CalculatorsInternational BusinessCybersecurityIFSC CodeVideosFinancial Literacy SummitEconomyToday's ePaperLiveBudgetIFSCPAN CardAadhaar CardIPOIncome TaxSavings Growth CalculatorNewsBusiness NewsIndia Business NewsFaceless system in place for central GST payersTrendingUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayFaceless system in place for central GST payersTNN / Oct 09, 2026, 06:46 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint FM Nirmala Sitharaman chairs the 57th meeting of GST Council. Rate changes will now be an annual feature, says Sitharaman NEW DELHI: Finance minister Nirmala Sitharaman on Thursday announced a faceless system for 2 lakh central GST payers operating in multiple states, following up on rate rationalisation and process reforms undertaken by the Centre and the states.After last year's rate cuts, the effective GST rate on all goods and services has come down from 11.8% to 10.8%. Together with the procedural simplification, 99% of the issues have been resolved, FM said, adding that rate changes will now be an annual feature that will be implemented from April every year.\"For the next-gen GST reforms, 99% of the issues have been addressed. Everything on the process and rate side has been driven from a principle of trust,\" she said, adding that govt was keeping the door open for further fine-tuning, as and when required.\"Going forward, we intend to bring a centralised tax administration for these multiple CGST jurisdiction for taxpayers with a unified window for all their CGST-related functions of scrutiny of returns, audit, adjudication, appeal, taxpayer services and grievance redressal,\" she told reporters, while asserting the Centre's initiative.A working group within the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs has submitted its report, and the proposed framework would be put up for public consultation before the next budget and will be implemented during the next financial year.The FM said the faceless approach adopted in income tax was appreciated by taxpayers and the Centre intended to bring in the same reform in the administration of GST.Sitharaman clarified that it was a govt initiative and had nothing to do with the GST Council. She said there were nearly 69.5 lakh taxpayers registered under the CGST administration, which included these two lakh taxpayers under multiple GST jurisdictions.Administrative control over GST taxpayers has been divided between the central and state tax authorities based on a specific turnover-based formula determined by the GST Council to ensure a single interface. While administrative control over 90% of taxpayers, who have turnover below Rs 1.5 crore, has been kept under state tax administration, the remaining 10% is with the central administration.Taxpayers with turnover above Rs 1.5 crore are divided equally between the central and state tax administrations.You use AI every day. Now get your AI Quotient. Take the AIQ test.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaPhotostoriesQuote of the day: “Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind”; William Shakespeare’s timeless words on how true love goes beyond what we seeGender pain gap: Women experience more pain than men across the whole body, major global study finds ‘shocking’ dataFrom historic tiaras to priceless heirlooms: The royal jewels Kate Middleton has left untouchedKangana Ranaut goes full Himachali royalty in a statement topi, Kullu shawl, and a cultural nod to President Droupadi Murmu5 habits that matter more than supplements: Doctor shares simple daily habits for better sleep, fitness, nutrition, stress management and preventive health that can support a longer, healthier life5 lipstick shades to pair with your chaniya choli this NavratriOptical illusion personality test: What you see first claims to reveal whether you are a practical thinker or a gut-feeling personKareena Kapoor Khan just gave motorsport a Bebo makeover in sleek black tailoring10 baby names inspired by astronauts, space pioneers and cosmic explorersGen Z is making Indian textiles cool again: 5 heritage weaves that are having a fashion comeback123Hot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn BusinessEntire WebsiteFaceless system in place for central GST payersPerfect storm: Sensex slips amid rising oil, bond yieldsStarlink entry row: Is Ambani the real boss of India, asks Elon MuskGST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers, eases refund rulesUS sanctions Indian firms, individuals as Iran oil crackdown widensWoman claims 2.5 acres was ancestral after stepmother sold it; why HC rejected pleaGST reforms: Top things announced by FM Sitharaman-led GST Council & what they mean for businessesAs Trump sours H-1B visa dream, which Indian IT professionals face biggest hit?GST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers; prosecution threshold raised to Rs 5 crTcs Q2 Results: TCS profit jumps 15% to ₹13,884cr, AI revenue tops $3.1bn; board announces ₹12 dividendCookie Recipe Funds Lunches: Donna Kelce shares family favorite on mugs; fundraiser clears lunch debtLauren Kyle Builds Business Empire: business coach reshapes her mindset; five-venture empire followsOld Leak Resurfaces Online: Gia Duddy’s new NFL romance revives 2024 video; fans revisit scandalMcGregor Donations Top $5m: whiskey deal helps fund first responders; hospitals and kids benefitWhy Travis Kelce's supposed farewell season is starting to look like a record chaseMahomes' Father Cleared To Travel: Texas judge allows trips to Chiefs games; Cincinnati bid deniedCPKC Stadium Expansion: Kansas City backs 