raryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Newsletter template with hero and spotlight sections | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/newsletter-template-hero-spotlight","id":203},{"content_hash":"74e9ed6f5e33d2e752ab2a81f36ce9c1b3953cb52cc5bca27bf790b29b157c22","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:25.539753+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-abbotts-first-year-31354","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Abbott’s first year Published: September 4, 2014 11:38pm EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-abbotts-first-year-31354 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-abbotts-first-year-31354 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics and the year in review for Prime Minister Tony Abbott. Has he shown his true colors or is there more to the ‘real’ Tony? Video Stephen Parker, Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Authors Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.qg5hkwvdt","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Abbott’s first year","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-abbotts-first-year-31354","id":204},{"content_hash":"9df3e9e764bb40b85ef8adcca9edfc85b19c013d98f58031fa00a00f843ad490","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:24.853807+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/internet-of-things-iot-device-security-your-home.html","language":"","text":"Securing the things you connect to the internet Connected or “smart” devices are fun and convenient — but they can gather and transmit sensitive information about your life and family. Here are steps you can take to maintain household security. AS HOME AUTOMATION ENTHUSIASTS RUSH to connect digital devices — a video doorbell, a wireless home security kit, a set of Wi-Fi lightbulbs — they are helping build an ecosystem of convenience, safety and efficiency. That ecosystem is the Internet of Things (IoT), the global network of connected smart devices. By 2030, the IoT may comprise 40 billion internet-connected objects.1 For consumers, these devices already have elevated household conveniences and services to new heights. Think sensor-based home-monitoring and communications systems, energy-efficient automated appliances, always-on virtual assistants and increasingly smart automobiles. Each device connected to a network, however, is also an endpoint criminals can exploit. And research shows they are doing exactly that: One 2024 survey found that 53% of companies suffered a security compromise from a mobile or IoT device, up from 30% in 2018.2 But the problems are not just a function of cybercriminals and unsecure devices. Many consumers don’t prioritize device security due to a lack of understanding of how risky an unsecure device can truly be and unwittingly open themselves to compromise. For instance, smartphone users often grant broad access to their device’s location services, camera, microphone, contacts and calendar — without knowing whether these requests for permissions are legitimate and how the data they generate will be secured. By providing this access, users may unintentionally give bad actors entry to additional data on the device, including sensitive information like system credentials, credit card numbers and contact information for colleagues, clients, friends and family. Simple ways to protect your home and its devices Smart security begins with an assessment of the devices that constitute your personal or family digital footprint. It’s a good idea to make a list of IoT devices in use, how they connect to the internet and what data they generate and share. All this will help you determine potential cybersecurity vulnerabilities. Some devices, like Wi-Fi videocams and car navigation systems, have been subject to highly publicized hacks. But the full spectrum of potential vulnerabilities doesn’t always make the news. Smart devices may collect data about your personal life, dwelling, habits or financial accounts, helping refine their performance and making them easier to use. But if the devices remain in public access mode, all this information may end up on the internet, where advertisers can harvest it to expand your consumer profile. This often results in an increase in unwanted pop-up ads as you browse the internet. Cybercriminals also use personal information to launch social engineering campaigns. In doing so, they take information gathered about individuals and craft communications that look legitimate but are scams. The risks associated with revealing personal information online demonstrate why it’s important to review any device’s security capabilities. Find out what data is collected, how it is protected and whether it is shared. Explore the product’s security and privacy settings to find out what data-generating features can — and should — be switched off. Also review the IoT device’s privacy settings to make sure you are not sending device information to unintended locations, such as your social media accounts. If you’re purchasing new equipment like wireless routers and tablets, find out if they have the latest security features. It is also important to know whether those standards can be updated as the technology evolves. The best security can provide powerful encryption and protection against attacks designed to compromise user credentials. 9 best practices to help secure your IoT devices These tips may help you safeguard your IoT devices and data while also limiting your digital footprint: Purchase IoT devices that offer ongoing security patches and long-term manufacturer support.Create and maintain an inventory of home IoT devices and applications.Review and manage the data that will be shared by each active IoT device.Consider maintaining non-computer IoT devices on a separate network from your computers, tablets and phones.Regularly update all device software. If available, turn on automatic updates and notifications for configuration changes.Use your router’s network configuration tool to see what devices are attached and disconnect unknown or suspicious devices or connections.Create strong passwords for devices and customize user and object names. Do not use default passwords. Enable multifactor authentication.Create a Wi-Fi guest network.Enable encryption on your router. Stay connected, stay protected To help keep your Merrill account information safe and secure, make sure your contact information is up to date and set up security and account alerts so we can stay in touch. Remember, if we need to reach out to you, we’ll NEVER ask for personal or financial information or an access code through email, text or unsolicited calls. Visit our Security Center for tips on how to recognize potential scams and learn more about how to keep your accounts safe. 1 IOT Analytics, “State of IoT 2024: Number of connected IoT devices growing 13% to 18.8 billion globally,” September 3, 2024. 2 Verizon, “2024 Mobile Security Index,” August 2024. Neither Bank of America nor its affiliates provide information security or information technology (IT) consulting services. This material is provided \"as is,“ with no guarantee of completeness, accuracy, timeliness or of the results obtained from the use of this material, and without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including, but not limited to warranties of performance, quality and fitness for a particular purpose. This material should be regarded as general information on information security and IT considerations and is not intended to provide specific information security or IT advice nor is it any substitute for your own independent investigations. If you have questions regarding your particular IT system or information security concerns, please contact your IT or information security advisor. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you Find additional cybersecurity and fraud prevention resources Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles What's trending Read more Be cyber secure: Hone your password-writing skills with this quiz Read more Be cyber secure: Dos and don’ts for your family Read more","title":"Securing the things you connect to the internet","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/internet-of-things-iot-device-security-your-home.html","id":205},{"content_hash":"f0e21a6b2de9c356bf6305687f48b0eee2e37269e602a3c59e52f707cf0f7304","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:24.380451+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/l-action-politique-et-economique-doit-redonner-confiance-a-la-majorite_2072397_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives L'action politique et économique doit redonner confiance à la majorité Par J. F. Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"L'action politique et économique doit redonner confiance à la majorité","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/l-action-politique-et-economique-doit-redonner-confiance-a-la-majorite_2072397_1819218.html","id":206},{"content_hash":"264436f7a9a8a9699ac2e324d57c6834057cdd13c45b87ef0d96980d2024e161","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:24.348051+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-five-foods-to-always-avoid-at-the-supermarket-27107","language":"","text":"Health Check: five foods to always avoid at the supermarket Published: September 7, 2014 9:06pm EDT Ideally lollies, biscuits, sugar-sweetened drinks, potato crisps and processed meats will never appear in your shopping trolley. Matt/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA https://theconversation.com/health-check-five-foods-to-always-avoid-at-the-supermarket-27107 https://theconversation.com/health-check-five-foods-to-always-avoid-at-the-supermarket-27107 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Want to stack the nutrition odds in your favour? The key is good food so here are five things to never let into your shopping trolley: lollies, biscuits, sugar-sweetened drinks, potato crisps and processed meats. Known as discretionary foods, all five are high in either added sugars, saturated fat or salt. Discretionary foods provide kilojoules but not many nutrients. Consuming a lot of discretionary foods and drinks increases your risks of weight gain, obesity, heart disease, type 2 diabetes and certain cancers. Unless you’re extremely active, it is unlikely that you can eat a lot of these foods and also be a healthy weight. Lollies Dental caries or cavities (holes in your teeth) are the most common and expensive preventable diet-related problem. It’s bad enough that one in five adults rate their oral health as fair or poor, the prevalence of dental caries in children is also increasing. If you or your kids are lolly addicts, the best way to avoid dental disease is to give up grazing on confectionery. Dump the lolly bag and swap to sugar-free chewing gum to save the kilojoules and your teeth. Susan/Flickr, CC BY Sugar and other fermentable carbohydrates from highly processed foods are major risk factors for both the start and progress of dental disease. The more lollies you eat, and the more often you eat them, the bigger the risk. What’s more, they’ll make you fat. Just 100 grams of jelly babies has over 1,400 kilojoules and over 50 grams of sugar, which is about ten teaspoons. Dump the lolly bag and swap to sugar-free chewing gum to save the kilojoules and your teeth. Sugar-sweetened drinks Sugar-sweetened beverages include sweetened soft drinks, sports drinks, energy drinks, fruit juice drinks and cordial. Drink soda water if you want the fizz of soft drinks without the kilojoules. arbyreed/Flickr, CC BY-NC-ND In a trial of over 15,000 adults who were followed up for 15 years, researchers found drinking one or more cups of soft drink a day increased the risk of developing type 2 diabetes by 29%, compared to drinking less than one glass a month. And a US study estimated drinking one can of soft drink a day could contribute to over six kilograms of weight gain in just a year, if the kilojoules were not offset by increasing physical activity or by cutting back on food intake. Since we know these extra kilojoules are usually not offset, you can see why drinking sweet drinks regularly increases the risk of weight gain. Swap to diet drinks, or soda water if you want the fizz without the kilojoules. Better still, drink water. Unless you’re an elite athlete, plain water is all you need during sport. Crisps of all kinds Crisps, including potato chips, Burger Rings, Twisties and corn chips, are some of the most popular snack foods in the developed world. And the bigger the bag, the more we eat. Crisps are one of the most popular snack foods in the developed world. Alan/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA A healthy low-kilojoule alternative is air-popped popcorn. So put the multi-bag of crisps back on the shelf, grab a bag of popcorn kernels, and pop them yourself at home. Popcorn is wholegrain, more satisfying and cheap. One cup of air-popped popcorn has 150 kilojoules, compared to 550 kilojoules in a 25-gram individual packet of potato crisps. This approximately 400-kilojoule saving is the equivalent of the energy burned up in about a 25-minute walk. Biscuits Most biscuits are consumed with a cup of tea or coffee. But the problem is that biscuits provide more than crunchiness. They contain large amounts of kilojoules, unhealthy fats and highly processed carbohydrates. What’s more, they’re mostly low in fibre and whole grains. Biscuits are mostly low in fibre and whole grains. Rob Faulkner/Flickr, CC BY Look at it this way: two cream-filled biscuits contain around 860 kilojoules. You’d need to push your shopping trolley for about an hour to burn that up. Instead load up on fruit and save on kilojoules. One cup of strawberries has 150 kilojoules, a small bunch grapes 350 kilojoules and a medium banana 365 kilojoules. Processed meat Processed meat includes meat products preserved by smoking, curing, salting or the addition of preservatives including nitrite, nitrates, phosphate, glutamate or ascorbic acid. They include bacon, ham, pastrami, salami, corned beef, chorizo, devon, fritz, luncheon meats, some sausages, hot dogs, cabanossi and kabana. There’s no completely safe level of intake for these foods. The more processed meats you eat, the greater your risk of developing bowel cancer over a lifetime. Processed meat are preserved by smoking, curing, salting or the addition of preservatives. Alpha/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA Keep processed meat for when there are no other choices available. Whenever possible, load up with tomatoes and mushrooms, and swap the breakfast bacon for an egg with baked beans and a mixed vegetable grill. Grab a pack of fresh chicken breast and cook it for use on sandwiches, or buy reduced-fat cheese, canned tuna and salmon, or small cans of four-bean mix. If you have a recipe that calls for chopped bacon, replace it with diced browned onion and garlic, mixed with a couple of tablespoons of sunflower seeds, pumpkin seeds or pine nuts. Avoiding the five foods discussed here and replacing them with the suggestions I have outlined will put you on track to a long, healthy life. Ideally these foods will never appear in your shopping trolley, unlike the five foods that should be there every time you’re filling up the shopping trolley. Public health Obesity Nutrition Health Check Clare Collins, University of Newcastle Author Clare Collins Professor in Nutrition and Dietetics, University of Newcastle Disclosure statement Clare Collins has received funding from NHMRC, ARC, MLA, HMRI and the University of Newcastle. Partners University of Newcastle provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.376arjte4","title":"Health Check: five foods to always avoid at the supermarket","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-five-foods-to-always-avoid-at-the-supermarket-27107","id":207},{"content_hash":"d49f5ade358e98a01d80c220a0965be0bf0810a7e00403b8c9c49509d67c38ce","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:24.065494+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/peter-de-kruijff/101581262","language":"","text":"Peter de Kruijff - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenPeter de KruijffPeter is the online environment reporter for ABC Science. He has previously worked in print and radio in Perth, Carnarvon, Kununurra, Karratha, Kalgoorlie and Albury-Wodonga.Contact Peter de KruijffLatest by Peter de KruijffFossil of 'platypus-like' animal from dinosaur era unearthed in ChinaScientists have found the earliest-known fossil of a mammal-like animal that lived more than 160 million years ago in the mid-Jurassic period and may have hunted fish and salamander.Topic:FossilsThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:58amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:58amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:58amYou can't sell a feather, but you can still sell ivory in AustraliaA new study has found hardly any of the $1.2 million worth of elephant ivory sold or advertised in a six-month period in Australia came with paperwork proving its origin.Topic:Endangered and Protected SpeciesSat 3 Oct 2026 at 5:00amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 5:00amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 5:00amScientists probe connection between deadly frog disease and coal wastewaterA study on the impact of chytrid fungus disease in Royal National Park frogs raises questions about regulation of coalmine wastewater.Topic:Environmental ManagementFri 25 Sep 2026 at 8:40amFri 25 Sep 2026 at 8:40amFri 25 Sep 2026 at 8:40amVideo captures ranger's joyful tears on finding rare night parrot chickNew video captures a sighting of the elusive bird in its grassy home in the remote Great Sandy Desert during the day.Topic:Endangered and Protected SpeciesThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:00amThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:00amThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:00amThese tiny crocodiles may be at risk, but they can't be listed as 'threatened'The Northern Territory's tiny crocs are freshwater crocodiles not a separate species. Despite this, some scientists think the crocs are unique and need special protection.Topic:ReptilesFri 18 Sep 2026 at 5:00amFri 18 Sep 2026 at 5:00amFri 18 Sep 2026 at 5:00am'Highly inbred' dingo population at risk of extinction, ecologists sayA controversial population of desert dingoes on the border of Victoria and South Australia could become the first of their kind to be recognised nationally as being in danger of extinction.Topic:Endangered and Protected SpeciesThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:00amThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:00amThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:00am'Pretty overwhelming': The 23 species in Australia hit by bird flu so farBird flu has quickly spread around Australia. Hidden in the numbers are more than 20 distinct species of birds and mammals that have fallen to the disease. Topic:ExplainerMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amWhat does bird flu do to humans and does it have pandemic potential?Can people get the bird flu from eggs and milk? Can you die if you become infected? Will this be the next pandemic? Here's what we know about bird flu in humans. Topic:ExplainerTue 25 Aug 2026 at 4:37amTue 25 Aug 2026 at 4:37amTue 25 Aug 2026 at 4:37amPhillip Island penguins to be vaccinated for bird fluThe targeted vaccination of 5,000 penguins is claimed to be the largest wild bird vaccination program undertaken globally.Topic:Avian InfluenzaWed 12 Aug 2026 at 1:30pmWed 12 Aug 2026 at 1:30pmWed 12 Aug 2026 at 1:30pmFire is both friend and foe for Australia's wildflowersMany of Australia's floral species have evolved over millions of years alongside fire, but are bushfires becoming too frequent for some flowers to survive?Topic:Native SpeciesWed 12 Aug 2026 at 4:30amWed 12 Aug 2026 at 4:30amWed 12 Aug 2026 at 4:30amNow bird flu has hit seagulls a 'super-spreader' event is more likelyUnlike other birds detected with the virus so far in Australia, seagulls travel beyond coastal environments to more urban and inland areas too.PAnalysis by Peter de KruijffMon 3 Aug 2026 at 3:53pmMon 3 Aug 2026 at 3:53pmMon 3 Aug 2026 at 3:53pmResearcher shocked as orca blows up a sunfish in front of herUnderwater video captures the moment killer whales in the Gulf of California slam into a sunfish, causing it to explode.Topic:Marine BiologyThu 23 Jul 2026 at 2:08pmThu 23 Jul 2026 at 2:08pmThu 23 Jul 2026 at 2:08pmWhat we know about how H5N1 bird flu is spreading in AustraliaBirds infected with a deadly type of bird flu have been found along the coastline of several states of Australia. What do we know about how it is spreading?Topic:ExplainerThu 30 Jul 2026 at 10:54amThu 30 Jul 2026 at 10:54amThu 30 Jul 2026 at 10:54amBird flu could be catastrophic for nation's vulnerable species, experts warnThe black swan, the Tasmanian devil and Australian sea lions are among the species at risk if a deadly strain of avian influenza takes hold in Australia, experts are warning.Topic:Avian InfluenzaSat 20 Jun 2026 at 6:20pmSat 20 Jun 2026 at 6:20pmSat 20 Jun 2026 at 6:20pmWorld's biggest whale graveyard found in Indian Ocean off AustraliaHundreds of whale fossils, including those from extinct species new to science, range in age from recent to 5 million years old and are also home to undescribed deep-sea creatures.Topic:OceansThu 11 Jun 2026 at 6:10amThu 11 Jun 2026 at 6:10amThu 11 Jun 2026 at 6:10amRichard Scolyer remembered by peers for contributions to cancer treatmentBefore being diagnosed with cancer, the 2024 Australian of the Year was a leading melanoma researcher, developing better therapies for those with the disease.Topic:CancerTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amKoalas at risk of death once seven-day temps rise beyond 27CThe risks of death or hospitalisation of koalas increases as soon as seven-day average maximum temperature is above 27 degrees Celsius, a new study shows.Topic:Endangered and Protected SpeciesWed 27 May 2026 at 10:28amWed 27 May 2026 at 10:28amWed 27 May 2026 at 10:28amPlastic waste collected in Sydney Harbour during Vivid festival risingData from floating rubbish bins near the Sydney CBD suggests there are higher levels of rubbish entering the water when major events such as Vivid are on.Topic:Water PollutionSat 23 May 2026 at 5:00amSat 23 May 2026 at 5:00amSat 23 May 2026 at 5:00amSir David Attenborough turns 100: Celebrate his life in his wordsNatural history broadcaster Sir David Attenborough turns 100 today. We look back at his work and interviews to capture some of his wisdom and endless sense of wonder.Topic:NatureFri 8 May 2026 at 6:00amFri 8 May 2026 at 6:00amFri 8 May 2026 at 6:00amWasp that eats host alive named after Attenborough for his 100th birthdayTaxonomists from London's Natural History Museum who named the Chilean species were inspired by the lifework of the famous broadcaster and naturalist.Topic:InsectsThu 7 May 2026 at 9:00pmThu 7 May 2026 at 9:00pmThu 7 May 2026 at 9:00pmScientists discover species of ancient 'dimpled' koala in WAA new analysis of fossilised koala skulls and bones has revealed an extinct species of koala lived in WA until a few thousand years ago.Topic:PalaeontologyWed 6 May 2026 at 9:04amWed 6 May 2026 at 9:04amWed 6 May 2026 at 9:04amAncient fossils reveal when the iconic Twelve Apostles formedAn analysis of tiny fossils of sea creatures found in rock samples around the Port Campbell region in south-western Victoria has refined our understanding of how the Twelve Apostles formed.Topic:GeologyFri 24 Apr 2026 at 5:00amFri 24 Apr 2026 at 5:00amFri 24 Apr 2026 at 5:00amDisabled NZ parrot uses broken beak to achieve alpha statusWithout a curvy upper beak to get in the way, Bruce the kea uses his lower beak to stab or joust at other birds in his social group. Topic:BirdsTue 21 Apr 2026 at 4:30amTue 21 Apr 2026 at 4:30amTue 21 Apr 2026 at 4:30amWhale species that inspired Moby Dick filmed headbutting each otherHistoric accounts dating back two centuries detail ships rammed and sunk by sperm whales, now scientists claim to have filmed the species for the first time displaying headbutting behaviour.Topic:Marine BiologyTue 24 Mar 2026 at 5:58amTue 24 Mar 2026 at 5:58amTue 24 Mar 2026 at 5:58amAustralia's biodiversity report card omits fossil fuel supportA target to identify financial support of fossil fuel industries harmful to the environment was unmet in Australia's self-assessment on its progress to meeting international biodiversity commitments.Topic:EnvironmentMon 16 Mar 2026 at 5:00amMon 16 Mar 2026 at 5:00amMon 16 Mar 2026 at 5:00amLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Peter de Kruijff","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/peter-de-kruijff/101581262","id":208},{"content_hash":"bea78486d182823df5fe00dd0c64c8e31e80c6a22322a8a46622cbae3ccb2d5d","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:23.598079+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/forms/renderers/","language":"","text":"The form rendering API¶ Django’s form widgets are rendered using Django’s template engines system. The form rendering process can be customized at several levels: Widgets can specify custom template names. Forms and widgets can specify custom renderer classes. A widget’s template can be overridden by a project. (Reusable applications typically shouldn’t override built-in templates because they might conflict with a project’s custom templates.) The low-level render API¶ The rendering of form templates is controlled by a customizable renderer class. A custom renderer can be specified by updating the FORM_RENDERER setting. It defaults to 'django.forms.renderers.DjangoTemplates'. By specifying a custom form renderer and overriding form_template_name you can adjust the default form markup across your project from a single place. Note that this will include forms in the admin site and third-party packages that use the default renderer. You can also provide a custom renderer per-form or per-widget by setting the Form.default_renderer attribute or by using the renderer argument of Form.render(), or Widget.render(). Matching points apply to formset rendering. See Using a formset in views and templates for discussion. Use one of the built-in template form renderers or implement your own. Custom renderers must implement a render(template_name, context, request=None) method. It should return a rendered template (as a string) or raise TemplateDoesNotExist. class BaseRenderer[source]¶ The base class for the built-in form renderers. form_template_name¶ The default name of the template to use to render a form. Defaults to the \"django/forms/div.html\" template. formset_template_name¶ The default name of the template to use to render a formset. Defaults to the \"django/forms/formsets/div.html\" template. field_template_name¶ The default name of the template used to render a BoundField. Defaults to \"django/forms/field.html\". bound_field_class¶ The default class used to represent form fields across the project. Defaults to BoundField class. This can be customized further using Form.bound_field_class for per-form overrides, or Field.bound_field_class for per-field overrides. get_template(template_name)[source]¶ Subclasses must implement this method with the appropriate template finding logic. render(template_name, context, request=None)[source]¶ Renders the given template, or raises TemplateDoesNotExist. Built-in-template form renderers¶ DjangoTemplates¶ class DjangoTemplates[source]¶ This renderer uses a standalone DjangoTemplates engine (unconnected to what you might have configured in the TEMPLATES setting). It loads templates first from the built-in form templates directory in django/forms/templates and then from the installed apps’ templates directories using the app_directories loader. If you want to render templates with customizations from your TEMPLATES setting, such as context processors for example, use the TemplatesSetting renderer. Jinja2¶ class Jinja2[source]¶ This renderer is the same as the DjangoTemplates renderer except that it uses a Jinja2 backend. Templates for the built-in widgets are located in django/forms/jinja2 and installed apps can provide templates in a jinja2 directory. To use this backend, all the forms and widgets in your project and its third-party apps must have Jinja2 templates. Unless you provide your own Jinja2 templates for widgets that don’t have any, you can’t use this renderer. For example, django.contrib.admin doesn’t include Jinja2 templates for its widgets due to their usage of Django template tags. TemplatesSetting¶ class TemplatesSetting[source]¶ This renderer gives you complete control of how form and widget templates are sourced. It uses get_template() to find templates based on what’s configured in the TEMPLATES setting. Using this renderer along with the built-in templates requires either: 'django.forms' in INSTALLED_APPS and at least one engine with APP_DIRS=True. Adding the built-in templates directory in DIRS of one of your template engines. To generate that path: import django django.__path__[0] + \"/forms/templates\" # or '/forms/jinja2' Using this renderer requires you to make sure the form templates your project needs can be located. Context available in formset templates¶ Formset templates receive a context from BaseFormSet.get_context(). By default, formsets receive a dictionary with the following values: formset: The formset instance. Context available in form templates¶ Form templates receive a context from Form.get_context(). By default, forms receive a dictionary with the following values: form: The bound form. fields: All bound fields, except the hidden fields. hidden_fields: All hidden bound fields. errors: All non field related or hidden field related form errors. Context available in field templates¶ Field templates receive a context from BoundField.get_context(). By default, fields receive a dictionary with the following values: field: The BoundField. Context available in widget templates¶ Widget templates receive a context from Widget.get_context(). By default, widgets receive a single value in the context, widget. This is a dictionary that contains values like: name value attrs is_hidden template_name Some widgets add further information to the context. For instance, all widgets that subclass Input defines widget['type'] and MultiWidget defines widget['subwidgets'] for looping purposes. Overriding built-in formset templates¶ BaseFormSet.template_name To override formset templates, you must use the TemplatesSetting renderer. Then overriding formset templates works the same as overriding any other template in your project. Overriding built-in form templates¶ Form.template_name To override form templates, you must use the TemplatesSetting renderer. Then overriding form templates works the same as overriding any other template in your project. Overriding built-in field templates¶ Field.template_name To override field templates, you must use the TemplatesSetting renderer. Then overriding field templates works the same as overriding any other template in your project. Overriding built-in widget templates¶ Each widget has a template_name attribute with a value such as input.html. Built-in widget templates are stored in the django/forms/widgets path. You can provide a custom template for input.html by defining django/forms/templates/django/forms/widgets/input.html, for example. See Built-in widgets for the name of each widget’s template. To override widget templates, you must use the TemplatesSetting renderer. Then overriding widget templates works the same as overriding any other template in your project.","title":"The form rendering API¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/forms/renderers/","id":209},{"content_hash":"beead54292c56a251002c42616c96972a989949015a5c0352f660c8fad4eba6f","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:23.374597+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-adverse-media-negative-news-screening-for-businesses","language":"","text":"Why Adverse Media Screening is Important for Businesses?ByPrince, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuApril 20, 2023•5mins ReadWhat is Adverse Media Screening and Why is it Important for Businesses?Understanding The Impact of Adverse Media on BusinessesTypes of Adverse Media ScreeningHow to Conduct an Effective Adverse Media ScreeningAdverse Media Screening Tools and SoftwareAdverse Media Screening in Different IndustriesAdvantages of Outsourcing Adverse Media ScreeningAchieving Real-Time Adverse Media Screening with YouverifyShare this postAdverse media screening is becoming increasingly important for businesses today, as the digital age has made it easier for negative news to spread quickly and widely. With the rise of social media and online news sources, it is crucial for businesses to stay on top of any negative information about employees and customers that could damage their reputation or financial stability. However, What exactly does adverse media screening mean, and why is it important for your business? This article takes a look at the most important points you should know. What is Adverse Media Screening and Why is it Important for Businesses? Adverse media screening, also known as negative news screening, is the process of monitoring media sources such as news articles, blogs, and social media for any negative information related to a company, individual, or entity. The goal of adverse media screening is to identify any potential risks or threats to a business, such as reputational damage, financial loss, or legal issues. Adverse media screening is important for businesses for several reasons. First and foremost, it helps to protect a company's reputation. Negative news can spread quickly in today's digital age, and even one negative article or social media post can damage a company's reputation for years to come. Adverse media screening allows businesses to identify and address any negative news before it becomes a major issue.Secondly, adverse media screening can help businesses identify potential financial risks. For example, if a company is doing business with a partner who has a history of fraud or financial misconduct, adverse media screening can help identify this risk before it becomes a problem.Finally, adverse media screening can help businesses stay compliant with regulations and laws. Many industries, for instance, healthcare and finance, are subject to stringent laws and compliance requirements. Adverse media screening can help identify any potential compliance issues before they become a problem. Understanding The Impact of Adverse Media on Businesses The impact of adverse media on businesses can be significant. Some. of the potential effects it can have on a business include: 1. Negative news can damage a company's reputation, and financial stability, and even lead to legal issues. For example, in 2015, Volkswagen was hit with a major scandal when it was revealed that the company had installed software in its diesel cars to cheat emissions tests. This scandal not only damaged Volkswagen's reputation but also led to billions of dollars in fines and legal fees. 2. Negative news can also lead to a loss of customers, as consumers may be hesitant to do business with a company that has a negative reputation. This can lead to a decline in sales and revenue, which can ultimately impact a company's financial stability. 3. Negative news can also impact a company's stock price. When negative news is reported about a company, investors may lose confidence in the company, leading to a decline in stock price. This can impact not only the company's financial stability but also the investments of shareholders. Types of Adverse Media Screening There are several types of adverse media screening that businesses can use to monitor for negative news: 1. Manual Screening Manual screening involves manually searching for negative news using search engines and other media sources. This can be time-consuming and may not be as effective as automated screening, but it can be a good option for businesses with a smaller media presence. 2. Automated Screening Automated screening uses software to scan media sources for negative news. This can be more effective and efficient than manual screening, but it can also be more expensive. 3. Hybrid Screening Hybrid screening combines manual and automated screening to provide a more comprehensive approach to adverse media screening. This can be a good option for businesses with a larger media presence or those that are subject to strict compliance requirements. Recommended - Adverse Media: How and Where to Check for Negative News How to Conduct an Effective Adverse Media Screening To conduct an effective adverse media screening, businesses should follow these steps: 1. Define Your Screening Criteria Before beginning the screening process, businesses should define their screening criteria. This includes identifying the media sources to be monitored, the types of negative news to be screened for, and the frequency of screening. 2. Choose a Screening Method Businesses should choose a screening method that best fits their needs and budget. This may include manual, automated, or hybrid screening. 3. Implement Your Screening Plan Once a screening method has been chosen, businesses should implement their screening plan. This may involve training employees on the screening process, setting up automated screening software, and establishing a process for manual screening. 4. Review and Analyze the Results Businesses should review and analyze the results of their screening on a regular basis. This can help identify any potential risks or threats and allow businesses to take action to address them. Adverse Media Screening Tools and Software There are several tools and software available to assist with adverse media screening. These include: 1. Media Monitoring Tools Media monitoring tools can help businesses monitor media sources for negative news. These tools can scan news articles, blogs, social media posts, and more. 2. Compliance Management Software Compliance management software can help businesses stay compliant with regulations and laws. These tools can help identify potential compliance issues and provide guidance on how to address them. 3. Risk Management Software Risk management software can help businesses identify and manage potential risks. These tools can help identify potential financial risks, reputational risks, and more. Adverse Media Screening in Different Industries Adverse media screening is important in many different industries, including: a. Finance In the finance industry, adverse media screening is important for identifying potential financial risks and compliance issues. This can include identifying partners or clients with a history of fraud or financial misconduct. b. Healthcare In the healthcare industry, adverse media screening can help identify potential reputational risks and compliance issues. This can include monitoring for negative news related to patient privacy or regulatory compliance. c. Retail In the retail industry, adverse media screening can help identify potential reputational risks and compliance issues. This can include monitoring for negative news related to product safety or labour practices. Advantages of Outsourcing Adverse Media Screening Outsourcing adverse media screening can offer several advantages for businesses, including: i. Reduced Costs Outsourcing adverse media screening can be more cost-effective than conducting screening in-house. This can be especially true for smaller businesses that may not have the resources to invest in screening tools and software. ii. Access to Expertise Outsourcing adverse media screening can provide businesses with access to expertise and experience that may not be available in-house. This can help ensure that the screening process is conducted effectively and efficiently. iii. Increased Efficiency Outsourcing adverse media screening can increase efficiency by allowing businesses to focus on their core operations. This can be especially true for larger businesses that may have a larger media presence and require more frequent screening. Achieving Real-Time Adverse Media Screening with Youverify In conclusion, adverse media screening is becoming increasingly important for businesses in today's digital age. Negative news can spread quickly and widely, and even one negative article or social media post can damage a company's reputation for years to come. Adverse media screening allows businesses to identify and address any negative news before it becomes a major issue. There are several types of adverse media screening that businesses can use, including manual, automated, and hybrid screening. Additionally, there are several tools and software available to assist with adverse media screening. To ensure an effective adverse media screening process, businesses should define their screening criteria, stay up-to-date with media sources, and regularly review and analyse results. Outsourcing adverse media screening can offer several advantages, including reduced costs, access to expertise, and increased efficiency. By implementing an effective adverse media screening process, businesses can better protect their reputation, financial stability, and compliance with regulations and laws. Youverify offers reliable adverse media screening solutions to protect your business reputation and help your business adhere to compliance with regulations and laws. Request a demo today to get started.Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"Why Adverse Media Screening is Important for Businesses?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-adverse-media-negative-news-screening-for-businesses","id":210},{"content_hash":"e96523e2a94a67503e03007537d150675558445f1996a435bf4e02000fe70958","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:23.253072+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-eden-s-efforcera-de-jouer-un-role-moderateur-a-l-o-n-u_2072848_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Londres, 6 novembre. - Les Britanniques se vantent d'être un peuple à sang-froid. Il est donc naturel qu'à Londres on affiche un peu plus de scepticisme que dans les autres capitales quant aux \" développements sensationnels \" attendus à l'assemblée des Nations unies. Quelques journaux du dimanche, qui avaient momentanément cédé à l'émotion, annonçaient \" la semaine de la destinée \", mais dans l'ensemble le mot d'ordre est maintenant la prudence. Ce manque d'enthousiasme a quelque chose de paradoxal si l'on songe que M. Churchill s'est fait le champion d'une nouvelle rencontre en tête à tête avec le maître du Kremlin. De son côté M. Churchill consacre cet après-midi une large part de son intervention devant les Communes aux problèmes internationaux. Le premier ministre reviendra encore sur ce sujet vendredi soir lors du traditionnel dîner que le lord-maire de Londres offre au nouveau chef du gouvernement. Cependant, même s'il faisait allusion à une éventuelle rencontre avec le généralissime Staline, M. Churchill se garderait sans doute de donner à ce projet un caractère trop sensationnel. Il est clair en effet que les Britanniques éprouvent un certain malaise devant des méthodes de propagande qui leur paraissent non seulement imprudentes, mais maladroites. En effet, les coups de clairon qui ont précédé l'offensive de paix occidentale ont également mis en garde l'U.R.S.S. et son maître, qui prend la parole ce soir à Moscou, aura eu tout le temps nécessaire pour devancer MM. Truman et Acheson. Favoriser les chances réelles de règlement Les réserves des Britanniques ne signifient certes pas qu'ils soient moins disposés à soutenir les prochaines démarches occidentales. Il parait d'ailleurs certain que le plan de M. Acheson est réellement le fruit d'un long travail diplomatique réalisé en commun avec les Français et les Anglais. Il n'en est pas moins probable que M. Eden va s'efforcer de jouer un rôle modérateur en favorisant les chances réelles d'un règlement avec les Soviets plutôt qu'en encourageant des gestes de propagande destinés à l'opinion publique. Il vous reste 51.17% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"M. Eden s'efforcera de jouer un rôle modérateur à l'O.N.U.","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-eden-s-efforcera-de-jouer-un-role-moderateur-a-l-o-n-u_2072848_1819218.html","id":211},{"content_hash":"fdbe537326725271d4f18552ff5ea348c2f2bfdf5deafc7b6ba4a1a0dc93cefa","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:23.142234+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/china-shows-theres-more-to-renewable-energy-than-fighting-climate-change-31471","language":"","text":"China shows there’s more to renewable energy than fighting climate change Published: September 11, 2014 2:05am EDT The Tangshanpeng Wind Farm in China. Flickr/Land Rover Our Planet, CC BY-SA https://theconversation.com/china-shows-theres-more-to-renewable-energy-than-fighting-climate-change-31471 https://theconversation.com/china-shows-theres-more-to-renewable-energy-than-fighting-climate-change-31471 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article With the failure of international agreements to fight climate change, the way is open to viewing the role of renewables as more than agents for reducing carbon emissions. Indeed is it possible for countries to build their manufacturing industries, enhance their energy security — and contribute to reducing carbon emissions? In an article published today in Nature, we argue that China shows us just such a way. By boosting markets in water, wind and solar power, China is driving down costs and accelerating the uptake of renewable energy. We argue that this is “contributing more than any other country to a climate-change solution”, and could be viable alternative to international climate agreements such as the Kyoto Protocol, which has been so ineffective in cleaning up the world’s still carbon-heavy energy supplies. But China’s large investments in renewables are best understood as enhancing the country’s energy security and not solely as a means of reducing carbon emissions. Expanding renewable industry This goes against the grain of most commentary on China, which sees the country as fully committed to a black, coal-fired energy future But there is another, green side to China’s energy story – as argued on The Conversation by Ross Garnaut. As the scale of Chinese manufacturing has grown — in our article we note that production of solar cells has expanded about 100-fold since 2005 — the costs of renewable-energy devices have plummeted. Countries such as Germany and South Korea, like China, are boosting their national renewable-energy industries and markets. But others, including the United States, seem yet to notice this shift and are pursuing ineffective energy policies, including considering alternative fossil-fuels sources like coal-seam gas and putting trade tariffs on Chinese-made solar panels. Indeed oil addiction in the US appears to be worse than ever. There are clear implications for Australia too. The current wave of anti-renewables rhetoric and negative reaction by the Australian government is out of step with the rest of world, and in particular with China and India, (and Germany) all of which countries see renewables as an important and growing element of their energy mix. And there is a second clear implication: as they build their renewables industries, these countries will come to depend less and less on fossil fuels – and (China in particular), less and less on imports of Australian fossil fuels. Energy security Today’s discussions about energy security are focused almost exclusively on access to fossil fuels. “Energy security” really means, from this perspective, “oil security” or “gas security”. Keeping the lights burning is, of course, a primary goal— but the means for achieving it are considered from a very narrow perspective. If the perspective is broadened, to include renewables, then many new vistas open up. Renewables are the products of manufacturing, which any country can practice. And the more that countries manufacture renewables, and expand the market for renewables devices like solar cells and wind turbines, the more their cost comes down, through the operation of the learning curve (which displays the reduction in costs as scale of production expands). In the case of solar PV cells, costs have fallen by 80% in just the last five years, as the global market has expanded. To see energy security in terms of manufacturing capacities of course plays to China’s strengths. While the rest of the world has been fixated on China’s build-up of black, fossil-fuelled energy systems, the country has been quietly building a mammoth green energy system, based on water, wind and solar power. China’s renewable power capacity now exceeds that of every other country. China’s current renewable energy capacity mix. REN21(2014) Global Status Report, Author provided By 2013 China had 378 gigawatts of electric power generating capacity based on renewable sources, primarily water, wind and solar sources. The US is a distant second, with its renewable energy sources reaching a capacity of 172 gigawatts; while Germany reached 84 gigawatts in 2013, and India, rapidly expanding, reached 71 gigawatts. REN21(2014) Global Status Report REN21(2014) Global Status Report REN21(2014) Global Status Report In terms of electric power generation, China generated over 1,000 terawatt hours of electricity from water, wind and solar sources in 2013, which is comparable to the entire power generation combined of France and Germany. But it is the rate of expansion that is so remarkable. China is rapidly expanding its renewable energy industries and its use of renewable devices to generate electric power. Its latest target is that renewables will have a capacity of 550 gigawatts — over half a trillion watts — by the year 2017. We calculate that this will exert a major impact in China — enhancing energy security; reducing emissions pollution; and reducing carbon emissions. But the primary impact will be on energy security. China became a net importer of oil in 1993; of natural gas in 2007; and of coal in 2011. If it can reach its 2017 target of 550 GW renewables, we calculate that this would translate into a saving of 45% on current imports of coal, oil and natural gas. China is leading the way to a world of decarbonized energy, by placing the emphasis of its policy on growing the markets for renewables and building the industries to supply wind turbines, solar cells, batteries and other devices. In this way it is driving down costs, through the learning curve, and making renewables more accessible to all countries. This is good for China, and for the world. Energy security Renewable energy China Manufacturing John Mathews, Macquarie University, Hao Tan, University of Newcastle Authors John Mathews Professor of Strategic Management, Macquarie Graduate School of Management, Macquarie University Hao Tan Senior Lecturer in International Business , University of Newcastle Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners Macquarie University and University of Newcastle provide funding as members of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.3rwkxp6te","title":"China shows there’s more to renewable energy than fighting climate change","url":"https://theconversation.com/china-shows-theres-more-to-renewable-energy-than-fighting-climate-change-31471","id":212},{"content_hash":"d123d3bf73cb90334dfd341c6df14ec8c30c107adb13ec92014852a629d35302","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:22.170922+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/as-hdfc-bank-ceo-bagchi-set-for-36-crore-target-pay/articleshow/134803841.cms","language":"","text":"As HDFC Bank CEO, Anup Bagchi set for 36 crore target pay - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIBusinessIndia BusinessBudget 2026Mutual FundsInfrastructureGold Rates TodaySilver Rates Today Platinum Rates TodayPetrol PriceDiesel PriceCNG PriceLPG PriceFinancial LiteracyDon't Get ScammedFinancial CalculatorsInternational BusinessCybersecurityIFSC CodeVideosFinancial Literacy SummitEconomyToday's ePaperLiveBudgetIFSCPAN CardAadhaar CardIPOIncome TaxSavings Growth CalculatorNewsBusiness NewsIndia Business NewsAs HDFC Bank CEO, Anup Bagchi set for 36 crore target payTrendingUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayUS Mortgage RateGold Rate PredictionStock Market CrashIndian RupeeTop Stocks TodayCrude Oil Prices TodayStock Market TodayAs HDFC Bank CEO, Anup Bagchi set for 36 crore target payTNN / Updated: Oct 09, 2026, 07:03 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint File photo MUMBAI: HDFC Bank's incoming MD and CEO Anup Bagchi will get target annual compensation of Rs 35.9 crore, with a Rs 7.3-crore one-time equity grant taking potential pay to Rs 43.2 crore. He takes charge Oct 27 after Sashidhar Jagdishan's exit.RBI approved Bagchi's appointment Oct 1 for a three-year term. His Rs 9-crore fixed pay includes Rs 3.3 crore basic, Rs 1.6 crore retirals and Rs 3.9 crore allowances/perquisites.Variable pay is capped at Rs 26.9 crore, or 300% of fixed pay, comprising Rs 8.9 crore cash and Rs 18 crore in 7,11,801 stock options, with equity vesting over four years.The joining grant comprises 44,796 RSUs worth Rs 3.2 crore and 1,64,207 ESOPs worth Rs 4.2 crore. Bagchi's proposed pay is sharply above his Rs 7.5-crore FY26 compensation at ICICI Prudential Life, though his Rs 3.3-crore basic broadly matches Jagdishan's.Jagdishan received Rs 15.1 crore in FY26, including a Rs 7.3-crore performance bonus and 4,28,405 stock options.You use AI every day. Now get your AI Quotient. Take the AIQ test.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaPhotostoriesQuote of the day: “Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind”; William Shakespeare’s timeless words on how true love goes beyond what we seeGender pain gap: Women experience more pain than men across the whole body, major global study finds ‘shocking’ dataFrom historic tiaras to priceless heirlooms: The royal jewels Kate Middleton has left untouchedKangana Ranaut goes full Himachali royalty in a statement topi, Kullu shawl, and a cultural nod to President Droupadi Murmu5 habits that matter more than supplements: Doctor shares simple daily habits for better sleep, fitness, nutrition, stress management and preventive health that can support a longer, healthier li