 & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"International Women's Day Non Profit | Page template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day-non-profit","id":274},{"content_hash":"7bd61c84a6485dcbe2679fe5cdd99fa8a6e30df240edaa219dc6c1a14ae1f2cd","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:07.599690+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/newsletters/template/opinion/","language":"","text":"Opinion October 9, 2026Sign up Opinion Welcome to The Globe and Mail’s Opinion newsletter. Here are the top pieces from Globe columnists, experts and everyday Canadians. Plus, keep scrolling for editorials, letters and today’s cartoon. Featured Carlos Osorio/Reuters Trump didn’t intimidate Canadians – he empowered Canadians Carol Off Carol Off’s latest book is True Patriot Love: Reporting from the Frontlines of Canadian Sovereignty, from which the following essay has been adapted. Canada has long defined itself by what it is not, meaning that it is not the United States. While we’re often content to not be Americans, we haven’t always had an idea of who we are. Canada is a jumble of contradictions. But for the most part, Canadians, both those recently arrived and those here for gen­erations, are okay with that, or at least tolerant of it. They know that the idea of Canada is a good one. The country that we are not is our almost constant preoccupa­tion. American power, money and technology have dominated the Canadian experience for more than a century. Many of us can actu­ally see the United States from where we live. It looms on the horizon, appear­ing as something formidable and cocksure. Americans produce much of the best and the worst of every­thing. We often hate them, but we love their stuff. We admire many of them, but we don’t want to be them. We talk about them even as we rarely come up in their conversations. And when we do, it’s usually not for our benefit. Adil Boukind/The Globe and Mail Quebec promised to help them become permanent residents. Now some families may have to leave Adil Boukind Shifting political tides have shaken the trust between the province’s immigrants and its government Christopher Katsarov/The Canadian Press A Nobel Prize for Mark Carney? Premature. Person of the Year award? Thumbs-up Lawrence Martin Public affairs columnist Either award would enrage Donald Trump, but bring Canada all the more prominence Editorial cartoon David Parkins/The Globe and Mail Editorial Cartoons for October, 2026 Editorials The real reason for the return of the Parti Québécois The Editorial Board The unsafe side effects of ‘safer supply’ The Editorial Board Letters to the editor ‘A pipeline approved does not necessarily translate into a pipeline built.’ Letters to the editor for Oct. 8 More opinion Why Canada needs warning labels on alcohol Monty Ghosh, Eric Yoshida and Robert Strang For many fraternities, entitlement is a feature, not a bug Lisa Wade A private airport concession need not be the bogeyman some think it is Jordan Eizenga and Tim Murphy In case you missed it Graham Hughes/The Canadian Press The weakness of its mandate will not deter the PQ from the chaos and division of its project Andrew Coyne ADVERTISEMENT The Globe and Mail invites you to join us on our tenth cruise being offered by Scenic and Emerald Luxury Cruises, The Globe and Mail Mediterranean Cruise, as we sail through the Mediterranean while our Globe journalists share behind the stories shaping your world. Through insider conversations, shared meals and curated experiences, gain new insight across remarkable coastal destinations. Thanks for reading. If you have any feedback on today’s newsletter, reach out to the newsletter team. Are you reading us on the web or did someone forward the e-mail version to you? If so, you can sign up for Opinion here. Finally, for technical problems with this newsletter, please contact Customer Support. Subscribe Sign up for other newsletters Listen to our podcasts Globe events Advertise with us Connect with us You received this email at [Contact.Email] because you subscribe to Opinion. To advertise with us in print and online, visit GlobeMediaGroup. Unsubscribe | Privacy Policy | Terms & Conditions | Contact Us © 2026 The Globe and Mail Inc. 351 King Street East, Suite 1600, Toronto, ON M5A 0N1 Andrew Saunders, President and CEO","title":"Opinion","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/newsletters/template/opinion/","id":275},{"content_hash":"9a5d8872da05968dc413955edac219c1fd7cf62e978d9c875bf57e6e5fb82090","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:07.572856+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/la-liberte-d-information-et-ses-abus_2072720_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives La liberté d'information et ses abus Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"La liberté d'information et ses abus","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/la-liberte-d-information-et-ses-abus_2072720_1819218.html","id":276},{"content_hash":"c2b5b52fd3a964aa58da4d496a54225d4175ea2bf362a3dd03fa18f248a25900","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:07.540112+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-do-my-blood-pressure-numbers-mean-29212","language":"","text":"Health Check: what do my blood pressure numbers mean? Published: September 29, 2014 12:25am EDT Blood pressure refers to how hard the blood is pushing against the wall of arteries. Image Point Fr/Shutterstock https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-do-my-blood-pressure-numbers-mean-29212 https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-do-my-blood-pressure-numbers-mean-29212 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article If you’re an adult and have ever visited a doctor, you’ve probably had your blood pressure measured. General practitioners tend to obsess over blood pressure. But with good reason: hypertension, or persistently high blood pressure, can lead to heart disease, stroke and diabetes, the nation’s biggest killers. What is blood pressure? Blood pressure refers to how hard the blood is pushing against the wall of arteries – these can be thought of as the pipes that deliver blood from the heart (the blood “pump”) to the rest of the body. This pressure is necessary for the blood to flow. Using a plumbing analogy, imagine the effect of a drop in water pressure to water flow from the garden hose. Blood pressure naturally varies throughout the day depending on posture, activity and stress. It can also be elevated or lowered as a consequence of illness or disease. Stephen Hales, an 18th-century English clergyman and scientist, first measured the blood pressure of animals in a series of experiments that involved inserting tubes into their arteries. In one experiment with a horse, he describes the blood rising to eight feet in height in the tube! Blood pressure measurement would almost certainly be unpopular if it involved inserting long glass tubes into people. Happily, Italian physician Scipione Riva-Rocci developed the conventional sphygmomanometer, or blood pressure meter, in 1896. The conventional blood pressure machine uses a column of liquid mercury. Jiří Sedláček (Frettie) Soon afterwards, Russian physician Nikolai Korotkoff discovered the sounds that can be heard with a stethoscope over the inner elbow while using a sphygmomanometer. His technique remains the standard method for measuring blood pressure today. Interestingly, the conventional sphygmomanometer uses a column of [liquid mercury](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_(element) to indicate the pressure. As mercury is so much denser than water or blood, even very elevated blood pressures result in it rising no more than about a foot. This quirk of medical history gives us the modern measurement unit for blood pressure: millimetres of mercury (mmHg). A blood pressure measurement of 140 mmHg literally means that the pressure will push up a column of liquid mercury 14 centimetres. Two numbers are given when reporting blood pressures, such as 140/90 mmHg. The first number is the systolic blood pressure. This is the pressure when the heart is contracting to pump its content of blood into the circulation. The second number is the diastolic blood pressure. This is the pressure when the heart is relaxing and is refilling with blood. The heart refills the blood (left = the bottom number) and pumps it out (right = the top number). udaix/Shutterstock What is normal? The National Heart Foundation guidelines define “normal” blood pressure withing the following range: Systolic blood pressure: 100 to 139 mmHg Diastolic blood pressure: 60 to 89 mmHg. These thresholds are arbitrary, but a blood pressure less than 140/90 mmHg is not labelled as high. We’re also moving away from categorical descriptions of hypertension, based on blood pressure reading alone, towards a model where blood pressure is considered alongside other risk factors to determine whether drug or lifestyle interventions are needed. Clinicians now calculate the patent’s absolute cardiovascular disease risk: the probability they will have a heart attack or stroke in the short and medium term, which includes age and cholesterol levels. This calculator can help you work out your absolute disease risk. All adults should consider having their blood pressure measured at least once every two years. If it’s elevated, and confirmed, you’ll work with your GP to create a management plan to lower your blood pressure. This is likely to lifestyle changes such as: getting regular physical activity quitting smoking reducing the amount of salt you eat increasing the amount of potassium you consume (from foods such as parsley, dried apricots, some nuts, bamboo shoots, bananas, avocados, soybeans and bran) losing weight drinking less alcohol. Keep in mind that visiting the doctor can provoke anxiety, so the blood pressure can be elevated in response to the testing procedure itself. This has been called the “white coat effect”. If this is suspected, your GP might try recording your blood pressure over a number of visits, or use ambulatory or home blood pressure monitoring to get an accurate reading. This article was co-authored by Dr Patrick Khoury, a GP registrar at the General Practice Unit, Fairfield Hospital. Blood pressure Hypertension High blood pressure Health Check Anti-hypertensives Michael Tam, UNSW Author Michael Tam General Practitioner, and Conjoint Senior Lecturer, UNSW Disclosure statement Michael Tam is a general practitioner and consults with patients who have hypertension. He otherwise does not work for, consult to, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has no relevant affiliations. Partners UNSW provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.gqdkrt4gy","title":"Health Check: what do my blood pressure numbers mean?","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-do-my-blood-pressure-numbers-mean-29212","id":277},{"content_hash":"b075025455dcd0e26b34cb26f7581b4ab4b570a717b0b5912cbe350f0c2842ca","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:07.496980+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/surprising-thing-about-retirement-today.html","language":"","text":"Are you ready to retire early? Following these three steps can help you adjust your financial plans if you choose to — or need to — retire sooner than you planned. March 13, 2026 Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Consider this: Early retirement means your savings may have to last for 30 years — or even longer. AS YOU LOOK AHEAD to retirement, you probably have a target date in mind for when you’ll finally leave the workforce. But 40% of retirees report leaving sooner than planned.1 For some, poor health is the reason; for others, a job loss. But it’s not only hardships that can change the timing. Forty-four percent of those who retire earlier than planned do so at least partly because they realize they can afford to retire earlier.1 Your retirement plan was crafted around when you thought you’d leave full-time work behind. Retire early, and you can find yourself scrambling to make recalculations, trade-offs and adjustments, especially if you’re leaving your job due to an illness, a layoff or the need to care for an ailing parent. 1 of 5 You have complex decisions to make and missteps to avoid. You might withdraw too much from your retirement accounts too quickly, for example. You could fail to account for the probability of increasing healthcare costs. And you risk making costly tax mistakes with your retirement accounts. This is when a financial advisor’s insights and steady guidance can prove invaluable. Here are three key steps to take if you choose to — or need to — retire earlier than you’d planned. 1. Come up with a full picture of your income The first step is to look at all your possible sources of income, which might include an early retirement or severance package in addition to a pension, Social Security benefits and withdrawals from retirement accounts. Then the question is whether to tap these sources now versus later. For instance, starting retirement account distributions earlier than expected can have damaging tax consequences — if you take distributions before age 59½, you may face an additional 10% tax on those withdrawals. And collecting Social Security benefits early will reduce the amount of guaranteed income you’ll have later in retirement, notes Merrill Wealth Management Advisor Mary Jo Harper. (Get insights on building an income stream no matter when you retire.) “Your advisor can help you project how long your existing retirement savings could potentially last and suggest ways to adjust — for instance, by rethinking your goals, cutting expenses or taking on consulting work — if you fall short of what you may need for a comfortable future,” says Nevenka Vrdoljak, managing director in the Chief Investment Office for Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank. 2. Get a handle on your expenses Early retirement may also affect your spending in surprising ways. You may jump at the chance to travel widely, but you could also face lingering expenses you thought you’d be done with by now. “Debt payments are often a problem for those who retire early,” says Lisa Kent, a Merrill Wealth Management Advisor. “That’s especially true for parents who may have taken on huge college tuition loans.” Talk to your advisor about options for refinancing or paying down any loans you still have. What’s more, since Medicare generally does not kick in until age 65, you may have to purchase a costly private health insurance policy in the interim. (Learn more about ways to help manage healthcare costs throughout what could be a long retirement.) Consider this: Selling any asset that has gone up in value can result in a large tax bill and have an impact on your Medicare premiums. You may also find yourself rethinking some spending goals. Do you need to continue to save for a second home, for instance? Or continue to own one given the potentially high carrying costs? Kent recalls one client who sold the family vacation home to keep debt payments manageable in retirement. Review the implications of this kind of major move with your advisor and tax professional. Selling any asset that has gone up in value can result in a large tax bill and have an impact on your Medicare premiums. 3. Revisit your investment strategy Early retirement means your savings may have to last for 30 years — or even longer. “A conservative portfolio built largely with investment-grade bonds and cash is unlikely to provide the growth you will need — especially if inflation increases down the line,” Vrdoljak says. At the same time, you’ll need assets, like dividend-paying stocks, that can provide a predictable income stream. “Work with your advisor to create a basket of diversified, high-quality investments that will generate retirement income but also provide the growth you may need,” adds Vrdoljak. “The underlying quality of securities is critically important for early retirees who may be exposed to extra years of funding requirements and inflation over time,” she says. “Clearly, there’s a lot of work to be done when you’re faced with an earlier-than-expected retirement,” Harper says. “But with the right adjustments to a sound strategy, you could turn an early retirement into the satisfying and secure time of life you’d always hoped it would be.” New to Merrill? Connect with a Merrill Advisor There are items below that require your attention before submitting. Something went wrong! Your form couldn’t be submitted correctly. Please try again. Would you like us to contact you? Yes No First name* Last name* Email address* Confirm email address* Primary phone* Zip code* By providing your contact information above, you agree that a representative of Merrill, the Brokerage affiliate of Bank of America Corporation, may contact you via telephone and/or email to discuss and/or offer investment products and services that may be appropriate for you. You agree that you are providing to us your consent for us to contact you regardless of any Do Not Call or Do Not Email privacy choices you may have previously expressed until you revoke this consent, or up to 90 days. You may revoke your consent at any time by notifying the Merrill representative. Submit Thank you. You’ll receive an automated email confirming receipt of your request shortly. A Merrill financial advisor will be in contact with you in the coming days. Discover more with Merrill Work one-on-one with a Merrill advisor for more insights and personalized guidance. Connect with us today. Loading... Have us contact you Contact me Try Merrill Advisor Match Match with advisors Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 Employee Benefit Research Institute and Greenwald Research, “35th Annual Retirement Confidence Survey,” 2025. Important Disclosures Opinions are as of 11/17/2025 and are subject to change. Investing involves risk, including possible loss of principal. Past performance is no guarantee of future results. Bank of America, Merrill, their affiliates, and advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. Clients should consult their legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. This information should not be construed as investment advice and is subject to change. It is provided for informational purposes only and is not intended to be either a specific offer by Bank of America, Merrill or any affiliate to sell or provide, or a specific invitation for a consumer to apply for, any particular retail financial product or service that may be available. The Chief Investment Office (CIO) provides thought leadership on wealth management, investment strategy and global markets; portfolio management solutions; due diligence; and solutions oversight and data analytics. CIO viewpoints are developed for Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., (“Bank of America”) and Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated (“MLPF&S” or “Merrill”), a registered broker-dealer, registered investment adviser and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). Asset allocation, diversification and rebalancing do not ensure a profit or protect against loss in declining markets. Investments have varying degrees of risk. Some of the risks involved with equity securities include the possibility that the value of the stocks may fluctuate in response to events specific to the companies or markets, as well as economic, political or social events in the U.S. or abroad. Bonds are subject to interest rate, inflation and credit risks. Dividend payments are not guaranteed, and are paid only when declared by an issuer’s board of directors. The amount of a dividend payment, if any, can vary over time. The case studies presented are hypothetical and do not reflect specific strategies we may have developed for actual clients. They are for illustrative purposes only and intended to demonstrate the capabilities of Bank of America and/or Merrill. They are not intended to serve as investment advice since the availability and effectiveness of any strategy are dependent upon your individual facts and circumstances. Results will vary, and no suggestion is made about how any specific solution or strategy performed in reality. Always consult with your independent attorney, tax advisor, investment manager, and insurance agent for final recommendations and before changing or implementing any financial, tax, or estate planning strategy. The BullPen Timely insights to stay ahead of the curve September 9, 2026 Today's Markets Market briefs August 18, 2026 Financial Planning Planning for college: Investment accounts to help you pay for education July 21, 2026 Financial Planning Charitable giving unlocked for business owners under the OBBBA July 17, 2026 Financial Planning HSAs explained: The top benefits of health savings accounts July 15, 2026 Retirement Ways to replace your salary in retirement Related Articles Will my Social Security benefits be taxed? It depends on your income. Retirement housing options: Deciding where you want to live How to prepare for unexpected financial events and risks","title":"Are you ready to retire early?","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/surprising-thing-about-retirement-today.html","id":278},{"content_hash":"152bcf537d0ef9e9d0040e47d8425ff9d0a7298f46b69fd606aecc1af34c8864","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:07.032062+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/25/WS6aba26f5e4b06d4aa0560971.html","language":"","text":"Who Decodes the Universe's Signals - Chinadaily.com.cn 635f3163a310fd2b29e7f620 WWW.CHINADAILY.COM.CN HOME VIDEO GRAPHICS POSTER PHOTOS INTERACTIVE Who Decodes the Universe's Signals Today marks the 10th anniversary of the completion of the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope, or FAST, coinciding with this year's Mid-Autumn Festival. As we admire the moon, FAST continues to \"listen\" to the stars, commemorating a decade of capturing whispers from billions of light-years away. Happy 10th birthday, China's FAST!解锁宇宙“快信” 中国“天眼”FAST落成十周年从2016年9月25日到2026年9月25日，中国“天眼”FAST落成十周年，恰逢今年中秋节。别人抬头望月，“天眼”仰头听星。一眼十年，捕捉百亿光年外的信号，解锁宇宙“快信”，这就是大国重器的宇宙级浪漫。（AI智绘工作室联合出品、中国日报国内频道） 分享到微信朋友圈 使用手机微信 “扫一扫” 即可将内容分享到我的朋友圈。 VIDEOS More Mid-Autumn Festival Qixi Festival: Love across the stars Qinghai-Xizang Railway: 20 years on Dragon Boat Festival: Midsummer blessings, health all year Shangsi Festival: A poetic spring ritual Longtaitou Festival: A celebration of fresh start China's environmental code: A gift to the future Cold snow, hot growth: China's winter economy heats up Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. Scan the QR code to download China Daily app","title":"Who Decodes the Universe's Signals","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/25/WS6aba26f5e4b06d4aa0560971.html","id":279},{"content_hash":"9674501bc2c6d8fda4a7904635f48e5eaa2715a916bca3c442d40ef03df5d8e8","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:06.851975+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/martinson-vainqueur-de-morizek_2073362_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Martinson vainqueur de Morizek Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Martinson vainqueur de Morizek","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/martinson-vainqueur-de-morizek_2073362_1819218.html","id":280},{"content_hash":"84251f517d8121f2410a89836ede5354f76a5b80d29b660d5dc19021e41d9979","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:06.747520+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-09/nasa-s-spacex-crew-12-splashes-down/107245974","language":"","text":"VIDEO: NASA's SpaceX crew 12 splashes down into Pacific Ocean from ISSPosted Fri 9 Oct 2026 at 6:09amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 6:09amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 6:09amHas Video Duration: 28 seconds.Watch 28sNASA's SpaceX crew 12 has splashed down into the Pacific Ocean from the International Space Station. Share optionsFacebookLinkedInX (formerly Twitter)NASA's SpaceX crew 12 has splashed down into the Pacific Ocean from the International Space Station. United StatesSpace","title":"VIDEO: NASA's SpaceX crew 12 splashes down into Pacific Ocean from ISS","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-09/nasa-s-spacex-crew-12-splashes-down/107245974","id":281},{"content_hash":"c5064149224dc010582373ff40d43a5ecd02c0f61d36f529d89b828aef3aaa61","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:06.516933+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/newsletters/template/morning-update/","language":"","text":"Morning Update October 9, 2026Sign up Morning Update Nick Faris Morning Update: Canada reshapes future of MAID Good morning. The federal government plans to bar access to medical assistance in dying on the sole basis of mental illness while providing an opening for those already eligible for MAID to request it in advance. More on that below, along with Mark Carney’s Person of the Year prospects and Terry Fox on the silver screen. Justice Minister Sean Fraser, left, and Health Minister Marjorie Michel outline next steps in the federal government's MAID laws in the House of Commons on Wednesday. Justin Tang/The Canadian Press TOP STORIES MAID to be barred for patients whose sole condition is mental illness The Liberal government is again reshaping Canada’s medical assistance in dying regime to close the door to those whose sole underlying condition is mental illness. At the same time, Justice Minister Sean Fraser announced that coming legislation will include measures to legally allow for advance requests for patients who will lose cognitive capacity to be able to consent to the procedure later. The context: The Liberals faced pressure to make a decision on MAID for mental illness on two fronts. The law that currently bars access to it expires March 17. As well, a Conservative private member’s bill that proposed amending the Criminal Code to bar those with mental disorders from accessing MAID was up for a vote in the Commons. Fraser said the government felt it important to declare its position in advance of that vote. The significance: The debate over who should be able to request a medical practitioner’s help to end their lives is one of the most fractious in Canada. The government has twice paused access on the basis of mental illness over concerns about the readiness of the health care system. From the archives: The faces of MAID: Family and friends of Canadians who chose to die tell the stories of their loved ones. Planes depart and taxi to a runway at Vancouver International Airport in Richmond, B.C. DARRYL DYCK/The Canadian Press Talks begin to privatize Vancouver airport Vancouver International Airport is poised to become the first of the country’s large airports privatized by the federal government, as Ottawa’s advisers offer British Columbia Indigenous groups an opportunity to be minority investors, four sources say. The plan: Along with Vancouver’s airport, Ottawa intends to sell concessions to run all or part of the airports in Calgary, Montreal and Toronto. The federal government, which wants Indigenous investment as part of a reconciliation strategy, will retain ownership of the assets, a structure that is common in Europe and Australia. Opinion: A private airport concession need not be the bogeyman some think it is, Jordan Eizenga and Tim Murphy write. A Lithuanian flag flutters in Vilnius, site of this week's Disinfo2026 conference. INTS KALNINS/Reuters Canada drops sponsorship of anti-disinformation event Ottawa pulled its sponsorship of Disinfo2026, an anti-disinformation conference that features panels talking about the U.S. as a foreign-interference threat, citing discomfort with how some panels are framed. The event: Disinfo2026 is an annual conference on countering disinformation, with this year’s taking place in Lithuania this week. The Lithuanian government said it also withdrew its sponsorship and the EU’s diplomatic service did the same, participants told The Globe. The dissent: The U.S. State Department in an e-mailed statement criticized the conference for proposing that the country is a source of foreign interference and manipulated information. It declined to say whether Washington raised concerns about Disinfo2026 with Canada and European countries. A customer enters a No Frills store in Toronto. Fred Lum/The Globe and Mail What’s the price of a Thanksgiving feast? Serving Thanksgiving dinner for four this weekend will cost $35.72, about three dollars more than last year, if you buy a humble seven-pound turkey and basic side dishes, a Dalhousie University study found. The Globe set out to price a more generous, cost-conscious spread at a variety of midtown Toronto grocery stores. The experiment: The goal was to get a sense of what it costs to put a holiday dinner on the table for six, with a larger turkey, familiar sides and a couple of desserts, while testing whether shopping around to save is worth the extra effort. Ultimately, the spending gap at different stores came down to the cost of the bird. Food for thought: Forget about turkey – stuffed cabbage can be the breakout star of your Thanksgiving table. The Take The Time designation is not a peace prize but is based rather on global influence and news impact. Prediction markets see Mr. Carney as a leading contender. As an international newsmaker he has eclipsed European and other leaders in becoming the standard bearer for an alternative geopolitical vision to the dictates of the megapowers. A Nobel Peace Prize for Carney? Premature. Time Magazine’s Person of the Year award? Lawrence Martin gives his thumbs-up. The Shot Building tops rise above a thick layer of fog at dawn in the Greater Vancouver cities of Burnaby and Surrey. Chris Helgren/Reuters In fast-growing cities such as Vancouver and Toronto, there are pockets where the population is shrinking. What if we put more housing there? The Wrap What else we’re following At home: The average asking price for Canadian rental units in September was $2,034, down 4.2 per cent from a year earlier, extending the streak of annual declines in the market to two full years. Abroad: Former Venezuelan president Nicolás Maduro is expected to face new criminal charges in the U.S. over allegations that Americans detained in the South American country were tortured. Sovereignty: Elon Musk advocated for Quebec, Alberta and Saskatchewan to leave Canada in the aftermath of the separatist Parti Québécois’s provincial election victory. Health: A 37-year-old Ontario father with Stage 4 melanoma expressed disappointment and heartbreak over the province’s repeated refusal to cover an out-of-country treatment that could save his life. Explained: The English soccer club Manchester City’s distortion of financial statements has jeopardized its future in the Premier League and become a fiasco for its UAE ownership. Review: Run Terry Run, the archival documentary about Terry Fox set to hit select theatres, deftly humanizes a Canadian mythological figure. In memoriam: The actor Eva Marie Saint, who won an Academy Award for her Hollywood debut and astonished the industry with a firm independence, has died at 102. The Decibel podcast New episode A border town on the front lines of the trade war Recent episodes Canada’s income tax law has become a ‘Frankenstein’s monster’ How some Alberta separatists are courting U.S. support All episodes Check out today’s horoscopes. Solve today’s puzzles. See today’s Editorial cartoon. Thanks for reading. If you have any feedback on today’s newsletter, reach out to the newsletter team. Are you reading us on the web or did someone forward the e-mail version to you? If so, you can sign up for Morning Update here. Finally, for technical problems with this newsletter, please contact Customer Support. Subscribe Sign up for other newsletters Listen to our podcasts Globe events Advertise with us Connect with us You received this email at [Contact.Email] because you subscribe to Morning Update. To advertise with us in print and online, visit GlobeMediaGroup. Unsubscribe | Privacy Policy | Terms & Conditions | Contact Us © 2026 The Globe and Mail Inc. 351 King Street East, Suite 1600, Toronto, ON M5A 0N1 Andrew Saunders, President and CEO","title":"Morning Update","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/newsletters/template/morning-update/","id":282},{"content_hash":"3d3631c0a244f1440095129008e156e6a38535585752b3abc24ce2bcf16c20d0","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:06.327235+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/science-graduates-are-not-that-hot-at-maths-but-why-32021","language":"","text":"Science graduates are not that hot at maths – but why? Published: September 29, 2014 12:28am EDT https://theconversation.com/science-graduates-are-not-that-hot-at-maths-but-why-32021 https://theconversation.com/science-graduates-are-not-that-hot-at-maths-but-why-32021 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Research suggests science graduates are struggling with essential quantitative skills and science degree programs are to blame. Quantitative skills are the bread and butter of science. More than calculating right answers, quantitative skills are defined by applying mathematical and statistical reasoning to scientific and everyday problems. They underpin national and international expectations for science graduates. But at two Group of Eight universities, 40% of final year science students reported low levels of confidence in quantitative skills. We know what students think, but what can they do? Applying maths and stats reasoning In the weeks before, 210 biosciences students were walking across the stage to receive their Bachelor of Science qualification and I asked them to answer 35 mathematical and statistical reasoning questions. Below is a preview of results presented today at the Australian Conference on Science and Mathematics Education and previously at the First Year in Maths Forum. The maths used in the biosciences are often not much more than school level maths. I used questions from online maths modules developed by university bioscientists, such as: The diameter of ribosomes start at about 11 nanometres. How many micrometres is this? The intent was to assess application of mathematical reasoning, not a student’s memory of metric units. So some additional information was provided: The metric vocabulary includes TERA (trillions), GIGA (billions), MEGA (millions), KILO (thousands), MILLI (thousandths), MICRO (millionths), NANO (billionths) and PICO (trillionths). The easiest way to convert metric measurements is to move the decimal place. Every time you move to the right on this list, the unit gets smaller (by 3 decimal places), so you need more of them to compensate (move the decimal place 3 to the right). It was almost giving them the answer but of the 210 students, only 69% were able to select the correct answer (try for yourself). Science involves data and science graduates need to be able to make sense of it. Flickr/Pink Sherbet Photography, CC BY Students were then presented with another metric conversion question that showed five numbers (2x103 cm; 0.3 cm; 4 mm; 7x10-4 km; 8 m) and asked to place them in ascending order. Only 50% selected the correct answer. Of the 10 basic mathematical application questions asked, many underpinned by proportional reasoning, the average score was 74%. Interpreting data and drawing sensible conclusions are central to science. I used statistical reasoning tasks developed by university statisticians with questions such as this hypothesis test. An electrician uses an instrument to test whether or not an electrical circuit is defective. The instrument sometimes fails to detect that a circuit is good and working. The null hypothesis is that the circuit is good (not defective). The alternative hypothesis is that the circuit is not good (defective). If the electrician rejects the null hypothesis, which of the following statements is true? The options were then given the students: The electrician decides that the circuit is defective, but it could be good The circuit is definitely not good and needs to be repaired The circuit is definitely good and does not need to be repaired The circuit is most likely good, but it could be defective Only 45% of students selected the correct answer with most students demonstrating a fundamental misconception of statistical hypothesis testing. In another question, probing understanding of correlation and causation, students were asked: Researchers surveyed 1,000 randomly selected adults in the United States. A statistically significant, strong positive correlation was found between income level and the number of containers of recycling they typically collect in a week. Please select the best interpretation of this result. Again, the students were asked to select from optons: We can not conclude whether earning more money causes more recycling among US adults because this type of design does not allow us to infer causation This sample is too small to draw any conclusions about the relationship between income level and amount of recycling for adults in the US This result indicates that earning more money influences people to recycle more than people who earn less money One-third of students answered incorrectly. Of the 25 statistical reasoning questions asked, exploring topics such as graphical representation of data, tests of significance and probability, the average score was 56%. Who is responsible for teaching the basic skills - schools or universities? Flickr/Kevin Dooley, CC BY-NC-ND So who’s to blame? We could easily blame the school sector or even students for these alarming results. But at the end of the day if universities give students degree qualifications, they have to take responsibility for the quality of those graduates. As I have argued before, science students can tell us a lot about the quality of science degree programs. I interviewed several students about the quantitative skills questions discussed above. A maths student (she answered 23 of the 35 questions correctly) said: I have never been asked questions like this in my degree program. A biosciences student (he answered 27 of the 35 questions correctly) added: I would have done better if I took this in first year. He then explained how little quantitative skills were required in science subjects. Empirical research indicates he is not alone with 400 science students indicating quantitative skills were not widely assessed across the curriculum. The student interviews resonated with findings of a recent study into bioscience degree programs that asked 46 academics from 13 universities to identify where students learn quantitative skills. The results showed that quantitative skills were not taught much and that academics rarely talked collectively about the teaching of quantitative skills across the degree program. The real problems to address in science higher education, if we expect students to graduate with quantitative skills or any complex learning outcome for that matter, are whole of degree program curriculum leadership and teaching development. Most science academics have no training in teaching or learning, and they do not have a picture of the curriculum beyond the modules they teach. Science departments typically do not have curriculum leaders who gather together academics to plan for student learning across subjects or year levels. We need to start discussing this nationally so when science students walk across the stage to get their science qualifications, we trust the quality of the qualification. Kelly E Matthews, The University of Queensland Author Kelly E Matthews Lecturer in Curriculum Development (Higher Education), The University of Queensland Disclosure statement Kelly E Matthews receives funding from The Office for Learning and Teaching. Partners University of Queensland provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.rhtn67u6j","title":"Science graduates are not that hot at maths – but why?","url":"https://theconversation.com/science-graduates-are-not-that-hot-at-maths-but-why-32021","id":283},{"content_hash":"765878653fde1df375c053195e37cf5d2e71e948ef3e24efff6f8e69682b9bc4","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:05.624255+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-proces-de-l-affaire-du-rhum-commence-demain-mercredi_2072924_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Bordeaux, 6 novembre. - Le tribunal correctionnel de Bordeaux va consacrer à partir Se demain mercredi, sous la présidence de M. Demichel, plusieurs audiences au \" scandale du rhum \", ou plus exactement aux multiples affaires dont le réseau complexe constitue l'affaire Laurent-Banque d'Indochine. Assez simple dans ses grandes lignes, cette affaire se présente dans ses détails, aux dires de certains experts, comme un \" chef-d'œuvre \" sans précédent dans le domaine de l'escroquerie. Le 12 janvier 1950 un quotidien bordelais, dont l'existence fut d'ailleurs éphémère, faisait éclater ce \" scandale du rhum \", qui couvait depuis un an et dont la première phase avait eu la veille son épilogue devant le tribunal de commerce de Bordeaux. Un négociant bordelais, Arthur Laurent, directeur de la Société des rhums Laurent, demandait en effet l'annulation de la vente forcée de neuf mille cinq cents actions de cette société, rachetées immédiatement par la Banque d'Indochine. Cette instance devait être la première manifestation d'un feu croisé de plaintes de la Société contre son directeur, de la Banque d'Indochine contre Laurent, de celui-ci contre inconnu pour vol de rhum dans ses chais, de Laurent encore contre la Banque d'Indochine, et finalement de celle-ci contre le directeur de son agence de Bordeaux, M. Vigier. Les premières conclusions de l'expert, M. Verlaguet - qui avait été commis en 1934 dans l'affaire Stavisky, - amenaient l'arrestation d'Arthur Laurent et de M. Vigier, accusés d'abus de confiance. Ces deux inculpés ont été mis depuis en liberté provisoire. Un milliard d'escroqueries ? On avançait comme mentant total des opérations frauduleuses le chiffre de 1 milliard de francs, dont la moitié environ serait rattachée à l'affaire Laurent proprement dite. La Banque d'Indochine reproche en effet au directeur de son agence à Bordeaux, M. Vigier, d'avoir accordé à la Société, pour l'importation de rhums de la Martinique, des découverts bien supérieurs aux 106 millions autorisés par le siège social. Elle reproche à Laurent d'avoir offert en garantie des traites fictives aux noms de maisons connues sur la place. Il vous reste 23.7% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"LE PROCÈS DE \" L'AFFAIRE DU RHUM \" commence demain mercredi","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-proces-de-l-affaire-du-rhum-commence-demain-mercredi_2072924_1819218.html","id":284},{"content_hash":"b45211f176ca9b39ba115f57e8a7dd4e4217a8ef80063953da0760cd555d4eb7","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:05.469030+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/forms/","language":"","text":"Forms¶ Detailed form API reference. For introductory material, see the Working with forms topic guide. The Forms API Bound and unbound forms Using forms to validate data Initial form values Checking which form data has changed Accessing the fields from the form Accessing “clean” data Outputting forms as HTML More granular output Customizing BoundField Binding uploaded files to a form Subclassing forms Prefixes for forms Form fields Core field arguments Checking if the field data has changed Built-in Field classes Slightly complex built-in Field classes Fields which handle relationships Creating custom fields Model forms Model form Meta API Model form factory functions Formset Functions formset_factory The form rendering API The low-level render API Built-in-template form renderers Context available in formset templates Context available in form templates Context available in field templates Context available in widget templates Overriding built-in formset templates Overriding built-in form templates Overriding built-in field templates Overriding built-in widget templates Widgets Specifying widgets Setting arguments for widgets Customizing widget instances Base widget classes Built-in widgets Form and field validation Raising ValidationError Using validation in practice","title":"Forms¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/forms/","id":285},{"content_hash":"3d9720badbc0264beecd8609dc277f51b7099db36b7045ad7718bf72a7459bb5","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:05.300979+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-a-risk-assessment-matrix","language":"","text":"What is a Risk Assessment Matrix?ByPrince, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuApril 11, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Is a Risk Assessment Matrix?What is a Risk Matrix?How Does the Risk Assessment Matrix Work?What Are the Benefits of Using a Risk Assessment Matrix?What are the Four Components of an RPN Matrix?How to Make a Risk Assessment MatrixChallenges of Making and Implementing a Risk Assessment MatrixTips for Using a Risk Assessment Matrix EffectivelyHow to Specify the Likelihood of a Risk HappeningHow You Can Take Care of Your Risk Assessment MatrixFinal ThoughtsShare this postAre you looking for a way to identify and manage risks in your business? If so, then the risk assessment matrix may be the perfect tool for you. A risk assessment matrix is an effective method of assessing and managing potential risks to a business. It enables you to identify risks before they occur, allowing you to plan and take action to reduce the impact of any negative event. The risk assessment matrix consists of three segments: The threat, The probability of the threat happening, and The effect that it would have if it were to happen. With this tool, businesses can systematically analyze their environment and create strategies to minimize any foreseeable risks. In this article, we will explain how a risk assessment matrix works and provide step-by-step instructions on how to create one. We will also look at examples of high-risk scenarios that can be identified through a risk assessment matrix. What Is a Risk Assessment Matrix? A risk assessment matrix is a tool used to analyze and assess the risks associated with a given activity or project. It is composed of two axes, one for likelihood and one for potential severity, which is used to measure each risk and assign it a rank or score. Using the matrix, you can gain insight into the probability of a risk occurring and its potential severity should it occur. This helps clarify which risks your company or organization should prioritize in its efforts to prevent losses. Additionally, the rankings you assign to each risk can be used to create a strategy that examines how each risk can be mitigated.The risk assessment matrix is an invaluable tool in helping you make informed decisions when it comes to identifying and managing potential risks. What is a Risk Matrix? A risk matrix or a risk rating matrix is a visual tool used in risk assessment to prioritize risks based on their likelihood and impact. A risk matrix typically takes the form of a grid or table, with one axis representing the likelihood of a risk occurring and the other axis representing the severity of its potential impact. Risk matrix can be used interchangeably with risk assessment matrix. How Does the Risk Assessment Matrix Work? The risk matrix typically defines low, moderate, and high probabilities for each risk as well as low, moderate, and high impacts. By assigning numerical values to each of these categories, companies can quickly prioritize areas requiring immediate attention before they become major problems. For example, if a low-probability event has a high impact on operations or customer satisfaction, it should be given higher priority than a high-probability event with only a moderate impact. By using the risk assessment matrix in tandem with other tools such as cost/benefit analysis or decision trees, organizations can better weigh the risks and rewards associated with various courses of action before making informed decisions. What Are the Benefits of Using a Risk Assessment Matrix? A risk assessment matrix, also known as a Risk Priority Number (RPN) matrix, is a tool used to identify, categorize, and prioritize risks. It helps project managers make more informed decisions on how to allocate resources to minimize the impact of the risks they have identified. The primary benefit of using this tool is that it provides a structured way to assess and quantify the risk posed by each identified risk. Recommended - What Is eKYC? What are the Four Components of an RPN Matrix? The 4 major components of an RPN Matric include: 1. Severity This measures the potential impact of an identified risk on the overall project objectives. 2. Occurrence Frequency This measures how often the potential impact may occur if the risk was not addressed or mitigated. 3. Detection Frequency This measures how often the development team can detect and proactively address the potential impacts of an identified risk. 4. Detection Difficulty This measures how difficult it is for the development team to detect and address the potential impacts of an identified risk. The points assigned for each of these four components are weighted differently to generate a total score for each specific identified risk. These scores are then used by project managers to prioritize which risks should be addressed first and which ones can safely be ignored. By using an RPN matrix, project managers are better equipped to make decisions on how best to allocate resources to mitigate risks while maintaining their focus on meeting deadlines and staying within budget. How to Make a Risk Assessment Matrix Making a risk matrix can help organizations make better, more informed decisions about their projects. The steps to creating a risk assessment matrix are: a. Identify potential risks Start by brainstorming a list of potential risks associated with your project. b. Analyze the risks Determine the likelihood and severity of each risk based on a predetermined scale, such as \"high,\" \"medium,\" or \"low.\" c. Assign Risk Categories Assign categories to each risk depending on its severity and likelihood (e.g. High Risk, Medium Risk, Low Risk). d. Rate risks Assign numerical values to each risk category for easier comparison between different risks (e.g., High Risk = 3; Medium Risk = 2; Low Risk = 1). e. Create matrix Use the data from your analysis to create your risk assessment matrix in an easy-to-understand format (e.g., table or chart). f. Prioritize Actions Review your risk assessment results to prioritize appropriate actions for managing different types of risks (e.g., preventive measures or contingencies). Read Also: Understanding Third-Party Risk Management- KYC for Fintechs Challenges of Making and Implementing a Risk Assessment Matrix Creating and implementing a risk matrix can be a complex process, especially if you are not familiar with the risk assessment process. This can lead to several challenges, including: 1. Issues Identifying Risks Risk identification can be tricky, and failing to identify all potential risks can lead to overlooking important details that could drastically alter your approach. 2. Resource Allocation Risk assessment requires addressing both potential and current risks. This can require substantial resources for planning, gathering data, and analyzing results. 3. Determining Thresholds When assessing risks, there is often a need to determine what level of risk is acceptable or unacceptable based on an organization’s priorities and goals. Without clearly defined thresholds, it is difficult to assess the severity of risks accurately. 4. Communication And Collaboration Effective communication between different departments is essential when creating a risk matrix as they must be informed of any changes or updates to the matrix for it to remain effective over time. Tips for Using a Risk Assessment Matrix Effectively Here are some practical tips for leveraging this tool to its fullest potential: i. Identify Risk The first step is to identify any potential risks that could affect the project or operational goal. These can range from external factors, such as changes in the market, to internal factors, such as employees leaving the organization. ii. Assign Probability and Impact Scores Once the risks have been identified, assign a probability score and an impact score to each risk. Probability scores can range from low (1) to high (5), while impact scores can range from low (1) to catastrophic (10). iii. Prioritize Risks Use the risk assessment matrix to prioritize risks by plotting them on the matrix with their corresponding probability and impact scores. Risks with higher scores should be tracked more closely, while those with lower scores should be monitored periodically. iv. Monitor and Mitigate Risk Over Time Finally, monitor the identified risks over time and implement strategies to mitigate them if they become more likely or their impact becomes higher. This will help ensure that the project or operational goal stays on track without any major disruptions. How to Specify the Likelihood of a Risk Happening Risk assessment matrices are data tables used to identify the probability of a risk occurring and the possible consequences if it does. It consists of two columns – one for the likelihood of the risk happening and one for the consequence of the risk. The higher the likelihood and consequence, the greater priority it should be given to reduce or manage it. To determine the likelihood of a risk occurring, quantitative measures can be used such as a percentage change or numerical rating. Qualitative ratings can also be used, such as low, medium, high, and extreme. To assess the potential consequences, qualitative ratings such as minor, moderate, serious, and catastrophic are used. The combination of qualitative and quantitative ratings can give you an indication of what level of priority should be given to each risk. These ratings are then recorded in the risk assessment matrix so that they can be referenced in future discussions and planning sessions around potential risks. Recommended: The Risk Intelligence Arm of Risk Management in Fintech Loan Companies How You Can Take Care of Your Risk Assessment Matrix Caring for your risk assessment matrix is essential to ensure its accuracy and effectiveness. You can take care of your assessment matrix in the following ways: a. Have Regular Reviews Regular reviews should be conducted to ensure that the risk assessment matrix remains up-to-date and relevant. This can help identify any changes in the risks associated with a particular project or process, and allow for adjustments to be made accordingly. b. Update Criteria As your organization grows and changes, so too should the criteria used in your risk assessment matrix. Ensure that the criteria used reflect both current industry best practices and your organization's unique requirements. c. Monitor Impacts and PrioritiesAny time a new project or process is introduced, it is important to review all potential risks associated with it and determine the potential impact on your business. Additionally, if priorities change - such as minimizing costs over minimizing risks - this should be reflected in any updates made to the risk assessment matrix. By following these simple steps, you can help ensure your risk assessment matrix provides accurate information that allows you to make informed decisions about potential risks. Final Thoughts A risk assessment matrix provides an organization with a systematic means to identify and prioritize potential risks associated with a particular project. By providing both the likelihood and potential severity of any given risk, the risk assessment matrix can help an organization determine which risks should be addressed first, and the extent to which resources should be allocated to mitigating them. Risk assessment matrices can also be used in the larger context of risk management, providing organizations with an efficient way to identify, prioritize, and address potential hazards in the long term. Ultimately, using a risk assessment matrix can help organizations to make more informed decisions, and create safer environments for their employees and customers. Ready to reduce the likelihood of risks? A risk assessment matrix is a great place to start! Youverify offers AI-powered and patent-pending risk assessment matrix solutions that help you monitor customer risks at every point in time to make informed decisions. Request a demo today to get started. Related ArticlesView All PostsRisk IntelligencePrince, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•May 8, 2023How Machine Learning is Used In Fraud Prevention For E-commerceRead MoreRisk IntelligencePriscilla Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•May 9, 2023Fraud Detection Using Machine Learning - How Does it Work? Read MoreRisk IntelligenceEmmanuel Agwu•August 3, 2023How to Assess and Mitigate Vendor Risks Using Compliance SolutionsRead More","title":"What is a Risk Assessment Matrix?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-a-risk-assessment-matrix","id":286},{"content_hash":"4bfda295d4c1d1f97d00c60f2a32fd692899627b538cdbe434e1c814ff013477","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:05.145114+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/education-is-a-public-good-not-a-private-commodity-31408","language":"","text":"Education is a public good, not a private commodity Published: September 30, 2014 3:41pm EDT “If you think education is expensive, try ignorance.” Educator Derek Bok. Shutterstock https://theconversation.com/education-is-a-public-good-not-a-private-commodity-31408 https://theconversation.com/education-is-a-public-good-not-a-private-commodity-31408 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article We are at an important juncture for education in Australia. With the government’s higher education reform agenda well underway and its school funding stasis, teacher education and Australian curriculum reviews, now is the time to be asking: what is education actually for and who does it benefit? Education tends to be argued about in terms of weighing up public and private benefit. At one end, you have the notion of education as a public good, where the benefits spread across society in terms of employment, economic prosperity, health and social cohesion. At the other end, you have education as a commodity, which fits into a user-pays system. Who benefits from education? The neoliberal position on education is that it is a private benefit, measured in terms of economic and social attainment. In other words: study hard, get a good job and you will reap the rewards of your individual efforts. A more nuanced position would consider the social benefits of education, which are well documented. Increasing literacy rates, for example, leads to improved health outcomes, broader participation in democratic processes, reduced crime and poverty rates, environmental sustainability and social equality. In a recent report, UNESCO outlined how education performs much more than an economic function, by enabling: individuals, especially women, to live and aspire to healthy, meaningful, creative and resilient lives. It strengthens their voices in community, national and global affairs. It opens up new work opportunities and sources of social mobility. Earlier this month, the OECD released its Education at a Glance report, which emphasised the need for equity in education, where all students have the same opportunities for educational success. This was also the underlying message of the Gonski funding review, which has been used as a political football ever since. It has been a very busy year for education politics. Funding and equity continue to feature in media reports and need to be understood in terms of the current policy agenda. Private vs public schooling and the mantra of choice The argument goes something like this: give parents choice over schools and competition will do the rest. This positions parents and their children as consumers, while schools, universities and education itself become commodities. Yet there are some significant problems with a user-pays approach to education, whether we are talking schools or universities. The PISA in Focus report shows that the mantra of “choice” is exacerbating inequality in schooling. The obsession with market-based thinking in education can work against the very narrative of improving educational opportunities. This is, of course, something that our ministers of education would not wish to hear, because the whole push is to go down this path in the name of school improvement. The question could be asked: after all the focus on competition and choice over the past couple of decades, why does Australia’s performance on international comparisons continue to slide, when we have one of the highest rates of children in private schooling in the OECD? There is little long-term benefit from private schooling, and studies show once at university public school graduates perform better. So why do we continue to denigrate and devalue public schooling in this country? This narrative has been stoked by the media and politicians claiming that schools and universities need to be “fixed” and that market-based notions of competition and choice are the solution. One example is in Chistropher Pyne’s hyperbolic claims that without deregulation, Australian universities will slide into mediocrity. Making the public private in the name of improvement There are serious concerns about the encroachment of corporations into education, which has been playing out on the global stage. The privatisation movement globally has some worrying implications for education as a public good. Swedish Free Schools have been around since the early 1990s, yet show limited educational gains for students. Instead, the focus becomes one of profit over student learning. However, there is little evidence that “profit-making would meet demand for good school places; be commercially viable; create better choice; increase competition; or raise standards”. In the UK, these are called Free Schools, whereas in the USA they are Charter Schools. In Chile, there is a push back against for-profit education, which has had disastrous effects for public education. Western Australia has been the forerunner for the privatisation of public schools in Australia, with its Independent Public Schools reform. Reports about its efficacy have been mixed, yet despite there being very little evidence that the reforms will have any impact on student performance, this is the path that we seem to be intent on heading down. The federal government have announced a $70 million package to support the rollout of Independent Public Schools across Tasmania, Victoria, Queensland and the Northern Territory. This push has been labelled “privatisation by stealth”. Equity and social justice in education More than 20 years ago, when describing the importance of social justice and equity in education, Raewyn Connell commented: An education that privileges one child over another is giving the privileged child a corrupted education, even as it gives him or her a social or economic advantage. We have known the importance of these notions for a long time, yet inequality is a persistent feature of education in Australia, which has been covered extensively here, here and here. Cut to 2014, and Gonski co-panellist Ken Boston claims that we have the most socially segregated education system in the Western world. It seems more important than ever to consider the importance of providing an education system that is built upon principles of equity and social justice, in order to give all kids the very best opportunities we can. Funding is at the heart of a more equitable system. One way of working towards this is to build the very best public schooling system that we possibly can: first, by investing in a needs-based funding model such as the one proposed by the Gonski review. A cornerstone of this model would work to address the Gonski review’s basic tenet, that “all students have access to a high standard of education regardless of their background or circumstances”. Stewart Riddle, University of Southern Queensland Author Stewart Riddle Lecturer in Literacies Education, University of Southern Queensland Disclosure statement Stewart Riddle does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Southern Queensland provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.nmu9hfwuw","title":"Education is a public good, not a private commodity","url":"https://theconversation.com/education-is-a-public-good-not-a-private-commodity-31408","id":287},{"content_hash":"c37de79533ba78869b60a3b1e6847adc9370dba17fdc15e697d41e6ff30de3ec","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:04.593378+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/adv/appointmentnotices/article-tim-b-haney/","language":"","text":"Tim B. Haney, Chief Executive Officer, Dentons CanadaSUPPLIEDShareDentons is pleased to announce that Tim B. Haney has been appointed to a second term as Chief Executive Officer of Dentons Canada, effective February 1, 2027.Since his appointment as CEO in February 2022, Tim has led the Firm through a period of significant growth, innovation and transformation. Under his leadership, Dentons has strengthened its position as Canada’s Global Law Firm, expanded cross-border collaboration and deepened its ability to support clients pursuing growth, expansion and strategic opportunities across Canada and around the world.During his tenure as CEO, Tim has championed enterprise-wide transformation across technology, innovation and artificial intelligence, while spearheading the material expansion of top-calibre legal talent and helping to foster a high-performing, inclusive culture recognized as one of Canada’s Top 100 Employers for four consecutive years (2023-2026).Widely regarded as one of Canada’s leading lawyers in corporate finance, mergers and acquisitions and private equity, Tim brings more than two decades of legal and business leadership experience to the role. In addition to serving as CEO of Dentons Canada, Tim is a member of the Dentons Global Management Committee and Global Board. In 2023, he was named CEO of the Year by the Canadian Law Awards, recognizing his leadership and the collective success of the Dentons Canada team.Dentons is Canada’s Global Law Firm and part of the world’s largest global law firm. With a presence in more than 80 countries, Dentons combines deep local market knowledge with global reach to help clients navigate growth, complexity and opportunity wherever business takes them.Open this photo in gallery:SUPPLIED","title":"Tim B. Haney","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/adv/appointmentnotices/article-tim-b-haney/","id":288},{"content_hash":"04d1b1de828acf0d354da42c3dd2d536a8e7636b95a19a307a783222fc0951b1","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:04.592114+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/country-singer-zach-bryan-quietly-bought-an-18-million-tennessee-estate-in-2026-the-secluded-property-sits-on-146-acres-and-includes-a-6000-square-foot-main-house-and-recording-studio/articleshow/134779303.cms","language":"","text":"Country singer Zach Bryan quietly bought an $18 million Tennessee estate in 2026; the secluded property sits on 146 acres and includes a 6,000-square-foot main house and recording studio - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIworldUSTimes EvokePakistanSouth AsiaUKEuropeChinaMiddle East US StreamersRest of WorldMad, Mad WorldVideosToday's ePaperLiveNewsWorld NewsUS NewsCountry singer Zach Bryan quietly bought an $18 million Tennessee estate in 2026; the secluded property sits on 146 acres and includes a 6,000-square-foot main house and recording studioTrendingChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsCountry singer Zach Bryan quietly bought an $18 million Tennessee estate in 2026; the secluded property sits on 146 acres and includes a 6,000-square-foot main house and recording studioTOI World Desk / TIMESOFINDIA.COM / Oct 09, 2026, 02:55 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint Country singer Zach Bryan has quietly added a sprawling Tennessee property to his real estate portfolio, purchasing a nearly 40-acre estate in the Leiper’s Fork area near Franklin for $18.25 million earlier this year. The purchase was first reported by TMZ, which cited property records and sources familiar with the transaction. Country music outlet Whiskey Riff later reported on the acquisition, placing the estate roughly 10 miles south of Franklin and about 40 miles from Nashville. The deal, which reportedly closed in February, gives Bryan a private countryside retreat with considerably more than a luxury residence. The property combines expansive outdoor spaces with entertainment facilities that appear particularly suited to a musician who spends much of his life writing and recording.Inside Zach Bryan’s Leiper’s Fork estateThe main residence measures about 5,035 square feet and has four bedrooms and five bathrooms. A wraparound porch takes advantage of the surrounding countryside, giving the home the kind of expansive views and privacy associated with the rural communities outside Nashville.The grounds are equally elaborate. The estate includes two stocked ponds, a saltwater infinity pool, an outdoor kitchen, formal gardens and walking trails. The combination gives the property the feel of a secluded country compound rather than a conventional celebrity home.There is also a major entertainment space separate from the main residence. The 5,300-square-foot barn includes a large bar, video wall, two stone fireplaces, a game room, gym, catering kitchen, two bedroom suites and a two-car garage. Most notably for Bryan, the building houses a professional recording studio. That studio could make the property particularly practical for the Grammy-winning singer-songwriter. Rather than having to leave his rural retreat to work on music, Bryan can record on the estate itself, combining a private home environment with a dedicated creative space.A private corner near NashvilleLeiper’s Fork has long been associated with Nashville’s country music community, partly because it offers considerable space and privacy while remaining within reach of Music City. Whiskey Riff noted that the wider Franklin and Leiper’s Fork area has attracted several major country artists over the years.For Bryan, the location also keeps him relatively close to Nashville without putting him directly in the middle of the city. His new estate is surrounded by the quieter landscape of Williamson County, making it a stark contrast to the constant movement that comes with touring.The purchase is also part of a busy year for Bryan away from the stage. Whiskey Riff reported that he had already purchased a townhouse in New York City for $13 million in March, adding another high-value property to his growing real estate holdings.Bryan’s Tennessee purchase comes as his touring schedule continues to keep him on the road. His official 2026 tour schedule lists an October 10 performance at Jordan-Hare Stadium in Auburn, Alabama, as one of the year’s final dates. For now, however, the Leiper’s Fork estate offers something very different from a stadium: privacy, countryside views and a fully equipped space where the singer can work on music without leaving home.While the exact location of the property has not been publicly disclosed, its combination of land, luxury amenities, entertainment facilities and a professional recording studio makes the $18.25 million purchase one of Bryan’s most notable real estate moves of 2026.You use AI every day. Now get your AI Quotient. Take the AIQ test.About the AuthorTOI World DeskAt TOI World Desk, our dedicated team of seasoned journalists and passionate writers tirelessly sifts through the vast tapestry of global events to bring you the latest news and diverse perspectives round the clock. With an unwavering commitment to accuracy, depth, and timeliness, we strive to keep you informed about the ever-evolving world, delivering a nuanced understanding of international affairs to our readers. Join us on a journey across continents as we unravel the stories that shape our interconnected world.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaVideos‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New PresserTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasJD Vance Exposes H-1B Scam!: 6,000 Americans Duped; Microsoft, Wipro & TCS On Visa HitlistWATCH: Trump Heckler Assaulted At Texas Rally; POTUS Immediately ReactsChrista HELPED Executioners? Pike Lawyers' SHOCKER Adds BIG Twist To Failed Execution Mystery‘Europe Gets New F-35’: Marco Rubio Flexes NATO Power With Massive Ammo Boost | Watch'Now I Am Depressed': Trump Admits Major Hair Loss Issue At Texas Rally | Health UpdateRiyadh Blasts Send Diplomats Into Shelter, Schools Take Precautions Amid Houthi AttacksRussia Plague Scare Takes A Shocking Turn; Mother Disputes Test Tube Accident ReportDolly Parton's Family Eyes BIOPIC Amid $650 Million Estate Feud; Who Will Play The Music Icon?123PhotostoriesKangana Ranaut goes full Himachali royalty in a statement topi, Kullu shawl, and a cultural nod to President Droupadi Murmu5 habits that matter more than supplements: Doctor shares simple daily habits for better sleep, fitness, nutrition, stress management and preventive health that can support a longer, healthier life5 lipstick shades to pair with your chaniya choli this NavratriOptical illusion personality test: What you see first claims to reveal whether you are a practical thinker or a gut-feeling personKareena Kapoor Khan just gave motorsport a Bebo makeover in sleek black tailoring10 baby names inspired by astronauts, space pioneers and cosmic explorersGen Z is making Indian textiles cool again: 5 heritage weaves that are having a fashion comeback7 things you should always have in your office bag: These everyday essentials can come in handy if you’re in a meeting that runs long or your day takes an unexpected turn‘I have a strong case’: Mary Kom’s ex-husband Onler takes her to court over ‘false allegations’Castor oil vs coconut oil: Which hair oil wins the winter hair growth game?123Hot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn USIn WorldAmerica has enjoyed the greatest free lunch in history: Vivek Wadhwa reacts to Green Card crackdown on 8 IT companies, says they aren't the visa abusersIndian-origin doctor mom arrested and charged in Florida as she watched 7-year-old drown. She said she froze, she was not strong enough to pull him from waterFloyd V. Bowlin-Lemon: Arkansas court upholds fence boundary; $12,000 treble damages standFederal executiv