Constitutional Law Criminal Law Customary Law Election Cases Environmental Law General Human Rights Law Insurance Law Labour Law Land Cases Matrimonial Cases Probate Matters Rulings Torts Law Forms and Precedents Legal Extras Blog Articles Top 7 Mistakes Every Personal Injury Lawyer Should Avoid in Their Career Contact RECENT POSTS ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IN COURT PROCEEDINGS MRS ITOHAN MERCY ERIOGHAYE V. MR UYI CHESTERSON ERIOGHAYE MR. SAMUEL DICKENS ORIE V. MR. DARREN QUANGBI & 2 ORS High Court of the FCT Civil Procedure Rules, 2025 REGULATING AI IN THE UNITED STATES ET AL ARCHIVES October 2026 (1) September 2026 (2) August 2026 (77) July 2026 (28) June 2026 (22) May 2026 (3) April 2026 (4) March 2026 (5) February 2026 (7) January 2026 (3) December 2025 (1) October 2025 (4) September 2025 (1) July 2025 (5) June 2025 (12) May 2025 (11) April 2025 (3) March 2025 (2) February 2025 (4) January 2025 (1) December 2024 (2) November 2024 (202) October 2024 (84) September 2024 (169) August 2024 (2) July 2024 (3) June 2024 (452) May 2024 (17) April 2024 (2) March 2024 (3) January 2024 (3) December 2023 (1) November 2023 (2) October 2023 (3) August 2023 (1) June 2023 (1) May 2023 (3) April 2023 (2) March 2023 (1) February 2023 (1) January 2023 (3) December 2022 (1) November 2022 (1) October 2022 (3) July 2022 (4) June 2022 (1) May 2022 (3) April 2022 (2) March 2022 (2) February 2022 (1) January 2022 (1) December 2021 (1) November 2021 (2) October 2021 (1) September 2021 (1) July 2021 (1) June 2021 (3) CALENDAR October 2026 M T W T F S S 1234 567891011 12131415161718 19202122232425 262728293031 « Sep Scroll to Top","title":"Category: Uncategorized","url":"https://nigerianlawguru.com/category/uncategorized/","id":309},{"content_hash":"de079d7630160150ef9c7b15d0c7b2837359fc5467c0703d8c63d9fbd4a24429","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:00.374307+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/entretiens-americano-bresiliens-sur-l-energie-atomique_2072492_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Entretiens américano-brésiliens sur l'énergie atomique Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Entretiens américano-brésiliens sur l'énergie atomique","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/entretiens-americano-bresiliens-sur-l-energie-atomique_2072492_1819218.html","id":310},{"content_hash":"a48249100a62aa65fec17b2fedb86fe77e4f5293b29065e71c27ea18576603ce","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:00.320735+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-as-usual-ailing-parties-fail-to-get-to-grips-with-social-media-32661","language":"","text":"Politics as usual? Ailing parties fail to get to grips with social media Published: October 12, 2014 10:03pm EDT Senator John Faulkner’s call for political parties to re-engage with Australians through social media is laudable, but his own efforts illustrate how much politicians have to learn. Facebook/John Faulkner, CC BY-SA https://theconversation.com/politics-as-usual-ailing-parties-fail-to-get-to-grips-with-social-media-32661 https://theconversation.com/politics-as-usual-ailing-parties-fail-to-get-to-grips-with-social-media-32661 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article After his speech about party renewal last week, I went to Labor Senator John Faulkner’s Facebook page. It has about 2700 likes. The page features links to speeches and pictures of events that Faulkner has been to, including meeting US President Barack Obama and a charity event for Alzheimer’s. But in reality it’s being used as just another medium to “broadcast” political messages and statements. There is no evidence that John Faulkner himself uses the page - it says it’s managed by his office. There is no interaction at all with the people who have taken time to comment on the page. This demonstrates a fundamental misunderstanding of the way social media works. Social media provides an everyday horizontal network between a group of connected individuals; it’s meant to be an informal space to interact and engage. Increasingly, ordinary citizens want to see that politicians are ordinary like them, just community members who happen to be politicians. They want politicians to be authentic and are sceptical if they see staff posting on their behalf. Social media is a primary source of source I recently completed a project, The Civic Network, in collaboration with USA and UK-based colleagues, Michael Xenos and Brian Loader. Our project examined how young people use social media for political engagement. We were especially interested in whether social media was making it easier for a broader range of young people to express themselves and take action on politics. Facebook has become ubiquitous for young people. About 90% of those aged 16-29 in each country have a Facebook page. Young people also learn about politics and major news events on Facebook rather than via traditional news outlets. That is, 65% of young Australians hear about major news events first on Facebook, before any other media outlet, and a majority regularly follow links to news stories from Facebook. Our research suggests that we need to take Facebook much more seriously as a space where young people - purposefully or incidentally - engage with politics, with their networks of friends and family. Furthermore, contrary to popular speculation, this does not lead to an echo chamber of hearing only one-sided views. A majority of young Australians say they learn from Facebook friends who have different political views from theirs. Many of our research participants believe that “liking” is an important way of showing symbolic support for political issues they and their friends care about. They are more likely to do this than to comment on posts about politics, which was just over a third in Australia and UK and 40% in the US. Old politics is for old media In qualitative online discussions we asked young people what they thought about posting on politics on social media and to explain the reluctance to make comments. One of the main reasons they are reluctant to comment on politics is that it could lead to conflict: they are wary of disagreement, arguments and offending someone. This means that young people in general equate politics with conflict that is best avoided. Some said they thought that political conversations were better done face-to-face. Those who weren’t actively engaged in politics really wanted social media to be kept as just a social space for family and friends. However, some of our young participants were optimistic about engagement with politics through social media platforms. One young woman stated: I do think it is good. Many people my age have switched off the traditional media and it is rare to meet somebody who regularly watches the news or reads a newspaper. It is therefore important to spark their engagement in other ways. If they are actively reading, engaging and being informed by conversations on social media sites, then it creates a more informed public. We also asked them whether they thought politicians should use social media more. Most thought it was a good idea as politicians should be available to be asked questions publicly and needed to be responsive to people, demonstrating that they listened to their views. They thought it was a way that politicians could focus on sharing information and policy, especially for young people who weren’t watching the news. For example, one person said: I think it’s a good thing our society is moving online and politicians are usually older people and it’s good to seem making an effort to stay up to date with technology and younger people who popularly use social media. Some suggested that more interaction should become a normalised practice for politicians: I think politicians using Facebook or Twitter is a good thing, as it allows them to interact with a younger audience on a more regular basis. However, I think that politicians need to use these social networks better, for example, perhaps doing weekly question and answer posts submitted from users. While some scoffed, Kevin Rudd’s ‘shaving cut’ selfie suited social media’s more personal political culture. Facebook/Kevin Rudd, CC BY Many also thought it could help show politicians were normal people, especially if their messages were positive and genuine. In a discussion about Kevin Rudd’s use of a selfie after cutting himself shaving during the 2013 election campaign, one young man said: “I think it’s funny, and it shows that Kevin Rudd has a humorous side to him and can be taken as not just a politician who’s always serious.” But some were concerned that politicians wouldn’t be authentic and that their staff would write the messages, not them. Part of authentic engagement was about being positive, not mired in adversarial and partisan conflict. For example, one woman said that politicians needed to “post relevant pieces of information that are interesting to people, without tearing to shreds the opposition”. In the end, this is the difficult challenge for politics and politicians. Politics is about contestations over power, resources and decision-making. Debate and conflict are core. Young people’s everyday social media use is often about positive affirmation, liking posts and engaging on issues that matter to them. The reconciliation of these two spaces is increasingly important if we want to reverse democratic disengagement. It will require a new form of interaction that cannot be politics as usual. Facebook Social media Kevin Rudd John Faulkner Democracy Party politics Labor Party Political reform Civic engagement Political participation Democracy Futures: Networked Politics Ariadne Vromen, University of Sydney Author Ariadne Vromen Associate Professor, University of Sydney Disclosure statement Ariadne Vromen received funding from The Spencer Foundation (USA) for the project The Civic Network, undertaken with Michael Xenos and Brian Loader. Partners University of Sydney provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.7xku3fxmj","title":"Politics as usual? Ailing parties fail to get to grips with social media","url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-as-usual-ailing-parties-fail-to-get-to-grips-with-social-media-32661","id":311},{"content_hash":"57aca744f4ae9fa6d7e096d770b3e2cfca8acbbd9015519fa01e8daffb34d948","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:29:00.171275+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/market-decode-how-bonds-work-and-what-they-can-do-for-you.html","language":"","text":"Market Decode: How bonds work — and what they can do for you Watch this video to get the basics on this key ingredient in a well-diversified portfolio Transcript GENERALLY CONSIDERED THE MORE BORING, conservative part of an investor’s portfolio, bonds typically don’t get as much press as stocks do. And because they function differently from stocks and come in so many different flavors — Treasurys, municipals, corporate, high yield, etc. — they can be confusing. In the video above, Matthew Diczok, managing director, head of Fixed Income Strategy for the Chief Investment Office at Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank, offers a clear, simple explanation of how bonds work and why they could be considered an important part of an investor's strategy. A well-diversified portfolio should include a mix of stocks, bonds and cash. A well-diversified portfolio should include a mix of stocks, bonds and cash (the three major asset classes). The amount of each you hold depends on your financial goals, risk tolerance, time horizon and liquidity (or cash) needs. When it comes to bonds (also referred to as fixed income), there’s a general rule of thumb: The more conservative you are as an investor, the higher proportion of bonds you may want in your portfolio relative to stocks (also known as equities). If you’re willing to accept a greater amount of risk — and have a longer time horizon to reach your investment goals — you may be more comfortable with stocks than with bonds. Watch our video and then check out our slideshow below to find what might be an appropriate asset allocation based on the type of investor you are. And be sure to speak with your financial advisor about any adjustments you might want to make to your long-term financial strategy. Show text version Hide text version What kind of investor are you?: Conservative A sample asset allocation for a conservative investor is 24% in stocks, 71% in bonds and 5% in cash. Source: Chief Investment Office, March 2025. The strategic allocations shown here are designed as guidelines for a 25-year investment horizon for investors with low tax sensitivity. Show text version Hide text version What kind of investor are you?: Moderately conservative A sample asset allocation for a moderately conservative investor is 40% in stocks, 59% in bonds and 1% in cash. Source: Chief Investment Office, March 2025. The strategic allocations shown here are designed as guidelines for a 25-year investment horizon for investors with low tax sensitivity. Show text version Hide text version What kind of investor are you?: Moderate A sample asset allocation for a moderate investor is 58% in stocks, 41% in bonds and 1% in cash. Source: Chief Investment Office, March 2025. The strategic allocations shown here are designed as guidelines for a 25-year investment horizon for investors with low tax sensitivity. Show text version Hide text version What kind of investor are you?: Moderately aggressive A sample asset allocation for a moderately aggressive investor is 75% in stocks, 24% in bonds and 1% in cash. Source: Chief Investment Office, March 2025. The strategic allocations shown here are designed as guidelines for a 25-year investment horizon for investors with low tax sensitivity. Show text version Hide text version What kind of investor are you?: Aggressive A sample asset allocation for an aggressive investor is 91% in stocks, 8% in bonds and 1% in cash. Source: Chief Investment Office, March 2025. The strategic allocations shown here are designed as guidelines for a 25-year investment horizon for investors with low tax sensitivity. 1 of 5 Note: These allocations may be subject to change based on periodic review and are for illustrative purposes only. Asset allocation cannot eliminate the risk of fluctuating prices and uncertain returns. Choose your advisor in a more personalized way All our advisors are committed to putting your needs and priorities first. Find some who match your personal preferences too. Loading... Try Advisor Match Answer a few questions Want us to contact you? Submit a request Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor Important Disclosures Investing involves risk, including the possible loss of principal. Past performance is no guarantee of future results. This material does not take into account a client’s particular investment objectives, financial situations or needs and is not intended as a recommendation, offer or solicitation for the purchase or sale of any security or investment strategy. Merrill offers a broad range of brokerage, investment advisory (including financial planning) and other services. There are important differences between brokerage and investment advisory services, including the type of advice and assistance provided, the fees charged, and the rights and obligations of the parties. It is important to understand the differences, particularly when determining which service or services to select. For more information about these services and their differences, speak with your Merrill financial advisor. Bank of America, Merrill, their affiliates, and advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. Clients should consult their legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. This information should not be construed as investment advice and is subject to change. It is provided for informational purposes only and is not intended to be either a specific offer by Bank of America, Merrill or any affiliate to sell or provide, or a specific invitation for a consumer to apply for, any particular retail financial product or service that may be available. The Chief Investment Office (CIO) provides thought leadership on wealth management, investment strategy and global markets; portfolio management solutions; due diligence; and solutions oversight and data analytics. CIO viewpoints are developed for Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., (“Bank of America”) and Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated (“MLPF&S” or “Merrill”), a registered broker-dealer, registered investment adviser and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). All recommendations must be considered in the context of an individual investor’s goals, time horizon, liquidity needs and risk tolerance. Not all recommendations will be in the best interest of all investors. Asset allocation, diversification and rebalancing do not ensure a profit or protect against loss in declining markets. Investments have varying degrees of risk. Some of the risks involved with equity securities include the possibility that the value of the stocks may fluctuate in response to events specific to the companies or markets, as well as economic, political or social events in the U.S. or abroad. Bonds are subject to interest rate, inflation and credit risks. Treasury bills are less volatile than longer-term fixed income securities and are guaranteed as to timely payment of principal and interest by the U.S. government. Investments in foreign securities (including ADRs) involve special risks, including foreign currency risk and the possibility of substantial volatility due to adverse political, economic or other developments. These risks are magnified for investments made in emerging markets. Investments in a certain industry or sector may pose additional risk due to lack of diversification and sector concentration. Investments in real estate securities can be subject to fluctuations in the value of the underlying properties, the effect of economic conditions on real estate values, changes in interest rates, and risk related to renting properties, such as rental defaults. There are special risks associated with an investment in commodities, including market price fluctuations, regulatory changes, interest rate changes, credit risk, economic changes and the impact of adverse political or financial factors. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you Risk tolerance: What is it and how can I measure it? Connect Find out how our advisors can help Explore Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related articles Investing for income — not just growth Read more What dividend stocks can offer Read more How much is too much cash in your portfolio? Read more","title":"Market Decode: How bonds work — and what they can do for you","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/market-decode-how-bonds-work-and-what-they-can-do-for-you.html","id":312},{"content_hash":"daea0bbaca8bc76b20c0718e9f4f48cb88290af15c9f28bff21fcb1656c043b7","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:59.850809+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/adv/appointmentnotices/article-stefanos-tsamousis/","language":"","text":"Stefanos Tsamousis, President and CEO of Roche Canada Pharma, Roche CanadaSUPPLIEDShareRoche Canada is pleased to announce the appointment of Stefanos Tsamousis as President and CEO of Roche Canada Pharma, effective September 1, 2026. He succeeds Interim President and CEO Daniel Edgcumbe following Brigitte Nolet’s departure earlier this year.A seasoned global leader with nearly three decades of healthcare experience, Stefanos brings a formidable background in biotechnology, strategic marketing, and complex regulatory landscapes. His distinguished career with Roche spans executive leadership roles across Europe and at global headquarters in Basel, Switzerland - most recently serving as General Manager of Roche Italy, following successful tenures leading operations in Spain and Sweden, as well as serving as Commercial Director Europe.In his new role, Stefanos will lead the over 1,600 Canadian employees from Roche’s Mississauga campus - a vibrant, cross-functional hub and AI Centre of Excellence - driving the next chapter of healthcare innovation, growth, and patient impact across Canada.About Roche CanadaRoche Canada is a healthcare company that unites leading science and technology across diagnostics, medicines and digital solutions to prevent, stop and cure diseases. We focus on areas including oncology, neurology, cardiovascular and metabolic diseases, ophthalmology, infectious diseases and immunology to provide real and positive change for patients, the people they love, and the professionals who care for them.Roche was founded in Basel, Switzerland in 1896 and began its journey in Canada in 1931. Today, Roche Canada employs nearly 2,000 people at its offices in Mississauga, Ontario, in Laval, Quebec, and across the country.For more information, please visit www.RocheCanada.com or follow Roche Canada on LinkedIn.Open this photo in gallery:SUPPLIED","title":"Stefanos Tsamousis","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/adv/appointmentnotices/article-stefanos-tsamousis/","id":313},{"content_hash":"ceb1c76921a4618e03b7bb49f63c903b32210dd17d88bde0d252b57b0ee376aa","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:59.199532+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/a-propos-du-remembrement-de-sahara_2072000_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives A propos du remembrement de Sahara Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"A propos du remembrement de Sahara","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/a-propos-du-remembrement-de-sahara_2072000_1819218.html","id":314},{"content_hash":"dc8e70f7e4b2a092a1d23202385a4ae0381ea6d017dc5adfb1d55e0f0095ab71","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:59.124494+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-cooking-oils-to-eat-and-avoid-31346","language":"","text":"Health Check: cooking oils to eat and avoid Published: October 12, 2014 11:59pm EDT Try to avoid too many saturated fatty acids by choosing oils that are liquid at room temperature. Marjan Lazarevski/Flickr, CC BY-ND https://theconversation.com/health-check-cooking-oils-to-eat-and-avoid-31346 https://theconversation.com/health-check-cooking-oils-to-eat-and-avoid-31346 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Health conscious consumers are increasingly ditching old favourites vegetable and canola oil for trendy alternatives like coconut and peanut oil. But are they any healthier? And how do they compare with other options such as heated olive oil and butter? The short answer is, it depends. The long version requires a quick lesson in food science. Fats and oils = lipids Edible fats and oils are all part of the lipid family. Fats, such as butter, are solid at room temperature and oils are liquid; their solidity depends on their chemical makeup. Both are comprised of triglycerides, which have a glycerol backbone and three fatty acids attached. The fatty acids are the important part of the molecule and can be of different lengths and have different numbers of double bonds. They are organised into three groups: Saturated fats are mainly derived from animal products such as meat and dairy. They have no double bonds. Monounsaturated fats are found in olive oil, avocados and macadamia nuts.They have one double bond. Polyunsaturated fats come from corn, seed and nut oils (omega 6) and seafood (omega 3). They have two or more double bonds. Fats and oils always include a range of saturated and unsaturated fatty acids. The greater the proportion of saturated fatty acids, the more solid it will be at room temperature. Why we need fats and oils Once digested, we use the fatty acids to maintain the function of our body’s cells and cell membranes, in hormones and in neurotransmitters. They’re also important for our skin, hair and nails, and they keep us warm and cushion our internal organs. Fats and oils are used for energy production and provide the most energy per gram of all the macronutrients (fat 37kJ/g; carbohydrate 16kJ/g; and protein 17kJ/g). Use sparingly: butter is a saturated fat. Taryn/Flickr, CC BY-SA But it’s important to balance the amount of fat we eat and the energy we expend in order to prevent obesity. Type of fat eaten is also important: saturated fats have been associated with chronic illnesses such as heart disease monounsaturated fats appear reasonably neutral polyunsaturated fats are recognised as protective. Therefore, the greater the proportion of mono and polyunsaturated fatty acids contained in a particular fat or oil, the healthier it is. Cooking with fats and oils The longer the fatty acid chain and the fewer the double bonds, the hotter the fat can be heated without it starting to breakdown – or in scientific terms, be oxidised. The temperature at which this oxidation occurs is called the smoke point and it is associated with unpleasant odours and flavours. To fry food you need a high temperature and therefore a high smoke point, so saturated fats with long chain lengths work best. Tallow (rendered beef fat) or ghee (rendered butter) are often used for frying, but these are saturated fats, so they’re among the least healthy. Butter is also a saturated fat, but has other components as well. The dairy proteins, relatively high water content and short chain fatty acids mean butter is great for browning food, but not for frying as it starts to splatter when heated to a high temperature. Vegetable oil is a term used for any (non animal-based) oil from vegetable or seed origin. Or it can be a blend of these oils. It is mainly polyunsaturated fats of different chain lengths, so it’s one of the healthier options. Canola oil, which was developed from rapeseed, was specifically developed for frying as it contains predominantly longer chain monounsaturated fatty acids and has a relatively high smoke point. Canola oil is harvested from rapeseed. rytc/Flickr, CC BY-NC-ND Peanut oil (from peanuts) is mainly long chain omega 6 (polyunsaturated) fatty acids. It has a high smoke point and is also good for frying. Olive oil is predominantly monounsaturated fatty acids, but has the added benefit of polyphenolic compounds which act as antioxidants and contribute to the health qualities of this oil. Olive oil does not have a high smoke point, so should only be used for low-temperature cooking. Cold pressed olive oil is the best choice, as it is not heated or processed by chemicals in the extraction of the oil from the olive. Olive oil is easily oxidised so should be stored in a dark place in a coloured bottle. Fats and oils are also used in baking, adding taste and texture to cakes, biscuits and pastry. The best fat for this role is saturated fat. Currently there is no other mono or polyunsaturated fats that can completely replace the role of saturated fats in baking, and this is one of the reasons we suggest eating these foods only occasionally. New kids on the block Coconut oil has 85-90% saturated fatty acids. It has traditionally been used in curries, but its recent popularity with health conscious consumers has seen it added to all kinds of foods from muesli to smoothies. The predominant fatty acid in coconut oil is Lauric acid. Its shorter chain length is thought to be why coconut oil may not have the same effects on LDL (bad) cholesterol as other saturated fatty acids. But there is little evidence for any health benefits of Lauric acid at this point. To be prudent, it is best to use coconut oil occasionally as part of a healthy eating plan. Adding coconut oil to everything won’t make it healthier. Meal Makeover Moms/Flickr, CC BY-ND Flaxseed oil (also known as linseed oil) is claimed to have similar health benefits to marine omega 3 fatty acids. Flaxseed oil contains a high proportion of α-Linolenic acid (ALA), which in theory can be converted to omega-3 fatty acids by the body. But we are not efficient at doing this and there is little evidence that flaxseed oil has the same protective effects as the omega 3 fatty acids from fish oil. Flaxseed oil degrades rapidly when heated or exposed to sunlight and should be stored in a dark place in coloured glass. It is mostly used on salads and as an addition to cold dishes and drinks, but should not replace the two fish meals a week suggested by the National Heart Foundation. Fats and oils are important in our diet, but should be used carefully and as part of a healthy eating plan. Try to avoid too many saturated fatty acids by choosing oils that are liquid at room temperature. Food and nutrition Cooking Cooking oil Saturated fat Health Check Lesley MacDonald-Wicks, Amanda Patterson, University of Newcastle Authors Lesley MacDonald-Wicks Senior Lecturer , University of Newcastle Amanda Patterson Senior Lecturer in the School of Health Sciences, University of Newcastle Disclosure statement Lesley MacDonald-Wicks received funding for her PhD research from 1999 to 2002 from Meadow Lea and GRDC. Amanda Patterson does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Newcastle provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners Languages Bahasa Indonesia English DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.quk7nrgtv","title":"Health Check: cooking oils to eat and avoid","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-cooking-oils-to-eat-and-avoid-31346","id":315},{"content_hash":"c70791328361cb1759e4c5da603f6cf9578498363d2afc092be440d896a66a33","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:59.018470+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202608/14/WS6a7f2e8ea31073853ec536a3.html","language":"","text":"Makeover of county shows holistic care - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Global EditionASIA 中文双语Français HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE China News Society Innovation HK/Macao Cross-Strait Cover Story Photo Environment Health Military Video Home / China / Latest Home China Latest Makeover of county shows holistic care Xi's sustained support for ecology reflects governing philosophy of putting people first By XU WEI in Changting county, Fujian | chinadaily.com.cn | Updated: 2026-08-14 23:04 Share Share - WeChat CLOSE A bird's-eye view of the Tingjiang River National Wetland Park in Changting county, Fujian province. [Photo by QIU JIARUI/FOR CHINA DAILY] When Ma Xuemei first decided to green the barren hills of Changting county in Fujian province more than two decades ago, few people believed anything could grow there. The slopes were bare and the soil was crimson and compacted. Under the scorching summer sun, the exposed ground could reach temperatures of 60 C to 70 C. Ma was among the first local farmers to contract a section of the mountain, hoping to restore it. What awaited her was not fertile land, but a landscape where every heavy rain erased months of hard work. \"The mountain looked lifeless. People said I was wasting my time,\" she recalled. As one of the regions in southern China hardest hit by soil erosion, the county had watched decades of deforestation strip its hills bare, leaving farmland degraded, rivers choked with sediment and many rural communities living in poverty. It was against this backdrop that Xi Jinping made soil erosion control in Changting a long-term priority. During his tenure as a provincial leader in Fujian, he made five fact-finding trips to the county, consistently pressing for stronger efforts to restore its fragile ecology and improve local livelihoods. In November 1999, while inspecting conservation efforts in Changting, Xi, then serving as the acting governor of Fujian, described soil erosion as a stubborn challenge requiring sustained commitment. Under his leadership, the restoration project was incorporated into one of Fujian's major livelihood initiatives, bringing unprecedented financial support and policy backing. An annual allocation of 10 million yuan ($1.5 million) was provided by the provincial government, up from the previous allocation of 800,000 yuan. \"The level of support exceeded anything we had imagined,\" said Zhong Binglin, a former official responsible for soil and water conservation in Changting. Xi urged local authorities to continue tilting resources toward Changting, \"even if we have to lean until our waists bend\", he said, recalling a remark that has stayed with him ever since. In terms of political commitment, sustained support, funding and effective implementation, the support Xi extended to Changting set a precedent for nationwide soil and water conservation and ecological development, Zhong added. Xi's attention to the county continued in the years that followed. During another inspection in October 2001, he returned to review conservation work, and watered a camphor tree that had been planted the previous year with a donation he had made to support the construction of an ecological park. Later that month, in a written instruction, Xi called for another eight years of sustained efforts to fundamentally solve Changting's soil erosion problem, and urged that soil and water conservation be incorporated into local economic development planning. More than a decade later, in 2012, Xi, then China's vice-president, described the county's restoration as having reached \"a critical juncture where to move forward is to prevail; to stand still is to fall behind\", calling for greater policy support from authorities. \"At every stage, Xi has consistently shown strong care and support, enabling Changting to make steady progress in soil erosion control,\" said Yue Hui, director of Changting's soil and water conservation center. Zhong, the former official, said Xi's sustained commitment reflected a governing philosophy that places people's well-being at the center of policymaking. \"Soil erosion wasn't simply an environmental problem,\" Zhong said. \"It affected virtually every rural household in Changting. It threatened farmland, livelihoods and, ultimately, people's ability to survive. That's why Xi attached such importance to tackling it.\" By 1985, soil erosion had affected 97,467 hectares of land, accounting for nearly one-third of the Changting's total area, and the county faced a crisis that extended far beyond its barren hillsides. \"As the topsoil was washed away, the land became increasingly compacted and infertile, making it difficult for vegetation to grow. Sediment carried into rivers raised riverbeds, increasing the risk of flooding and threatening farmland downstream,\" said Yue, the conservation center's director. The challenge was compounded by the county's geology. Changting's granite-derived red soil is naturally loose and highly vulnerable to erosion. Poor in organic matter and unable to retain water or nutrients, it offered little resistance to heavy rainfall once the thin layer of fertile topsoil had disappeared. For local farmer Ma, restoring the section of the mountain she had contracted became a lesson in persistence. After every heavy downpour, it was another climb up the mountain to assess the damage. Entire sections of newly planted slopes could disappear in a single rainstorm. \"There were times when I felt completely hopeless. Seeing the seedlings either washed away or scorched by the heat was heartbreaking,\" she recalled. The turning point came after years of experimentation. Guided by soil and water conservation experts, Ma abandoned the conventional approach of planting trees first, and instead covered the barren slopes with Bahia grass. The fast-growing, heat- and drought-tolerant grass proved remarkably helpful. Its dense root system bound the loose soil together, while its broad cover shielded the ground from direct sunlight, lowering surface temperatures and slowing rainwater runoff. As the grass gradually improved the soil quality, shrubs began to take root, followed by trees. Year after year, the once-barren hills slowly turned green. \"I've watched this mountain change little by little. I devoted much of my life to it, and it never let me down,\" Ma said. Today, the hillsides Ma once struggled to stabilize are covered with orchards producing chestnuts, oranges and other fruits, creating new sources of income for local families. Jin Lian, a resident of Sanzhou township in the county, said that as life was \"really hard back then\", people left home to find work elsewhere. \"Now, many are coming back because there are jobs here,\" she added. The ecological recovery has also been recorded in another way — through the growing number of winged visitors. Before dawn each day, photographer Liu Hui quietly scans Changting's wetlands using a spotting scope. \"If the environment is good, the birds know first,\" he said. \"They choose places where there's enough food, suitable habitat and clean water.\" When Liu began photographing birds in 2016, he would spot only a handful of northern lapwings. Today, flocks of more than 300 arrive each year. Changting has also recorded 345 bird species, around 85 more than when the county published its first bird atlas in 2019. For him, every newly recorded species tells the same story that the ecosystem is taking flight. \"Birds are among the best indicators of ecosystem health.They settle wherever the climate suits them best and food is plentiful,\" he noted. Standing beneath the shade of mature trees that did not exist a generation ago, Ma, the farmer, looked toward the mountain where she once watched rain wash away months of hard work within hours. \"We grew grass and planted trees because we wanted the mountain to live again,\" she said. \"Now it feels as if the mountain has breathed life back into us.\" xuwei@chinadaily.com.cn Photo National Ecology Day: Charting China's ecological progress In Xinjiang, chili peppers bring vivid color at harvest time Purple verbena blooms in tiered swathes on mountain slopes in Hubei Changchun agricultural expo opens with 1,800 enterprises Shoton Festival opens in Lhasa with giant thangka unveiling Sichuan panda center livestreams birthing season enrichment event Latest Mountains, rivers, forests, fields, lakes, grasslands and deserts National Ecology Day: Charting China's ecological progress Public security ministry details action on environmental crimes Typhoon Dolphin downpour threatens Henan harvest China's postal bureau reports jump in mail revenue Special teams drive post-disaster relief State Council News Xi Focus: Charting a green path to development Xi Story: The rain, the caves and the quest to preserve Mogao Grottoes Xi extends condolences to Colombian president over deadly earthquake Exclusive National Ecology Day: Charting China's ecological progress Taiwan investor helps cross-Strait startups thrive in Beijing Taiwan youth putting down roots in Daliang Mountains Newsmaker Space industry pioneer Wang Xiji dies at 105 Traditional Miao musical instrument finds new life Special Coverage 105th founding anniv. of the CPC 75th anniv. of Xizang's peaceful liberation Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"Makeover of county shows holistic care","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202608/14/WS6a7f2e8ea31073853ec536a3.html","id":316},{"content_hash":"d994c53ab854e89fa9c123e6ec0076b2c3f89f43392cc5cc5f744dca2dcde660","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.827260+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/customer-credit-check","language":"","text":"What is Customer Credit Check ByLola, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuApril 5, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Is A Customer Credit Check?Why A Customer Credit Check Is Important For BusinessesHow Are Customer Credit Checks Done?Share this postAlthough it may be perceived as relatively new, customer credit checks are essential measures for businesses in order to make informed decisions regarding customers. The cluster of words in the term gives a vague insight into what the process entails already. it’s about understanding a person’s credit score and financial reliability before extending any credit or service. A customer credit check is an important evaluation process. Reading further in this article will leave you better informed about customer credit checks and why it is truly important. But of course, a better explanation and definition of customer credit checks will be a better consequent section for this article. including how to check your credit score and interpret your credit report effectively. What Is A Customer Credit Check? This refers to a process that entails evaluating the creditworthiness of a person or business by reviewing the person's credit score. This process is undertaken to ensure that a customer will be able to repay any debt or credit they may be seeking from the company. The process involves a request for permission to review the person's credit report or that of a business from credit reporting agencies. The credit report provided will then provide information about the customer's or business's history and current credit balances. All of which shape the final credit score. With the information obtained, an informed decision is possible. The company decides whether to approve a loan, a credit card, or any other form of credit. A customer credit check is a constituent of the KYC process, which is of the customer onboarding process as well. There are two types of credit checks: 1. Soft CreditCheck This is a credit inquiry that does not affect the borrower's credit score. It is used by lenders for preapproval or prequalification purposes. It can also be used by landlords to verify a borrower's creditworthiness. Soft credit checks typically involve a review of a borrower's credit report without any deep analysis of information. 2. Hard Credit Check: A hard credit check, often referred to as a hard inquiry, is a credit inquiry that has the potential to lower a borrower's credit score. When a lender makes a credit determination, such as approving an application for a loan or credit card, it conducts a hard credit check. Before accepting services that demand recurring payments, other organizations like landlords, utility providers, or cell phone carriers might also run hard credit checks on potential customers. A borrower's credit report contains information on hard credit checks, and having several in a short period of time can harm their credit score. which is why many people now explore how to check their credit score for free before applying for credit. Why A Customer Credit Check Is Important For Businesses Customer Credit Checks are not only utilized by credit card companies or loan companies, or any other financial-related institution. It can also be utilized by other types of corporate companies and businesses that sell or provide services for payment. In order to prevent cash flow issues, customers can be assessed in order to determine whether they will default in payments or not. Asides from that, customer credit checks can also inform sales strategies. Some of the importance of a customer credit check for efficacy in business include; 1. Customer Credit Checks Help Mitigate Risks: It not only mitigates risks but identifies them; this is how risks are then mitigated because they have been initially mitigated. This means that risks are reduced. When credit checks are conducted, it reduces the risks of bad debts and non-payment. Credit checks enable a business to assess a customer's ability to pay back their debts and to pay for services as well. This way, businesses that offer credit can make informed decisions on whether to extend credit or not. 2. Customer Credit Checks Helps Businesses Make Better Decisions: Because credit reports are undertaken, businesses are able to gain valuable insights into a potential customer's behaviour. These include payment history and outstanding debts. Access to this sort of valuable information will help businesses make informed decisions about extending credit and setting credit limits. 3. Improves Customer Relationships: Customer credit checks will help businesses build trust with customers. In a case where credit reports come back with positive numerical, a company is able to see that the potential customer takes their financial obligations seriously. Companies can demonstrate a commitment to responsible lending practices; this will put a business in a positive light and therefore attract more customers. 4. Protects Cash Flow: Accumulating debts can have major negative impacts on a company's cash flow. By conducting credit checks, businesses can avoid extending credit to high-risk customers and ensure that customers do not default on payment. 5. Resources Can Be Effectively Managed: With credit checks; businesses can allocate resources more effectively. When high-risk customers are identified, they may require additional attention and monitoring. It can also help businesses avoid wasting time and resources on customers who may likely flaunt payments. 6. Targeting Creditworthy Customers: Credit Customer credit checks enable businesses to identify customers with solid credit histories and low defaulting on payments. This means they are more likely to be financially stable and have more resources to make larger purchases or engage in long-term relationships with businesses. Information obtained from customer credit checks can be useful to sales and marketing teams to target these beneficial potential customers and tailor their messaging and promotions to appeal to their needs and preferences. 7. Compliance: Certain industries or governments require businesses to perform credit checks on customers before extending credit to them. Failure to comply with this regulation attracts fines and legal issues. 8. Identifying Fraud: Customer credit checks help identify potential fraudsters or customers that may attempt to obtain credit with false information. This helps businesses avoid financial losses and maintain a solid reputation. How Are Customer Credit Checks Done? Customer Credit checks can be done by businesses, banks, and other financial institutions or entities to assess the creditworthiness of individuals or companies before extending credit to them or doing business with them. Customer Credit Checks involve multiple steps, which include; a. Obtaining the customer's personal information: Which includes the customer's full name, address, date of birth, driver's license, passport, and national id cards. b. Checking credit history: In Kenya, credit history is checked through the Credit Reference Bureau (CRB) system. The CRB system is a centralized database that collects and maintains credit information on individuals and businesses from various lenders, such as banks, microfinance institutions, and mobile lending platforms. c. Reviewing financial information: The customer's income, employment history, and other financial information may be requested to assess their ability to repay credit. d. Analyzing credit scores: Credit scores are generated based on the customer's credit history, and lenders typically use this information to determine the likelihood of default. e. Making a decision: Based on the information obtained, businesses can now make decisions that are beneficial for the company, taking into account many factors like cash flow. Bottom Line Customer credit checks are a substantial part of the KYC process and also seek to protect companies from customers who will not have favourable commercial relationships with them. It will therefore help companies flourish and grow while avoiding problems with cash flow. With the Youverify automated OS KYC processes can be done much more faster and efficiently. Book a demo session today to see how Youverify can help automate your customer credit check for a more efficient and cost-effective process. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What is Customer Credit Check","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/customer-credit-check","id":317},{"content_hash":"00481d8cbc854a19c64bfff2f8556d4369aa07244b2742dbe14eb71ab126e134","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.467192+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/files/storage/","language":"","text":"File storage API¶ Getting the default storage class¶ Django provides convenient ways to access the default storage class: storages¶ A dictionary-like object that allows retrieving a storage instance using its alias as defined by STORAGES. storages has an attribute backends, which defaults to the raw value provided in STORAGES. Additionally, storages provides a create_storage() method that accepts the dictionary used in STORAGES for a backend, and returns a storage instance based on that backend definition. This may be useful for third-party packages needing to instantiate storages in tests: >>> from django.core.files.storage import storages >>> storages.backends {'default': {'BACKEND': 'django.core.files.storage.FileSystemStorage'}, 'staticfiles': {'BACKEND': 'django.contrib.staticfiles.storage.StaticFilesStorage'}, 'custom': {'BACKEND': 'package.storage.CustomStorage'}} >>> storage_instance = storages.create_storage({\"BACKEND\": \"package.storage.CustomStorage\"}) class DefaultStorage[source]¶ DefaultStorage provides lazy access to the default storage system as defined by default key in STORAGES. DefaultStorage uses storages internally. default_storage¶ default_storage is an instance of the DefaultStorage. The FileSystemStorage class¶ class FileSystemStorage(location=None, base_url=None, file_permissions_mode=None, directory_permissions_mode=None, allow_overwrite=False)[source]¶ The FileSystemStorage class implements basic file storage on a local filesystem. It inherits from Storage and provides implementations for all the public methods thereof. Note The FileSystemStorage.delete() method will not raise an exception if the given file name does not exist. location[source]¶ Absolute path to the directory that will hold the files. Defaults to the value of your MEDIA_ROOT setting. base_url[source]¶ URL that serves the files stored at this location. Defaults to the value of your MEDIA_URL setting. file_permissions_mode[source]¶ The file system permissions that the file will receive when it is saved. Defaults to FILE_UPLOAD_PERMISSIONS. directory_permissions_mode[source]¶ The file system permissions that the directory will receive when it is saved. Defaults to FILE_UPLOAD_DIRECTORY_PERMISSIONS. allow_overwrite¶ Flag to control allowing saving a new file over an existing one. Defaults to False. get_created_time(name)[source]¶ Returns a datetime of the system’s ctime, i.e. os.path.getctime(). On some systems (like Unix), this is the time of the last metadata change, and on others (like Windows), it’s the creation time of the file. The InMemoryStorage class¶ class InMemoryStorage(location=None, base_url=None, file_permissions_mode=None, directory_permissions_mode=None)[source]¶ The InMemoryStorage class implements a memory-based file storage. It has no persistence, but can be useful for speeding up tests by avoiding disk access. location[source]¶ Absolute path to the directory name assigned to files. Defaults to the value of your MEDIA_ROOT setting. base_url[source]¶ URL that serves the files stored at this location. Defaults to the value of your MEDIA_URL setting. file_permissions_mode[source]¶ The file system permissions assigned to files, provided for compatibility with FileSystemStorage. Defaults to FILE_UPLOAD_PERMISSIONS. directory_permissions_mode[source]¶ The file system permissions assigned to directories, provided for compatibility with FileSystemStorage. Defaults to FILE_UPLOAD_DIRECTORY_PERMISSIONS. The Storage class¶ class Storage[source]¶ The Storage class provides a standardized API for storing files, along with a set of default behaviors that all other storage systems can inherit or override as necessary. Note When methods return naive datetime objects, the effective timezone used will be the current value of os.environ['TZ']; note that this is usually set from Django’s TIME_ZONE. delete(name)[source]¶ Deletes the file referenced by name. If deletion is not supported on the target storage system this will raise NotImplementedError instead. exists(name)[source]¶ Returns True if a file referenced by the given name already exists in the storage system. get_accessed_time(name)[source]¶ Returns a datetime of the last accessed time of the file. For storage systems unable to return the last accessed time this will raise NotImplementedError. If USE_TZ is True, returns an aware datetime, otherwise returns a naive datetime in the local timezone. get_alternative_name(file_root, file_ext)[source]¶ Returns an alternative filename based on the file_root and file_ext parameters, an underscore plus a random 7 character alphanumeric string is appended to the filename before the extension. get_available_name(name, max_length=None)[source]¶ Returns a filename based on the name parameter that’s free and available for new content to be written to on the target storage system. The length of the filename will not exceed max_length, if provided. If a free unique filename cannot be found, a SuspiciousFileOperation exception will be raised. If a file with name already exists, get_alternative_name() is called to obtain an alternative name. get_created_time(name)[source]¶ Returns a datetime of the creation time of the file. For storage systems unable to return the creation time this will raise NotImplementedError. If USE_TZ is True, returns an aware datetime, otherwise returns a naive datetime in the local timezone. get_modified_time(name)[source]¶ Returns a datetime of the last modified time of the file. For storage systems unable to return the last modified time this will raise NotImplementedError. If USE_TZ is True, returns an aware datetime, otherwise returns a naive datetime in the local timezone. get_valid_name(name)[source]¶ Returns a filename based on the name parameter that’s suitable for use on the target storage system. generate_filename(filename)[source]¶ Validates the filename by calling get_valid_name and returns a filename to be passed to the save() method. The filename argument may include a path as returned by FileField.upload_to. In that case, the path won’t be passed to get_valid_name but will be prepended back to the resulting name. The default implementation uses os.path operations. Override this method if that’s not appropriate for your storage. listdir(path)[source]¶ Lists the contents of the specified path, returning a 2-tuple of lists; the first item being directories, the second item being files. For storage systems that aren’t able to provide such a listing, this will raise a NotImplementedError instead. open(name, mode='rb')[source]¶ Opens the file given by name. Note that although the returned file is guaranteed to be a File object, it might actually be some subclass. In the case of remote file storage this means that reading/writing could be quite slow, so be warned. path(name)[source]¶ The local filesystem path where the file can be opened using Python’s standard open(). For storage systems that aren’t accessible from the local filesystem, this will raise NotImplementedError instead. save(name, content, max_length=None)[source]¶ Saves a new file using the storage system, preferably with the name specified. If there already exists a file with this name name, the storage system may modify the filename as necessary to get a unique name. The actual name of the stored file will be returned. The max_length argument is passed along to get_available_name(). The content argument must be an instance of django.core.files.File or a file-like object that can be wrapped in File. size(name)[source]¶ Returns the total size, in bytes, of the file referenced by name. For storage systems that aren’t able to return the file size this will raise NotImplementedError instead. url(name)[source]¶ Returns the URL where the contents of the file referenced by name can be accessed. For storage systems that don’t support access by URL this will raise NotImplementedError instead. There are community-maintained solutions too! Django has a vibrant ecosystem. There are storage backends highlighted on the Community Ecosystem page. The Django Packages Storage Backends grid has even more options for you!","title":"File storage API¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/files/storage/","id":318},{"content_hash":"6aa1867cb5b6bd28df7e57f7b914af56fefbd53671c0e3bb8699fbb811524627","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.394343+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-06-24/oliver-curtis-stockbroker-insider-trading-court-jailed/7540000","language":"","text":"Sydney stockbroker Oliver Curtis jailed for insider trading conspiracyBy Dom VukovicTopic:CourtsPosted Fri 24 Jun 2016 at 10:22amFri 24 Jun 2016 at 10:22amFri 24 Jun 2016 at 10:22am, updated Fri 24 Jun 2016 at 5:19pmFri 24 Jun 2016 at 5:19pmFri 24 Jun 2016 at 5:19pmIt was accepted Curtis was not the insider when the illegal trades were made. (AAP: Dan Himbrechts)abc.net.au/news/oliver-curtis-stockbroker-insider-trading-court-jailed/7540000Link copiedShareShare articleSydney stockbroker Oliver Curtis has been jailed for two years after an insider trading scam hatched with his then best friend made them more than $1.4 million in illegal profits.Curtis, 30, was found guilty of conspiring to commit insider trading on 45 separate occasions, earlier this month.The trial heard how he shared in illegal profits from insider trading using confidential information between May 2007 and June 2008.Prosecutors had pushed for a five-year prison term, arguing his crimes were a \"form of cheating\".However, New South Wales Supreme Court Justice Lucy McCallum handed down a minimum term of one year, saying a prison sentence would provide \"real bite\" as a deterrent to white-collar crimes.Justice McCallum said white-collar crime was a serious offence and evidence in the case satisfied her that a custodial sentence was appropriate.The father of two then embraced his wife, PR executive Roxy Jacenko, before being led from the court by guards.'Best friends' hatched illegal plan to buy and sell sharesDuring the trial, the court heard Curtis received tip-offs from his former best friend, John Hartman.Prosecutors said Mr Hartman, who worked for Orion Asset Management, told Curtis when to buy and sell \"contracts for difference\" and at what price, using information not available to the public.At the time of the trades, they were sharing a $3,000-per-week Bondi apartment, allegedly funded by the proceeds of the transactions. Mr Hartman was jailed in 2010 and served a 15-month prison sentence for insider trading and related offences. Curtis's defence said Mr Hartman played a much larger role in the offending than he did, determining when it started and when it finished.In her decision, Justice McCallum said she accepted both men came up with the plan together.\"I am satisfied beyond reasonable doubt that Mr Curtis was an equal and willing participant in the discussions that resulted in the unlawful agreement,\" she said.\"Mr Curtis also provided the funds to begin the trading.\"Curtis 'knew source of information he received'But Justice McCallum also accepted Mr Hartman \"was the insider\" and there was \"no breach of trust\" by Curtis.\"His culpability [Curtis] is less on that account,\" Justice McCallum said.\"But it must be accepted, as submitted by the Crown, that he knew the source of the information he was to receive.\"The trial heard Mr Hartman and Curtis grew up in neighbouring homes in Mosman, on Sydney's north shore, and went to school together. Mr Hartman previously said the pair saw the offending as \"a bit of a game\" and a way to make money.Prosecutors had argued that insider trading was not a victimless crime and that Curtis had seen an opportunity and run with it.No remorse for actions, Judge saysUp until the guilty verdict, Justice McCallum said Curtis expressed \"no contrition to any degree whatsoever\" for his crimes.The father of two was supported by his wife Roxy Jacenko throughout the trial. (AAP: Dan Himbrechts)But after the verdict, Justice McCallum said that Curtis very quickly settled the case against him under the Proceeds of Crime Act, by agreeing to forfeit a sum of $1.43 million.\"That is the amount of profit the two men [Curtis and Hartman] made during their 45 trades,\" she said.\"The fact that Curtis has made reparation for the whole of the unlawful profit, including Mr Hartman's share, is relevant and is to be taken into account in favour of Mr Curtis.\"However Justice McCallum was not convinced that his decision to repay the money showed any contrition because it was made \"without admissions\" to the crimes.\"In that context the forfeiture of the sum of $1.43 million without admissions between verdict and sentence may be be regarded as cynical,\" she said.Nature of offences 'reduces weight of good character'Justice McCallum noted Curtis had no prior convictions and took into consideration a number of positive character references submitted to the court.Former colleagues and family friends described Curtis as the hardest worker, a role model, and a doting father and husband.But Justice McCallum said the bearing she could give to this evidence in her sentencing decision was limited.\"The fact that Mr Curtis's offending occurred over an extended period of time, involving many deliberate decisions... [this] reduces the weight that can be given to his good character,\" she said.The Australian Securities and Investments Commission (ASIC) said the punishments for white-collar crime should be more severe.\"I would actually make it harsher because they are informed,\" ASIC chairman Greg Medcraft said.\"But I think if we as a community could at least get to the level where they're treated equally, that would be a good start.\"Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesBanker convicted of conspiring to commit insider tradingTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeInvestment banker shared in $1.4m from insider trading deal, court hearsTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeRelated topicsBusiness, Economics and FinanceCourtsLaw, Crime and JusticeNSWSydneyTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Sydney stockbroker Oliver Curtis jailed for insider trading conspiracy","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-06-24/oliver-curtis-stockbroker-insider-trading-court-jailed/7540000","id":319},{"content_hash":"f96ec96472aabb982fa54a307bae2bdf2d36b8a9350c36cdc913ba81b95763e6","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.321525+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day-celebration-event","language":"","text":"International Women’s Day Celebration Event | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"International Women’s Day Celebration Event | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day-celebration-event","id":320},{"content_hash":"a3736e9b339017ccbc904207741397c652a0162a5caf43ea248dd374c4eb4681","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:58.011449+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-conseil-general-du-nord-vote-60-millions-pour-les-etablissements-et-facultes-libres_2071888_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives LE CONSEIL GENERAL DU NORD VOTE 60 MILLIONS POUR LES ÉTABLISSEMENTS ET FACULTÉS LIBRES Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"LE CONSEIL GENERAL DU NORD VOTE 60 MILLIONS POUR LES ÉTABLISSEMENTS ET FACULTÉS LIBRES","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-conseil-general-du-nord-vote-60-millions-pour-les-etablissements-et-facultes-libres_2071888_1819218.html","id":321},{"content_hash":"65cf1b13a532624f0d602d766375a78dfb35373a4d29c5380bd6ed6b0d9f304c","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.934863+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/asian-states-in-crisis-can-choose-more-democracy-or-more-conflict-31829","language":"","text":"Asian states in crisis can choose more democracy or more conflict Published: October 14, 2014 11:20pm EDT Thailand’s military coup in May is a sign of political malaise without an obvious cure in the absence of a new social contract. EPA/Pongmanat Tasiri https://theconversation.com/asian-states-in-crisis-can-choose-more-democracy-or-more-conflict-31829 https://theconversation.com/asian-states-in-crisis-can-choose-more-democracy-or-more-conflict-31829 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Rather than a new dawn for democracy, political and social reform in the region has led to less representation and more contestation. This has potentially far-reaching consequences. What does the May coup in Thailand have to do with the Bharatiya Janata Party’s landslide election victory in India? What does the recent liberalisation in Myanmar have to do with either? The connections are not immediately obvious. Yet close inspection reveals intriguing links between these events and others occurring throughout Asia. In a recent study, we argue that Asian states are caught in a cycle of instability based on their post-independence experience. Because the political, social and economic evolution of many Asian states followed similar patterns, over time the forces of economic and social change exerted similar stresses on them. These stresses have produced a pattern of crisis across the region. Growth exposes governing agenda’s flaws Post-independence Asian states worked to balance three objectives: constructing an identity, maintaining government control and building a functional economy. Many relied heavily on pre-colonial legacies and dominant languages to give an aura of permanence to their new borders. Although democracy prevailed at first, movement towards single-party dominance followed. This included periodic use of internal and external insecurities to tighten the dominant party’s grip on power. Government’s role in economic development grew steadily. Each of the three objectives – identity, control, economic growth – contained internal contradictions that could only be contained by the other two. Identity construction relied on increasingly heavy-handed rule and partisan economic frameworks to reconcile majority-minority relations. Strong governments relied on powerful appeals to nationalism and rapid economic growth to justify their lurch into authoritarianism. An assertion of distinctively Asian approaches to governance and development and the freedom of (semi-)authoritarian government to perform command-like economic functions underpinned development. Perhaps ironically, extraordinary economic growth upset the delicate triangular balance. From this emerged an unsettling mixture of political and social forces inequality, urbanisation, broadening education and awareness, and rising expectations. That led to two waves of political and social contestation across Asia. The first wave of change The first occurred between 1986 and 1998. The fall of two dictators, Philippines president Ferdinand Marcos and Indonesian president Suharto, book-ended this period. From 1986-98, in ten of the 19 states of east, south and southeast Asia, regimes that had held power for decades lost or ceded it to the opposition or consented to democratisation. Another three (in China, Malaysia and Burma) clung to power by forcibly repressing popular democracy movements. All these transitions reflected demands from the new middle classes for greater political representation. Ultimately, in ten countries the crisis ended with a procedural solution. Democratic processes were established (generally through constitutional rewrites) and single-party dominance was toppled. Almost 30 years after people power elevated Corazon Aquino to the Philippines presidency, Asia is again beset by mass protests. Wikimedia Commons/Malacañang Palace archives Of the many strands woven into these Asian people-power movements, perhaps most dominant was growing fatigue and dwindling self-confidence within the regimes as generational change approached and economic problems mounted. Leadership transitions within single-party regimes exposed the patronage and nepotism lurking beneath the procedural veneer. Some panicking ageing autocrats fatally undermined their legitimacy, as in the killing of Benigno Aquino in the Philippines and the Kwangju massacre in Korea. With few exceptions, however, reforms enacted between 1986 and 1998 produced poor-quality governments. Rather than full political representation, hybrid regimes emerged. These combined old-fashioned patronage with a widening of the channels for participation but with continuing constraints on political representation and accountability. As a result, in country after country, voter participation fell and popular frustration with the quality rather than the process of government grew. The second wave rolls on The result? A second wave of contestation began in 1999 and is still with us. Mass protests have returned. In some cases the new democratic politics have deepened social divisions, delegitimising any government for a large number of voters. In Thailand, the distributional politics and generous handouts of whichever party is elected have widened the urban-rural divide. Populations that long enjoyed the benefits of the developmental state have proved deeply reluctant to see others enjoy them. In Malaysia, minority groups have begun to repudiate the elite inter-ethnic compact underlining the ruling Barisan Nasional’s power and to vote for opposition parties. This has raised new ethnic and religious tensions. What is dangerous is that these dissatisfactions and confrontations can only mount. It is hard to see how competing claims can be resolved to general satisfaction. Circumventing established adjudication mechanisms, whether judicial or electoral, delegitimises the political processes for maintaining social integration. Thailand today illustrates the result: political malaise with little hope of a decisive solution. Where ethnic, religious or linguistic suspicion is even deeper, the results could be much more violent. Half a century after independence then, Asian countries are suffering a crisis of legitimacy. Challenged by new social demands and new political actors, most urgently need a new social contract. The stakes are high – but so are the opportunities. The question of whether Asia redefines its compact around shared and inclusive governance, growth and identity is critical to its fate in the 21st century. How its states choose to make the necessary changes will have implications far beyond the region. China Protest Democracy Indonesia Thailand India Asia Myanmar Malaysia Philippines People power Björn Dressel, Michael Wesley, Australian National University Authors Björn Dressel Senior Lecturer, Crawford School of Public Policy,, Australian National University Michael Wesley Professor and Director of School of International Political and Strategic Studies , Australian National University Disclosure statement Björn Dressel receives funding from the Australian Research Council (ARC). Michael Wesley does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners Australian National University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.34dnjv3tm","title":"Asian states in crisis can choose more democracy or more conflict","url":"https://theconversation.com/asian-states-in-crisis-can-choose-more-democracy-or-more-conflict-31829","id":322},{"content_hash":"4ca8f972fe0e5abfc72519431ff139da3c8fb35aa9474ab3aa22adfa47cf5381","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.865137+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/margaret-hamilton-scientist-who-worked-on-nasas-apollo-programme-dies/articleshow/134803936.cms","language":"","text":"Margaret Hamilton, scientist who worked on Nasa's Apollo programme, dies - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIworldUSTimes EvokePakistanSouth AsiaUKEuropeChinaMiddle East US StreamersRest of WorldMad, Mad WorldVideosToday's ePaperLiveNewsWorld NewsUS NewsMargaret Hamilton, scientist who worked on Nasa's Apollo programme, diesTrendingChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War NewsMargaret Hamilton, scientist who worked on Nasa's Apollo programme, diesAgencies / Oct 09, 2026, 07:09 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint Margaret Hamilton sitting in a mock-up ofthe Apollo command module in 1969. Thefamed Apollo programme allowed US tobeat the Soviet Union in the race to becomethe first humans to land on the Moon American scientist Margaret Hamilton, who led development of the computer system used by Nasa's Apollo lunar program, has died at the age of 90, her former employer said Wednesday. In a statement, the Massachusetts Institute of Technology called Hamilton, who taught at the university from 1959 to the mid-1970s, a \"computing pioneer\" and said she died on Sept 30.\"To say Margaret Hamilton was a pioneer - to say she was ahead of her time - would be an understatement,\" said Olivier de Weck, the Apollo Program Professor and interim head of the MIT department of aeronautics and astronautics. \"She was a software engineer at a time when that field was in its infancy, and she not only developed advanced code herself but also led a team in using that nascent technology to develop one of the most complex systems humanity had ever achieved,\" de Weck added.The famed Apollo programme allowed US to beat the Soviet Union in the race to become the first humans to land on the Moon. Nasa chief Jared Isaacman paid tribute to Hamilton on X, calling her \"a pioneer whose work helped make Apollo and humanity's first journeys to the moon possible.\"Born in Indiana in 1936, Hamilton studied mathematics before moving to Boston, Massachusetts with her husband in 1959. She found employment at MIT, working in meteorology before she joined a lab in 1965 that was seeking people to develop software to \"send a man to the Moon.\"Hamilton \"was hired as the first programmer for the Apollo project at MIT, as well as the first female programmer in the project,\" the university said. \"By 1968, she was assistant director in charge of (Apollo's) Command and Service Module team, and more than 400 people were working on Apollo's software,\" MIT said.Hamilton was credited with making critical onboard systems more robust and accident-resistant. Her software intervened approximately three minutes before the Eagle lander of the Apollo 11 mission touched down on the Moon, overriding a faulty script and ensuring the mission could safely continue, according to Nasa.Jerry Wohletz, CEO of the Draper Laboratory - which was spun out of the MIT Instrumentation Lab - said Hamilton left \"an indelible mark\" on the company. \"Through her leadership and contributions to the development of the onboard flight software for Nasa's Apollo Guidance Computer, she helped ensure that Apollo astronauts landed safely on the lunar surface and safely returned home,\" he said.Hamilton was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom by former president Barack Obama in 2016 for her achievements. Hamilton went on to publish more than 130 papers and started her own software companies Higher Order Software and Hamilton Technologies.(BY AFP)You use AI every day. Now get your AI Quotient. Take the AIQ test.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaVideosChina Russia Could Hit U.S_ Hegseth Faces Nuclear Reality On Cam; Iran War EXPOSES Trump _ Watch‘Trump's Reckless War!’: Hakeem Jeffries Blasts Hegseth Amid LA-San Diego 'Bombing' RowON CAM: Saudi Jet BLOWN TO PIECES! First Images Emerge After Houthis Strike Riyadh Airport‘Rusty, Battered & Broken!’: USS Lincoln ‘Scarred’ By Iran War; New Visuals Shock Families | WatchTrump Readies MASSIVE Iran Attack? Pentagon Prepares For Strikes On Nuclear SitesNidal Hasan Execution: Pete Hegseth Orders Ford Hood Shooter's 'Firing Squad' Live Streaming‘Why The Silence On Oct 7?’: Abdul El-Sayed Walks Away From Reporter; Then Rips Trump‘Not Playing This Game’: Marco Rubio Unloads On Reporter Over Kimberly Guilfoyle’s $100,000 BribeZohran Mamdani Grilled Over Oct. 7 Post; Reporter Asks: ‘Will You Apologize To Netanyahu?’‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New Presser123PhotostoriesQuote of the day: “Love looks not with the eyes, but with the mind”; William Shakespeare’s timeless words on how true love goes beyond what we seeGender pain gap: Women experience more pain than men across the whole body, major global study finds ‘shocking’ dataFrom historic tiaras to priceless heirlooms: The royal jewels Kate Middleton has left untouchedKangana Ranaut goes full Himachali royalty in a statement topi, Kullu shawl, and a cultural nod to President Droupadi Murmu5 habits that matter more than supplements: Doctor shares simple daily habits for better sleep, fitness, nutrition, stress management and preventive health that can support a longer, healthier life5 lipstick shades to pair with your chaniya choli this NavratriOptical illusion personality test: What you see first claims to reveal whether you are a practical thinker or a gut-feeling personKareena Kapoor Khan just gave motorsport a Bebo makeover in sleek black tailoring10 baby names inspired by astronauts, space pioneers and cosmic explorersGen Z is making Indian textiles cool again: 5 heritage weaves that are having a fashion comeback123Hot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn USIn WorldAmerica has enjoyed the greatest free lunch in history: Vivek Wadhwa reacts to Green Card crackdown on 8 IT companies, says they aren't the visa abusersIndian-origin doctor mom arrested and charged in Florida as she watched 7-year-old drown. She said she froze, she was not strong enough to pull him from waterBryan Cranston rebuilt a $2.5M beach bungalow into a net-zero home, sold it for $5.45MZach Bryan’s New Retreat: singer buys $18.25 million Leiper’s Fork estate; adds on-site studioFloyd V. Bowlin-Lemon: Arkansas court upholds fence boundary; $12,000 treble damages standFederal executive created fake program, diverted $145M; FBI found 298 gold bars in $193.6M fraudFlorida Land Acquisition: Disney secretly bought 27,443 acres; deal reshaped Walt Disney WorldUS bill proposes raising H-1B violation fines to $250,000 and 10-year banPalm Valley School closes after 74 years amid bankruptcy; 36-acre campus listed for $14 millionJ-1 visa fraud: Harvard, Yale among 9 elite universities under investigation for alleged scam; 'Nobody will be getting a free pass because their name is carved into an expensive building'Family Asks For Privacy: FGTeeV Duddy’s ‘demons’ rant alarms fans; KreekCraft says he needs helpLogo Sketch Auction: MrBeast’s 2018 tiger art heads to sale; estimate tops $20,000America has enjoyed the greatest free lunch in history: Vivek Wadhwa reacts to Green Card crackdown on 8 IT companies, says they aren't the visa abusersIndian-origin doctor mom arrested and charged in Florida as she watched 7-year-old drown. She said she froze, she was not strong enough to pull him from waterTrump honours CEO Satya Nadella as Vance goes hard at MicrosoftBryan Cranston rebuilt a $2.5M beach bungalow into a net-zero home, sold it for $5.45MNew extinct crocodile found in Sphinx rocks, with a missing snout tipDoe swatted amid threats: Police respond to fake shooting call; FBI investigates caseZach Bryan’s New Retreat: singer buys $18.25 million Leiper’s Fork estate; adds on-site studioFloyd V. Bowlin-Lemon: Arkansas court upholds fence boundary; $12,000 treble damages standTired of too many ads?go ad free nowDaily PuzzlesConnectThe Perfect TracePlay Now Loop The LoopPuzzle CircuitPlay Now Daily CrosswordSpecial puzzlePlay Now Explore More PuzzlesExplore Every CornerAcross The GlobeCarey PriceAaron RodgersPatrick Mahomes ContractPatrick Mahomes FatherDonna KelceCoco GauffTravis Kelce FoundationBrittany vs Randi MahomesTom BradyJoe BurrowNFL RefereesRahul GandhiPatrick and Brittany FundingRoman ReignsAnne CarsonJon RahmCaitlin ClarkJayden Daniels InjuryFrance Protest 2026Josh Allen DonationArc RaidersJake PaulRic FlairTravis KelceKay AdamsPatrick Mahomes MomAyesha CurryJason Kel