bus JetPresley Gerber’s Drug Battle Blamed For Tragic Final Chapter As Years-Long Addiction Struggle EmergeTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasMen Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Jessie Buckley and Rory Kinnear Starrer Men Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​JD Vance Exposes H-1B Scam!: 6,000 Americans Duped; Microsoft, Wipro & TCS On Visa HitlistLatest News“Take the family down there and make a donation”: How Travis Kelce’s mother Donna Kelce and Tammy Reid helped bring Christmas cheer to families in needIndia joins US-led group on structural excess capacityVaibhav Sooryavanshi's explosive net session raises big question: Will he play in Ranchi?Trumpled talentMDR on UPI may now take effect in January 2027'Baththa' box office collection day 8 vs 'Dorothy': Vijay Sethupathi's film earns Rs 15.42 cr India net; Karthik Subbaraj's thriller reaches Rs 11.97 crFaceless system in place for central GST payersWhat students wantBhagyank vs Namank: Which Number Matters More When Your Name and Birth Date Disagree?Pisces Shani Horoscope Today, 9 October 2026: Saturn Says State Your Terms Early; Uttara Phalguni Moon Brings Partners ForwardTom Brady’s strategic investment pays off as Las Vegas Raiders reach a $13 billion valuation in the middle of the seasonVishal Parekh on 'Mitrata': 'I didn't want to do the same Vishal that people already see on Instagram' - EXCLUSIVESupreme Court: For post-split suicide, can't always accuse ex of abetmentIIT Bombay fire: How gas leak triggered hostel flare-up, 5 injured, including 2 minorsPerfect storm: Sensex slips amid rising oil, bond yields'Tell your story': Pat Cummins' blunt response to Usman Khawaja's book on SandpapergateChris Hemsworth and Elsa Pataky helped return Tasmanian devils to mainland Australia after 3,000 years; the endangered marsupials could help restore ecosystems'Workers with AI skills in demand, but GenAI is hitting white-collar hiring'Copyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Margaret Hamilton, scientist who worked on Nasa's Apollo programme, dies","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/margaret-hamilton-scientist-who-worked-on-nasas-apollo-programme-dies/articleshow/134803936.cms","id":323},{"content_hash":"93b4621af398afbadedf5aa53adc3abf826e8caaca0b608e51fe160c62b28752","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.643592+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/dotun-oladipo-sudden-death/","language":"","text":"Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked? By Yushau A. Shuaib - August 26, 2026 Dotun Oladipo Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked? By Yushau A. Shuaib On the afternoon of Tuesday, August 25, 2026, I exchanged messages with Dotun Oladipo — respected journalist, former President of the Guild of Corporate Online Publishers (GOCOP), and publisher of The Eagle Online. I sent him a report on the unfolding political rivalry in Katsina State — between the camps of Governor Dikko Radda and Tinubu’s adviser, Ibrahim Masari — which allegedly led to the arrest of security aides attached to the governor — for publication on his platform. In his characteristically simple and professional manner, Dotun asked me to forward it to his Gmail address instead of the Yahoo account I had previously used. I obliged. Within minutes, the article was published, and a few hours later, he promoted it as the lead story in his WhatsApp news broadcast. That was Dotun: no bureaucracy, no excuses, no endless promises. If a material was newsworthy and suitable for his platform, he acted promptly. The next day, as I prepared to attend a programme, my son Gidado called. “Dotun Oladipo is trending everywhere,” he said. I smiled instinctively. When good people trend, one hopes it is for good reasons — an appointment, an award, a promotion, or another professional milestone. Then he completed the sentence. “They say he is dead.” “What? What!” I screamed. It struck like a thunderbolt. I immediately returned to our last conversation. I checked the messages. I looked again at the article he had published. Everything had happened less than twenty‑four hours earlier. How could a man who had just attended to a professional request suddenly become the subject of an obituary? Reports said Dotun died at 56 after suddenly taking ill at his office in Lagos. Colleagues said he had appeared well earlier in the day and had even gone to the gym before proceeding with his normal activities. He reportedly became unwell at work and sought medical attention, but he did not survive. Some reports suggested a heart attack, though the precise medical cause should be left to those competent to establish it. But in that moment of grief, my mind rebelled against the cold logic of mortality. Why does sudden death sometimes appear to pick the good ones — even those, like Dotun, who had just observed the physical exercise recommended for medical fitness? Why would it take a patriotic journalist, a gentleman, and a friend, while bandit kingpins, terrorist commanders, and others who inflict misery on innocent citizens continue to roam? Why do people who work for peace and national stability depart without warning, while those who threaten the peace seem to survive one violent episode after another? Of course, these are not questions mortals can answer. Death does not operate according to our understanding of fairness or fitness. My relationship with Dotun spanned many years. I came to know him more closely during Nigeria’s most challenging security period, when the Forum of Spokespersons of Security and Response Agencies (FOSSRA) was coordinated under Major General Chris Olukolade and supported by the Office of the National Security Adviser under Colonel Sambo Dasuki (Rtd). It was also the era when PRNigeria strengthened its role in security reporting and crisis communication. Read Also: FRSC, BATMedics Screen 500 Drivers to Mark World Sight Day Troops Foil Lakurawa Assault on Kebbi Military Base, Recover 17 Motorcycles, Weapons Troops Nab 9 Suspected Terrorist Collaborators in Yobe, Rescue 6 Hostages in Plateau Those were difficult years. Boko Haram dominated headlines, security agencies were under immense pressure, and public confidence was constantly shaken by conflicting reports and propaganda. The relationship between security institutions and the media was therefore critical. Journalists had to balance the public’s right to know with the responsibility not to compromise operations, while security agencies had to recognise that journalists were not adversaries simply because they asked hard questions. Dotun understood that delicate balance. He believed in journalism, but he also understood national responsibility. Our interactions continued long after that period. Until the article he published barely hours before his death — which he even promoted as the lead story in his final WhatsApp news broadcast — Dotun remained remarkably consistent. Whenever I sent him something newsworthy, he responded promptly. Sometimes it was purely professional; at other times, it carried the quiet solidarity colleagues extend to one another without making a ceremony of friendship. Dotun’s professional journey spanned nearly three decades across major Nigerian newsrooms — Nigerian Tribune, Nigerian Compass, The Sun, and PUNCH, where he served as Politics Editor — before establishing The Eagle Online. His contribution to digital journalism was significant. There was a time when online journalism in Nigeria was treated as the unruly younger cousin of traditional media — associated with sensationalism, poor verification, and weak gatekeeping. People like Dotun helped change that perception. Through The Eagle Online and his leadership in GOCOP, he belonged to a generation of publishers who proved that online journalism could be fast without being reckless, competitive without abandoning ethics, and independent while still respecting professional standards. He understood something that remains relevant today: the platform may change, but the fundamentals of journalism should not. Holding leaders accountable does not mean abandoning responsibility to the nation. Dotun represented the bridge between the traditional newsroom and the digital environment that now defines journalism. Beyond the profession, I remember the human being — a patriotic Nigerian, a dependable colleague, a gentleman who valued friendship and professional solidarity, and a publisher who could receive an article and, without drama, publish it within minutes if he considered it worthy. As I mourn Dotun Oladipo, I also reflect on the peculiar vulnerability of journalists. We chase deadlines, manage breaking news, respond to calls at odd hours, travel unexpectedly, confront professional pressures, and carry burdens audiences rarely see. We report everyone else’s emergencies while sometimes ignoring the warnings from our own bodies. The circumstances surrounding Dotun’s passing should remind media professionals — and indeed all of us — that health deserves attention even amid the pressure of deadlines and obligations. At 56, we shared the same age, and by human reckoning he left far too soon. Yet life is ultimately measured not by how long we stay, but by what we leave in the minds and lives of others. My brother Dotun, I still struggle to reconcile our last exchange with the obituary that followed. Yesterday, you were the publisher receiving my article; today, you are the subject of one. The deadline has finally come for a story none of us ever wished to publish. Good night, Dotun Oladipo — and with tears, I send this one last message to your favourite email, [email protected]. Yushau A. Shuaib Publisher, PRNigeria & Economic Confidential [email protected] VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSFOSSRAGOCOPGovernor Dikko RaddaIbrahim Masari Previous articleAdeniyi Reaffirms Customs’ Commitment to Humanitarian Relief, Receives International AwardNext articleFG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servants’ Transport Burden Yushau A. Shuaib Xing","title":"Dotun Oladipo: Why Does Sudden Death Take the Good and Spare the Wicked?","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/dotun-oladipo-sudden-death/","id":324},{"content_hash":"9614bdaf669bbfae7db61dc3fb83f68fb5a4726fb5f667d222ddfafb8cbc36a1","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.213287+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-03/the-incredible-story-of-oliver-curtis/107222356","language":"","text":"From jailbird to billionaire — the incredible story of Oliver Curtis and the company he is about to floatBy chief business correspondent Ian VerrenderThe BusinessTopic:Stock MarketPosted Sat 3 Oct 2026 at 4:59amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 4:59amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 4:59amTim Rosenfield and Oliver Curtis are founders of Firmus Technologies. (ABC News: Kelsey Reid)abc.net.au/news/the-incredible-story-of-oliver-curtis/107222356Link copiedShareShare articleAs redemption tales go, they don't come any bigger than that of Oliver Curtis.A decade ago, Curtis was a corporate pariah. Convicted of conspiracy to commit insider trading, he was sentenced to two years in prison.Sent to a relatively low-key facility in Cooma, where it is said he became a hairdresser for inmates, he was released after one year on good behaviour.Stockbroker Oliver Curtis and his wife Roxy Jacenko arrive at the NSW Supreme Court, June 17, 2016. (AAP: Joel Carrett)By next Friday, Oliver Curtis is expected to emerge a billionaire; the driving force behind one of Australia's biggest ever stock market listings, second only to Telstra's privatisation in the 1990s.If all goes to plan, Firmus, the data centre builder and operator which has existed in various forms for just seven years, will raise $7.2 billion in new capital as part of the listing.When added to the existing capital, the Singapore based Firmus will be valued at an incredible $43.7 billion.Already, it boasts some of the world's most powerful backers. US investment giant Blackstone has close to 7 per cent of the existing stock while chip giant Nvidia — which routinely vies for pole position as the world's most valuable corporation — holds slightly more.AI data centre provider could become one of Australia's biggest stock floatsAI data centre developer Firmus Technologies is seeking investors ahead of a multi-billion-dollar share market float next month.Curtis, however, will be the big winner.With more than 13 per cent of the company, he will officially cement his role on the rich list.His father Nick, a pioneer of Australian critical minerals mining and the driving force behind Lynas Rare Earths, is also slated to profit handsomely from his 5.6 per cent stake.But the float, and the industry, has its detractors.Amid the ongoing investor frenzy for all things AI, there is a growing apprehension on artificial intelligence from governments globally while on the data centre front line, community opposition could dent the rosy forecasts for future earnings.And then there are the financial risks, and the circular financing and interdependence within the industry; risks highlighted this week by the Reserve Bank of Australia.A past that continues to hauntOliver Curtis and business partner Tim Rosenfield have faced more obstacles than most in their quest to convince the globe's biggest tech players and investors to jump aboard the Firmus express.There is the uncomfortable reality that neither has any experience running data centres or a background in technology.But foremost among them, is Curtis's past.His transgression wasn't a one-off or a simple spur of the moment lack of judgement.Stockbroker jailedSydney stockbroker Oliver Curtis is jailed for two years after being found guilty of conspiring to commit insider trading.He and his former best friend from school, John Hartman, engaged in a cleverly planned and executed scheme to profit from insider trades.In 2007, just before the global financial system began its meltdown, Hartman approached Curtis with a plan.Whenever his employer, investment group Orion, was about to engage in a large transaction, he would notify Curtis to trade in what is known as Contracts For Difference, which ordinarily are high risk instruments that routinely clean out investors.Not so for Curtis.According to the evidence, over the course of a year, he engaged in these trades on 45 separate occasions, trousering a total profit of more than $1.43 million. The then 21-year-olds split the profits with Curtis splurging on lavish gifts for his mate.But in 2009, not long after the corporate regulator began investigating Hartman, he spilled the beans and was the first to do time.He has admitted in interviews that his time behind bars was tough and that it deeply impacted his family.Why Firmus is hot propertyIn the AI lexicon, Firmus is what's known as a neocloud.Traditional data centres, the kind employed until now by the likes of Google, Meta, Amazon et al, are designed to handle a range of applications, everything from search engines to database management and content storage.Neoclouds are data centres equipped with Graphics Processor Units (GPUs) and specifically designed to cater to the needs of artificial intelligence.And the key point of difference, the selling point, between Firmus and its rivals is an innovation that allows it to use a mere fraction of the water than that required by the existing technology.It also claims to have the lowest operating costs in the industry. Ask a question about what AI centres actually produce, and you'll be bombarded with constant references to \"tokens\".Tasmania Premier Jeremy Rockliff alongside Firmus co-CEOs Tim Rosenfield and Oliver Curtis at the Firmus Technologies facility in Launceston. (Supplied: Tasmanian government)It's simply a measure of a neocloud operator's output. And according to Firmus, their AI tokens are about the cheapest on the planet.It's that selling point that has attracted the likes of Meta and OpenAI as customers, Blackstone as an investor and Nvidia as a supplier and investor.The company, however, is still making a loss. And the planned rollout of its technology will require a huge amount of capital expenditure. Which is why it wants to list on the stock market.It hopes to turn a profit within two years, according to its prospectus, with about $US5 billion ($7.2 billion) in annual earnings from its data centres in Malaysia, Indonesia along with the centres it is developing in Tasmania.LoadingWhat's to worry about?Stock markets live and breathe hype.There's been plenty of that with Firmus which, along with its well-heeled backers, have helped generate a whirlwind of FOMO among would be punters.It's also created concerns among regulators.The RBA, back in July, said it was concerned that the huge spending spree on data centres may create shortages of workers, materials and capital, thereby adding to the inflation wave that has beset the economy.This week, it added another concern.In its Financial Stability Review, the RBA highlighted the threat to the global economy from so called neocloud companies, and particularly in the way they secure finance.In the past 18 months, there have been numerous round robin deals between the big tech firms, chip makers and data centre operators. They invest in each other, and they buy products from each other.It's almost a closed shop.Data centre appeals 'unreasonable' generator restrictionsA data centre that received recent council approval to have diesel generators as backup power is now appealing against the \"unreasonable\" conditions on how they can be used.And given they are the world's most valuable companies, the RBA is concerned that a problem in one could ricochet through the entire industry, thereby triggering global stock market instability which could then impact the financial system.\"Some cloud providers have invested in AI developers who subsequently use those providers' infrastructure to train and deploy their models,\" it said.\"Similarly, chipmakers have provided financial support to neocloud firms who subsequently purchase their products.\"Like its US competitors, Firmus has happily exploited this model of extreme interdependence.The big question is whether Firmus's impending stock market debut will deliver for those who tip their cash in once it becomes a public company or simply is a means for early investors to cash out.Oliver Curtis's redemption tale is riding on it.Firmus may have hit the big time. Can it last a long time?Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesCouncil investigates if data centre company started work illegallyTopic:AIPlans to cool AI data centre using trucked-in water and rainwater Topic:AI'We do not want to highlight this yet': AI data centre's FAQ slip-upTopic:AIRelated topicsAIAI EthicsAustraliaCorporate CrimesData CentresStock MarketTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"From jailbird to billionaire — the incredible story of Oliver Curtis and the company he is about to float","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-03/the-incredible-story-of-oliver-curtis/107222356","id":325},{"content_hash":"6e75f36463d85c86998a06e620d05550ad241b9a0993c4376c1c9e732aedcc4e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:57.056340+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/soixante-pays-et-six-mille-personnes-inaugurent-aujourd-hui-les-nouveaux-batiments_2072901_1819218.html","language":"","text":"L'Onusien qui a gardé le souvenir de la session de 1948 se sent dépaysé. La place du Trocadézo est dégagée, et la statue du maréchal Foch domine maintenant la perspective de la four Eiffel. D'autre part les nouvelles installations, dites \" bâtiments des conférences \", s'étendent à présent sur plusieurs étages de part et d'autre de la pièce d'eau centrale. Il faut donc se mettre au courant de la topographie nouvelle : ce qui est relativement facile car tous les mètres vous croisez des figures de connaissance. On se retrouve, on se congratule, comme des curistes qui se rencontrent chaque année dans une ville d'eaux. Évidemment il y a de nouvelles fêtes, ne serait-ce que les agents de sécurité de l'O.N.U., au nombre de huit, habillés de bleu gris et portant une plaque de garde-chasse sur la poitrine. Aujourd'hui les couloirs sont plus qu'animés : il fait très chaud, l'air sent la poussière et le tabac américain. On rencontre un ouvrier portant un appareil sanitaire, dix secrétaires aux jambes gainées de nylon et aux bras surchargés de dossiers, des délégués venus en reconnaissance, des gardiens de la paix arborant la fourragère rouge, des barmen... En bas, au rez-de-chaussée, le bureau des P.T.T. est déjà envahi par les amateurs de timbres avides d'obtenir le précieux coup de tampon du jour. Au vestiaire les mêmes ouvreuses vous accueillent : elles reconnaissent leurs \" clients \" de 1948 et s'échangent quelques propos. Dans la salle de réunion de l'assemblée générale et du Conseil de sécurité on note quelques modifications ; les traducteurs se trouvent maintenant sur la scène du théâtre, derrière le président, le secrétaire général et le chef de cabinet du secrétaire général. Il fait environ 45 degrés dans les cabines réservées à l'interprétation simultanée ; pour peu que le débat s'échauffe, nous plaignons les interprètes... Les tables vertes destinées à chaque délégation sont cette fois-ci au nombre de soixante, contre cinquante-huit il y a trois ans. L'Indonésie et Israël sont en effet venus ajouter leurs voix à ce concert pacifique. Au premier rang siègent le Pérou, le Paraguay, le Panama et le Pakistan ; la Grande-Bretagne (United Kingdom) coudoie toujours l'U.R.S.S. Il vous reste 15.88% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"SOIXANTE PAYS et six mille personnes inaugurent aujourd'hui les nouveaux bâtiments","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/soixante-pays-et-six-mille-personnes-inaugurent-aujourd-hui-les-nouveaux-batiments_2072901_1819218.html","id":326},{"content_hash":"ff9da87778d0effaed177dd07b6e5f91562506d6be57f0b58150440cca57181e","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:56.735679+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-ebola-33130","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Ebola Published: October 16, 2014 9:51pm EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-ebola-33130 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-ebola-33130 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan and Deupty Vice-Chancellor Frances Shannon discuss the week in politics including the Ebola outbreak, the Prime Minister’s comments regarding ‘shirt fronting’ Russian President Vladimir Putin and how Labor has handled the situation. Video Ebola Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Author Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement Michelle Grattan does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.3m634efxa","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Ebola","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-ebola-33130","id":327},{"content_hash":"9a083ff778063ce0eac4c1e86feec0ba627a86af875b620e6780710a1c0f5ec9","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:56.419157+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/give-with-greater-impact.html","language":"","text":"Give with greater impact Gifts to your donor-advised fund can now be invested in actively managed investment portfolios that target competitive financial returns and seek positive environmental and social effects Donor-advised funds (DAFs) offer a convenient, flexible, tax-advantaged way of giving that offers your irrevocable contributions, because they are invested, the opportunity to potentially grow over time. These benefits help make DAFs one of the fastest-growing philanthropic vehicles, with contributions increasing an average of 24% annually over the past decade.1 They can be even more appealing when you know that your contributions can be invested in sustainable investing portfolios. “Sustainable investments offer donors the potential to magnify the impact of their charitable giving — aligning their preferences and charitable priorities to both their grant and asset management strategies,” explains Donald J. Greene, national donor-advised fund executive, Bank of America Private Bank. To that end, seven portfolios with sustainable investment strategies are available through the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund. “Our sustainable portfolios offer donors the potential to stay true to their values regardless of the investment objective they’ve selected to reflect their charitable giving strategy with the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund,” says Greene. Donors can make gifts directly through the Charitable Gift Fund’s online portal to any of the more than 1.8 million IRS-recognized charities in the U.S. Show text version Hide text version 4 reasons to consider putting DAF contributions in sustainable investing portfolios Reason #1 Popularity The number of individual DAF accounts increased 0.6% from 2022 to 2023. The annual growth rate for the number of donor-advised funds from 2014 to 2024 is 22.3%.1. In 2014, there were 238,293 DAFs, and in 2024 there were 1,782,281. Reason #2 Performance Over the last 3, 5, 7, and 10 years, the MSCI World Selection Index either modestly outperformed or performed in line with the MSCI World Index on an annualized basis (period ending 10/31/25).2 Reason #3 Adoption US sustainable investment assets under management remain robust.3 The US Sustainable Investment Total AUM (in USD Billions) was: 180.6 in 2018262.9 in 2019376.2 in 2020413.3 in 2021348.2 in 2022431.4 in 2023577.0 in 2024 Reason #4 Tax efficiency DAFs have higher tax deduction thresholds for cash donations than private foundations. For Donor Advised Funds, the deduction limits are up to 60% of adjusted gross income for cash contributions and up to 30% of AGI for securities and appreciable assets.4 For Private Foundations, the deduction limits are up to 30% of AGI for cash contributions and up to 20% of AGI for securities and appreciable assets.5 Ask your advisor for more information on how a donor-advised fund works, and whether the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund might help you meet your giving goals. Choose your advisor in a more personalized way All our advisors are committed to putting your needs and priorities first. Find some who match your personal preferences too. Loading... Try Advisor Match Answer a few questions Want us to contact you? Submit a request Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 National Philanthropic Trust, “2024 Donor-Advised Fund Report,” August 2025. 2 Bloomberg, 3-year returns for the MSCI World Selection Index were 22.54% annually, while the MSCI World Index returned 22.27% annually (10/31/2022-10/31/2025). 5-year returns for the MSCI World Selection Index were 16.12% annually, while the MSCI World Index returned 16.14% annually (10/31/2020-10/31/2025). 7-year returns for the MSCI World Selection Index were 14.42% annually, while the MSCI World Index returned 14.09% annually (10/31/2018-10/31/2025). 10-year returns for the MSCI World Selection Index were 12.44% annually, while the MSCI World Index returned 12.40% annually (10/31/2015-10/31/2025). 3 BofA Global Research. December 2024. 4 American Endowment Foundation, “5 Primary Tax Benefits to Donors” May 2024 (latest data available). 5 FoundationSource, Charitable Deductions – Private Foundations & Donor-Advised Funds, 2025. Important Disclosures Investing involves risk. There is always the potential of losing money when you invest in securities. Past performance is no guarantee of future results. Bank of America, Merrill, their affiliates, and advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. Clients should consult their legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. Donor-advised fund management is provided by Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., Member FDIC and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). Trust, fiduciary, and investment management services are provided by wholly owned banking affiliates of BofA Corp., including Bank of America, N.A. and its agents. Brokerage services may be performed by wholly owned brokerage affiliates of BofA Corp., including Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated (also referred to as “MLPF&S” or “Merrill”). Merrill is a registered broker-dealer, a registered investment adviser and Member SIPC. Sustainable and Impact Investing and/or Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) managers may take into consideration factors beyond traditional financial information to select securities, which could result in relative investment performance deviating from other strategies or broad market benchmarks, depending on whether such sectors or investments are in or out of favor in the market. Further, ESG strategies may rely on certain values-based criteria to eliminate exposures found in similar strategies or broad market benchmarks, which could also result in relative investment performance deviating. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you Learn more about the Bank of America Charitable Gift Fund (CGF) Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles How to integrate complex assets into your giving strategy Read more Charitable vehicles comparison Read more Keep charitable giving alive year-round with a donor-advised fund Read more","title":"Give with greater impact","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/give-with-greater-impact.html","id":328},{"content_hash":"ba7f0d1421879b27b75dd0e3182b724ead7d7c3baa7e458665e49a9f0e4b718c","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:56.217826+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202608/20/WS6a86545ca3106bc57421c744.html","language":"","text":"See China, from Liangjiahe - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS LIFESTYLE CULTURE TRAVEL WATCHTHIS SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL FORUM NEWSPAPER MOBILE Home China Latest Home / China / Latest See China, from Liangjiahe chinadaily.com.cn | Updated: 2026-08-20 09:11 Share Share - WeChat CLOSE British Ambassador to China Peter Wilson visited Liangjiahe village in Yan'an, Shaanxi province, where President Xi Jinping spent seven years in his youth. Wilson walked into cave dwellings and talked with locals. More than five decades later, the remote mountainous village has been greatly transformed. A visit to Liangjiahe is more than a journey into history; it is also a window into the roots of Xi's commitment to \"doing practical things for the people\", the Communist Party of China's people-first philosophy, and why the Party has continued to lead China forward. Photo Qixi customs spur new consumption trends China achieves its first rocket recovery on land Xi and Peng: Their love for family and country China achieves successful land recovery of a rocket Sharing cultures, building connections China achieves successful land recovery of a rocket Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"See China, from Liangjiahe","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202608/20/WS6a86545ca3106bc57421c744.html","id":329},{"content_hash":"837f31d9d1387861d30d73f400c0f7447b77a710f02ddd2e6786956cc8ca8b2f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.606104+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-robert-schuman-donne-des-precisions-sur-le-calendrier-diplomatique_2072543_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. ROBERT SCHUMAN donne des précisions sur le \" calendrier diplomatique \" Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 2 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. ROBERT SCHUMAN donne des précisions sur le \" calendrier diplomatique \"","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-robert-schuman-donne-des-precisions-sur-le-calendrier-diplomatique_2072543_1819218.html","id":330},{"content_hash":"840a0803cfddaa17161b290cac1947b29f8e321f55ee81602484d1df718cc38c","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.519401+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-commitment-issues-on-display-at-the-g20-32295","language":"","text":"Global commitment issues on display at the G20 Published: October 19, 2014 4:05pm EDT The G20’s impressive declaratory record on energy cooperation hides serious practical shortcomings. www.shutterstock.com https://theconversation.com/global-commitment-issues-on-display-at-the-g20-32295 https://theconversation.com/global-commitment-issues-on-display-at-the-g20-32295 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article With the Brisbane G20 Leaders Summit only a matter of weeks away, Australia is increasingly succumbing to G20 fever. Rarely does a self-declared middle power such as Australia get to play host to such a key global institution, arguably the most important intergovernmental dialogue held here since the Sydney 2007 APEC Summit. So what should we expect this G20 Summit to deliver for global economic governance? The ultimate effectiveness of the Summit will not be decided in Brisbane in November. Rather it will be decided months (and possibly years) later once leaders return home and wrangle with the question of how to implement what has been agreed. Commitment issues at the G20? Apart from the normal anxieties about traffic chaos in the Brisbane CBD, most Australian discussion of the G20 has been overwhelmingly positive. The Commonwealth government has claimed it will allow us to “influence the global economic agenda”, while commentators have labelled it a “diplomatic coup” that may allow Australia to exercise “both regional and global leadership”. However, not all analysts share such a sanguine view of the G20’s role in global economic governance. The Summit stands accused of being a self-appointed club of the powerful with little legitimacy outside the minds of its own members. Its track record at making promises is unfortunately much stronger than actually delivering on them, and some have argued its crisis management functions have been little more than “window-dressing”. One of the major difficulties facing the G20 is a problem I have somewhat cutely labelled “commitment issues”. As a summit body, the G20 does not make formal rules or treaties, but simply issues “declarations” which governments must then implement in the following months and years. Unfortunately, G20 members often fail to follow through on promises made, due to a lack of commitment to the policy reforms declared at summits. These commitment issues points to a rhetoric-reality gap between the promises made at G20 summits and their actual impact on global economic problems. Energy at the G20 - lots of talk, only some action Energy cooperation provides an instructive example of the commitment issues currently facing the G20. Soaring world prices, extreme market instability and the challenges associated with a move away from hydrocarbons, make energy security one of the most important global economic issues today. The G20 has made energy security one of its major focus areas, including energy policy commitments in every one of its summit declarations since 2009. However, its impressive declaratory record on energy cooperation hides some serious practical shortcomings. First, the G20 has placed considerable emphasis on the promotion of energy efficiency and clean energy technologies. Governments have committed to enact such policies at the 2009, 2010, 2011 and 2013 summits. Yet though promoting clean and efficient energy technologies is certainly a laudable goal, it is also an extremely easy promise to make. As several analysts have pointed out, practically every member of the G20 already had such policies several years prior to 2009. This G20 commitment was little more than an “agreement to do what we’re already doing”. Second, the G20’s energy efforts suffer from vagueness. This is particularly true of the agreement to take steps towards phasing-out inefficient fuel subsidies made at the 2009 Summit. The agreement has already been reaffirmed three times since then. However, the commitment is weak as it does not require members to actually reduce fuel subsidies - members simply have to make an attempt. It also allows members themselves to define whether their subsidies are inefficient or not. Indeed, seven members claimed their fuel subsidies are not inefficient, and therefore not covered by the commitment at all. Third, the lack of enforcement or monitoring powers means compliance with G20 energy commitments has at best been patchy. According to compliance reports prepared by the G20 Research Group, six members failed to fully implement energy efficiency commitments made at the 2013 Summit. Only 11 have followed through on fuel subsidy reforms agreed in 2012. The result is considerable unevenness in energy policy reform. Some governments are paying little more than lip-service to G20 energy policy promises. The case of energy security demonstrates the inherent difficulties facing a summit body like the G20. While it has proven capable of making powerful statements on matters of significant global importance, ensuring these commitments are both well-designed and complied with is another matter entirely. Therefore, the success of the G20 Brisbane Summit must be measured not be the promises made on the day, but whether leaders follow-through once they return home. Energy security Energy G20 Clean energy G20 Brisbane Jeffrey Wilson, Murdoch University Author Jeffrey Wilson Fellow of the Asia Research Centre, Murdoch University Disclosure statement Jeffrey Wilson does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners Murdoch University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.6hqaryetf","title":"Global commitment issues on display at the G20","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-commitment-issues-on-display-at-the-g20-32295","id":331},{"content_hash":"8f01222dad3fe708ca69c4b5160a249d1164b92da20384e53083f6c54ef9df96","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.421007+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/article-freedom-of-information-cities-audit/","language":"","text":"CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an account Audit highlights Timeliness of FOI process Extent of redactions Levels of government, compared Transparent cities Opaque cities Trash pickup. Bus routes. Snow clearing. Libraries, pools, parks.The municipal governments behind Canada’s cities, towns and villages play an outsize role in people’s day-to-day lives. A federal immigration policy change can take years to be felt in a community, after all, but if the garbage truck doesn’t show, you’re likely to notice.Much like their federal and provincial counterparts, municipalities across the country must adhere to freedom of information (FOI) laws, which are intended to promote transparency by allowing individuals to request documents from public institutions. These laws require institutions to disclose requested information with limited exceptions.But a Globe and Mail audit of Canada’s 53 largest municipalities has found vast differences in how local governments perform on FOI. While they completed access requests in 25 days, on average – twice as fast as provincial and territorial ministries, and more than three times as quickly as federal departments – some cities still took much longer, including Edmonton (69 days), Hamilton (66) and Greater Sudbury (50). The audit also reveals that some municipalities apply redactions much more heavily than others. Mississauga, for instance, released 78 per cent of its files in full – that is, without any redactions whatsoever. Halifax, by comparison, released just 2 per cent of its files in full.To assemble this national picture of municipal access performance, The Globe filed requests to every municipality in Canada with a population of at least 100,000 people, asking for data on FOIs completed between July 1, 2022, and June 30, 2023. The findings reflect the performance of 53 municipalities across eight provinces. (Three additional cities – Thunder Bay, Lévis, Que., and Ajax, Ont. – were excluded from the analysis because they said they had no FOI tracking system, claimed they could not produce the requested information or provided incomplete data.) The audit was conducted as part of Secret Canada, a national Globe and Mail investigation that examined the country’s dysfunctional access systems and found that public institutions are routinely breaking access laws. These institutions violate statutory time limits, overuse redactions and claim no records exist when they do – and face few, if any, consequences for ignoring the precedents set by courts and information commissioners. Last year, a Secret Canada audit of FOI requests to federal, provincial and territorial ministries found that they managed to complete only half of their requests within 30 days. (By default, most jurisdictions require a file to be completed within this time frame, though FOI laws allow for time extensions in certain circumstances.)The Globe chose to audit municipal governments’ FOI performance because of their close relationship to the public. To Kevin Walby, a criminology professor at the University of Winnipeg who also studies freedom of information, some of the audited municipalities’ performance was “just abysmal – like, their FOI offices should be put into receivership, or something like that.”“Some of the lack of compliance is just totally wild,” he continues. “There aren’t a lot of sites where you get great compliance. Great compliance, I think, would be where you have most of the records released in full, and most of the disclosures released within 30 days, or in a reasonable time.” Prof. Walby says the findings are evidence that some municipalities haven’t adequately resourced their FOI offices. “It’s a really contradictory message for an apparatus of the state to be sending: You guys comply with all these other laws, but we don’t have to follow the laws that pertain to us.”“How do they expect Canadians to want to believe in the legitimacy of the state and believe in these kinds of transparency initiatives when you see this kind of lack of compliance across the board?”Open this photo in gallery:Kristan Cook, former FOI director for the City of Edmonton, says cities have much smaller teams to handle requests for information, but must be held to the same standards as other levels of government.Megan Albu/The Globe and MailKristan Cook, a former FOI director for Edmonton, says municipalities make decisions that “actually affect” people’s day-to-day lives. “It’s people wanting to know, why is my bus route changing? How much did this bridge cost? Why is the train going through my yard?”Ms. Cook, who is now the director of privacy at PBC Solutions, a health benefits technology company, says that Canada has thousands of municipal governments across the country, and despite their varying sizes, they follow the same access rules as higher levels of government. “Whether you’re a government administration of three people or 300, that three-person government administration still has to provide the same service under FOI.”Municipalities handle FOI very differently from provincial or federal governments: City clerks usually act as ranking officials on FOI matters, and local governments tend to have fewer layers of review – that is, fewer people who need to approve the release of a document – before an FOI response goes out the door, which likely speeds up the process, Ms. Cook says.The FOI requests received by municipalities are also very different from those going to the provinces or federal government. “So many dog bites, like, an incredible number of dog bites,” Ms. Cook says. “Fire inspections, that’s a big one. And then requests about infrastructure, things like bridges and buildings and mass transit – those have a very municipal flavour.”The Secret Canada website, which allows users to search through hundreds of thousands of completed FOI requests across the country, was recently updated to include summaries for requests at the federal, provincial, territorial and municipal level. To browse the database and learn more about the Secret Canada project, visit www.secretcanada.com.Here are some highlights from The Globe’s municipal FOI audit.TimelinesWhile audited municipalities took an average of 25 days to respond to a request, coming in under the 30-day benchmark The Globe used last year for its examination of federal and provincial ministries, the timeliness of individual municipalities varied greatly.Quebec City and Longueuil, Que., both closed FOI files within 12 days, and managed to complete virtually all requests they received within a 30-day period. Edmonton (69 days), Hamilton (66), Greater Sudbury, Ont. (50) and Toronto (49), meanwhile, were the slowest.Unsurprisingly, the municipalities that took the longest, on average, to respond to requests also managed to close a much smaller share of their requests within 30 days. Despite taking an average of 37 days to close their requests, Richmond, B.C., completed just 36 per cent of its FOIs within a month, meaning nearly two-thirds of requests took longer than the benchmark.Ms. Cook says that these contrasts in timeliness often come down to records management practices.“If you don’t know where your records are, then you can’t locate them and you can’t respond to an FOI request,” she says.“Organizations of a significant size have hundreds of systems that store records. Some of these systems talk to each other, and some of them don’t. So it really depends on where the information is stored and how easy it is to gather the information and put it into the form that an applicant is seeking.”Extent of redactionsAfter assembling the documents relevant to a request, FOI offices read through the records to identify information that must be redacted under access law. Some responses are released as-is, without redactions, while others can be withheld in their entirety. The final status of an FOI package, also known as its “disposition,” is a useful indicator of how much information a public institution is actually releasing.Mississauga released 78 per cent of its FOI responses without any redactions whatsoever, the highest full disclosure rating for any major Canadian municipality. Halifax, meanwhile, released just 2 per cent of its files in full.(Some of these disclosure statistics are complicated by the types of FOIs they are processing. Winnipeg, for instance, also processes files for the Winnipeg Police Service, and police requests often carry personal information redactions. The Globe’s analysis aimed to measure how municipalities were responding to their requests, so it kept the request data it received mostly as-is, removing only FOIs that were abandoned or transferred to another public institution.)Ms. Cook says that the variation in how extensively local governments redact records could be read many ways. Take a city that has high full disclosure rates: “Maybe it’s an organization that believes in transparency, or maybe it’s an organization that isn’t paying attention,” she says. Meanwhile, a municipality with high redaction rates may be “more on the cautious side of releasing information.”Levels of government, comparedOverall, municipal governments outperformed federal, provincial and territorial ministries across all FOI metrics in the audit. They closed requests more rapidly – 25 days on average, compared with 47 provincially/territorially and 83 federally – and completed the lion’s share of their files within 30 days. Municipalities also released files in full much more frequently: 38 per cent of the time, compared with 20 per cent provincially and 24 per cent federally.To Ms. Cook, these differences can be explained in large part by the complexity of a public institution’s bureaucracy. People empowered to make final FOI decisions – the ones given “delegations of authority” – often have more levels of oversight at the provincial and federal level, she says, slowing down the process considerably.“In some delegations, the people who are actually the people that you’re talking to when you’re doing the access request, they have the authority to sign something and send it out to you themselves,” she says. “In other places, they don’t. So, any time you have that – more people, more levels – the amount of time that it’s going to take for you to get something is going to increase. That’s a really important difference between municipal, provincial and federal.”Transparent citiesWhile several municipalities fared well in this audit, some stood out for how quickly and fulsomely they responded to requests. Red Deer, Alta., and Mississauga released more than 70 per cent of their files in full, and managed to do so while keeping FOI responses to an average of 22 and 23 days, respectively.Nearly every request completed by Longueuil, Que., within the audit window was closed within 30 days – despite the municipality having completed more than 1,500 requests during the period.Several Quebec municipalities achieved exceedingly fast completion times. Montreal wrapped up FOIs in an average of 15 days; Gatineau closed files in 13 days, on average, and Quebec City and Longueuil averaged 12 days.Speaking for Gatineau, Cynthia Lauzon said the administration was “proud of this result,” and that it was “important to highlight the diligent work of all the people in the access to information unit who help respond to these requests.” Gonzalo Nunez with Montreal said that the city aimed for an “average processing time” of 18 days in 2023.Opaque citiesWinnipeg and Greater Sudbury, Ont., both notched a particularly low full disclosure rate of 7 per cent – far short of the 38 per cent average across all municipalities.April Low, a spokesperson for the City of Greater Sudbury, largely attributed the city’s high rate of partial disclosures (78 per cent) to personal information redactions, particularly for requests regarding building permits, bylaw complaints and fire services.A spokesperson for Winnipeg, meanwhile, said the municipality’s low full disclosure rate is tied to the fact the city processes requests for the Winnipeg Police Service. In an e-mail, David Driedger said that 50 to 70 per cent of the city’s FOIs are police-related, and that 90 per cent of those are “requests from individuals for copies of their police reports,” most of which carry some level of redaction.While Halifax registered the lowest full disclosure rate, at 2 per cent, it also stood out on another metric: The east coast capital responded to more than two-thirds of its requests by saying there were no records whatsoever. The municipal average was 22 per cent.Laura Wright, a Halifax Regional Municipality spokesperson, echoed Greater Sudbury, pointing to personal information redactions. Ms. Wright said that a “no records” response didn’t necessarily mean there were no records whatsoever – only that there were no records responsive to what had been requested.Hamilton had the second-slowest average response time (behind Edmonton), but it also ranked poorly across other metrics: It closed 40 per cent of its files within 30 days (third-worst, after Richmond, B.C., and Saguenay, Que.) and released 19 per cent of its files in full (13th-worst).In a e-mailed statement, Hamilton city clerk Matthew Trennum said the municipality “takes its role as a steward and guardian of personal and confidential information very seriously,” and that Hamilton is “looking to invest in modernized freedom of information request tracking software to assist in managing timelines and more generally for project management.”Secret Canada: More from The Globe and MailExplainer: How to file an FOI requestRobyn Doolittle has filed many freedom-of-information requests in her work with The Globe's investigative team. She explains how you can get started using the FOI system to find the documents you want.The Globe and MailThe Decibel podcastThe lion’s share of federal access-to-information requests are from immigration applicants left in the dark about the status of their cases. Reporter Tom Cardoso spoke with The Decibel about how things got that way. Subscribe for more episodes.","title":"How transparent is your city? Audit of freedom of information requests finds vast differences","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/article-freedom-of-information-cities-audit/","id":332},{"content_hash":"db4306bd81e525fe25d287530714f2537ef6dd1d80022f709fecbdb63286e864","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.172872+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-ekyc","language":"","text":"What Is eKYC? ByEmmanuel AgwuApril 5, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Does eKYC Mean?How is eKYC Process Carried Out?Why eKYC is The Future Of KYC?BottomlineShare this postSince The emergence of Know Your Customer as a precautionary measure with financial institutions, fintech, and businesses, advanced versions of KYC like perpetual KYC and eKYC, eKYC is a relatively new concept. It can be a great way to execute the KYC process better. This article aims to highlight eKYC as a progressive of the KYC process, discussing all that needs to be known about eKYc. What Does eKYC Mean? eKYC is an acronym for Electronic Know Your Customer and refers to the remote, paperless process that minimizes the costs and traditional bureaucracy that are employed in KYC processes. It enables businesses and organizations to verify the identity of their customers in a more efficient and secure manner without the need for physical documents or face-to-face interactions. eKYC can be used to authenticate customers and meet regulatory requirements in a variety of businesses, including banking, insurance, telecom, and e-commerce. It entails retrieving people's personal information from the government database and confirming their identities using Aadhaar-based authentication methods, such as biometric scanners and OTP (one-time password) verification. The information is subsequently utilized to open accounts, offer services, or carry out other necessary transactions. Related: What is a Qualified Electronic Signature How is eKYC Process Carried Out? In Kenya, the eKYC process is primarily used in the financial sector to verify the identity of customers and comply with regulatory requirements. Failure to meet up with these regulations may attract legal issues. The eKYC process in Kenya involves the use of various digital tools, such as biometric identification, mobile phones, and online platforms, to verify the identity of individuals. The process is generally used by financial institutions, such as banks, insurance companies, and microfinance institutions, to comply with regulatory requirements while providing a seamless customer experience. To perform eKYC in Kenya, customers typically provide their personal information, such as their name, address, and date of birth, along with their government-issued ID number. This information is then verified against the government's database through the use of biometric identification, such as fingerprints or facial recognition. The eKYC process in Kenya has several benefits, which include increased security, reduced fraud, and improved customer experience. By using digital tools to verify customer identity, financial institutions can ensure that their customers are who they claim to be, reducing the risk of fraud and increasing security. Additionally, eKYC can be performed quickly and easily, providing a seamless customer experience. This includes: 1. Customer onboarding The potential customer is required to provide their personal information, such as their name, address, and date of birth, along with their government-issued ID number. This can be done through an online form or mobile app. Many businesses today employ perpetual Know Your Customer processes to stay up to date with emerging customer risks. 2. Biometric verification In biometric verification, the customer's biometric data, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, is collected and verified against the government's database. This way, authentication of an individual's identity can be carried out through a digital process. 3. ID verification In ID verification, the customer's ID information is verified against the government's database, and any discrepancies are flagged for further review. 4. Risk assessment The customer's risk profile is assessed based on their personal information and transaction history. This is used to determine the level of due diligence required for the customer. 5. Verification completion Once the customer's identity is verified and their risk profile is assessed, the eKYC process is complete. The customer can then access the financial institution's services, such as opening a bank account or applying for a loan. The eKYC process in Kenya is facilitated by various digital tools, such as mobile apps, online platforms, and biometric verification devices. These tools are used to securely collect and verify customer information, ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements while providing a seamless customer experience. It's worth noting that the eKYC process may vary slightly depending on the financial institution and the specific requirements of the regulatory authorities. However, the overall process typically follows the steps outlined above. Recommended - Customer Credit Check - All You Need to Know Why eKYC is The Future Of KYC? Electronic Know Your Customer (eKYC) is considered the future of KYC (Know Your Customer) because it offers several advantages over traditional KYC processes. a. Speed and convenience: eKYC allows for quick verification of customer identity through electronic channels, such as online or mobile platforms. This eliminates the need for customers to physically visit a bank or financial institution to complete the KYC process, saving time and effort. b. Cost-effectiveness: eKYC significantly reduces the cost of the KYC process by eliminating the need for manual data entry and verification. This is especially important for small businesses and startups that may not have the resources to invest in expensive KYC solutions. c. Increased accuracy: eKYC reduces the risk of errors in the KYC process by using automated data validation and verification checks. This helps ensure that the information provided by the customer is accurate and up-to-date. d. Enhanced security: eKYC uses advanced security measures, such as biometric authentication and encryption, to protect sensitive customer data from fraud and identity theft. e. Compliance: eKYC helps financial institutions comply with regulatory requirements, such as Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and Counter-Terrorism Financing (CTF) regulations. f. Digital transformation: With the increasing digitization of financial services, eKYC enables financial institutions to offer a fully digital customer onboarding experience. This can help attract tech-savvy customers who prefer to conduct their financial transactions online. g. Improved customer experience: eKYC offers a seamless customer experience, allowing customers to complete the KYC process from the comfort of their own homes or offices. This can help improve customer satisfaction and loyalty. h. Scalability: eKYC can be easily scaled to accommodate large volumes of customers, making it ideal for businesses that experience rapid growth. I. Flexibility: eKYC can be customized to meet the specific needs of different financial institutions, depending on their customer base and regulatory requirements. j. Reduced fraud: eKYC uses advanced fraud detection techniques, such as facial recognition and document authentication, to reduce the risk of fraudulent activity. This way, resources are managed more effectively, and customers and businesses are at lesser risk. k. Accessible Digital Tools Available: There are many accessible digital tools available for paperless KYC that can be easily utilized. Youverify offers automatable eKYC solutions that help businesses perform adequate customer risk assessment and satisfy compliance. Bottomline eKYC offers numerous benefits over traditional KYC processes, making it a popular choice among financial institutions looking to streamline customer onboarding procedures while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements. With the increasing digitization of financial services, eKYC is expected to become the norm in the near future. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for eKYC screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Is eKYC?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-ekyc","id":333},{"content_hash":"7e24d47c6af9a386e6cfaaa96ad60bfae05157225364a5e63b605c5fd88170b8","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:55.073268+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/files/file/","language":"","text":"The File object¶ The django.core.files module and its submodules contain built-in classes for basic file handling in Django. The File class¶ class File(file_object, name=None)[source]¶ The File class is a thin wrapper around a Python file object with some Django-specific additions. Internally, Django uses this class when it needs to represent a file. File objects have the following attributes and methods: name¶ The name of the file including the relative path from MEDIA_ROOT. size[source]¶ The size of the file in bytes. file¶ The underlying file object that this class wraps. Be careful with this attribute in subclasses. Some subclasses of File, including ContentFile and FieldFile, may replace this attribute with an object other than a Python file object. In these cases, this attribute may itself be a File subclass (and not necessarily the same subclass). Whenever possible, use the attributes and methods of the subclass itself rather than those of the subclass’s file attribute. mode¶ The read/write mode for the file. open(mode=None, *args, **kwargs)[source]¶ Open or reopen the file (which also does File.seek(0)). The mode argument allows the same values as Python’s built-in open(). *args and **kwargs are passed after mode to Python’s built-in open(). When reopening a file, mode will override whatever mode the file was originally opened with; None means to reopen with the original mode. It can be used as a context manager, e.g. with file.open() as f:. __iter__()[source]¶ Iterate over the file yielding one line at a time. chunks(chunk_size=None)[source]¶ Iterate over the file yielding “chunks” of a given size. chunk_size defaults to 64 KB. This is especially useful with very large files since it allows them to be streamed off disk and avoids storing the whole file in memory. multiple_chunks(chunk_size=None)[source]¶ Returns True if the file is large enough to require multiple chunks to access all of its content give some chunk_size. close()[source]¶ Close the file. In addition to the listed methods, File exposes the following attributes and methods of its file object: encoding, fileno, flush, isatty, newlines, read, readinto, readline, readlines, seek, tell, truncate, write, writelines, readable(), writable(), and seekable(). The ContentFile class¶ class ContentFile(content, name=None)[source]¶ The ContentFile class inherits from File, but unlike File it operates on string content (bytes also supported), rather than an actual file. For example: from django.core.files.base import ContentFile f1 = ContentFile(\"esta frase está en español\") f2 = ContentFile(b\"these are bytes\") The ImageFile class¶ class ImageFile(file_object, name=None)[source]¶ Django provides a built-in class specifically for images. django.core.files.images.ImageFile inherits all the attributes and methods of File, and additionally provides the following: width[source]¶ Width of the image in pixels. height[source]¶ Height of the image in pixels. Additional methods on files attached to objects¶ Any File that is associated with an object (as with Car.photo, below) will also have a couple of extra methods: File.save(name, content, save=True)¶ Saves a new file with the file name and contents provided. This will not replace the existing file, but will create a new file and update the object to point to it. If save is True, the model’s save() method will be called once the file is saved. That is, these two lines: >>> car.photo.save(\"myphoto.jpg\", content, save=False) >>> car.save() are equivalent to: >>> car.photo.save(\"myphoto.jpg\", content, save=True) Note that the content argument must be an instance of either File or of a subclass of File, such as ContentFile. File.delete(save=True)¶ Removes the file from the model instance and deletes the underlying file. If save is True, the model’s save() method will be called once the file is deleted.","title":"The File object¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/files/file/","id":334},{"content_hash":"b888df03b0a0be2e4500c2079b859f8b4f2acdfadbb6ba895873d44e6154e3de","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:54.842140+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/plants","language":"","text":"PlantsOctober 2026How a plant selfie in Colombian jungle led to ‘jaw dropping’ giant waterlily findSpecies with leaves large enough to be seen from space is already critically endangered – and plans are afoot to protect it00.00 EDTCountry diaryCountry diary: A gleaming beetle that could be bad news for my garden favouritesAllendale, Northumberland: On a hot autumn day, I see rosemary beetles here for the first time. This could be a problem for many thriving plantsOct 7 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsMaking scents: could AI help perfumers take pressure off endangered plants?Oct 6 2026 09.00 EDTFrom urban rooftops to working cattle farms: Australia’s best home gardens – in picturesGallery25Falling glass and ‘decapitated’ plants: the race to protect Edinburgh botanic garden’s plants Oct 1 2026 19.01 EDTSeptember 2026Country diaryCountry diary: A young shieldbug has found a safe place to hide – or so it hopes Sep 28 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsCountry diaryCountry diary: An autumn bounty of fruits is well on its waySep 23 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsCountry diaryCountry diary: A little love at Larkrise has worked wonders for wildlifeSep 22 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsCountry diaryCountry diary: A lesson from my final diary after nearly 40 years – keep looking closelySep 19 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsCountry diaryCountry diary: Facing the flax – this city was once built on the stuffSep 17 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsPlantwatchPlantwatch: The insect-eating alpine plant that proves Darwin was rightSep 16 2026 01.00 EDTBlankets of colour: Western Australia’s wildflower season bursts into life – in picturesGallery16Country diaryCountry diary: A little garden mystery – why are the wood mice hoarding opium seeds?Sep 10 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsCountry diaryCountry diary: Wetland plants have prospered this year – and I love this understated beautySep 9 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsAugust 2026Trump rollback of US forest protections could accelerate extinction of 400 endangered speciesAug 28 2026 08.00 EDT‘Sad moments ahead’: Kew Gardens to fell rare trees in race to save Victorian greenhouseAug 27 2026 01.00 EDT‘I love it. It’s hope’: the mysterious self-willed wildflower meadow that seeded itselfAug 23 2026 10.00 EDTCould the macadamias hidden in Queensland’s rainforests be the key to saving ‘the world’s finest nut’?Aug 22 2026 16.00 EDT…… commentsMonstera magic: Barbican hands out cuttings as ‘green lung of the city’ closesAug 20 2026 01.00 EDTCountry diaryCountry diary: All hail the horsetail, a plant that could survive anythingAug 20 2026 00.30 EDT…… commentsAbout 2,296 results for Plants1234...","title":"Plants | The Guardian","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/plants","id":335},{"content_hash":"f943d9a98380d6d694f0a3cd9bb8e999dc3541fa3ad0bc4c27d30b16ac36eaa5","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:54.496140+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day-celebration","language":"","text":"International Women's Day Celebration | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-U