th Radhika Sarathkumar, 'Karuppu' actress leaves fans guessing again'Don't particiapte in any activity': Delhi Police reach out to religious places, RWAs ahead of CJP stir\"I don’t use equipment': Milind Soman runs barefoot at 60: KNOW the surprising benefits this habit may have for his foot musclesRise and Fall 2: Karan Patel exits the show over health concerns; Kajal Raghwani, Swara Bhaskar and others get emotionalRepeat. Reinvent. Turn up the style: the festive flex is no longer a new outfit — it’s making an old one look brand newCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"‘The Orange Plague’: Why a golden, naked Trump is sitting on another man","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/the-orange-plague-why-a-golden-naked-trump-is-sitting-on-another-man/articleshow/134808403.cms","id":358},{"content_hash":"262d232a845cb6306a41bcb6dca8db4a3c6926c22de1ab9640871081d21d6d11","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:50.241103+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day","language":"","text":"International Women's Day | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"International Women's Day | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-womens-day","id":359},{"content_hash":"92dbee306acd3adb6abcb147f7781932bccba92b7fafaf7b7654768b0f2dc3e5","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:49.691555+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/beyond-certificates-rehashed/","language":"","text":"2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ By Kabir Akintayo - August 26, 2026 Atiku Abubakar, President Bola Ahmed Tinubu and Peter Obi 2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ By Kabir Akintayo Nigeria is once again approaching that familiar political season when presidential campaigns risk becoming less about the future and more about the past. Instead of asking who can fix the economy, secure the country, create jobs or keep the lights on, political actors may once again spend precious months arguing over certificates, academic records, old court cases and controversies that have already dominated previous elections, that would be a disservice to Nigerians. The 2027 presidential election should be different. It should be an election of ideas, records, policies and measurable commitments. Candidates should be compelled to tell Nigerians not only what they have done, but what they intend to do differently. The campaign season formally got underway in August, with major political parties and presidential candidates beginning to activate their campaign structures. The All Progressives Congress (APC), which is seeking President Bola Ahmed Tinubuâs re-election, unveiled a 2027 Presidential Campaign Council with 223 members. The list generated controversy, particularly over the inclusion of serving public officials, including officials occupying sensitive government positions. Even though the list, according to reports was withdrawn for further review, but that controversy, important as it may be for questions about conflict of interest and the use of public office, should not define the presidential contest. More importantly, the opposition must resist the temptation to allow the campaign to become another certificate war. For example, Peter Obi, presidential candidate of the National Democratic Congress (NDC), while declaring his campaign kick-off on Friday, said that Nigerians should take advantage of the campaign period to examine each candidateâs academic records and proposals and ask âtough questions.â The certificate controversy was already one of the dominant issues of the 2023 election. Yet President Tinubu emerged from that election and subsequently assumed office. Whatever oneâs opinion about the controversy, reopening the same argument four years later without connecting it to a broader assessment of governance risks turning the 2027 election into a referendum on old controversies rather than a contest over Nigeriaâs future. INEC has also published the personal particulars and academic credentials submitted by presidential candidates in accordance with the electoral framework. The real question now should therefore be: What will each candidate do with the enormous powers of the Nigerian presidency? That is where the opposition should concentrate its fire. Subsidy removal: Follow the money Few issues offer a better opportunity for an issue-based campaign than the removal of petrol subsidy. The government has consistently defended the policy as necessary to save resources and prevent the country from sinking deeper into fiscal crisis. The latest government figures provide opposition parties with an opportunity to ask legitimate and uncomfortable questions. Finance Minister and Coordinating Minister of the Economy, Taiwo Oyedele, recently disclosed that reforms involving subsidy removal and foreign-exchange liberalisation generated an estimated â¦15.8 trillion in resources for the Federation between June 2023 and December 2025. The Federal Government received about â¦5.43 trillion, states â¦6.52 trillion and local governments â¦3.88 trillion. Oyedele also said the Federal Government generated about â¦20.4 trillion in incremental resources when other revenues and borrowing were included, while additional expenditure reached approximately â¦30.64 trillion. This is precisely where an intelligent opposition campaign should begin. Where did the money go? What measurable improvements have Nigerians received in return for the enormous sacrifice imposed by subsidy removal? How much was spent on infrastructure, social intervention, transportation, healthcare, education and poverty reduction? What independent mechanism can verify the governmentâs figures? Rather than simply declaring subsidy removal a failure, the opposition should demand an independent audit of the savings, allocations and expenditure. It should present its own alternative: If subsidy is not returning, what replaces it? If it should return, how will the government finance it without recreating the fiscal problems that led to its removal? Electricity should become an election test Electricity is another area where the 2027 campaign can become a genuine accountability contest. The administration inherited a deeply troubled power sector and has implemented reforms, including the Electricity Act and measures aimed at strengthening the national grid. The Federal Government itself acknowledged in June that the sector had suffered from generation shortages, unreliable gas supply, fragile transmission infrastructure and significant legacy debts. Read Also: COAS Tasks Senior Commanders on Strategic Leadership Amidst Evolving Security Threats EFCCâs Asset Recovery Hits N673bn in Three Years, Says Olukoyede 2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ But Nigerians are ultimately interested in one thing: Does electricity work? The President made electricity a major part of his 2023 political pitch, including a pledge that Nigerians should hold him accountable if he failed to deliver on his promise. The Presidency has since argued that the promise was misrepresented and has pointed to reforms in the sector. That disagreement should not be settled by propaganda, but figures. How many hours of electricity does the average Nigerian receive today compared with 2023? What is the actual generation capacity? How much electricity is lost in transmission and distribution? What is the metering gap? What has been done with the billions allocated to the sector? What is the roadmap to reliable electricity? The opposition should put those questions before Nigerians and offer a credible alternative. Cost of living is bigger than certificates For millions of Nigerians, the most important political issue is not whether a politician attended a particular school. It is whether they can afford food. The International Monetary Fund said in its 2026 assessment that conditions remained difficult for many Nigerians, with poverty estimated at 63 per cent and about 27 million people estimated to have experienced food insecurity in late 2025. It also warned that higher fuel and food prices could worsen the situation. This gives presidential candidates a wide policy field. They should be debating how to reduce food prices without destroying farmersâ incentives; how to improve agricultural productivity; how to reduce transportation costs; how to make the naira more stable; how to create productive employment; and how to protect poor Nigerians while pursuing economic reforms. The question should not simply be, âWhat certificate do you have?â It should be: âWhat is your economic plan, how much will it cost, and how will you pay for it?â Security cannot be pushed to the background . Security should arguably be the biggest issue of the 2027 election. Recent mass kidnappings and continuing attacks have demonstrated that insecurity remains a serious national challenge. The latest incidents in Niger State, including a major mass abduction reported on Monday, have again raised questions about the ability of the state to protect vulnerable communities. Every presidential candidate should therefore be required to present a detailed security doctrine. How will the next government defeat banditry? What happens to ransom payments? How will intelligence gathering be improved? What is the strategy for securing rural communities, schools, highways and farms? How will security agencies be better equipped without creating avenues for corruption? And perhaps most importantly, what institutional reforms will ensure that security policy survives changes in government? These questions deserve more campaign airtime than scandals from decades ago. Debt, taxation and public accountability Nigeriaâs rising fiscal pressures also demand serious debate. The next president will inherit an economy where debt servicing, revenue mobilisation and infrastructure spending must be carefully balanced. The IMF has warned about limited fiscal space, high interest-to-revenue pressures and the need to strengthen budget processes, fiscal reporting, transparency and accountability. Candidates should therefore publish their position on taxation, borrowing and public expenditure. Will they borrow more? At what interest rate? For what projects? What happens when revenue falls short? Will they publish a comprehensive register of government borrowing and major projects? How will they reduce leakages? The recently introduced tax reforms also deserve serious debate. Candidates should tell Nigerians whether they support them, which aspects they would retain, which they would amend and how their preferred tax system would protect businesses and low-income households. A credible opposition candidate should be able to say: This is what the government did; this is where it failed; this is what we would change; this is what it would cost; and this is how Nigerians would benefit. That is what presidential politics should look like. For example, opposition candidates should be able to tell Nigerians about a policy like NELFUND. Is it a good policy, will you scrap it, review it or expand it? How about the Petroleum Industry Act which has deregulated the downstream sector of the petroleum industry? The candidate who has promised to return fuel subsidy immediately after swearing-in must tell Nigerians how he intends to do that without amending the PIA. He has to tell voters whether he plans to convince the National Assembly to amend the law or he wants to run a lawless administration. And when the campaign ends, voters should be able to choose not simply the candidate with the most damaging allegation against an opponent, but the candidate with the most convincing plan for making Nigeria work. Kabir Akintayo is a journalist and a public affairs analyst. He writes from Abuja, and can be reached at [email protected] VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAPCNDCPeter ObiTinubu Previous articleFG Unveils 100 Electric Buses to Ease Civil Servantsâ Transport BurdenNext articleEFCCâs Asset Recovery Hits N673bn in Three Years, Says Olukoyede Kabir Akintayo Xing","title":"2027: Beyond Certificates and Other Rehashed Nothingness, Nigerians Deserve an Issue-based CampaignÂ","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/26/beyond-certificates-rehashed/","id":360},{"content_hash":"d46daecddd5ba5559dcd9fb4ab2fb04d72cae809b93dffa9bd29556598a21ea3","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:49.561779+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/nouvelles-de-paris_2072443_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives NOUVELLES DE PARIS Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"NOUVELLES DE PARIS","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/nouvelles-de-paris_2072443_1819218.html","id":361},{"content_hash":"7590b93ec6aa90ec5dffe2aa80c9dbb24b768412c57ae7ac7f258bf159b2781a","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:49.517919+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/technology-is-changing-the-face-of-northern-australian-cattle-farming-31552","language":"","text":"Technology is changing the face of northern Australian cattle farming Published: October 29, 2014 8:36pm EDT https://theconversation.com/technology-is-changing-the-face-of-northern-australian-cattle-farming-31552 https://theconversation.com/technology-is-changing-the-face-of-northern-australian-cattle-farming-31552 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Farming cattle in northern Australia can be tough, especially in times of drought, but producers are increasingly turning to digital technologies to help them look after their herds. As these new technologies take on more of the load in cattle farming we need to change the way we think about training people for the grazing industries of northern Australia. We need to start by teaching those in traditional agriculture industries how to make better use of new technologies as well as creating more opportunities for people in the IT and other high-tech sectors. Will Wilson is a Central Queensland cattle producer and founder of a company that is developing an app called iHerd. Will spoke about his app at a recent conference and agriculture innovation and he told how a growing group of cattle managers around the world were now downloading and using his app. His presentation was at the Belmont Research and Education Centre, located a half hour drive north of Rockhampton in Central Queensland. Will certainly looked at home on the cattle property with his big boots and big belt. Knowing that Will was an out and out cattleman I wasn’t sure how he was going to go giving a talk on technology. The Belmont set-up had provided some technical challenges for the previous speakers – an old laptop can be temperamental at the best of times. But Will stood up, pulled his smart-phone from his pocket and without any fuss linked into the projector via Bluetooth. He then proceeded to seamlessly run through his talk using interactive slides that he had prepared on his smart-phone. He told how the app allows a producer to track and monitor mobs of cattle as they move around the farm, effectively enabling farmers to track management interventions such as animal health issues. Will represents a growing number of northern cattle producers that are engaging in the development and use of digital technologies to enable precision livestock management in extensive and complex cattle productions systems. Northern Australian beef production systems have traditionally been low input. CSIRO Typically northern cattle production systems are low input and mustering cattle is expensive. So producers aim to minimise the number of times cattle have to be brought through a set of yards. On properties that have well managed watering points it is possible to set up cattle yards with one-way gates or spear traps at the watering trough. When cattle come to drink from the trough they can be held in the yards. This system has been traditionally used to reduce mustering costs. The digital farm The Cooperative Research Centre for Remote Economic Participation (CRC-REP) has been working on a project that links automated monitoring using electronic identification tags that are fitted to the cattle. As cattle come to water they walk across a set of weigh scales, and using sophisticated walk-over-weighing algorithms their weight and electronic ID are recorded. Walk-over-weighing automatically records cattle weights each time they go to water. CSIRO The work from the CRC-REP project is being developed and refined to incorporate a drafting system that not only allows automated monitoring of cattle but also automatic management, selecting animals which meet a predefined weight range. Since the introduction of Brahman cattle in northern Australia, Central Queensland has provided a proofing ground for new and emerging beef cattle technologies. Belmont Cattle Station is an AgForce owned cattle station that has a long history of supporting the latest beef cattle scientific research. AgForce in Central Queensland has now established a partnership with my university to enable the property to be further developed to support emerging research and importantly to make direct links with education and training activities. Our research work on precision livestock management has established a wireless sensor network to monitor and track the location and movement of cattle across the property. Researchers are working to develop real-time data processing algorithms that can be used to determine reproductive status, health and the productivity of the cattle. Technology that allows farmers to automatically monitor their livestock means they will be able to collect more information with less effort. Linking the information to automatic management systems will further reduce the time farmers spend working cattle. Precision livestock management data systems will require farmers that can capture the benefits from large complex datasets. Managing technology that can manage cattle. Farmers of the future will need to be more tech savvy. CSIRO Agricultural training programs need to provide the next generation of farmers with skills to capitalise on the benefits of digitally enabled automated monitoring and management systems. Maintaining and supporting IT hardware platforms that have a dedicated agricultural application is a big jump from simply working out a feed budget. Ironically, the unique challenges of making electronics work in remote rugged locations may well mean that a broader range of industries will seek to recruit the next generation of agricultural graduates. As farmers acquire and apply new technical skills so these skills might end up being used by a wider range of industries. A broader uptake of agriculturally derived innovation might just lead to a more agriculturally minded nation. Farmers Farms Cattle Dave Swain, CQUniversity Australia Author Dave Swain Professor of Agriculture, CQUniversity Australia Disclosure statement Dave Swain receives funding from Meat and Livestock Australia. He is affiliated with CQUniversity and represents CQUniversity on the Northern Australian Beef Research Council. Partners CQUniversity Australia provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.sguegggvu","title":"Technology is changing the face of northern Australian cattle farming","url":"https://theconversation.com/technology-is-changing-the-face-of-northern-australian-cattle-farming-31552","id":362},{"content_hash":"bf549f8ea8f08280f78c6a23f232fb0d44e4a105c6fda4e8a15dbc2fd80dee33","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:49.066021+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/college-students-avoiding-scams.html","language":"","text":"Stay ahead of student cyber scams Students can reduce their risk of falling for common cybersecurity scams by limiting what they share online, learning how scammers operate and verifying unexpected requests before taking action. September 29, 2026 By Merrill. Reviewed by Bank of America’s Global Information Security team Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Key takeaways Scammers continually adapt their tactics and actively target students, particularly during periods of transition and increased financial activity.Many scams rely on social engineering, using publicly available information and psychological pressure to build trust and push students to act quickly.Limiting what you share online, especially personally identifiable information (PII) such as your address, phone number, class schedule or financial details, is a critical first line of defense against targeted scams.Always verify unexpected requests using a known, trusted contact method before sharing information or sending money. COLLEGE IS A TIME OF TRANSITION, with many students managing money, housing, tuition, jobs, loans and digital accounts on their own for the first time. With so much to keep track of, students may be more vulnerable to scammers who use social engineering tactics to create urgency, build trust and prompt quick action. Consider this: A student gets a call from someone claiming to be from their college’s financial aid office. The caller says the school has not received the student’s tuition payment and warns they will be dropped from their classes unless they provide payment information right away. The request feels urgent and credible because the caller already knows the student’s full name, age, school within the university and area of study. It’s a classic social engineering tactic, and the scale of these scams is growing. The Anti-Phishing Working Group (APWG) reported that phishing attacks increased 13.8% in early 2026. Scammers often use information gathered online, exposed through data breaches or found on social media to make their messages seem legitimate. By understanding the scammers’ tactics, students can take the steps necessary to avoid falling victim to them. Explore the growing problem — and possible solutions: What is social engineering? Common scams targeting students How can students reduce their risk? What should students do if they have already interacted with the scammer? FAQs: Student scams and social engineering It’s never too early to become cyber aware What is social engineering? Social engineering is a manipulation tactic that tricks people into sending money, sharing personal information or giving access to their accounts. Rather than hacking a device directly, scammers focus on influencing a person’s decisions. They may pose as a school employee, employer, lender, landlord, scholarship organization, friend or government representative to make the request seem legitimate. Social engineering works because it combines personal details with psychological pressure. A scammer may create urgency, offer a deal that seems too good to miss or pretend to represent a trusted organization. The goal is to get the student to act before they have time to pause, verify and think through the request. The risk is not limited to email. Verizon’s 2026 Data Breach Investigations Report (DBIR) notes that social engineering is evolving, with attackers increasingly using voice and mobile-focused techniques to catch people off guard.1 Scammers may contact students through: Phone callsText messagesEmailsSocial mediaMessaging appsFake websitesQR codesOnline marketplaces Common scams targeting students Scammers are always on the lookout for new ways to steal your personal and financial information. The following scams are commonly reported, and criminals are constantly creating new variations: 1. Tuition payment scams A scammer may pretend to work for a college or university and claim a tuition payment is late, missing or incomplete. They may threaten the student will be dropped from classes unless payment is made immediately. How to help protect yourself: Contact the school directly using the official website, student portal or verified phone number. Do not rely on contact information provided in the suspicious message. 2. Fake housing, textbook, moving or service listings These scams often begin with an online ad offering housing, used books, movers or other student services at a low price. After the student sends a deposit or partial payment, the listing turns out to be unavailable, nonexistent or fraudulent. How to help protect yourself: Be cautious with listings that require payment before receiving service, a contract or verified confirmation. Avoid payment methods that are difficult to reverse. 3. Student loan debt-relief scams Scammers may promise to reduce, eliminate or forgive student loans for a fee. They may claim to be affiliated with the U.S. Department of Education or ask for account credentials. Federal Student Aid warns that legitimate student loan help is available for free through StudentAid.gov and that the Department of Education and its partners will never ask for a StudentAid.gov username or password. How to help protect yourself: Do not pay upfront fees for student loan debt relief. Use StudentAid.gov or your official loan servicer for help with student loans. 4. Fake employment offers A job posting may seem ideal for a student: flexible hours, strong pay and minimal experience required. But after the student responds, the “employer” may ask for an upfront fee, bank details, a Social Security number or other sensitive information before confirming the job is legitimate. How to help protect yourself: Research the company, verify the recruiter and be cautious if a job asks for money or sensitive information early in the process. 5. Unsolicited scholarships and grants Students may receive calls, emails or messages saying they have won a grant or scholarship from an organization they do not recognize. The scammer may ask for a Social Security number, bank information or payment to “release” the award. How to help protect yourself: Verify the scholarship through the official organization or your school’s financial aid office before sharing information. 6. Social media scams Social media can reveal details about where students live, what school they attend, who they know and what groups they belong to. Criminals can use this information to build trust, impersonate others or tailor scams to a student’s interests and routines. Anti-Phishing Working Group, Inc (APWG) reported that in Q1 2026, threat volume increased on every social media platform it tracked, with scams and impersonation representing major threat categories.2 How to help protect yourself: Limit public personal details, review privacy settings and be cautious when someone you do not know asks for money, favors, account codes or personal information. 7. Ghost student and identity scams Criminals may use stolen or synthetic identities to enroll in schools or apply for financial aid. These “ghost students” may collect scholarships, aid payments or other funds and then disappear. In some cases, stolen identity information may also be used for additional fraud. How to help protect yourself: Monitor your financial accounts, credit reports and school records for unfamiliar activity. How can students reduce their risk? Students can take practical steps to make scams easier to spot and harder to execute. 1. Pause before acting Scammers often depend on urgency. If a message says you must act immediately, slow down. Take time to verify the request. 2. Verify unexpected requests Before sending money, clicking a link or sharing information, verify the request through a trusted contact method. Use a bookmarked website, type the official website into your browser or call a verified phone number. 3. Share less personal information online Limit sharing public details such as your address, phone number, class schedule, travel plans, student ID information and financial details. 4. Review privacy settings Check privacy settings on social media, messaging apps, financial apps and school accounts. Make sure personal information is visible only to people who need it. 5. Protect account credentials Do not share passwords, one-time passcodes, StudentAid.gov credentials or banking credentials with anyone who contacts you unexpectedly. Federal Student Aid states that the U.S. Department of Education and its partners will never ask for your StudentAid.gov username and password. 6. Be skeptical of “too good to be true” offers Deep discounts, guaranteed loan forgiveness, high-paying jobs with little effort and surprise scholarships should all be verified before you act. 7. Report suspected scams If you think you have been targeted, report the scam. Federal Student Aid recommends contacting your loan servicer, bank or credit card company when relevant and filing complaints with appropriate agencies, including the FTC and CFPB. What should students do if they have already interacted with the scammer? If you sent money, clicked a suspicious link or shared personal information, act quickly. Contact your bank or credit card company to stop or dispute payments. Change affected passwords, especially for email, banking, school and StudentAid.gov accounts. Contact your loan servicer if the scam involved federal student loans. Report the scam to the FTC at ReportFraud.ftc.gov and to other relevant agencies.Monitor accounts and credit reports for unfamiliar activity. FAQs: Student scams and social engineering How do scammers get personal information about students? Scammers may gather personal details from social media, public directories, exposed data from breaches, school-related posts or information shared across online platforms. They use those details to make a message sound more credible. What is the biggest warning sign of a student scam? A major warning sign is pressure to act immediately, especially when the request involves money, account credentials, personal information or a threat of losing access to classes, housing, loans or financial aid.3 Are student loan forgiveness calls always scams? Not every student loan communication is fraudulent, but students should be cautious with calls, texts or emails that promise fast forgiveness, ask for upfront fees or request StudentAid.gov login information. Federal Student Aid says federal loan help is available through official servicers and StudentAid.gov at no cost.4 What is the safest way to verify a message from a school or lender? Use a trusted contact method. Visit the official website by typing the URL into your browser, use a bookmarked portal or call a verified phone number. Do not use links, phone numbers or attachments from a suspicious message.4 Why are students targeted by scammers? Students are often managing tuition, loans, housing, jobs, scholarships and new digital accounts, creating opportunities for scammers to impersonate schools, employers, landlords, lenders or service providers. It’s never too early to become cyber aware Cyber awareness is not just a college skill. As students move from campus life into the workplace, the same habits help protect their identities, finances, families and future employers. By learning how scammers operate, limiting what they share online and verifying unexpected requests, students can reduce their risk and help others do the same. Stay connected, stay protected To help keep your Merrill account information safe and secure, make sure your contact information is up to date and set up security and account alerts so we can stay in touch. Remember, if we need to reach out to you, we’ll NEVER ask for personal or financial information or an access code through email, text or unsolicited calls. Visit our Security Center for tips on how to recognize potential scams and learn more about how to keep your accounts safe. Discover more with Merrill Work one-on-one with a Merrill advisor for more insights and personalized guidance. Connect with us today. Loading... Have us contact you Contact me Try Merrill Advisor Match Match with advisors Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 Verizon, 2026 Data Breach Investigations Report, accessed August 2026. 2 APWG, Phishing Activity Trends Reports, Q1 2026 summary, accessed August 2026. 3 Federal Trade Commission, Student Loan and Education Scams, accessed August 2026. 4 Federal Student Aid, How To Avoid Student Loan Forgiveness Scams, accessed August 2026. Neither Bank of America nor its affiliates provide information security or information technology (IT) consulting services. This material is provided “as is,” with no guarantee of completeness, accuracy, timeliness or of the results obtained from the use of this material, and without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including, but not limited to warranties of performance, quality and fitness for a particular purpose. This material should be regarded as general information on information security and IT considerations and is not intended to provide specific information security or IT advice nor is it any substitute for your own independent investigations. If you have questions regarding your particular IT system or information security concerns, please contact your IT or information security advisor. The BullPen Timely insights to stay ahead of the curve September 9, 2026 Today's Markets Market briefs August 18, 2026 Financial Planning Planning for college: Investment accounts to help you pay for education July 21, 2026 Financial Planning Charitable giving unlocked for business owners under the OBBBA July 17, 2026 Financial Planning HSAs explained: The top benefits of health savings accounts July 15, 2026 Retirement Ways to replace your salary in retirement More for you Be cyber secure: Dos and don’ts for your family Help protect against online shopping scams Be cyber secure: Hone your password-writing skills with this quiz","title":"Stay ahead of student cyber scams","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/college-students-avoiding-scams.html","id":363},{"content_hash":"51581e68699044363c9be0d77e62286c60b82eb8cea210d9a3e93af8a4e4c37a","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:48.867007+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-29/what-the-rba-cash-rate-hike-means-for-mortgages-rents-savings/107203364","language":"","text":"What an interest rate hike means for your mortgage, savings account and the Australian dollarHBy Hanan DervisevicStory LabTopic:Interest RatesPosted Tue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:39pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:39pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:39pm, updated Tue 29 Sep 2026 at 4:19pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 4:19pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 4:19pmThe RBA rate rise impacts more than just your home loan. (ABC News: Teresa Tan/Declan Bowring)abc.net.au/news/what-the-rba-cash-rate-hike-means-for-mortgages-rents-savings/107203364Link copiedShareShare articleFor the fourth time this year, the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) has lifted the cash rate by 0.25 of a percentage point in a bid to bring inflation under control.While the RBA's decisions are most felt through mortgage repayments, their impact extends well beyond home loans.Here's a breakdown of how the latest increase will affect households differently, depending on whether Australians are paying off a mortgage, renting, or relying on savings.Where are we at with the cash rate?To first understand how the cash rate impacts different parts of our hip pocket, let's take a step back.The cash rate, set by the RBA, is Australia's official interest rate charged on unsecured overnight loans between banks.In simple terms, it's how much it costs banks to borrow from each other. The RBA's decision to increase the official cash rate makes it more expensive for lenders to borrow money.That means banks will almost certainly charge customers a higher interest rate to still make a profit because they are paying more to borrow the money in the first place.On Tuesday, the RBA increased the cash rate to 4.6 per cent in an attempt to slow down inflation in the economy.Commonwealth Bank head of Australian economics Belinda Allen says the central bank has lost its patience, given inflation has sat above the target band for most of the past six years.\"Higher interest rates make borrowing more expensive and saving more attractive,\" Ms Allen says.\"That tends to slow household spending and business investment, reducing some of the demand pressures in the economy and, over time, helping bring inflation back towards the RBA's 2–3 per cent target.\"How the cash rate increase could affect youMortgagesRead more Read more Rate rises are felt fastest, and most sharply, by Australians with variable-rate mortgages because banks typically pass on RBA increases in full.AMP chief economist Shane Oliver says the cash rate is the main tool steering borrowing costs and households should expect lenders to adjust quickly.\"As we've seen with the first three hikes this year, the banks will likely announce a 0.25 per cent increase in their mortgage rates on the same day,\" Dr Oliver says.Ms Allen says households with a mortgage may lower spending or use savings to keep spending as a result of higher interest rates.\"This cash flow channel is one way monetary policy works to impact demand in the economy,\" she says.Each 0.25 per cent rise in the cash rate adds roughly $91 per month to repayments on a $600,000 variable-rate loan with 25 years remaining. The four 2026 increases totalling 0.75 per cent have added approximately $360 per month for borrowers on a $600,000 loan.That equates to about $4,320 extra per year.Here's how much a September rate hike will cost borrowers:It is not just existing mortgage borrowers hit by the rate rises though.For prospective buyers, Canstar estimates that the borrowing capacity of someone on an average full-time wage of $108,650 would be reduced by more than $47,000, while a couple both on average wages would see a reduction of nearly $95,000.If you are on a fixed-rate home loan, you won't see any changes. Those rates are locked in.But when the fixed term expires and rolls to a variable rate, the full impact of any rate cycle will apply at once.RentsRead more Read more For renters, the interest rate impact is more indirect.Some landlords may try to offset the impact of rate rises on their cash flow by raising rents over time, although there are constraints on how often and how much rent can be increased each year in most states.The RBA has also dismissed the idea that landlords pass their increased borrowing costs on to renters.Speaking on 9Now's The Pay Off podcast, RBA chief economist Sarah Hunter said what was really driving rents came down to supply and demand.\"If you're an individual landlord, you own one property in one rental market, you might want to put the rent up when your interest rate goes up if you've got a mortgage,\" Dr Hunter said.\"But you're competing with all the other properties that are available … and so, it's demand and supply; it's the local market that will equilibrate rent.\"If rents are tracking up, it's usually because demand growth is stronger than supply growth.\"Ms Allen and Dr Oliver agree with this logic.Cotality's quarterly rental review found rents rose by 1.6 per cent nationally in the June quarter, driven by a severe lack of available stock across the country.The national dwelling vacancy rate sat at 1.6 per cent, unchanged from March and remaining below the five-year average of 1.8 per cent.\"The main factor driving rents is the shortage of rental property,\" Dr Oliver says.SavingsRead more Read more There is one silver lining in the rate rise, for some. Savings accounts and term deposit interest rates usually rise after an RBA hike, making it more attractive to park your money.This is because, while the cash rate also affects borrowing costs for banks, it also sets the rate of return for their own deposits. Cash rate hikes improve their returns, which may be passed on to consumers through higher savings interest rates.Dr Oliver says while short-term deposit rates largely move in line with the cash rate, longer-term term deposit rates are also shaped by government bond yields and other borrowing costs.With today's RBA increase and bond yields already rising, he says savers are likely to benefit from higher returns.\"The odds are, yes, we will see deposit rates continue to rise.\"Australian dollarRead more Read more Higher interest rates tend to strengthen the Australian dollar (AUD).And that's because it's more attractive for foreign investors to park their money in Australian interest-bearing assets (like savings accounts, term deposits, and government bonds) because they can get a higher rate of interest here.This increases demand for the AUD, making the currency more valuable.That generally means imported products can cost less in Australian-dollar terms and overseas travel becomes cheaper.But Ms Allen says the Australian dollar's value depends on far more than the RBA's cash rate decisions.\"The Australian dollar is influenced by many things — global interest rates, commodity prices, China, investor sentiment and expectations about the Australian economy — so you can't say that a 25-basis-point rate hike will automatically result in a particular move in the dollar,\" she says.Dr Oliver agrees, noting that with the US Federal Reserve also tightening monetary policy, any upward pressure on the Australian dollar may be offset.\"I suspect it probably just keeps the Aussie dollar around where it is,\" he says.Car and personal loansRead more Read more Car loans and personal loans can be affected by increases in the cash rate.But it all comes down to what type of loan you have.Australians collectively borrowed about $4.7 billion in fixed-term personal loans for road vehicles in the three months to June 2026, according to the Australian Bureau of Statistics.These loans would not be affected by the cash rate until their fixed-rate period ends.For those on a variable rate, Dr Oliver says banks may not necessarily hike rates by the full 0.25 per cent, but \"the odds are they'll probably go up over time as well\".Is this the last rate hike for the year?We don't know.The majority of analysts surveyed by Bloomberg expect the RBA to be done with hikes for the rest of the year.Loading...Westpac, NAB and CBA are also in agreement.In a weekly market update last Friday, Dr Oliver summed up the majority view.\"By the time it gets to the November meeting, there is likely to be more evidence of a cooling economy, falling home prices, a softer jobs market and rising recession risks, so we don't think a second hike let alone a third will be necessary,\" he wrote.ANZ is the only major bank so far officially tipping a November rise as well, alongside HSBC and UBS.Ms Allen says it all depends on the near-term movements in inflation data.The RBA will deliver its next cash rate decision on Tuesday, November 3.Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsAustraliaBankingCredit and LendingEconomic Trends and IndicatorsHousing Construction IndustryInterest RatesMonetary PolicyStock MarketTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"What an interest rate hike means for your mortgage, savings account and the Australian dollar","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-29/what-the-rba-cash-rate-hike-means-for-mortgages-rents-savings/107203364","id":364},{"content_hash":"890f54412c50ba6b6e39cfa319977066c7826fd42c66b9952ef0e18f95dab3d8","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:48.465443+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-supply-chains-link-us-all-to-shame-of-child-and-forced-labour-33593","language":"","text":"Global supply chains link us all to shame of child and forced labour Published: October 30, 2014 1:09am EDT Over half of Australia’s imported goods come from the Asia Pacific, which has 78 million child labourers, including these three in a Bangladesh balloon factory. EPA/Abir Abdhullah https://theconversation.com/global-supply-chains-link-us-all-to-shame-of-child-and-forced-labour-33593 https://theconversation.com/global-supply-chains-link-us-all-to-shame-of-child-and-forced-labour-33593 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The fragmentation of global production has dramatically increased the length and complexity of supply chains. The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) estimates that more than half of the world’s manufactured imports are intermediate goods. These are used as inputs in the production of other goods, sourced from different parts of the globe. A serious problem with such long and complex supply chains is that this can lead to a lack of oversight and worker exploitation such as the use of child and forced labour, for estimated profits of US$150 billion a year. At our end of the supply chain, demand for low-cost goods can push suppliers towards abusive practices. These malpractices can affect individuals, producers and consumers anywhere in global supply chains. This makes Australia part of the problem and the potential solution. Increasingly, companies and investors work with trade unions and NGOs to deal with labour and human rights abuses in supply chains. Yet, in a report launched this week, Catalyst Australia shows that misunderstandings about child labour persist and room for improvement remains. The report coincides with the Australian government’s announcement of a Supply Chains Working Group to tackle these problems. Child labour remains widespread Global initiatives and national laws have not made child labour history. Although it is estimated to have declined by 33% since 2000, 168 million children continue to be exploited worldwide. While global conventions are in place, their existence does not guarantee local take-up, nor does the existence of national child labour laws mean they are actively enforced. A false perception persists that child labour affects only developing countries. However, in 2013, more than half of Australia’s imported goods came from the Asia-Pacific region, which has the largest absolute number of child labourers: 78 million. It is also incorrect that only isolated industries use child labour. While 59% of child labour happens in agricultural settings, the manufacturing and services sector are significant contributors. Catalyst Australia finds that self-regulatory standards and voluntary initiatives alone do not drive change. They are often merely public relations tools. Neither do charitable donations absolve companies of their responsibilities. As John Ruggie, author of the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, states: There is no equivalent to buying carbon offsets in human rights: philanthropic good deeds do not compensate for infringing on human rights. Forced labour generates huge profits The International Labour Organisation (ILO) estimates that 20.9 million people globally are subjected to forced labour, 90% of whom are exploited in the private sector. Of these individuals, 68% are forced to work in agriculture, construction, domestic work, mining and manufacturing. The Asia-Pacific region accounts for 11.7 million individuals in forced labour – 56% of the global total. The ILO estimates that forced labour in the private economy generates US$150 billion in profits annually. Two-thirds is estimated to come from sexual exploitation. The rest comes from forced labour in construction, manufacturing, mining and utilities (US$34 billion), agriculture, forestry and fishing (US$9 billion) and households not paying or underpaying domestic workers held in forced labour (US$8 billion). Supply chains put under scrutiny Michael Keenan has announced plans to tackle import supply chains that exploit child labour. AAP/Julian Smith Federal justice minister Michael Keenan spoke this week of the formation of a Supply Chains Working Group, which will examine ways to overcome exploitative practices in the production of goods and services. The government is developing a National Action Plan to Combat Human Trafficking and Slavery, to be launched in coming months. The working group has the challenge of tackling labour and human rights abuses in the supply chain of goods imported into Australia. The composition of the group will be critical to its success. Ideally, it will include representatives of government, industry bodies, businesses and investment funds, as well as members with academic and civil society backgrounds. The Catalyst Australia report identified that a common obstacle to improving labour and human rights in supply chains involves stakeholders going their own way. Distinctive partnerships are pivotal to an effective response. Flagging concerns, consulting (local) experts and expanding existing knowledge are essential elements of such a response. What can we do to end abuses? While active government involvement through legally enforceable standards is desirable, merely having laws against labour exploitation does not stop abuses. Many abuses occur outside legal frameworks, such as in the informal economy. Increasing global co-ordination can cause discrepancies between proposed measures and their local effect, whether through legislation or self-regulation. This underlines the need for closer alignment of initiatives and partnerships at all levels. Proposed measures should avoid a “one size fits all” approach. Solutions require pragmatic mapping of the local landscape and the issue, ongoing dialogue to see which stakeholders are on board, who can be influenced and which approaches best suit the country and industry context. Examples of sector and country-specific approaches can provide useful guidance, but only when these suit particular circumstances do they drive improvement and have maximum impact. It is important to note that labour and human rights risks are significantly reduced where workers are allowed to organise freely and have representative trade unions. Consequently, any stakeholder who is serious about tackling these issues must be serious about supporting a free trade union movement and be willing to engage in continuing worker dialogue. Finally, due diligence must include responsibility for human rights. Companies often narrowly define due diligence as economic and reputational risk. By scrutinising potential business partners in advance, the notion of business responsibility takes a precautionary turn. We need to shift from merely auditing existing activities, towards promoting, protecting and advancing labour and human rights. In this way businesses can help minimise abuses and play a transformative role in all regions where they operate. Asia Pacific Labour Child abuse Slavery Forced labour Supply chains Child labour manufactured imports Global supply chains Imported goods Michael Keenan Rights abuses Martijn Boersma, University of Technology Sydney Author Martijn Boersma Researcher in Corporate Governance, University of Technology Sydney Disclosure statement Martijn Boersma works for Catalyst Australia. Partners University of Technology Sydney provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.chnygsksv","title":"Global supply chains link us all to shame of child and forced labour","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-supply-chains-link-us-all-to-shame-of-child-and-forced-labour-33593","id":365},{"content_hash":"036a7d2bd2f098cc739879b0b28039475cc3430c4f3c0956028f80211969554f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:48.364428+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-journees-d-etudes-du-logement-se-sont-ouvertes-ce-matin_2071960_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives LES JOURNÉES D'ÉTUDES DU LOGEMENT se sont ouvertes ce matin Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"LES JOURNÉES D'ÉTUDES DU LOGEMENT se sont ouvertes ce matin","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-journees-d-etudes-du-logement-se-sont-ouvertes-ce-matin_2071960_1819218.html","id":366},{"content_hash":"72f8749adcbf5ab2d34b3acfc67fd5f4a4c801fbb67afd3f9a204941a5237686","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.656572+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/topic/economy","language":"","text":"Economy - Topic - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenEconomyBig bank defends offshoring of jobs at parliamentary inquiry into AILIVETopic:Stock MarketTopic:Financial MarketsANZ, Westpac and NAB are giving evidence about how they are using artificial intelligence. Follow the day's events and insights from our business reporters on the ABC News live markets blog.Former RBA member explains what happens inside interest rate meetingsTopic:Interest RatesRecession strikes fear into many and pain isn't evenly sharedTopic:EconomyASX edges higher supported by miners, housing market weakens as auction clearance rates fall — as it happenedTopic:Stock MarketAustralia faces 'biggest property downturn in 40 years', raising recession riskTopic:Property PricesASX edges higher, Northern Star jumps on $38bn takeover offerThe ASX has edged higher after Wall Street closed a volatile week on an upbeat note. Follow the day's events and insights from our business reporters on the ABC News live markets blog. LIVETopic:Stock MarketMon 28 Sep 2026 at 1:19pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 1:19pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 1:19pmMigration cuts, AI and rising interest rates mean an era of long-term disinflationThe Reserve Bank will increase interest rates on Tuesday, but three new factors entirely outside the bank's control are pulling rates in different directions.AAnalysis by Alan KohlerTue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:48pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:48pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 2:48pmChina, US agree to AI dialogue, $42b in tariff cuts during state visitThe tariff reductions are set to apply to US exports such as agricultural goods, wood and cosmetics, as well as US imports including small appliances, toys, decorations.Topic:World PoliticsSun 27 Sep 2026 at 6:33amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 6:33amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 6:33amThree decades of work to restore one of Australia's most degraded catchmentsCommunity effort transforms a degraded waterway, reducing erosion, increasing fish breeding and improving the quality of water that flows into the Great Barrier Reef. Topic:AgricultureSat 26 Sep 2026 at 5:48amSat 26 Sep 2026 at 5:48amSat 26 Sep 2026 at 5:48amMyer posts $276m annual loss on 'weak' consumer spending, ASX edges higher, NAB hit by IT outage — as it happenedDepartment store chain Myer reported a full-year loss of $276 million amid a \"material downturn\" in consumer sentiment. The ASX edged higherTopic:Stock MarketWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:07pmWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:07pmWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:07pmRates set to rise as RBA governor says 'heat' needs to be taken out of job marketAll four major banks and the bulk of money market traders believe the Reserve Bank will raise interest rates next week and governor Michele Bullock did nothing to dissuade them during a speech in Sydney.Topic:Interest RatesTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:12pmTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:12pmTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:12pmWhy younger Australians are facing a tough economic futureIn 40 years, with an estimated population of 40 million, Australia will be more indebted and face what increasingly appears to be a permanent budget deficit.IAnalysis by Ian VerrenderTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:33amTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:33amTue 22 Sep 2026 at 5:33amCBA, ANZ bring forward rate hike forecast, ASX closes flat — as it happenedCBA and ANZ switch their calls to RBA rate hike next week while the ASX lacked direction and closed flat.Topic:Stock MarketMon 21 Sep 2026 at 5:43pmMon 21 Sep 2026 at 5:43pmMon 21 Sep 2026 at 5:43pmRBA leaders warn that interest rates are just back to 'sensible level'If you are struggling with current interest rates, the Reserve Bank's leaders delivered the sobering message that they are only just getting back to a \"more sensible level\".Topic:Interest RatesFri 18 Sep 2026 at 2:31pmFri 18 Sep 2026 at 2:31pmFri 18 Sep 2026 at 2:31pmKenilworth Dairies offers lifeline to Queensland dairy farm facing closure A Queensland family that feared 80 years of dairying would end after a contract was not renewed has been offered a last-minute lifeline.Topic:Dairy FarmingWed 16 Sep 2026 at 1:22pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 1:22pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 1:22pmTasmania comes last in latest CommBank economic rankingsTasmania ranks last in CommBank's State of the States economic report, a significant decline from four years ago when it repeatedly topped the ranking. Topic:EconomyTue 15 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmThere are three main reasons inflation is rising. Higher rates won't fix themSome of the main drivers of high inflation are supply issues caused by the US-Iran war and American tech giants spending huge amounts on AI, which are not problems a rate hike will solve.MAnalysis by Michael JandaTue 15 Sep 2026 at 8:03amTue 15 Sep 2026 at 8:03amTue 15 Sep 2026 at 8:03amHealthscope to be taken over by Calvary Health Care consortium — as it happenedA consortium led by Calvary Health Care will take over the remaining 25 Healthscope hospitals it does not already own. The ASX finished flat as higher oil prices stoked inflation and rate rise worries.Topic:Stock MarketMon 14 Sep 2026 at 3:11pmMon 14 Sep 2026 at 3:11pmMon 14 Sep 2026 at 3:11pmNew pest insect breaches biosecurity and destroys berry cropsInvasive flower thrips, detected in Australia for the first time, are destroying millions of dollars' worth of berry crops. The pest could spread to vegetables and flowers, and increase food prices.Topic:AgricultureFri 11 Sep 2026 at 9:28amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 9:28amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 9:28am'People are furious': RBA deputy governor says more needs to be done on inflationThe RBA's deputy governor issues a warning that interest rates could rise further if the ongoing scourge of inflation does not come under control soon.Topic:Reserve Bank Of AustraliaWed 9 Sep 2026 at 10:20amWed 9 Sep 2026 at 10:20amWed 9 Sep 2026 at 10:20amASX closes at six-week low as Westpac predicts November rate hike — as it happenedThe Australian share market closed at a six-week low on Tuesday, while Westpac became the last of the big four banks to predict an interest rate hike by the RBA in November.Topic:Stock MarketTue 8 Sep 2026 at 5:11pmTue 8 Sep 2026 at 5:11pmTue 8 Sep 2026 at 5:11pm Bathla standdowns start in short-term rescue package, ASX inches higher as oil price rises — as it happenedStanddowns at the collapsed property developer Bathla have started as its administrator secures a two-week funding lifeline to keep parts of the business running.Topic:Stock MarketMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:47pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:47pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:47pmGovernment offers small businesses a trust tax workaroundThose with discretionary trusts can be exempt from the new minimum 30 per cent tax if they agree to hold their trust distributions constant.Topic:Federal GovernmentThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmThu 3 Sep 2026 at 4:07pmWhat is Australia’s economic outlook?The Australian economy has defied expectations as it has grown slightly despite a cost-of-living crunch that hit household spending across the country.Topic:EconomyWed 2 Sep 2026 at 9:21pmWed 2 Sep 2026 at 9:21pmWed 2 Sep 2026 at 9:21pmHas Video Duration: 9 minutes 48 seconds.9m Oil prices surge on latest US-Iran flare-up, ASX slips as housing slump continues — as it happenedThe latest flare-up in hostilities between the US and Iran has led to a 3 per cent surge in the oil price. The Australian share market slips while house prices are falling in most of the nation's capital cities. Topic:Stock MarketTue 1 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmTue 1 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmTue 1 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmLoad more stories aboveBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Economy","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/topic/economy","id":367},{"content_hash":"e8e1d23af3f9f280f04500001d29b37a103a082fc259cacf83ea057391cdb3c8","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.367593+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/id-verification-in-kenya","language":"","text":"ID Verification in Kenya: How to Check Your ID Status Online in KenyaByTemitope LawalApril 4, 2023•5mins ReadKey Takeaways1. You can check your ID status online in Kenya through the eCitizen portal or by sending your waiting card serial number via SMS to the official Huduma short code.2. Kenya's identity system is transitioning to the Maisha Card and Maisha Namba, which replaced the paused Huduma Namba/NIIMS rollout after August 20243. If your ID is lost, stolen, or damaged, you'll need to apply for an ID replacement separately; this is a different process from checking the status of a new ID application.How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya (2026 Update)Quick Answer: Check Your ID Status in KenyaWhere to Find Your Waiting Card Serial NumberHow to Check ID Status in Kenya by SMSMaisha Namba and the Maisha Card: What Changed in Kenya's ID System \"Since 2024How to Check ID Status Online in KenyaHow to Check If Your ID Is Ready for CollectionHow Long Does It Take to Get a National ID in Kenya?Checking Your ID Status at the Chief's or DO's OfficeWhat If Your ID Was Lost, Stolen, or Damaged?How to Do a Kenyan ID Number CheckWhat is ID Verification in Kenya?What is IPRS ID Verification Kenya?What is the use of IPRS ID Verification Kenya?ID Verification Methods for Businesses in Kenya?How to Choose a KYC and ID Verification Provider in KenyaVerify IDs in Kenya with YouverifyHow to Choose a KYC Solution Provider for ID Verification in KenyaHow Businesses Verify Kenyan IDs at Scale (vs. Manual Checking) Outline:Achieve a Seamless ID Verification In Kenya with YouverifyShare this post How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya (2026 Update) To check your ID status online in Kenya, send the 9-digit serial number on your waiting card by SMS to 20031, or enter the same serial number on the National ID status portal at identity.go.ke/search. Both methods tell you whether your ID card is still processing or ready for collection. SMS is the most reliable option, because the online portal is sometimes unavailable. Quick Answer: Check Your ID Status in Kenya By SMS (most reliable):Open a new text message.Type only the 9-digit serial number printed on your waiting card.Send it to 20031.You receive a reply from 20031 with your ID status.The SMS costs about KES 5, and the reply can occasionally take up to a few days during busy periods. If it delays, resend the same serial number.Online:Go to identity.go.ke/search.Open the National ID section.Select Status.Enter your 9-digit waiting card serial number.Submit to view your status.If the online portal does not load or returns nothing, use the SMS method instead. Portal availability has been inconsistent, and SMS to 20031 is the method that works most consistently. Where to Find Your Waiting Card Serial NumberYou cannot check your ID status without your waiting card serial number, so this is the first thing to locate.The waiting card is the paper slip issued to you at the Huduma Centre or National Registration Bureau (NRB) office immediately after your fingerprints and photo were captured. Printed on it is a unique 9-digit serial number tied to your application. That number is what both the SMS service and the online portal use to look up your status. Keep the waiting card safe and dry: it is your only proof of a pending application, and you must surrender it when you collect your finished ID.If you have lost your waiting card, you will need to visit the Huduma Centre or NRB office where you applied to retrieve your serial number, as it is not recoverable by name online. How to Check ID Status in Kenya by SMSThe SMS method works on any phone, needs no internet, and is the method most Kenyans rely on because it does not depend on the portal being online.Create a new text message.Type only your 9-digit waiting card serial number, with no extra words.Send the message to 20031.Wait for the reply from 20031, which states whether your ID is still being processed or is ready for collection.Keep at least KES 5 of airtime on your line. If you do not get a reply immediately, the system is sometimes slow, so wait and resend the same serial number after a while. Maisha Namba and the Maisha Card: What Changed in Kenya's ID System \"Since 2024 Kenya’s digital identity program is transitioning from the older second-generation national ID system toward the Maisha ecosystem, which includes Maisha Namba, a lifelong unique personal identifier, and the chip-enabled Maisha Card. The program faced court challenges and delays, and reporting in 2024 indicates the rollout moved forward again after legal hurdles were cleared. How to Check ID Status Online in KenyaIf you prefer to check on a browser, use the National ID status portal. Visit identity.go.ke/search.Click into the National ID section.Select Status.Enter the 9-digit serial number from your waiting card.Submit to see whether your ID is ready. One honest caveat: the online national ID search portal is not always available, and at times it has been down for extended periods. If the page fails to load or returns no result, this is a known issue with the portal rather than a problem with your application. Fall back to the SMS method to 20031, which is more consistently available. How to Check If Your ID Is Ready for CollectionBoth the SMS and online methods return one of two outcomes. Either your ID is still being processed, or it is ready for collection.Once the status shows ready, collect your ID from the same office where you applied, whether that is a Huduma Centre, the National Registration Bureau, or your local Chief's or District Officer's (DO's) office. Bring your waiting card, because you must hand it over to receive your ID. First-time IDs are free to collect.If weeks have passed and your status has not changed, it is worth visiting the office in person. Occasionally a card is ready but the status has not updated, and staff can confirm on the spot. How Long Does It Take to Get a National ID in Kenya?For most first-time applicants, a Kenyan national ID takes roughly two to four weeks to process after biometric capture, though official guidance sometimes quotes about 10 working days. The actual wait depends on application volumes and how quickly cards are printed and distributed to collection points.Because timelines vary, the practical approach is to start checking your status by SMS to 20031 about two weeks after your biometrics were taken, then check every few days until it shows ready. Checking Your ID Status at the Chief's or DO's OfficeBefore SMS and online checks existed, the only way to check an ID status was to visit the local Chief's or District Officer's office in person. This method still works and remains useful in rural areas with limited network coverage.Bring your waiting card to the office, and staff can look up whether your ID has arrived for collection. For most people, though, the SMS method to 20031 is faster and avoids the trip. What If Your ID Was Lost, Stolen, or Damaged?The steps above are for checking a new ID application. If your existing ID was lost, stolen, or damaged, that is a different process called ID replacement, handled through the National Registration Bureau and the eCitizen portal, and it involves a police abstract and a separate fee.For the full replacement process, see our dedicated guide on how to replace a lost or damaged ID card in Kenya. How to Do a Kenyan ID Number CheckA Kenyan ID number check confirms that an ID number is valid and matches the person presenting it, by checking it against the government's population register. This is different from checking your ID application status, which tracks a pending card.For individuals, your own ID details sit in your eCitizen profile once your ID is issued. For businesses, verifying a customer's ID number is done against the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS), explained below, usually through a licensed verification provider rather than manually. Identity verification in Kenya has become increasingly important as more businesses shift towards online transactions and remote operations in Kenya, making it essential to have a reliable way to verify identities. For instance, many organizations now recommend a Kenyan ID number check before proceeding with any transaction. Also, digital commerce in Kenya is expected to grow annually between 13% and 16.4%, anticipating a market worth over $1 billion by 2029, driving demand for robust digital ID. Quick Answer: How to Check Your ID Status Online in KenyaIf you simply want to check my ID status online in Kenya right now, here's the fastest way:Visit the official eCitizen ID status portal.Go to the \"National ID\" section.Enter your waiting card serial number.Click \"Submit\" to view your status.Alternatively, send your serial number via SMS to the official Huduma short code. Kenya, like many other countries, has established regulations and protocols to ensure that individuals and businesses can authenticate their identities for legal and compliance reasons. In this blog post, we will explore the ID verification landscape in Kenya, including IPRD ID verification in Kenya, and some FAQs, such as how to check ID verification status in Kenya. Additionally, some services even offer a search ID number name in Kenya feature to assist users who may not have their ID number readily available. What is ID Verification in Kenya? Id verification in Kenya refers to the process of confirming the identity of an individual by verifying their personal information against the Kenyan government's official records and databases. The process is usually carried out by government agencies, financial institutions, and other organizations that require proof of identity from their clients or customers. In Kenya, the government has put in place various measures and systems for ID verification, including the National Integrated Identity Management System (NIIMS), commonly known as Huduma Namba, which is a unique identification number that is assigned to all Kenyan citizens and residents. This system collects biometric and personal information from individuals, such as fingerprints and facial recognition, ensuring a thorough Kenyan ID number search when needed. Financial institutions in Kenya also use identity verification systems to comply with the Know Your Customer (KYC) regulations. KYC requires that banks and other financial institutions verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. In summary, id verification in Kenya involves verifying an individual's personal information against official records to ensure that they are who they claim to be. The process is used by various organizations for different purposes, including government programs, financial transactions, and access to various services. What is IPRS ID Verification Kenya? The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya is a computerized database that collects and stores demographic data on Kenyan citizens and residents. It is used to maintain a comprehensive record of the population of Kenya, which includes personal identification details, biometric data, and other relevant information. This system serves as a robust IPRS ID verification Kenya tool. The IPRS is managed by the National Registration Bureau (NRB), which is a government agency under the Ministry of Interior and Coordination of National Government. The system is designed to provide a unique identification number to every citizen and resident in Kenya and is used for various government programs and services, such as voter registration, census taking, social services provision, and identity verification. In essence, the IPRS also functions as an effective IPRS checker for verifying identities across different sectors. What is the use of IPRS ID Verification Kenya? The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya is used for identity verification purposes by various organizations, including government agencies, financial institutions, and other private entities. The system collects and stores personal identification details and biometric data of Kenyan citizens and residents, which can be used to verify an individual's identity. The use of IPRS id verification in Kenya is aimed at reducing the risk of identity fraud and improving security. The system enables organizations to confirm that the personal details provided by an individual match those in the government's database, making it easier to verify the identity of individuals. For example, financial institutions in Kenya use the IPRS for KYC compliance, which requires them to verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. This process essentially performs an ID number check online that is both quick and efficient. For example, financial institutions in Kenya use the IPRS for Know Your Customer (KYC) compliance, which requires them to verify the identity of their customers before providing them with services or products. The IPRS ID verification in Kenya is used to confirm the identity of individuals and to ensure that they are who they claim to be. This helps to prevent identity theft and other forms of financial fraud. The IPRS is also used for voter registration in Kenya. During the voter registration process, the IPRS is used to verify the identity of individuals and ensure that only eligible voters are registered. This helps to prevent electoral fraud and to ensure the integrity of the electoral process. In summary, the use of the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) in Kenya for identity verification purposes is aimed at reducing the risk of identity fraud and improving security. The system is widely used by various organizations to verify the identity of individuals, including financial institutions, government agencies, and electoral bodies. Related: Benefits of Business Identity Verification for Kenyan Startups ID Verification Methods for Businesses in Kenya? An accurate and secure ID verification in Kenya is important for businesses, government agencies, banks, fintech, and the payment industry. There are several methods commonly used to verify customers' identities, which include: 1. Document VerificationDocument verification is the process of checking or verifying the authenticity of identity documents such as national IDs, driver's licenses or passports. Today, technologies such as MRZ (machine-readable zone), is used to extract and match data on documents against the government database to confirm validity. In Kenya, it is one of the most commonly used methods of ID verification in basic KYC processes. 2. Government Data SourcesCustomer ID Verification in Kenya can also be carried out by directly verifying the details on the government database, a process known as government KYC check. Examples of documents commonly verified from the government databases in Kenya may include: i. National ID: The national ID card in Kenya is issued by the National Registration Bureau (NRB) to citizens 18 years and above. This ensures that every citizen has a unique identifier. The ID card includes details such as name, date of birth, and ID number, all of which can be verified through the official NRB website.ii. Passport:The Kenyan passport is issued by the Department of immigration services, and it serves as bth an international travel document and a valid means of identification. It contains personal details such as nationality, date of birth that match the government's record.iii. Driver's License: The Kenyan driver's license is issued by the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA). It contains the driver's name, license number and DOB. The Kenyan driver's license can be verified against the NTSA's record to confirm its authenticity.Aside from the above-mentioned documents for ID verification in Kenya, other documents can be used such as Kenyan refugee ID, KRA pin certificate, alien card, etc. 3. Commercial and Credit Data Sources i. Credit Data Sources:Credit bureaus provide credit reports that not only help businesses to verify their customers' identities, but also give them insight into an individual's financial behaviour and creditworthiness.ii. Commercial Data Sources:Commercial databases allow businesses to confirm their customers' information, such as employment records and financial history. This offers an additional layer of verification. If your ID verification is complete, you will be able to access various government services and programs. If your ID verification is incomplete, you may need to visit a Huduma Namba registration center to complete the process. In summary, checking your ID verification status in Kenya is easy and can be done online through the Huduma Namba website too, https://www.hudumanamba.go.ke/. You only need to provide your ID number, and you will receive a message indicating whether your ID verification is complete or not. For those wondering how to apply for a Kenyan ID online, the official site also provides guidance on the application process. Moreover, once you submit your details, you can perform a www.identity.go.ke check ID status to confirm that your ID verification is complete. If your verification is incomplete, you may need to visit a Huduma Namba registration center to finalize the process. How to Choose a KYC and ID Verification Provider in Kenya If you are selecting a provider to handle ID verification for your business in Kenya, weigh these factors.Local coverage: Confirm the provider actually verifies Kenyan documents against Kenyan sources such as IPRS and NTSA, and covers every other market on your roadmap so you can scale without switching vendors.Document verification quality. The provider should reliably verify the documents your customers actually use, national IDs and passports first, with high accuracy and low system downtime.Fraud prevention. Look for biometric verification, facial recognition, liveness detection, spoof detection, and automated deduplication, so the provider catches impersonation rather than only reading documents.User experience. Verification should be fast and should hold up on low bandwidth, which matters in areas with unstable connectivity, to avoid onboarding drop-offs.Integration. The solution should fit your existing systems through a clean API, work across devices, and not require months of implementation. Verify IDs in Kenya with Youverify Checking your own ID status is a personal task, but for businesses, verifying customer identities in Kenya is a compliance obligation with real financial and legal consequences if it is done poorly.Youverify provides identity verification built for Kenya and the wider EMEA region, including document verification, government KYC checks against sources such as IPRS, AML screening, enhanced due diligence, and fraud prevention with biometric and liveness checks. This lets compliance teams onboard genuine customers quickly while filtering out fraudulent ones, and it keeps your business aligned with Kenyan KYC and AML requirements.Book a free demo with our compliance experts to see how Youverify verifies Kenyan IDs at scale. Related: ID Verification in Canada How to Choose a KYC Solution Provider for ID Verification in Kenya Choosing the right Know Your Customer (KYC) solution provider is very important as it helps businesses ensure they comply with regulatory requirements, improve security, and enhance customer experience. This KYC solution guide This detailed guide will help you know the factors to consider when choosing the best KYC solution provider for your business: Step 1: Determine Your Business's KYC RequirementsFirst off, understand your business’s KYC requirements as stated by regulatory authorities. With this, you can identify the type of KYC software provider you need. Step 2: Analyze Market Coverage by LocationNow that you understand your business's KYC needs, compile a list of top KYC solution providers and analyse them based on their geographical coverage. If you operate your business across multiple jurisdictions, you will need a KYC partner, such as Youverify, with a presence and solution coverage in all the countries in your business roadmap. This ensures seamless scalability when you expand into new markets.Step 3: Ensure ID Document Verification CapabilitiesAn important quality of the best KYC solution provider is its ability to accurately verify identification documents such as ID cards, passports. Although there are several identity verification methods for businesses today, to verify customer IDs, document verification is often the most reliable and less likely to have system failures, and scalable. Ensure that your ID verification solution provider can verify the most commonly used documents among your customer base.Step 4: Evaluate the Fraud Prevention Capabilities.Fraud prevention is important in preventing financial loss and protecting your business’s reputation. Your ID verification solution provider should have robust fraud prevention solutions such as biometric verification, facial recognition, liveness detection, spoof detection, and automated deduplication checks.Step 5: Consider User Experience ImpactThe ID verification solution provider's suite of solutions should support your customer onboarding process in terms of speed, accessibility, and customizability. For instance, in countries with unstable internet connections, it is important to work with a provider optimised for low-bandwidth environments to reduce the risk of user drop-offs.Step 6: Confirm Technology CompatibilityEnsuring technology compatibility with your KYC provider is very important. Your KYC provider’s solution must integrate seamlessly with your existing systems to prevent prolonged implementation delays. Also, if you operate your business in multiple jurisdictions, it is important that the solution be optimized across different devices and access data points, the solution should be optimised for different devices and access points. The more options a provider offers, the better and stronger it will be for your long-term strategy.Step 7: Test Prospective ProvidersLastly, test the KYC providers that meet your criteria from steps 1 to 6. This testing phase will give you a clear picture of their strength, limitations, and how well they align with your business goals. How Businesses Verify Kenyan IDs at Scale (vs. Manual Checking) Outline: Manual ID checking through eCitizen, SMS, or ad hoc back-and-forth may work for a few records, but it breaks down fast when you’re onboarding hundreds or thousands of customers, employees, or vendors. For banks, fintechs, lenders, employers, and other regulated businesses, manual checks are slow, inconsistent, harder to audit, and more vulnerable to human error and fraud. Automated verification changes the workflow entirely. Instead of staff comparing documents one by one, a business sends ID data through a verification platform that can cross-check records against trusted sources such as IPRS, eCitizen-linked services, and BRS, then return a structured result in seconds. That means a compliance team can verify identity details, detect mismatches, reduce duplicate records, and keep a cleaner audit trail across every onboarding journey. It also makes it easier to scale without adding the same amount of manual review headcount. For businesses handling higher volumes, automation is especially useful because it standardizes decision-making. The same rules can be applied consistently to every applicant, whether they are a customer, employee, agent, or supplier. With the right verification layer, teams can also route only risky or incomplete cases for manual review. That helps reduce friction for legitimate users while keeping controls strong enough for KYC, KYB, and fraud prevention workflows. Want to verify Kenyan IDs faster and with less manual effort? Explore Youverify’s Customer Onboarding product to automate identity checks at scale. Achieve a Seamless ID Verification In Kenya with Youverify ID verification in Kenya is a critical aspect of doing business and protecting oneself from potential fraud. The various methods available for ID verification provide businesses and individuals with options to choose from based on their needs and budget.However, it is important to comply with regulations to avoid potential legal and financial repercussions. Automating the ID verification process can save time, money, and resources while increasing accuracy and security.Youverify provides identity verification services across countries in the EMEA region to help businesses meet their KYC and AML compliance needs. The services include document verification, KYC checks, AML checks, enhanced due diligence checks, government KYC checks, enhanced document verification, fraud prevention solutions and a lot more.Ready to simplify ID verification in Kenya? Discover how Youverify helps you stay compliant and prevent fraud. To get started, book a demo today to see how Youverify can help you achieve KYC compliance in Kenya. FAQsFrequently Asked Questions1. How do I check my ID status online in Kenya?Enter the 9-digit serial number from your waiting card on the National ID status portal at identity.go.ke/search, under the National ID section, then select Status and submit. If the portal is unavailable, send the same serial number by SMS to 20031 and you will receive a reply with your status. SMS is the more reliable method.2. How do I check my ID status by SMS in Kenya?Open a new text message, type only your 9-digit waiting card serial number, and send it to 20031. You will receive a reply confirming whether your ID is still processing or ready for collection. The SMS costs about KES 5, and the reply can occasionally take up to a few days during busy periods.3. How long does it take to get a national ID in Kenya?Most first-time IDs take about two to four weeks after biometric capture, though official guidance sometimes quotes around 10 working days. Start checking your status by SMS to 20031 about two weeks after your biometrics were taken, then check every few days until it shows ready for collection.4. What is a waiting card serial number?It is the unique 9-digit number printed on the waiting card you received after your fingerprints and photo were captured. Both the SMS service and the online portal use this number to look up your ID status. Keep the waiting card safe, because you must surrender it when you collect your finished ID.5. Can I check my ID status without the waiting card serial number?No. Both the SMS and online methods require the 9-digit serial number. If you have lost your waiting card, visit the Huduma Centre or National Registration Bureau office where you applied to retrieve your serial number, as it cannot be recovered by name online.6. What is IPRS in Kenya?The Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) is Kenya's central register of citizen and resident records, managed by the National Registration Bureau. It is used to verify that a person's details match official records, supporting KYC compliance, voter registration, and access to government services. For official enquiries, the National Registration Bureau can be reached at iprs.interior@gmail.com.7. How do businesses verify a Kenyan ID number?Businesses verify a customer's ID number against the Integrated Population Registration System (IPRS) and other government sources such as NTSA for driving licences, usually through a licensed identity verification provider. This confirms the ID number is valid and belongs to the person presenting it, which manual checks cannot reliably do at scale.Related ArticlesView All PostsKenyaTemitope Lawal•June 13, 2024Benefits of Business Identity Verification for Kenyan StartupsRead MoreKenyaTemitope Lawal•May 12, 2023Easy Way: How to Replace Lost ID Card in KenyaRead MoreKenyaVictoria okere•January 15, 2026The New Kenyan ID Card: Maisha Namba ExplainedRead More","title":"ID Verification in Kenya: How to Check Your ID Status Online in Kenya","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/id-verification-in-kenya","id":368},{"content_hash":"8e71bae7d3877ecdfba1263e58c32b48f2d8969a65c863798910e2d6c67594ae","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.200184+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-mossadegh-accuse-l-a-i-o-c-d-avoir-fomente-les-manifestations-d-etudiants-a-teheran_2073305_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. MOSSADEGH ACCUSE L'A.-I.O.C. d'avoir fomenté les manifestations d'étudiants à Téhéran Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. MOSSADEGH ACCUSE L'A.-I.O.C. d'avoir fomenté les manifestations d'étudiants à Téhéran","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/m-mossadegh-accuse-l-a-i-o-c-d-avoir-fomente-les-manifestations-d-etudiants-a-teheran_2073305_1819218.html","id":369},{"content_hash":"3892c9d36d5ec97ccfc209ce93e7bc399f5bb6c321166495ceafae394c879213","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.123474+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-legislative-wins-33678","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the government’s legislative ‘wins’ Published: October 30, 2014 10:16pm EDT https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-legislative-wins-33678 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-legislative-wins-33678 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The University of Canberra’s Amanda Jones and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics including the government’s “wins” before a three-week parliamentary recess, Bill Shorten’s accommodation of the government’s national security legislation, Joe Hockey, and Ian Watt’s retirement. National security Video Bill Shorten Direct Action Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Author Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement Michelle Grattan does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.4yr79sd6g","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the government’s legislative ‘wins’","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-legislative-wins-33678","id":370},{"content_hash":"0c7e913c13d97a8218875f24f90dcf0f7d11895be4273bc141e7ece703d18742","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:47.101710+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/exceptions/","language":"","text":"Django Exceptions¶ Django raises some of its own exceptions as well as standard Python exceptions. Django Core Exceptions¶ Django core exception classes are defined in django.core.exceptions. AppRegistryNotReady¶ exception AppRegistryNotReady[source]¶ This exception is raised when attempting to use models before the app loading process, which initializes the ORM, is complete. ObjectDoesNotExist¶ exception ObjectDoesNotExist[source]¶ The base class for Model.DoesNotExist exceptions. A try/except for ObjectDoesNotExist will catch DoesNotExist exceptions for all models. See get(). ObjectNotUpdated¶ exception ObjectNotUpdated[source]¶ The base class for Model.NotUpdated exceptions. A try/except for ObjectNotUpdated will catch NotUpdated exceptions for all models. See save(). EmptyResultSet¶ exception EmptyResultSet[source]¶ EmptyResultSet may be raised during query generation if a query won’t return any results. Most Django projects won’t encounter this exception, but it might be useful for implementing custom lookups and expressions. FullResultSet¶ exception FullResultSet[source]¶ FullResultSet may be raised during query generation if a query will match everything. Most Django projects won’t encounter this exception, but it might be useful for implementing custom lookups and expressions. FieldDoesNotExist¶ exception FieldDoesNotExist[source]¶ The FieldDoesNotExist exception is raised by a model’s _meta.get_field() method when the requested field does not exist on the model or on the model’s parents. MultipleObjectsReturned¶ exception MultipleObjectsReturned[source]¶ The base class for Model.MultipleObjectsReturned exceptions. A try/except for MultipleObjectsReturned will catch MultipleObjectsReturned exceptions for all models. See get(). SuspiciousOperation¶ exception SuspiciousOperation[source]¶ The SuspiciousOperation exception is raised when a user has performed an operation that should be considered suspicious from a security perspective, such as tampering with a session cookie. Subclasses of SuspiciousOperation include: DisallowedHost DisallowedModelAdminLookup DisallowedModelAdminToField DisallowedRedirect InvalidSessionKey RequestDataTooBig SuspiciousFileOperation SuspiciousMultipartForm SuspiciousSession TooManyFieldsSent TooManyFilesSent If a SuspiciousOperation exception reaches the ASGI/WSGI handler level it is logged at the Error level and results in a HttpResponseBadRequest. See the logging documentation for more information. PermissionDenied¶ exception PermissionDenied[source]¶ The PermissionDenied exception is raised when a user does not have permission to perform the action requested. ViewDoesNotExist¶ exception ViewDoesNotExist[source]¶ The ViewDoesNotExist exception is raised by django.urls when a requested view does not exist. MiddlewareNotUsed¶ exception MiddlewareNotUsed[source]¶ The MiddlewareNotUsed exception is raised when a middleware is not used in the server configuration. ImproperlyConfigured¶ exception ImproperlyConfigured[source]¶ The ImproperlyConfigured exception is raised when Django is somehow improperly configured – for example, if a value in settings.py is incorrect or unparseable. FieldError¶ exception FieldError[source]¶ The FieldError exception is raised when there is a problem with a model field. This can happen for several reasons: A field in a model clashes with a field of the same name from an abstract base class An infinite loop is caused by ordering A keyword cannot be parsed from the filter parameters A field cannot be determined from a keyword in the query parameters A join is not permitted on the specified field A field name is invalid A query contains invalid order_by arguments FieldFetchBlocked¶ Added in version 6.1. exception FieldFetchBlocked[source]¶ Raised when a field would be fetched on-demand and the FETCH_RAISE fetch mode is active. ValidationError¶ exception ValidationError[source]¶ The ValidationError exception is raised when data fails form or model field validation. For more information about validation, see Form and Field Validation, Model Field Validation and the Validator Reference. NON_FIELD_ERRORS¶ NON_FIELD_ERRORS¶ ValidationErrors that don’t belong to a particular field in a form or model are classified as NON_FIELD_ERRORS. This constant is used as a key in dictionaries that otherwise map fields to their respective list of errors. BadRequest¶ exception BadRequest[source]¶ The BadRequest exception is raised when the request cannot be processed due to a client error. If a BadRequest exception reaches the ASGI/WSGI handler level it results in a HttpResponseBadRequest. RequestAborted¶ exception RequestAborted[source]¶ The RequestAborted exception is raised when an HTTP body being read in by the handler is cut off midstream and the client connection closes, or when the client does not send data and hits a timeout where the server closes the connection. It is internal to the HTTP handler modules and you are unlikely to see it elsewhere. If you are modifying HTTP handling code, you should raise this when you encounter an aborted request to make sure the socket is closed cleanly. SynchronousOnlyOperation¶ exception SynchronousOnlyOperation[source]¶ The SynchronousOnlyOperation exception is raised when code that is only allowed in synchronous Python code is called from an asynchronous context (a thread with a running asynchronous event loop). These parts of Django are generally heavily reliant on thread-safety to function and don’t work correctly under coroutines sharing the same thread. If you are trying to call code that is synchronous-only from an asynchronous thread, then create a synchronous thread and call it in that. You can accomplish this is with asgiref.sync.sync_to_async(). URL Resolver exceptions¶ URL Resolver exceptions are defined in django.urls. Resolver404¶ exception Resolver404[source]¶ The Resolver404 exception is raised by resolve() if the path passed to resolve() doesn’t map to a view. It’s a subclass of django.http.Http404. NoReverseMatch¶ exception NoReverseMatch[source]¶ The NoReverseMatch exception is raised by django.urls when a matching URL in your URLconf cannot be identified based on the parameters supplied. Database Exceptions¶ Database exceptions may be imported from django.db. Django wraps the standard database exceptions so that your Django code has a guaranteed common implementation of these classes. exception Error[source]¶ exception InterfaceError[source]¶ exception DatabaseError[source]¶ exception DataError[source]¶ exception OperationalError[source]¶ exception IntegrityError[source]¶ exception InternalError[source]¶ exception ProgrammingError[source]¶ exception NotSupportedError[source]¶ The Django wrappers for database exceptions behave exactly the same as the underlying database exceptions. See PEP 249, the Python Database API Specification v2.0, for further information. A __cause__ attribute is set with the original (underlying) database exception, allowing access to any additional information provided. exception models.ProtectedError¶ Raised to prevent deletion of referenced objects when using django.db.models.PROTECT. models.ProtectedError is a subclass of IntegrityError. exception models.RestrictedError¶ Raised to prevent deletion of referenced objects when using django.db.models.RESTRICT. models.RestrictedError is a subclass of IntegrityError. HTTP Exceptions¶ HTTP exceptions may be imported from django.http. UnreadablePostError¶ exception UnreadablePostError[source]¶ UnreadablePostError is raised when a user cancels an upload. Sessions Exceptions¶ Sessions exceptions are defined in django.contrib.sessions.exceptions. SessionInterrupted¶ exception SessionInterrupted[source]¶ SessionInterrupted is raised when a session is destroyed in a concurrent request. It’s a subclass of BadRequest. Transaction Exceptions¶ Transaction exceptions are defined in django.db.transaction. TransactionManagementError¶ exception TransactionManagementError[source]¶ TransactionManagementError is raised for any and all problems related to database transactions. Testing Framework Exceptions¶ Exceptions provided by the django.test package. RedirectCycleError¶ exception client.RedirectCycleError¶ RedirectCycleError is raised when the test client detects a loop or an overly long chain of redirects. Python Exceptions¶ Django raises built-in Python exceptions when appropriate as well. See the Python documentation for further information on the Built-in Exceptions.","title":"Django Exceptions¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/exceptions/","id":371},{"content_hash":"0b1bba42bd090fffd03e3e8632e8bcd2899419310c4e851509126c62b95e7f93","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:46.481301+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us","language":"","text":"US NEWSUSOct 9, 2026‘The Orange Plague’: Why a golden, naked Trump is sitting on another manUSOct 9, 2026In 2011, an Ohio man was served foreclosure papers on his $4.5 million, 17,000-sq-ft mansion; after nearly 15 years of hearings, mediation and court fights, an eviction notice has now been approvedUSOct 9, 2026US plans to livestream Fort Hood shooter’s death; first public execution in 90 yearsUSOct 9, 2026‘Fixing broken immigration by taking hammer to it’: Lawmaker defends Microsoft after Trump’s Green Card move'ICE out of our neighbourhoods’: Shooting of Dominican man sparks protests in New YorkUS suspends 8 tech firms from PERM process: Full list and what it meansMargaret Hamilton, scientist who worked on Nasa's Apollo programme, diesTrending TopicsChrista PikeZohran MamdaniMarco RubioSaudi Syria RelationsSaudi Arabia Airport AttackDonald TrumpIran War News Videos 14:14 ON CAM: Putin Delivers 2 Unexpected Iran Shocks To Trump; Sends ‘Personal Message’ To Khamenei 17 minutes ago 14:14 ON CAM: Putin Delivers 2 Unexpected Iran Shocks To Trump; Sends ‘Personal Message’ To Khamenei 17 minutes ago 08:50 Iran's Deadly Trap In Hormuz; Massive Explosions Rock Waterway; Ships Hit Sea Mines | Report an hour ago 08:07 'You've Done No Wrong': Putin Openly Backs Iran In War Against U.S, Then Makes This Big Offer 2 hours ago 14:27 'GET OUT OF MY CITY': New York EXPLODES In Revolt Against ICE After Trump's Fed Agents Shoot Man 2 hours ago 08:38 Explosions Rock Iraqi Kurdistan; Iran's Shahed-136 Drone Blitz Targets Kurdish Separatists In Erbil 3 hours ago 15:56 China Russia Could Hit U.S_ Hegseth Faces Nuclear Reality On Cam; Iran War EXPOSES Trump _ Watch 4 hours ago More Stories In 2007, Bryan Cranston bought a California beach bungalow for $2.5 million; after rebuilding it as a 2,450-sq-ft net-zero energy home, he sold the property 14 years later for $5.45 millionA CEO who was not a cyclist trained for months, then rode about 2,000 miles from Mexico to Canada in 12 days; along the route, his team raised and distributed more than $766,000 to over 70 people and organisationsCountry singer Zach Bryan quietly bought an $18 million Tennessee estate in 2026; the secluded property sits on 146 acres and includes a 6,000-square-foot main house and recording studioAn Arkansas family treated the same fence as its property line for nearly six decades; after new neighbours removed fencing and trees, an appeals court upheld $12,000 in treble damages and an order to rebuild the fenceAmerica has enjoyed the greatest free lunch in history: Vivek Wadhwa reacts to Green Card crackdown on 8 IT companies, says they aren't the visa abusersA $132,000 GoFundMe was raised for radio host Ronn Owens; federal review found thousands spent on mortgages, businesses and family transfersFederal executive with top-secret clearance created a fake classified program and diverted $145 million into luxury property and gold; after FBI found 298 gold bars, he pleaded guilty to a $193.6 million fraudMark Zuckerberg’s sister Arielle Zuckerberg reveals secret DJ career, 37-year-old has played techno at clubs, festivals and Burning Man since 2018 while working in venture capitalIndian-origin doctor mom arrested and charged in Florida as she watched 7-year-old drown. She said she froze, she was not strong enough to pull him from waterFDR’s grandson bought 150 acres of Texas ranch land in 1999; 27 years later, he has assembled a 1,300-acre family retreat with seven miles of trailsWalt Disney quietly assembled more than 27,000 acres in Florida through secret transactions for about $5 million, averaging roughly $182 an acre; after the buyer’s identity emerged, the land became Walt Disney WorldDonald Trump honours CEO Satya Nadella as JD Vance goes hard at MicrosoftIn 2021, 94-year-old Texas teacher Opal Lee saw Juneteenth become a federal holiday after more than 40 years of activism and a 1,400-mile walk campaign‘She’s biased’: Cornell sex assault accused seeks removal of NY AG Letitia James as special prosecutorCornell Jane Doe faces death threats, doxxing after armed cops swarm family homeIn 2023, California shifted a levee 1,500 feet back and flooded fields in winter; the new floodplain produced unexpectedly huge amounts of food for young salmonUS House bill proposes raising serious H-1B violation fines from $35,000 to $250,000 and extending employer bans from at least 3 years to 10 years as Washington’s visa crackdown intensifiesIn 2016, Michigan paved a school-bus route with recycled tyre rubber; after 3 years, inspectors found no visible cracking or ruttingPalm Valley School closes after 74 years amid bankruptcy, leaving parents devastated; its 36-acre California campus with 53 classrooms is up for sale for $14 millionGreen Cards red-carded: MAGA benches India-linked IT firms from PERM processIn 2022, Washington replaced conventional riverbank defenses with engineered log structures and native plants; early tests showed the approach could protect banks while improving habitatJ-1 visa fraud: Harvard, Yale among 9 elite universities under investigation for alleged scam; 'Nobody will be getting a free pass because their name is carved into an expensive building'In 2024, Washington opened a first-in-the-nation eight-story spiral passage through a dam; young sockeye salmon could finally reach the river downstream2 Latvian men arrested near UK airbase housing US, Nato intelligence operationsIn 2013, Delaware spread carbon pellets across a polluted lake bottom in a US-first cleanup; the toxic chemicals were trapped and a new wetland improved habitatMeet Franz Lozano Jr., the Texas student who returned to education after more than a decade away; he is now part of a national award-winning student organisation recognised for leadership, service and community engagement'Having productive discussions with Iran': Trump rules out strikes before US midtermsIn 2019, Metallica's foundation launched a job-training initiative at 10 US colleges; seven years later, its investment topped $17 million and the programme expanded to 93 colleges across all 50 statesIn 2022, California put plastic from discarded printer cartridges into a 500-foot Highway 99 lane; after thousands of trucks in hot and cold weather, it had no cracking or ruttingIn 2016, Washington installed 26 human-made beaver dams in a damaged creek; after one flood, sediment rose nearly 5 feet, and water depth increased 11%In 1964, Lyndon B Johnson signed the Wilderness Act, giving about 9 million acres statutory protection and creating the system that now safeguards wilderness nationwideUS charges former Venezuela president Maduro with conspiracy to torture opponentsIn 2016, Barack Obama expanded that Hawaiian ocean refuge by 442,781 square miles, creating a protected area nearly the size of the Gulf of MexicoIn 1994, Bill Clinton signed a desert-protection law that impacted nearly 9.2 million acres of California, creating Mojave National Preserve and expanding Joshua Tree and Death ValleyNew lawsuit against Green Card rules: Why separate family members by sending the applicants out of countryA California boy asked his father for a backyard roller coaster and he said yes; six weeks and about $3,500 later, a 180-foot homemade ride was winding through the family’s quarter-acre yardA Texas physician spent 40 years restoring family land; $10M property is now a 2,178-acre wildlife area filled with deer, wild turkeys and black-capped vireosIn 1928, supporters had raised $5 million for Great Smoky Mountains National Park but rising land prices stalled the effort; a Rockefeller memorial fund matched it with another $5 million, helping make the park possiblePatrón co-founder John Paul DeJoria turned a 96-acre Texas ranch into a home for more than 120 exotic animals; when it sold for $6.93 million, the buyer inherited the herd and a 2,700-square-foot underground shelterMeet Advay Jain: Indian-American kid who made Guinness record as the youngest person to publish a book. He was 3 when he wrote 'Dino Learns Not To Waste Food'Meet John Reznikoff, the US collector who owns $1 million worth of hair from Abraham Lincoln, Marilyn Monroe and EinsteinHow FBI tracked an ISIS-linked teen, stopped him from carrying out Mall of America mass shootingMarriage fraud, duplicate identity: 4 Pakistani men to lose US citizenship in largest denaturalization move by the Trump administrationA Maryland DoorDash driver’s brakes failed and his car was totalled during a delivery, yet he still told the customer where to collect the food; the video went viral and his fundraiser later climbed past $15,000On a 160-acre Nebraska farm parcel, one wind turbine and its equipment use about 1 acre; the developer says a typical 30-year lease averages $10,000 to $12,500 a year, versus about $600 for top-dollar corn on that acreJon Stewart and his wife Tracey paid $4 million for a historic 45-acre New Jersey farm in 2016; the property now grows crops and cares for rescued cows, sheep, goats, pigs and chickens through their sanctuary foundationA 2,600-acre South Carolina timber property at the rivers where Revolutionary War commander Francis Marion hid from British forces has been transferred to the state, advancing a 7,600-acre conservation project with 60+ miles of riverfrontEast Hampton wants to spend $36.3 million to buy 38.3 acres of a former New York sand mine once slated for a 50-lot commercial subdivision; 33.3 acres would instead become open space'Not bragging, just telling facts': Trump makes fresh pitch for Nobel Peace Prize, citing 'eight settled wars'‘Iran will never have a nuclear weapon’: Rubio draws red line for Tehran'No excuse for victim blaming': Mamdani under fire from Democrats and pro-Palestinian left over Oct 7 message12345Quick LinksCoronavirus NewsJoe BidenUS NewsPakistan NewsDonald TrumpChina NewsSouth Asia NewsWorld NewsBoris JohnsonExplore Every CornerAcross The GlobeTravis Kelce Ohio EstateBypoll Election Result 2026Pat CumminsTom Brady DonationDonna KelceTom Brady InvestmentPatrick and Brittany FundingLauren KyleStephen and Ayesha CurryTravis Kelce MotherConnor McDavid WifeCharlotte TilburyPatrick and Brittany FundingRandi Mahomes CharityGracie HuntRob GronkowskiCaitlin Clark DonatePranavi UrsFlydubai AttackVivek WadhwaDoctor Sukanthini SubbiahIndia Vs Australia Match ScoreNew Zealand Cricket TeamJason KelceAustralian Cricket Team ControversyCJP ProtestMadurantakam election resultRejinagar election resultNandigram election resultDharapuram election resultAbout usCreate Your Own AdTerms of Use and Grievance Redressal Policy Privacy policyAdvertise with usRSSNewsletterFeedbackePaperContact UsEditorial PolicyLiving and EntertainmentCricbuzzLifestyleNewspaper SubscriptionFood NewsTVTimes PrimeTravel DestinationsWhats HotETimesTimes PetsServicesBreaking NewsTimes MobileGadgets NowCity NewsWork with UsColombiaTOI AutoLuxury TOI HomesTOI Home & DecorEcopreneurWellnessAIQHot on the WebNana PatekarNana Patekar Passes AwayShraddha KapoorLeo Tolstoy QuoteRajpal YadavVirat KohliGene HackmanZendayaDrishyam 4Halle BerrySalman KhanNana Patekar Last RitesDrishyam 3 Box Office CollectionNana Patekar WifeTravis BarkerColin FarrellAvengers: DoomsdayRanabaali TrailerDolly PartonHanuman Ansh CollectionEllen DeGeneresDionne WarwickThe Paradise Movie ReviewHanuman Ansh Movie ReviewSanjay DuttSonu NigamBen AffleckBaby NamesVietnam Cambodia TourHoroscope TodayIndia Powerus AgreementAir Force DayEnemy PropertyCumulative Matrimonial ConductApple iPhone CompensationVoltas AC CompensationIAF 94th AnniversaryFPV DronesChina US F-35 Stealth FighterChang'E-6 SoilRarest Rattlesnake BreedCaptions For Instagram PostsTrending TopicsCJPAbhijeet DipkeRiyadh Flight SuspensionErode Caste DiscriminationMumbai MurderMadurai Boar NewsDelhi ProtestMad Sandhu MurderAbhijeet DipkeMP High CourtDelhi Dry DayDelhi Train CancellationMumbai Dal Price HikeWeather ForecastLA KnightWWE Money In The Bank EventErling HaalandMumbai TrafficBombay High CourtGuwahati MurderMumbai SuicideDelhi AccidentPriyanka GandhiMad Sandhu MurderTN Dementia CasesWeather ForecastDelhi Airport Drone SeizedGulzar Singh CaseJaipur Live in MurderCJPTouch Screen MacBookCaden Terence PohlkampClaude Usage PolicyHubSpot LayoffsClaude Haiku 5.5Red Teaming AI AgentMark Zuckerberg BiohubBudget SmartPhone MarketAmazon LayoffsJeff Bezos MarsFired OpenAI ResearchersPopular CategoriesHeadlinesSports NewsBusiness NewsIndia NewsWorld NewsInternational SportsHealthBreaking NewsTravelAstrologyDeorhiTOI Young LeadersTOI Home Decor AwardsPower Creator Awards 2026The Times of India EcopreneurGold Rate TodayWeather TodaySilver Price TodayDelhi Weather TodayPetrol Price TodayDiesel Price TodayCNG Price TodayLPG Price TodayPetrol Price MumbaiFIFA 2026Is Bank Open TodayFIFA 2026 ScheduleIs Bank Open TomorrowPublic Holidays in JuneBank Holidays in JuneTrending VideosON CAM: Putin Delivers 2 Unexpected Iran Shocks To Trump; Sends ‘Personal Message’ To KhameneiThe Vvaan | Song - Chal BhawaniSummer Holidays | Lyrical - Friendship SongItllu Arjuna | Song - Yellow Chunni (Lyrical)SYG: Sambarala Yeti Gattu - Official Hindi GlimpseSYG: Sambarala Yeti Gattu - Official Malayalam GlimpseSYG: Sambarala Yeti Gattu - Official Kannada GlimpseSYG: Sambarala Yeti Gattu - Official GlimpseIran's Deadly Trap In Hormuz; Massive Explosions Rock Waterway; Ships Hit Sea Mines | ReportKaitlan Collins Challenges JD Vance Over Trump’s ‘A+’ Economy RatingDeSu7 - Official TrailerRanabaali - Official Tamil TrailerRanabaali - Official Hindi TrailerRanabaali - Official Malayalam TrailerRanabaali - Official Kannada Trailer'You've Done No Wrong': Putin Openly Backs Iran In War Against U.S, Then Makes This Big Offer'GET OUT OF MY CITY': New York EXPLODES In Revolt Against ICE After Trump's Fed Agents Shoot ManEyewitness Video Captures Chaos After Ice Shooting In New YorkLatest NewsAccelerating GST refunds: Unlocking liquidity for India’s next growth phase‘The Orange Plague’: Why a golden, naked Trump is sitting on another manJeff Bezos may have just told Americans, stop 'hating' AI, it may make '3-day work week' possible'How can you raise hands against children?' CJP sends message to police day before October 10 Delhi protestPolice made little progress in 10 months; Delhi high court grants bail to cyberfraud accusedWhen Nana Patekar recalled being offered ‘lousy role of one of the rapists’ in 'Aaj Ki Awaz'; Smita Patil helped him get the film‘Thousands detained ahead of October 10 protest, this intimidation won’t work, Modi’: CJP’s Abhijeet Dipke‘I would have died 10, 15 or 25 years ago’: Nana Patekar’s old video explaining the ‘Natsamrat curse’ resurfaces after his death39% Mumbadevi voters out of draft roll, alleges Congress MLA Amin Patel5 Simple and smart ways to add more protein to your kid’s dietKarnataka CM DK Shivakumar claims plot to trap him in sting operationAs US government presents Science medals, Elon Musk shares 'congratulations message' for Sergey Brin, Jensen Huang, Lisa Su, Michael Dell and Satya NadellaIndian IT stocks rally despite US PERM suspension as TCS Q2 results lift sentimentJubin Nautiyal pays tribute to Nana Patekar; remembers veteran actor as ‘warm and welcoming’; calls his demise a ‘great loss’ to the film industryBorn on October 9? What birth number 9 says about personality, career, love and lifeWhy has New Zealand's T20I squad changed ahead of the India series?Railways cancel all Mail/Express trains reaching New Delhi, Hazrat Nizamuddin from 4am to 2pm on October 10No sugar, no flour! Mom’s unusual ‘bone marrow cake’ for baby's first birthday divides the internet, netizens says 'Jesus Christ, someone called the child helpline'Copyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"US NEWS","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us","id":372},{"content_hash":"59d8db0f183d47341238097709d350ba1346239f7553e4624304e392047c68be","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:46.091548+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-does-my-blood-group-mean-32042","language":"","text":"Health Check: what does my blood group mean? Published: November 2, 2014 11:08pm EST Blood is categorised by the naturally occurring proteins and sugars on the surface of red blood cells. Jon Åslund/Flickr, CC BY https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-does-my-blood-group-mean-32042 https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-does-my-blood-group-mean-32042 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Few discoveries have revolutionised the practice of medicine as much as the discovery of human red blood cell groups. Unlike modern vampire and Time Lord mythologies, blood groups don’t have a particular flavour (Tru blood) or make humans susceptible to hypnotic mind control (Dr Who). Rather, blood is categorised by the naturally occurring proteins and sugars on the surface of red blood cells. For most people, which blood group you are does not matter one jot, unless your life is at risk and you need a blood transfusion. In this case, the proteins and sugars can act as a barrier to safe transfusion of red blood cells from one person to another. The ABO blood group system Prior to the 20th century, early attempts at transfusing blood into humans could be unpredictable and lead to death. Looking for a solution, immunologist Karl Landsteiner found that the blood of two people could be either “compatible” or “incompatible” in a predictable way: “incompatible” blood led to “clumping” (agglutination) of red blood cells in a test tube, while “compatible blood” did not. Along with Alfred von Decastello and Adriano Sturli, Landsteiner identified the four major blood groups: O, A, B and AB or what is known as the ABO blood group system. Landsteiner realised what made blood types compatible or incompatible were antibodies produced against specific red blood cell sugar molecules, causing agglutination of the red cells in the test tube and destruction of the red cells in the circulation. For this work, Landsteiner was awarded the Nobel Prize in Medicine. Blood transfusions are no longer analogous to medical ‘Russian roulette’. sfam_photo/Shutterstock Ludwig Hektoen developed rigorous testing procedures of blood grouping and cross matching in 1907 which were first performed by Reuben Ottenberg. These methods have been used to reliably select the correct donor blood ever since. Beyond the ABO blood group In general, everyone can be classified within ABO blood group system, based on the ABO sugars. Red blood cells also contain many other proteins and sugars, known as red cell antigens. Researchers have discovered more than 300 blood group antigens, each representing different proteins on the red blood cell that perform different functions. The importance of these red cell blood groups, however, is not necessarily what they do; you could live without several blood group antigens and be none the wiser. Their importance of lies in how they elicit an immune antibody response, and whether these new antibodies can destroy foreign red cells against which they are targeted. The immune system of person who doesn’t have these antigens can be stimulated to make antibodies against red cell antigens that aren’t their own. This can cause red cell destruction (haemolysis) which, in its most immediate and dramatic form, can lead to severe stress to the heart and circulation, kidney failure and death. It can also lead to a delayed form of red cell destruction that occurs a week or so after a blood transfusion. Positive or negative One of the most important non-ABO blood groups is the Rhesus blood group system, which Landsteiner discovered in 1940 in collaboration with Alexander Weiner. This system categorises people as either positive or negative, depending on whether they have Rhesus D antigen on their red cells (O- or O+ for example). Since the late 1800s, physicians had noted that following a successful first pregnancy, some mothers could lose their subsequent babies through a disease called haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn. For the majority of babies, this condition was attributable to the Rhesus D antigen. Researchers realised that Rhesus D-negative mothers were exposed to Rhesus D antigen through the course of their pregnancy. The baby had inherited this red cell antigen from their father. The mother’s immune system therefore reacted against this foreign red cell antigen by producing antibodies which could cross the placenta and destroy the red cells of the baby in subsequent pregnancies. ABO and Rhesus are the most important red cell blood groups. Ian Humes/Flickr, CC BY-NC-ND The understanding of the Rhesus system led to strategies that have dramatically reduced haemolytic disease among newborns. Rhesus D-negative women of child-bearing age are only given blood transfusions of Rhesus D-negative blood to prevent stimulating the immune system against this antigen. Pregnant Rhesus D-negative women are also given special anti-D immunoglobulin during their pregnancy, after delivery and following any trauma or pregnancy-related procedure. Again, this helps prevent foreign Rhesus D-positive red cells from stimulating the mother’s immune system. Rare blood groups What ultimately determines our blood group is our genetics: our parents, our ancestors and also their migration patterns. Rare blood groups arise from inheritable mutations of red blood cell genes in different populations. This can make it very difficult to find correctly “matched” blood donor units for people with rare blood groups simply because they are uncommon in the usual volunteer blood donor population. The red cell blood group Kidd(null) phenotype (Jka-b-) which is more common in Finnish and Polynesian populations, for instance, is very rare in Australian blood donors. Unless a correctly matched Jka-b- blood is found, there is a risk of developing a haemolytic transfusion reaction or haemolytic disease of the newborn, as patients develop antibodies that are acquired to Kidd red cell blood groups on the transfused blood. People with the Bombay red cell phenotype have only a one in a million chance of finding a suitable blood donor. In this setting, transfusion may be sought from a family member, who is much more likely to have the correct blood phenotype. It’s likely that you or someone you know will require a blood transfusion at some stage in life. One of the simplest things we can do as individuals is to become a volunteer blood donor. Blood Health Check Haemoglobin Haemorrhagic fever Ashley Ng, WEHI (Walter and Eliza Hall Institute of Medical Research) Author Ashley Ng Post Doctoral Fellow and Haematologist, WEHI (Walter and Eliza Hall Institute of Medical Research) Disclosure statement Ashley Ng currently receives funding from The National Health and Medical Research Council. He has previously received funding from the Leukaemia Foundation Australia and Cure Cancer Australia. He has previously been the Lions Fellow of the Cancer Council of Victoria and has been awarded a Sir Edward Dunlop Fellowship. Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.xjryaytku","title":"Health Check: what does my blood group mean?","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-does-my-blood-group-mean-32042","id":373},{"content_hash":"e39b989d4631b06ef3ae47fc03bbcb2d6801e99b6215be217c9b20689bf7c741","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:46.036397+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-trois-puissances-alliees-demandent-l-inscription-a-l-ordre-du-jour-de-la-designation-d-une-commission-d-enquete_2073092_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Les délégations des États-Unis, de la France et de la Grande-Bretagne ont demandé lundi au secrétaire général de l'O.N.U. d'inscrire à l'ordre du jour de la sixième session de l'assemblée générale, à la suite du vœu exprimé par M. Adenauer, chancelier de la République fédérale allemande, une question ainsi libellée : \" Désignation d'une commission internationale impartiale, sous le contrôle des Nations unies, pour effectuer une enquête simultanée dans la République fédérale allemande, à Berlin et dans la zone soviétique d'Allemagne afin de déterminer si les conditions qui y règnent permettent de tenir des élections réellement libres dans l'ensemble de ces zones. \" Les trois délégations co-auteurs de cette proposition ont présenté au secrétaire général des lettres identiques dans lesquelles elles soulignent le caractère important et urgent de ce problème. L'aide-mémoire que les délégations américaine, française et britannique ont remis lundi après-midi à l'appui de leur proposition comprend un bref rappel historique sur ce problème. Il est indiqué notamment que depuis 1945 les trois gouvernements alliés ont constamment soutenu que \" l'Allemagne devrait être réunifiée dès que les circonstances permettraient le rétablissement d'un gouvernement démocratique capable de conduire une Allemagne libre au sein de l'organisation pacifique des nations libres de l'Europe \". Le mémorandum rappelle également que depuis cette date de nombreuses démarches concrètes ont été faites par les alliés dans ce sens, notamment lors de la réunion des ministres des affaires étrangères des Quatre Grands en mai 1949, et par des lettres envoyées au commandant en chef soviétique en Allemagne par les trois hauts commissaires alliés. En outre le gouvernement fédéral de Bonn a fait plusieurs propositions tendant à préparer la réunification de l'Allemagne. Le gouvernement de l'Allemagne de l'Est ayant dans une déclaration adressée au gouvernement fédéral le 15 septembre dernier développé le même point de vue, le gouvernement de M. Adenauer, dans une lettre adressée au président de la haute commission alliée en Allemagne le 4 octobre 1951, a demandé aux trois gouvernements alliés de porter ce problème devant rassemblée générale des Nations unies lors de sa sixième session à Paris. Il vous reste 11.73% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"Les trois puissances alliées demandent l'inscription à l'ordre du jour de la désignation d'une commission d'enquête","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/les-trois-puissances-alliees-demandent-l-inscription-a-l-ordre-du-jour-de-la-designation-d-une-commission-d-enquete_2073092_1819218.html","id":374},{"content_hash":"1667b93635028e698d3f9a9c95adf2f166ff81e0b47f0a8ac393d8a012ab0a79","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:45.944402+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-woman-day","language":"","text":"International Woman Day | Page template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"International Woman Day | Page template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/international-woman-day","id":375},{"content_hash":"3f991897702aef1d141def9b5676a6ffe630b4156b0021f094e8a27e61cfbfcc","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:45.525689+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-08/federal-politics-live-blog-ato-surcharge-oct-8/107240250?utm_campaign=abc_news_web&utm_content=link&utm_medium=content_shared&utm_source=abc_news_web#live-blog-post-346919","language":"","text":"Federal politics: Taylor plays down concerns migration policy could deter foreign students — as it happenedBy political reporter Joshua BoscainiTopic:Government and PoliticsPosted Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:12amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:12amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:12am, updated Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:55pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:55pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:55pmSkip to timelineabc.net.au/news/federal-politics-live-blog-ato-surcharge-oct-8/107240250Link copiedShareShare articleOpposition Leader Angus Taylor has played down concerns the Coalition's migration policy could deter international students from studying in Australia.Take a look back at the day's coverage below.Submit a comment or question Log in to commentLive updatesPinnedThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:49pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:49pmThat's the end of our live blog!JBy Joshua BoscainiAnd on that note, it's time to wrap up this blog for today friends!You can look back at today's developments below, or download the ABC News app and subscribe to our range of news alerts for the latest updates.We'll be back again tomorrow for more politics blog news. Until then, enjoy the rest of your day!LoadingReactReactCollapse all postsoffSortSort posts Latest Select an optionLatestOldestNew: FiltersChoose what information you see below by using filtersFilter PostsAll26Key Events1Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:46pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:46pmWilson pokes fun at government over differing opinions on ATO decisionNBy Niddal MustafaShadow Treasurer Tim Wilson has poked fun at the government, after ministers put forward different opinions about the tax office's decision to ban credit card payments.This morning, Housing Minister Clare O'Neil told Channel Nine Labor has \"real concerns\" with the ATO call to ban credit card payments, following the Reserve Bank's decision to scrap surcharges.However, Assistant Technology Minister Andrew Charlton told ABC Radio National Breakfast that the government backed the ATO's decision.Tim Wilson says the disagreement is an audition for the role of treasurer.\"I think what we are seeing is an audition for who his [Chalmers] replacement is going to be between Clare O'Neil and Andrew Charlton over this,\" Wilson told News24.\"One of the problems we have is an inconsistency where the government is turning around to small businesses and is saying you've got to cop the extra costs that are being passed on by the banks, but we the government won't.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:28pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:28pmAustralia outlines plans to make AI companies prove their safety systems workCBy Clare ArmstrongAustralia would require AI companies to prove they are properly testing models and preventing harm under proposed new laws modelled on banking and aviation safety rules.The federal government is finalising plans to regulate frontier artificial intelligence (AI) through national standards due by the end of the year, with recent incidents involving rogue agents underscoring the risks of the rapidly advancing technology.Assistant Minister for Science, Technology and the Digital Economy Andrew Charlton has signalled the government wants to make AI companies demonstrate their safety systems are effective, rather than dictate how they should manage every risk.He outlined the case for tougher frontier AI oversight in a speech in Sydney today, arguing Australia should put the \"burden\" where the risk was created.\"AI needs regulating because its harms are severe, hard to undo, borne by people who never chose them and sometimes invisible until they arrive,\" he said.Read the full story from chief digital political correspondent Clare Armstrong in the link below.Australia wants to regulate AI like banks and airlinesReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:16pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:16pmAnalysis: Was pre-COP a flop? Questions loom over government's Pacific climate gatheringSBy Stephen DziedzicAs the Fiji Airways flight carrying dignitaries to Tuvalu sat on the airport runway at the heart of its tiny capital, Funafuti, an official struggled with a broken umbrella in the driving rain.A pile of sodden brollies lay next to him as locals prepared to ferry leaders, ministers and assorted bigwigs out of the plane, across the tarmac, through the downpour, and into a traditional welcome ceremony.The rather forlorn image seemed to fit neatly with the headlines back home in Australia, which had already declared the pre-COP summit a farce and a failure, particularly as the government had only managed to coax two non-Pacific leaders to fly to Tuvalu to see the climate emergency up close.On the face of it, this might seem a little unfair.By all accounts, the vibe on the ground was not nearly so grim.Read the full analysis from foreign affairs reporter Stephen Dziedzic in the link below.Was pre-COP a flop? Questions loom over the Australian governmentReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:37pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:37pmChaney calls on ATO to reinstate credit card payments for businessesJBy Joshua BoscainiCrossbench MPs have reacted to the Australian Taxation Office's (ATO) decision to stop accepting credit cards for tax bill payments.Yesterday, the ATO met with business groups angry at the change. But the department refused to change tack.Independent MP Kate Chaney says the ATO's decision adds to pressure on businesses and has asked the department to reconsider its decision.\"Credit cards are one way businesses manage cashflow when a tax bill lands. Removing that option with around eight weeks' notice makes things harder,\" Chaney says.Community Strong MP Allegra Spender says government must work with the ATO and business groups to find a workable solution.\"The use of credit cards by small business to pay tax bills might be small, but having the option is essential,\" Spender says.\"Managing cashflow is difficult at the best of times and businesses need to have the flexibility offered by credit.\"Independent MP Dai Le says taxpayers shouldn't absorb the cost of credit card surcharges. But she says the ATO needs to help people under financial pressure.\"I don't suggest that the taxpayer should foot the bill, but, the ATO must provide clear communication, accessible alternatives, flexible payment plans and genuine hardship support for the businesses that need it, to transition away from card payments without further hurting their bottom line,\" Le says.ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:05pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:05pmAged care body flags initial concerns about Coalition’s migration planEBy Evelyn ManfieldThe boss of Ageing Australia Tom Symondson said upon first hearing about the Coalition's plan to massively slash migration, he couldn't get an answer on how big the effect on aged care would be.Angus Taylor initially said there would be \"some\" impact on aged care workers, but has since argued the sector would be protected.Symondson said after reaching out to the Coalition for clarity, he was feeling \"much better\" about their proposal, which includes a plan to increase skilled migration, but still had questions about their intentions for unskilled visa holders.The Coalition said it did conduct consultation before releasing its plan.ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:01pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:01pmAged care sector dependent on migrant workersEBy Evelyn ManfieldThe peak body representing aged care providers said the sector \"couldn't exist\" without migrants and has raised concerns about the overarching immigration debate.Tom Symondson from Ageing Australia said the industry not only depends on skilled workers, but also people who work as cleaners, cooks and gardeners who are not on skilled visas but are crucial to the functioning of aged care facilities.\"There aren't enough people who were born in Australia wanting to do these jobs,\" he told ABC Radio National Breakfast.He estimated a quarter of the aged care workforce was on temporary visa.Symondson also urged political parties to think about migration from the perspective of its effect on workforces, rather than beginning with a reduction target and working backwards.\"We've seen this from all of the parties that have revealed immigration policies, they start with the big number … and the detail comes after they have frightened everyone,\" he said.\"People have to stop doing that\".ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:26amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:26amTaylor quizzed on warnings about migration changes from rideshare companiesJBy Joshua BoscainiChinese rideshare company Didi told the Nine newspapers changes to Australia's migration settings could lead to longer wait times for drivers.Asked about the reports, Opposition Leader Angus Taylor said: \"We're not going to set immigration policy in this country based on lectures from Uber Eats.\"If big overseas American companies want to lecture us on immigration policy, we'll do it for what's right for Australians,\" Taylor says.And that's the end of that press conference!ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:18amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:18amTaylor says Indian High Commissioner didn't raise concerns about policyJBy Joshua BoscainiAngus Taylor says the Indian High Commissioner raised no concerns about the Coalition's migration policy.The opposition leader also said he spoke with the Nepali foreign minister today.\"I do it on a regular basis keeping them up to speed on our policies and talking through how our countries, how great countries can work together,\" Taylor says.\"I laid out what our policies were.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:11amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:11amLiberal leader responds to aged care sector's concerns about migration policyJBy Joshua BoscainiOpposition Leader Angus Taylor says international students will still be able to work in aged care, despite sector concerns about how the Coalition's policy will affect them.Under its policy, the Coalition will provide people on temporary graduate visas working in critical sectors with a pathway to a skilled working visa.The Ageing Australia chief executive told ABC Radio National Breakfast he wasn't sure how the Coalition's policy would affect people on visas working as cleaners or cooks in aged care.The opposition leader says international students will still be able to work in aged care.\"There will still be a very significant number of international students coming to this country, and they will be eligible, to do a certain amount of work, including in that sector. And we know that's important to the sector,\" Taylor says.ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:04amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:04amTaylor speaks with Indian High Commissioner about migration policyJBy Joshua BoscainiOn Tuesday, the Coalition released its policy to bring net overseas migration down to 100,000 for the first two years of government.Under the opposition's policy, it would increase to 130,000 in the third year and 160,000 in the fourth.The Coalition will cut graduate, refugee and bridging visas to meet the number.The opposition leader has been asked about analysis pointing to the fact that it might be difficult for the Coalition to reach that number.But Angus Taylor has sidestepped the question and says the Coalition doesn't want a labour market that is \"dependent on guest workers\".But asked if that could disincentivise international students, Taylor says he's spoken with the Indian High Commissioner about the policy.\"Our universities right now should be selling educational excellence. That's what they should be selling; sadly, some of them are selling visa pathways and migration pathways,\" Taylor says.\"We strongly welcome Indian students coming here, building skills, getting ahead and if they have their skills we need to continue on as doctors or engineers or whatever it might be, because we need those skills in this country, then great.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:53amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:53amTaylor says PM should step in if NSW doesn't change legislation on planningJBy Joshua BoscainiAngus Taylor says Anthony Albanese should step in if the NSW government doesn't repeal legislation that led to a landmark High Court decision.The court found that planning authorities must consider the emissions caused by burning fossil fuels overseas when approving projects.It was in relation to the expansion of the Mount Pleasant mine last year in the New South Wales Court of Appeal.\"This is legislation that has been put in place by the [Chris] Minns government. They should repeal it. And if they don't repeal it, if they don't repeal it, Anthony Albanese should put in place overriding legislation to repeal it,\" Taylor says.\"It's completely unacceptable that a coalmine expansion or a coalmine development not proceed because of legislation where there's a requirement to take into consideration the customer's emissions.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:47amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:47amTaylor accuses Labor of being 'all over the shop' on ATO credit card paymentsJBy Joshua BoscainiOpposition Leader Angus Taylor has stepped up for a press conference at Naremburn in Sydney's lower north shore.Taylor accused the federal government of being \"all over the shop\" on the Australian Taxation Office's (ATO) ban on using credit cards to pay tax.This morning, Housing Minister Clare O'Neil told Channel Nine Labor has \"real concerns\" with the ATO's decision to ban credit card payments, following the Reserve Bank's decision to scrap surcharges.But Assistant Minister Andrew Charlton told ABC Radio National Breakfast that the government backed the ATO's decision to end credit card payments.\"We've had the housing minister in one place, a small business minister somewhere else, and the de facto treasurer has been saying he's been defending the ATO,\" Taylor says.\"In the last couple of days, they've said to small businesses you've got to absorb the surcharges. The hypocrisy of this government knows no bounds because they don't understand enterprise.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:59amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:59amCanavan calls for state, federal parliaments to overturn High Court mine decisionJBy Joshua BoscainiMatt Canavan has weighed in on the High Court's landmark decision about the expansion of the Mount Pleasant coalmine in NSW's Hunter.The High Court ruled that planning authorities must consider the emissions caused by burning fossil fuels overseas when approving projects.The Nationals leader has called on \"all governments\" to overturn the High Court's decision by passing laws through state or federal parliaments.\"The courts get to make decisions on these issues but it's the parliament that gets to decide with the laws and it is within the power of the New South Wales government, potentially the federal government as well, to give certainty to coal communities, to coal jobs,\" Canavan says.ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:32amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:32amHousing institute hits back at One Nation's rental and migration claimsABy Alex Turner-CohenThe Australian Housing and Urban Research Institute (AHURI) has distanced itself from One Nation, after its research was used for the party's latest policy on migration and rents.On Wednesday, Senator Pauline Hanson's party pledged that, if elected, One Nation would cut 750,000 temporary visas over three years.This would reduce rent inflation by 6.5 per cent and save $2,818 a year on average for a renter by the third year of the party's immigration cuts, according to One Nation.The party said the internal modelling relied on rental figures from the AHURI, to assume a substantial reduction in demand for rented properties as a result of the migration cuts.The AHURI said in a statement to the ABC that it was not consulted, the research attributed to it was actually ABS 2021 data, and to be cautious about the modelling claims.\"AHURI was not consulted on, or involved in, the modelling One Nation has undertaken, so we are unable to comment on its reliability or validity,\" its managing director Tom Alves said.\"We would encourage caution when interpreting this ABS data. For example, it should not be assumed that each temporary migrant occupies an entire property, as several people may share a single home.\"Some temporary migrants may also live in accommodation owned or provided by their employer or education provider, which may not be available or practical for the general rental market.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:58amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:58amCoalition claims Greens are 'outsourcing' defence policy to Russia is 'bollocks', says ShoebridgeJBy Joshua BoscainiGreens leader David Shoebridge has hit back at criticism of his party's policy of not sending military aid to Ukraine, maintaining the Greens support humanitarian and civilian aid to the country.On the weekend, Shoebridge told The Sydney Morning Herald the Greens opposed Russia's illegal invasion of Ukraine but did not support \"military aid to Ukraine\".Ukraine's ambassador to Australia Vasyl Myroshnychenko told the ABC he hoped the Greens would \"reconsider\" its position. Earlier today on News24, Shadow Defence Minister James Paterson accused the Greens of wanting to \"outsource\" Australia's defence and foreign policy to Russia.Shoebridge has called Paterson's comments \"complete bollocks\" and says the Greens have not changed their position on support for Ukraine.\"We absolutely support Australia giving humanitarian aid, civilian aid to the people of Ukraine, to the Ukrainian government in resisting the illegal invasion from Russia,\" Shoebridge says.\"And we have been from day one the strongest proponents for sanctions against Russia.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:39amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:39amResources minister plays down High Court ruling on coalmine expansionJBy Joshua BoscainiResources Minister Madeleine King has also played down the impact of a landmark High Court decision on the expansion of a NSW mine.Yesterday, the High Court ruled planning authorities are required to consider the emissions caused by burning fossil fuels overseas when approving projects.The decision was in relation to the expansion of the Mount Pleasant coalmine in the NSW Hunter Valley region.But King has told ABC's AM program the ruling relates to a \"particular\" application under a particular law in NSW.\"The High Court ruling is a pretty technical ruling about what a planning commission has to consider when it goes through a planning approvals process,\" King says.\"The main thing is that people should be reassured that there are no jobs at risk because of this ruling. The Mount Pleasant mine will continue. It will continue under its current operations in 2032.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:26amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:26amDuniam says aged care workers are classified as skilled workers under Coalition policyJBy Joshua BoscainiShadow Home Affairs Minister Jonno Duniam says aged care workers on visas were always carved out of the Coalition's migration policy.On Tuesday, the Coalition announced plans to slash net overseas migration (NOM) to 100,000 for the first two years, followed by 130,000 in the third and 160,000 in the fourth year.It will do that with deep cuts to graduate, refugee and bridging visas.The opposition says temporary visa holders in the aged care sector will be able to transition to skilled visas, meaning they'll stay in the system.Speaking to ABC Radio National Breakfast, Duniam says aged care workers have been on the Coalition's skilled workers list.\"The provision for aged care workers to be, as required by the industry, be included in the skills list has been part of the policy all along. And provision for the skills needs we have as a country is at the heart of our policy,\" Duniam says.\"We're not going to pick and choose randomly and add things in and take them out just at a whim because someone picks up the phone.\"ReactReactThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:14amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:14amAssistant minister says cost for goods shouldn't go up due to surcharge banJBy Joshua BoscainiAndrew Charlton says he doesn't see why businesses would have to increase prices given credit card process fees have fallen.The Reserve Bank decided to scrap credit card surcharges from October 1, which cost consumers $1.6 billion a year.But some businesses say they will have to either absorb the cost of that surcharge or pass the cost onto consumers.The assistant minister has told ABC Radio National Breakfast the government has reduced processing fees to 0.3 per cent.\"That reduction means that card payments are cheaper for businesses to process. So there's no reason as a result of this reform that consumers should be paying more when they tap their card for a coffee,\" Charlton says.\"The cost to the business has gone down, there's no reason why that should be greater than what they were paying before in terms of the price and the surcharge.\"ReactReactKey EventThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:50amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:50amCharlton says Labor supports ATO's decision to end credit card paymentsJBy Joshua BoscainiDigital Economy Assistant Minister Andrew Charlton says the government supports the Australian Tax Office's (ATO) decision to ban credit card tax payments.The ATO held a snap meeting with angry business groups yesterday that were angered by the department's move. But it didn't back down from its decision to stop accepting credit card payments.Yesterday, Small Business Minister Anne Aly told ABC's Afternoon Briefing she expected the tax office to \"keep speaking\" to small businesses and \"find a way forward\".This morning, Housing Minister Clare O'Neil told Channel Nine Labor had \"real concerns\" about the ATO's decision not to accept credit card payments.Speaking to ABC Radio National Breakfast, Charlton says the ATO can be flexible about tax payments.\"The government supports the ATO's decision. The ATO has made that decision because of the principle of consistency between the private sector and the public sector,\" Charlton says.\"The reason for this is a reason of consistency between the private sector and the public sector. And that is that if we're saying that surcharges can't exist in the private sector, then surcharges shouldn't exist in the public sector.\"ReactReactShow more postsLatestThat's the end of our live blog!PinnedThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:49pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:49pmWilson pokes fun at government over differing opinions on ATO decisionThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:46pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:46pmAustralia outlines plans to make AI companies prove their safety systems workThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:28pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:28pmAnalysis: Was pre-COP a flop? Questions loom over government's Pacific climate gatheringThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:16pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:16pmChaney calls on ATO to reinstate credit card payments for businessesThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:37pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:37pmAged care body flags initial concerns about Coalition’s migration planThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:05pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:05pmAged care sector dependent on migrant workersThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:01pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 12:01pmTaylor quizzed on warnings about migration changes from rideshare companiesThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:26amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:26amTaylor says Indian High Commissioner didn't raise concerns about policyThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:18amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:18amLiberal leader responds to aged care sector's concerns about migration policyThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:11amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:11amTaylor speaks with Indian High Commissioner about migration policyThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:04amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 11:04amTaylor says PM should step in if NSW doesn't change legislation on planningThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:53amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:53amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:53amTaylor accuses Labor of being 'all over the shop' on ATO credit card paymentsThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:47amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:47amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 10:47amCanavan calls for state, federal parliaments to overturn High Court mine decisionThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:59amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:59amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:59amHousing institute hits back at One Nation's rental and migration claimsThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:32amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:32amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 9:32amCoalition claims Greens are 'outsourcing' defence policy to Russia is 'bollocks', says ShoebridgeThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:58amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:58amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:58amResources minister plays down High Court ruling on coalmine expansionThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:39amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:39amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:39amDuniam says aged care workers are classified as skilled workers under Coalition policyThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:26amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:26amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:26amAssistant minister says cost for goods shouldn't go up due to surcharge banThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:14amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:14amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:14amCharlton says Labor supports ATO's decision to end credit card paymentsKey EventThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:50amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:50amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:50amATO gave small business a day's notice of credit card banThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:40amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:40amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:40amSteggall says cost of pre-COP is 'nothing in comparison' to new PNG NRL teamThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:33amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:33amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:33amPre-COP meeting in Fiji to conclude todayThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:28amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:28amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:28amAyres plays down concerns over landmark High Court rulingThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:24amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:24amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:24amLabor has 'real concerns' about ATO's decision not to accept credit card payments: O'NeilThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:17amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:17amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:17amGood morning 👋Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:07amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:07amThu 8 Oct 2026 at 7:07amShare optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Federal politics: Taylor plays down concerns migration policy could deter foreign students — as it happened","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-08/federal-politics-live-blog-ato-surcharge-oct-8/107240250?utm_campaign=abc_news_web&utm_content=link&utm_medium=content_shared&utm_source=abc_news_web#live-blog-post-346919","id":376},{"content_hash":"f37e251b40e925b2d24b37a153103518657efe69032d3f7702430e133a3d58e1","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:44.796198+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/cent-soixante-dix-personnes-suspectees-de-trafic-sur-les-changes-ont-ete-interpellees-par-la-police_2072313_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives CENT SOIXANTE-DIX PERSONNES SUSPECTÉES DE TRAFIC SUR LES CHANGES ont été interpellées par la police Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"CENT SOIXANTE-DIX PERSONNES SUSPECTÉES DE TRAFIC SUR LES CHANGES ont été interpellées par la police","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/cent-soixante-dix-personnes-suspectees-de-trafic-sur-les-changes-ont-ete-interpellees-par-la-police_2072313_1819218.html","id":377},{"content_hash":"bc047367fe7095f972b3aec8a6f86bb6d0d97681f7cd58613080936cc5301a2f","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:44.763347+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/financial-events-affect-taxes.html","language":"","text":"4 times you should always ask, ‘How will this affect my taxes?’ Considering the tax implications of key financial moves could save you money. Here’s what you need to know. WE MAKE BIG FINANCIAL DECISIONS all the time. But we don’t always ask, “How will this affect my taxes?” Here are four times when considering the federal income tax consequences — and possibly adjusting your strategy — could make a difference. Did you know you may be eligible for a capital gains tax exclusion on the first $250,000 of profit — $500,000 if you’re married and file jointly — when selling your primary residence? “Remember, too, that tax laws change frequently, and anticipating future shifts in tax rates and rules could help to influence the financial decisions you make today.” — Vinay Navani, tax accountant, WilkinGuttenplan You and your spouse, if you’re married, must have lived in the house for at least two of the past five years prior to the date of the sale. With the maximum capital gains tax rate generally at 20%, this exclusion could save you up to $100,000 in federal income tax. The exclusion can’t be used if you excluded the gain from the sale of another home within the prior two years from the date of the sale. A move to consider: Staying until the two-year threshold has been met, as the capital gains exclusion applies only to a home used as your primary residence for at least two years. Don’t forget: When calculating the amount of federal income tax owed on the sale, you can add the cost of certain qualifying home improvements you make before you sell to the cost basis of the home. The higher the basis of the home, the smaller your capital gain — which should result in a lower federal income tax liability. See if your employer offers a high-deductible health plan that enables you to make contributions to a qualifying health savings account (HSA). If you otherwise meet the eligibility requirements for an HSA, when you link a high-deductible health plan to an HSA, your contributions are tax deductible or may be made by pre-tax salary deductions if allowed by your employer. Earnings and withdrawals for qualified medical expenses are federal income tax-free. A move to consider: If your HSA allows you to invest the money you contribute, its potential growth could help you pay for healthcare costs as you age and even cover the cost of Medicare premiums. Don’t forget: Money contributed to an employer-sponsored flexible spending account (FSA) for healthcare expenses offers another way to lower your federally taxable income. Participating in certain types of FSAs may have an impact on your eligibility for HSA contributions. “In most cases, there are tax consequences when you sell investments to realize gains. But not all investments are taxed at the same rate,” says tax accountant Vinay Navani of WilkinGuttenplan.1 “In most cases, there are tax consequences when you sell investments to realize gains. But not all investments are taxed at the same rate.” — Vinay Navani, tax accountant, WilkinGuttenplan Bond interest and dividends from real estate investment trusts (REITs) are generally taxed as ordinary income for federal income tax purposes, at rates as high as 37%. Qualified dividends and gains from the sale of investments owned for more than a year are eligible for long-term capital gains rates, which currently are generally capped at 20%. In either case, an additional 3.8% net investment income tax may apply for taxpayers with incomes above a certain threshold. A move to consider: In general, think about having non-income-producing investments, such as growth stocks, in your taxable accounts and keeping investments that generate income, such as corporate bonds, in tax-deferred accounts. Don’t forget: It’s possible to offset capital gains by selling investments that have dropped in value. You can generally deduct up to $3,000 (or $1,500 if married and filing separately) of capital losses in excess of capital gains per year from your ordinary income. Any unused capital losses may be carried forward to the next taxable year. Traditional 401(k) contributions are made on a pre-tax basis, giving you immediate savings by reducing your federal taxable income. In addition, investment income in your traditional 401(k) account is not subject to federal income taxes until the money is taken out at retirement, at which time it’s taxed as federal ordinary income. Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax income, but then qualified withdrawals starting at age 59½ are potentially federal income tax-free. A move to consider: Splitting your contributions, putting in enough as traditional 401(k) contributions to limit your taxable income and keep from rising into a higher tax bracket, then putting the rest in as Roth 401(k) contributions. Don’t forget: Be sure to contribute enough to your 401(k) to earn the maximum matching contribution from your employer. Until recently, companies could allocate matching contributions only to a pre-tax account in your 401(k) plan, meaning any match of the employee’s Roth contributions would be made on a pre-tax basis. Since the enactment of the SECURE 2.0 Act of 2022, employers can now offer the option to match contributions on a Roth basis as well; it would then be up to the employee to elect the option. This is just a sampling of the kinds of situations where you might benefit from being more tax aware, notes Navani. “Remember, too, that tax laws change frequently, and anticipating future shifts in tax rates and rules could help to influence the financial decisions you make today.” You should discuss tax-related strategies with your tax advisor. Your financial advisor can work with your tax advisor to help ensure your wealth management strategies are aligned. New to Merrill? Connect with a Merrill Advisor There are items below that require your attention before submitting. Something went wrong! Your form couldn’t be submitted correctly. Please try again. Would you like us to contact you? Yes No First name* Last name* Email address* Confirm email address* Primary phone* Zip code* By providing your contact information above, you agree that a representative of Merrill, the Brokerage affiliate of Bank of America Corporation, may contact you via telephone and/or email to discuss and/or offer investment products and services that may be appropriate for you. You agree that you are providing to us your consent for us to contact you regardless of any Do Not Call or Do Not Email privacy choices you may have previously expressed until you revoke this consent, or up to 90 days. You may revoke your consent at any time by notifying the Merrill representative. Submit Thank you. You’ll receive an automated email confirming receipt of your request shortly. A Merrill financial advisor will be in contact with you in the coming days. 1 As a CPA and shareholder at WilkinGuttenplan, Vinay Navani is not affiliated with Merrill or any of its affiliates. This article is for information and educational purposes only. Opinions and views expressed are his, do not necessarily reflect those of Merrill or any of its affiliates and are subject to change without notice. Important Disclosures The information contained in this material does not constitute advice on the tax consequences of making any particular investment decision. This material does not take into account your particular investment objectives, financial situations or needs and is not intended as a recommendation, offer or solicitation for the purchase or sale of any security, financial instrument, or strategy. Before acting on any recommendation in this material, you should consider whether it is in your best interest based on your particular circumstances and, if necessary, seek professional advice. Merrill, its affiliates, and financial advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. You should consult your legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. Investing involves risk. There is always the potential of losing money when you invest in securities. Past performance is no guarantee of future results. All recommendations must be considered in the context of an individual investor’s goals, time horizon, liquidity needs and risk tolerance. Not all recommendations will be in the best interest of all investors. This material should be regarded as educational information on healthcare and is not intended to provide specific advice. If you have questions regarding your particular situation, you should contact your healthcare, legal and/or tax advisors. Real estate investment trusts (REITs) involve a significant degree of risk and should be regarded as speculative. Private REITs are only made available to qualified investors under the terms of a private offering memorandum. Holdings in a REIT may be highly leveraged and, therefore, more sensitive to adverse business or financial developments. Holdings in a REIT are generally long term and unlikely to produce a realized return for investors for a number of years. Interests in a private REIT are not transferable. The holdings may be illiquid — very thinly traded or assets for which no market exists. A REIT may use leverage, which even on a short-term basis can magnify increases or decreases in the value of the private equity investment. The business of identifying REIT opportunities is competitive, and there is no assurance that the REIT will be able to complete attractive investments or fully commit its capital. In addition, a REIT’s high fees and expenses may offset the fund's profits. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Personal Retirement Calculator Track your progress toward retirement with our easy-to-use calculator. Try the calculator More for you More for you Read this next: “The dos and don’ts of taxes in retirement” Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles Planning to relocate in retirement? Consider 5 ways this could impact your taxes Read more How should taxes factor into your investment decisions? Read more Selling stocks and bonds? 6 steps to help trim your tax bill Read more","title":"4 times you should always ask, ‘How will this affect my taxes?’","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/financial-events-affect-taxes.html","id":378},{"content_hash":"64aa82c6c4ef8b2df8c72a6d2aa60a7affab0e6177087d936779391f5e3cafae","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:44.731521+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-tony-abbotts-world-stage-spotlight-33942","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Tony Abbott’s world stage spotlight Published: November 6, 2014 9:19pm EST https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-tony-abbotts-world-stage-spotlight-33942 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-tony-abbotts-world-stage-spotlight-33942 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Professorial Fellow Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics, including Russia and the G20 Leaders’ Summit, Australia and China’s impending free trade agreement, the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank and Ebola assistance in West Africa. G20 Video Ebola Vladimir Putin The Week in Politics Michelle Grattan, Stephen Parker, University of Canberra Authors Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.wr9rjkqeh","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on Tony Abbott’s world stage spotlight","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-tony-abbotts-world-stage-spotlight-33942","id":379},{"content_hash":"ab23bb45dd48af0eebe88da06f508c0cc45930f4711f95104828879841ce159f","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:44.049184+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/topic/federal-parliament","language":"","text":"Federal Parliament - Topic - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenFederal ParliamentATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentTopic:Government and PoliticsThe federal government will temporarily cover credit card surcharges after businesses warn that losing the payment option will put them under pressure.Angus Taylor tries to find his place on the migration middle roadTopic:Federal ParliamentGovernment under pressure to intervene over ATO credit card banTopic:Federal GovernmentAustralia could adapt airline and bank laws for AI safetyTopic:AIMarrickville Golf Club received closed non-competitive grants on five past occasionsTopic:Federal GovernmentATO will not budge on credit card ban after meeting with business groupsSmall business groups say the ATO has indicated it will not reverse its decision to stop accepting credit card payments.Topic:TaxWed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:17pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:17pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:17pmTaylor concedes aged care workers would be hit by migration planAngus Taylor acknowledges some aged care workers would be affected by the Coalition's proposed migration changes, as business and education groups warn of wider economic damage. Topic:ImmigrationWed 7 Oct 2026 at 2:34pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 2:34pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 2:34pmHere's how Labor, the Coalition and One Nation's migration cuts compareOne Nation wants negative migration in its first years, the Coalition has promised to more than halve it, while Labor has announced measures to ensure migration actually falls to its target of 225,000. Here is what the parties want to do on migration, and how they propose to do it.Topic:ImmigrationTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:22pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:22pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:22pmGraduate visas face the axe in Coalition migration overhaulThe Coalition would cut net overseas migration to 100,000 for two years under a sweeping overhaul targeting graduate and bridging visas.Topic:Migration PolicyTue 6 Oct 2026 at 10:44pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 10:44pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 10:44pmSoldier who'd give 'shirt off his back' to mates killed in NT training exerciseThe Australian soldier who died in a military training exercise near Darwin yesterday has been identified as 28-year-old Corporal Brendan Hannam.Topic:ArmyTue 6 Oct 2026 at 6:31pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 6:31pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 6:31pmPublic supermarkets could slash food bills by quarter, Greens think tank saysTaking inspiration from New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani, \"Fair Go Grocers\" public supermarkets would offer essential goods at a discount and act as a competitor to drive down prices at Coles and Woolworths.Topic:Retail IndustryTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:28amTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:28amTue 6 Oct 2026 at 5:28amThe Liberals and Greens appear to be heading in the same direction Anthony Albanese promised to be \"the adult in the room\" and govern from the centre. But is that still the political mood as we enter the last part of 2026? PAnalysis by Patricia KarvelasMon 5 Oct 2026 at 10:27amMon 5 Oct 2026 at 10:27amMon 5 Oct 2026 at 10:27amTreasurer downplays chance of cost-of-living relief amid surging debtIt seems unlikely households will get cost-of-living relief this year after Treasurer Jim Chalmers revealed the government is looking to \"tighten\" the budget as borrowing costs boom. Topic:Federal GovernmentSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:57pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:57pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:57pmVoter anger is fuelling Greens' populist move to take on the rightDavid Shoebridge has big ambitions for the Greens, but his party is still wrestling with how to turn voter disillusionment into greater support.CAnalysis by Clare ArmstrongSat 3 Oct 2026 at 11:58amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 11:58amSat 3 Oct 2026 at 11:58amLabor will go after lawyers trying to 'game' the visa systemLabor is moving against lawyers and migration agents accused of profiting from visa applications that have little prospect of success.Topic:Federal GovernmentThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:59pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:59pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:59pmThe last thing Albanese needed was free advice from his own sideUnder pressure from opponents after another interest rate hike and more bad inflation news, the last thing Anthony Albanese needed was free advice from his own side.CAnalysis by Courtney GouldThu 1 Oct 2026 at 5:32pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 5:32pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 5:32pmKeogh says he can still work with veterans after health cap backdownThe Veterans' Affairs Minister has rebuffed calls to resign, stating there's \"still more to do\".Topic:VeteransFri 2 Oct 2026 at 2:17pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 2:17pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 2:17pmLabor MP accuses Greens leader of 'prejudice' over Jewish community commentsLabor MP Josh Burns accuses new Greens leader David Shoebridge of \"prejudice\" over comments about the party's relationship with Jewish Australians.Topic:Federal GovernmentThu 1 Oct 2026 at 3:50pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 3:50pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 3:50pmAI leaders warn US 'tech bros' must be forced to invest in AustraliaUnion figures and leading academics want big tech companies building Australian data centres to help fund the local AI industry through a levy.Topic:AI EthicsThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:29amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:29amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:29amChalmers promised action on inflation and productivity. The scorecard on both is grimAs political pressure mounts from the left and right, the risk is that pursuing more short-term relief will only make the government's long-term challenge harder.DAnalysis by David SpeersThu 1 Oct 2026 at 4:48amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 4:48amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 4:48amShoebridge emotional as he remembers mentor and 'bloody fun friend'New Greens leader David Shoebridge gets emotional during his first interview as leader while paying tribute to his political mentor before critiquing the federal government over the cost of living.Topic:The GreensWed 30 Sep 2026 at 8:55pmWed 30 Sep 2026 at 8:55pmWed 30 Sep 2026 at 8:55pmDavid Shoebridge named new Greens leader in hotly contested ballotAfter a rare leadership ballot for the Greens, the NSW senator becomes the sixth leader in the party's history.Topic:The GreensWed 30 Sep 2026 at 3:08pmWed 30 Sep 2026 at 3:08pmWed 30 Sep 2026 at 3:08pmThe Greens are about to pick a new leader. Who are the contenders?The Greens will select a new leader after the shock resignation of Larissa Waters, as the party considers its future direction amid a surging One Nation.Topic:The GreensWed 30 Sep 2026 at 6:44amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 6:44amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 6:44amLabor defends economic record following interest rate hikeAnother interest rate rise has turned attention to government spending, weak productivity and other domestic pressures contributing to inflation.Topic:Federal GovernmentWed 30 Sep 2026 at 4:49amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 4:49amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 4:49amOpenAI hack sparks crackdown on weak government websitesActing Home Affairs Minister Richard Marles says government systems must \"keep up\" with the rapid changes sparked by artificial intelligence.Topic:Cyber SecurityWed 30 Sep 2026 at 12:17amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 12:17amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 12:17amLoad more stories aboveBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Federal Parliament","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/topic/federal-parliament","id":380},{"content_hash":"1c4090e86a9810769e54a1268f5936d27219bd4d60828ddade5444092877fdca","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:43.595647+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/pour-appliquer-le-degrevement-sur-les-carburants-en-faveur-des-agriculteurs-un-systeme-de-bons-est-institue_2072196_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives POUR APPLIQUER LE DÉGRÈVEMENT SUR LES CARBURANTS EN FAVEUR DES AGRICULTEURS un système de bons est institué Le Monde Publié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 07 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"POUR APPLIQUER LE DÉGRÈVEMENT SUR LES CARBURANTS EN FAVEUR DES AGRICULTEURS un système de bons est institué","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/pour-appliquer-le-degrevement-sur-les-carburants-en-faveur-des-agriculteurs-un-systeme-de-bons-est-institue_2072196_1819218.html","id":381},{"content_hash":"abc3bb571c16d9b73030063c46657f68259d2281fba26a0e80c6007472eb17db","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:43.528703+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-clash-of-the-orgasms-clitoral-vs-vaginal-32732","language":"","text":"Health Check: clash of the orgasms, clitoral vs vaginal Published: November 9, 2014 10:29pm EST Most women are just happy to have an orgasm, any old way. Ares Tavolazzi/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA https://theconversation.com/health-check-clash-of-the-orgasms-clitoral-vs-vaginal-32732 https://theconversation.com/health-check-clash-of-the-orgasms-clitoral-vs-vaginal-32732 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Controversy over vaginal versus clitoral orgasm is nothing new; it’s a debate that has consumed sexologists and psychoanalysts for the last 100 years. Now, new research has added fresh fuel to the controversy. Completed by a team of Italian sexologists and published in the journal Clinical Anatomy, the review concludes vaginal orgasms don’t exist. Female orgasm is only possible if the clitoris is stimulated during masturbation, cunnilingus, partner masturbation or with a finger during intercourse, the researchers say. Penetration alone is not enough. This latest swing of the pendulum – from the view that vaginal orgasm is the ideal that women should aspire to and anything else is second rate – is unlikely to actually affect women. Indeed, one of the more interesting threads in this whole debate is the predominance of men’s voices. Perhaps what we should be talking about is why male experts dictate the parameters of women’s pleasure. Frigidity and failure Sigmund Freud was one of the first to investigate the “dark continent” of female sexuality. He declared the clitoral orgasm “infantile and immature”. A woman could claim sexual maturity only when she experienced a vaginal orgasm, he said, ignoring her “amputated penis”, the clitoris. Inability to achieve vaginal orgasm meant a woman was “frigid” or “not a real woman”, claimed Freud and many of his followers. This failure was attributed to deep-rooted neurotic problems. Sigmund Freud declared the clitoral orgasm ‘infantile and immature’. Max Halberstadt/Wikimedia Commons The pressure was on. To be “normal” and “mature”, women had to orgasm during sexual intercourse. And successive generations were diagnosed with sexual dysfunction when they failed to achieve this holy grail of sexual response. Many felt like failures; their bodies had let them down. Unsurprisingly, faking orgasms during intercourse became the norm. No one wants her partner to think she is failing to be a “real woman”. Celebrating the clitoral orgasm Then US sexologists William Masters and Virginia Johnson came along. Observing couples having sex in the laboratory in the 1960s, they concluded women’s orgasms started in the clitoris and then extended to the vagina. Any pleasure women experienced through penetration was due to the connection between clitoris and vagina. They reported “frigidity” as resulting from poor sexual technique, not women’s ambivalence about their social role. And that women were capable of multiple orgasm, while men were not. Feminists in the 1960s took up this research with glee, declaring the clitoral orgasm the mark of a liberated woman. Some went further, arguing women should eschew penile penetration altogether. Now a symbol of women’s oppression, it was unnecessary for sexual pleasure. The feminist argument went mainstream when Shere Hite appeared on the cover of Time magazine in 1987. She had interviewed 1,844 American women and declared the “true” female orgasm was clitoral. The female sexual revolution seemed to have been won with women speaking for their own sexual pleasure. Phallocentric backlash Then came the inevitable backlash. In recent years, there has been a proliferation of sex research attempting to establish the superiority of the vaginal orgasm, and the role of the penis in producing it. In echoes of Freud, we are told the vaginal orgasm is the only way for women to achieve sexual, life and relationship satisfaction, as well as good psychological health. Women who don’t have vaginal orgasms are described as emotionally unstable, with immature defence mechanisms and low emotional intelligence. Apparently, you can even identify a woman who has a history of vaginal orgasm by her walk – it is that central to her very being. So what causes a vaginal orgasm, according to these researchers? Not stimulation of the clitoris during intercourse. Rather, a long penis, which allegedly gives an evolutionary advantage to well-endowed men. Or long-lasting intercourse, which we are told is much better than “foreplay”, with simultaneous orgasm during intercourse being the best of all. Would it surprise you if I told you this phallocentric research is all conducted by men? Would their interest in the vaginal orgasm possibly have something to do with maintaining the primacy of the penis? After all, the implications of the clitoral orgasm are grave for heterosexual men. Women can pleasure themselves (or be pleasured by each other) as effectively as they can be pleasured by a man if the penis is superfluous to their ability to orgasm. A man’s fingers become more important, or his smell, which some heterosexual women rate more highly than penis size. A woman’s perspective From a woman’s perspective, this whole debate is a little irrelevant. Some women enjoy vaginal penetration – with penis or fingers – and gain considerable sexual pleasure as a result. Other women prefer to be touched, use a vibrator, or receive oral sex. A lucky few have orgasms in their sleep, in the absence of any physical stimulation. And some prefer to have a cup of tea. To imply that all women are the same, that we should have any sort of orgasm and are dysfunctional if we don’t, is the most damaging part of this controversy. Regardless of how orgasm is achieved, it is, by definition, an extremely pleasurable experience. And no woman I know would rate one form of orgasm as more “mature” than another. Most would just be happy to have one, any old way. Sex Orgasm Sigmund Freud Sexual health Female sexuality Health Check Vaginal orgasm Clitoral orgasm Sexology Jane Ussher, Western Sydney University Author Jane Ussher Professor of Women's Health Psychology, Translational Health Research Institute (THRI), Western Sydney University Disclosure statement Jane Ussher receives funding from the Australian Research Council, Cancer Council NSW, Family Planning NSW, Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia, and the Community Migrant Resource Centre, for research on sexuality. Partners Western Sydney University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.ma5aastak","title":"Health Check: clash of the orgasms, clitoral vs vaginal","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-clash-of-the-orgasms-clitoral-vs-vaginal-32732","id":382},{"content_hash":"4e99cd1ac59c8d2a2200ccd2e702250e5775cc47a2d7f1e5c18ab1eb87c4aa0b","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:43.299956+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-proofing","language":"","text":"What Is Identity Proofing?ByEmmanuel AgwuMarch 31, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Does Identity Proofing Mean?Why does Identity Proofing Matter?How is Identity Proofing Carried Out As A Crucial Part Of The KYC Process?What is Digital Identity Proofing?The Importance of Digital Identity ProofingIdentity Verification Methods That Are Commonly Adopted In KYC ProcessesBottom LineShare this post The identity of an individual facilitates recognition. Recognition of an individual comes in handy in many ways, that is, in different contexts and provisions. When it comes to financial transactions and business, identification and recognition will always be pertinent. Definitely, it is rational for every business entity, individual, company, and financial institution to confirm an individual's identity through proper recognition processes. This is done in order to avoid the successful perpetuation of malicious activities. What Does Identity Proofing Mean? To the layman or mind, Identity Proofing may mean or involve verbal confirmation of the genuine of one's identity to accelerate recognition. However, Identity Proofing refers to the process of verifying a user’s identity, that is, authenticating that they are who they say they are. If a certain customer claims to be; John Doe, there must be evidence to authenticate that he is John Doe. It may sound like mere authentication based on a username and password. However, identity proofing comes before all of that. It comes into play even before a user or a customer gets to have a username or a password. Identity proofing refers to verifying an individual's identity to ensure that they are who they claim to be. It is a critical step in many industries, such as finance, healthcare, and government, where personal and confidential information is exchanged. Identity proofing involves collecting and verifying various forms of identification and personal information, such as government-issued IDs, biometric data, and personal background information. This process is used to confirm an individual's identity and ensure they are authorized to access certain resources or services. The aim of the process of identity proofing is to establish trust in the identity of an individual to prevent fraud and identity theft. It is an important part of identity and access management (IAM). It is often used with other security measures, such as multi-factor authentication, to protect sensitive information and systems. Why does Identity Proofing Matter? According to credible sources, identity fraud or theft costs the United Kingdom about four billion a year. There are an estimated 28 million data breaches across the UK every year. Using an email address and a phone number alone is not enough for a secure identity and access management system. It tells very little about an individual. This is why it is important to go beyond those two pieces of information and go through a full KYC process as a business. Proper Identity proofing reduces data breaches by eighty percent since eighty percent of data breaches and identity fraud are associated with weak, reused, and stolen credentials. For one, identity proofing is an essential component of KYC regulations. KYC refers to the process adopted by financial institutions and businesses or corporate entities to accurately verify the identity of a potential customer or incoming customer, and their risk levels are also assessed. KYC regulations aim to prevent money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit activities that can be perpetrated or facilitated through financial processes. Identity proofing is important to KYC because it enables financial institutions to verify their customers' identities accurately. This process involves collecting and verifying various forms of identification and personal information, such as government-issued IDs, biometric data, and personal background information. It ensures that the person applying for financial services is who they claim to be and that the personal information they provide is accurate. Fraud and money laundering risks can be mitigated through the KYC process by conducting appropriate identity proofing. It also helps financial institutions comply with regulatory requirements and avoid potential fines and reputational damage associated with non-compliance. It is important to know that Identity proofing is crucial to the KYC process. It enables financial institutions to accurately verify identify their customers and comply with the regulations provided and, to help prevent financial crimes and protect the financial system's integrity. Read Also: Electronic ID Verification How is Identity Proofing Carried Out As A Crucial Part Of The KYC Process? Identity proofing is done on KYC by collecting and verifying different authentic forms of identification and personal information from the customer. Here are the usual ways or steps which are involved with KYC. a. Customer’s Information Is Collected Customers are asked to provide personal information like; name, date of birth, address, a form of identification issued by the government, such as a passport, photo card, driving license, or national id card. These will serve as a form of proof for a person's identity and existence. b. Identity Documents Are Verified Identification documents provided by the customer will be verified for authenticity and validity. Verification may involve checking the documents' holograms, watermarks, and other security features. c. Personal Information Provided Are Verified Personal information provided by customers is verified through multiple sources, like credit bureaus, public records, and other official databases. d. Biometric Verification KYC processes can also include biometric verification, such as facial recognition and fingerprint scanning, to confirm the identity of the customer. It will also help determine if the customer has been on any watchlists or has a history of suspicious or criminal activity. e. Risk Assessment The total information collected is analyzed to assess the customer's risk level. This also determines if the customer is on any watchlists or has a history of suspicious activity. f. Ongoing Or Continuous Monitoring KYC requirements will involve monitoring the customer’s activity in order to detect any suspicious behaviour or changes in risk level. Certainly, identity proofing for KYC involves a comprehensive verification process that ensures the customer's identity is accurately verified and their risk level is assessed. The procedures may vary depending on the financial institution's policies and regulatory requirements. What is Digital Identity Proofing? Digital identity proofing process is an important process that verifies a person's identity in the digital realm. It involves collecting and validating various pieces of information, including name, date of birth, address, and government-issued identification documents. This technology plays a vital role in enhancing online security by ensuring that individuals are who they claim to be. Implementing a robust digital identity proofing measures, businesses and organizations can mitigate risks, protect sensitive data, and foster trust in the digital ecosystem. The Importance of Digital Identity Proofing As the digital landscape continues to evolve, the need for secure and reliable digital identity proofing solutions becomes increasingly essential. By implementing robust digital identity proofing measures, businesses and organizations can:Mitigate Fraud: Protect against identity theft and fraudulent activities.Enhance Customer Experience: Streamline onboarding processes and improve customer satisfaction.Comply with Regulations: Adhere to industry standards and government regulations.Build Trust: Establish credibility and build strong customer relationships. Key Technologies in Digital Identity Proofing Process Several technologies are employed in digital identity proofing to ensure accuracy and security. They include biometric authentication, knowledge-based authentication, and document verification. Identity Verification Methods That Are Commonly Adopted In KYC Processes It is not confusing to ascertain materials and ways to facilitate identity proofing. However, as time advances, methods advance as well. This section will explain and discuss identity verification methods that are commonly adopted. Identity verification aims to ascertain or, more technically, authenticate a potential customer's acknowledged identity in the KYC process. This is a crucial step to preventing the preparation of financial fraud and other malicious or illegal activities that may be facilitated with financial irregularities, like money laundering to drug trafficking. 1. Social media verification It may seem unconventional, however, with the widespread use of social media and the equivalent growth of users on various social platforms. Social media verification may be a method to consider. However, it may be easily manipulated in some ways. Social media platforms can be used to verify an individual's identity. This involves confirming their account details and comparing them to their government-issued ID. 2. Biometric Verification Biometric verification: Biometric verification involves using unique physical characteristics, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, to establish a customer's identity. This method is becoming increasingly popular, particularly in the financial industry. 3. Knowledge-based authentication Knowledge-based authentication involves answering a series of questions about the personal information only the true individual would know, such as the name of a first pet or the street where they grew up. However, this is not used in an initial KYC process. And may be considered an outdated method, but it was commonly used in the 2000s. 4. Government-issued Identification Using government-issued identification is one of the most common ways to prove a customer’s identity is by presenting a government-issued ID, such as a passport, driving photo license, or national ID card. It still remains one of the standard methods to adopt. 5. Utility Bill Verification Some organizations may require individuals to provide a utility bill as proof of their address. This can be used together with other forms of identification to verify an individual's identity. 6. In-person Verification Organizations may sometimes require individuals to present themselves in person to confirm their identity. This can involve meeting with a representative of the organization or going to a designated location for identification purposes. 7. Notarized documents Notarized documents can be used to prove an individual's identity in certain circumstances, such as when applying for a loan or opening a bank account. 8. In-person verification Organizations may sometimes require individuals to present themselves in person to confirm their identity. This can involve meeting with a representative of the organization or going to a designated location for identification purposes. 9. Background Check Background checks can also be used to verify an individual's identity. This involves conducting a search of public records and databases to confirm personal information. Recommended: Understanding the difference between authentication, identification and verification Bottom Line Various organizations adopt different methods to verify the identities that potential customers are presenting. It is a crucial part of the KYC process and will serve as a major shield against illicit financial activities. Businesses can quickly perform KYC, biometrics verification, fraud detection, and anti-money laundering checks with Youverify. Join 100+ leading companies that use Youverify for identity spoofing for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Is Identity Proofing?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-identity-proofing","id":383},{"content_hash":"b525c69d1419d758fbb182436ede0a24fe5be2e157cf6765e485d37169052a6f","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:43.155367+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/django-admin/","language":"","text":"django-admin and manage.py¶ django-admin is Django’s command-line utility for administrative tasks. This document outlines all it can do. In addition, manage.py is automatically created in each Django project. It does the same thing as django-admin but also sets the DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE environment variable so that it points to your project’s settings.py file. The django-admin script should be on your system path if you installed Django via pip. If it’s not in your path, ensure you have your virtual environment activated. Generally, when working on a single Django project, it’s easier to use manage.py than django-admin. If you need to switch between multiple Django settings files, use django-admin with DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE or the --settings command line option. The command-line examples throughout this document use django-admin to be consistent, but any example can use manage.py or python -m django just as well. Usage¶ Unix/macOS Windows $ django-admin <command> [options] $ manage.py <command> [options] $ python -m django <command> [options] ...\\> django-admin <command> [options] ...\\> manage.py <command> [options] ...\\> py -m django <command> [options] command should be one of the commands listed in this document. options, which is optional, should be zero or more of the options available for the given command. Getting runtime help¶ django-admin help¶ Run django-admin help to display usage information and a list of the commands provided by each application. Run django-admin help --commands to display a list of all available commands. Run django-admin help <command> to display a description of the given command and a list of its available options. App names¶ Many commands take a list of “app names.” An “app name” is the basename of the package containing your models. For example, if your INSTALLED_APPS contains the string 'mysite.blog', the app name is blog. Determining the version¶ django-admin version¶ Run django-admin version to display the current Django version. The output follows the schema described in PEP 440: 1.4.dev17026 1.4a1 1.4 Displaying debug output¶ Use --verbosity, where it is supported, to specify the amount of notification and debug information that django-admin prints to the console. Available commands¶ There are community-maintained solutions! Django has a vibrant ecosystem. There are developer tool packages highlighted on the Community Ecosystem page. The Django Packages Developer Tools grid has even more options for you! check¶ django-admin check [app_label [app_label ...]]¶ Uses the system check framework to inspect the entire Django project for common problems. By default, all apps will be checked. You can check a subset of apps by providing a list of app labels as arguments: django-admin check auth admin myapp --tag TAGS, -t TAGS¶ The system check framework performs many different types of checks that are categorized with tags. You can use these tags to restrict the checks performed to just those in a particular category. For example, to perform only models and compatibility checks, run: django-admin check --tag models --tag compatibility --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to run checks requiring database access: django-admin check --database default --database other By default, these checks will not be run. --list-tags¶ Lists all available tags. --deploy¶ Activates some additional checks that are only relevant in a deployment setting. You can use this option in your local development environment, but since your local development settings module may not have many of your production settings, you will probably want to point the check command at a different settings module, either by setting the DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE environment variable, or by passing the --settings option: django-admin check --deploy --settings=production_settings Or you could run it directly on a production or staging deployment to verify that the correct settings are in use (omitting --settings). You could even make it part of your integration test suite. --fail-level {CRITICAL,ERROR,WARNING,INFO,DEBUG}¶ Specifies the message level that will cause the command to exit with a non-zero status. Default is ERROR. compilemessages¶ django-admin compilemessages¶ Compiles .po files created by makemessages to .mo files for use with the built-in gettext support. See Internationalization and localization. --locale LOCALE, -l LOCALE¶ Specifies the locale(s) to process. If not provided, all locales are processed. --exclude EXCLUDE, -x EXCLUDE¶ Specifies the locale(s) to exclude from processing. If not provided, no locales are excluded. --use-fuzzy, -f¶ Includes fuzzy translations into compiled files. Example usage: django-admin compilemessages --locale=pt_BR django-admin compilemessages --locale=pt_BR --locale=fr -f django-admin compilemessages -l pt_BR django-admin compilemessages -l pt_BR -l fr --use-fuzzy django-admin compilemessages --exclude=pt_BR django-admin compilemessages --exclude=pt_BR --exclude=fr django-admin compilemessages -x pt_BR django-admin compilemessages -x pt_BR -x fr --ignore PATTERN, -i PATTERN¶ Ignores directories matching the given glob-style pattern. Use multiple times to ignore more. Example usage: django-admin compilemessages --ignore=node_modules --ignore=outdated/*/locale createcachetable¶ django-admin createcachetable¶ Creates the cache tables for use with the database cache backend using the information from your settings file. See Django’s cache framework for more information. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database in which the cache table(s) will be created. Defaults to default. --dry-run¶ Prints the SQL that would be run without actually running it, so you can customize it or use the migrations framework. dbshell¶ django-admin dbshell¶ Runs the command-line client for the database engine specified in your ENGINE setting, with the connection parameters specified in your USER, PASSWORD, etc., settings. For PostgreSQL, this runs the psql command-line client. For MySQL, this runs the mysql command-line client. For SQLite, this runs the sqlite3 command-line client. For Oracle, this runs the sqlplus command-line client. This command assumes the programs are on your PATH so that a call to the program name (psql, mysql, sqlite3, sqlplus) will find the program in the right place. There’s no way to specify the location of the program manually. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database onto which to open a shell. Defaults to default. -- ARGUMENTS¶ Any arguments following a -- divider will be passed on to the underlying command-line client. For example, with PostgreSQL you can use the psql command’s -c flag to execute a raw SQL query directly: Unix/macOS Windows $ django-admin dbshell -- -c 'select current_user' current_user -------------- postgres (1 row) ...\\> django-admin dbshell -- -c 'select current_user' current_user -------------- postgres (1 row) On MySQL/MariaDB, you can do this with the mysql command’s -e flag: Unix/macOS Windows $ django-admin dbshell -- -e \"select user()\" +----------------------+ | user() | +----------------------+ | djangonaut@localhost | +----------------------+ ...\\> django-admin dbshell -- -e \"select user()\" +----------------------+ | user() | +----------------------+ | djangonaut@localhost | +----------------------+ Note Be aware that not all options set in the OPTIONS part of your database configuration in DATABASES are passed to the command-line client, e.g. 'isolation_level'. diffsettings¶ django-admin diffsettings¶ Displays differences between the current settings file and Django’s default settings (or another settings file specified by --default). Settings that don’t appear in the defaults are followed by \"###\". For example, the default settings don’t define ROOT_URLCONF, so ROOT_URLCONF is followed by \"###\" in the output of diffsettings. --all¶ Displays all settings, even if they have Django’s default value. Such settings are prefixed by \"###\". --default MODULE¶ The settings module to compare the current settings against. Leave empty to compare against Django’s default settings. --output {hash,unified}¶ Specifies the output format. Available values are hash and unified. hash is the default mode that displays the output that’s described above. unified displays the output similar to diff -u. Default settings are prefixed with a minus sign, followed by the changed setting prefixed with a plus sign. dumpdata¶ django-admin dumpdata [app_label[.ModelName] [app_label[.ModelName] ...]]¶ Outputs to standard output all data in the database associated with the named application(s). If no application name is provided, all installed applications will be dumped. The output of dumpdata can be used as input for loaddata. When result of dumpdata is saved as a file, it can serve as a fixture for tests or as an initial data. Note that dumpdata uses the default manager on the model for selecting the records to dump. If you’re using a custom manager as the default manager and it filters some of the available records, not all of the objects will be dumped. --all, -a¶ Uses Django’s base manager, dumping records which might otherwise be filtered or modified by a custom manager. --format FORMAT¶ Specifies the serialization format of the output. Defaults to JSON. Supported formats are listed in Serialization formats. --indent INDENT¶ Specifies the number of indentation spaces to use in the output. Defaults to None which displays all data on single line. --exclude EXCLUDE, -e EXCLUDE¶ Prevents specific applications or models (specified in the form of app_label.ModelName) from being dumped. If you specify a model name, then only that model will be excluded, rather than the entire application. You can also mix application names and model names. If you want to exclude multiple applications, pass --exclude more than once: django-admin dumpdata --exclude=auth --exclude=contenttypes --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database from which data will be dumped. Defaults to default. --natural-foreign¶ Uses the natural_key() model method to serialize any foreign key and many-to-many relationship to objects of the type that defines the method. If you’re dumping contrib.auth Permission objects or contrib.contenttypes ContentType objects, you should probably use this flag. See the natural keys documentation for more details on this and the next option. --natural-primary¶ Omits the primary key in the serialized data of this object since it can be calculated during deserialization. --pks PRIMARY_KEYS¶ Outputs only the objects specified by a comma separated list of primary keys. This is only available when dumping one model. By default, all the records of the model are output. --output OUTPUT, -o OUTPUT¶ Specifies a file to write the serialized data to. By default, the data goes to standard output. When this option is set and --verbosity is greater than 0 (the default), a progress bar is shown in the terminal. Fixtures compression¶ The output file can be compressed with one of the bz2, gz, lzma, or xz formats by ending the filename with the corresponding extension. For example, to output the data as a compressed JSON file: django-admin dumpdata -o mydata.json.gz flush¶ django-admin flush¶ Removes all data from the database and re-executes any post-synchronization handlers. The table of which migrations have been applied is not cleared. If you would rather start from an empty database and rerun all migrations, you should drop and recreate the database and then run migrate instead. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to flush. Defaults to default. inspectdb¶ django-admin inspectdb [table [table ...]]¶ Introspects the database tables in the database pointed-to by the NAME setting and outputs a Django model module (a models.py file) to standard output. You may choose what tables or views to inspect by passing their names as arguments. If no arguments are provided, models are created for views only if the --include-views option is used. Models for partition tables are created on PostgreSQL if the --include-partitions option is used. Use this if you have a legacy database with which you’d like to use Django. The script will inspect the database and create a model for each table within it. As you might expect, the created models will have an attribute for every field in the table. Note that inspectdb has a few special cases and limitations in its output: If inspectdb cannot map a column’s type to a model field type, it’ll use TextField and will insert the Python comment 'This field type is a guess.' next to the field in the generated model. The recognized fields may depend on apps listed in INSTALLED_APPS. For example, django.contrib.postgres adds recognition for several PostgreSQL-specific field types. If the database column name is a Python reserved word (such as 'pass', 'class' or 'for'), inspectdb will append '_field' to the attribute name. For example, if a table has a column 'for', the generated model will have a field 'for_field', with the db_column attribute set to 'for'. inspectdb will insert the Python comment 'Field renamed because it was a Python reserved word.' next to the field. Database defaults are not introspected and will be absent from the generated output. Generated fields are mapped to their output field type. Models that reference each other may not be in the correct order. If models were managed by Django’s migration framework, Python-level field options that have no database-level representation will not be included. In particular, Field.default will not be included, and Python variants for on_delete are always emitted as DO_NOTHING. As such, after you run it, you’ll want to look over the generated models yourself to make customizations. This feature is meant as a shortcut, not as definitive model generation. By default, inspectdb creates unmanaged models. That is, managed = False in the model’s Meta class tells Django not to manage each table’s creation, modification, and deletion. If you do want to allow Django to manage the table’s lifecycle, you’ll need to change the managed option to True (or remove it because True is its default value). Database-specific notes¶ Oracle¶ Models are created for materialized views if --include-views is used. PostgreSQL¶ Models are created for foreign tables. Models are created for materialized views if --include-views is used. Models are created for partition tables if --include-partitions is used. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to introspect. Defaults to default. --include-partitions¶ If this option is provided, models are also created for partitions. Only support for PostgreSQL is implemented. --include-views¶ If this option is provided, models are also created for database views. listurls¶ django-admin listurls¶ Added in version 6.2. List URL patterns from the project’s root URLconf with optional filtering by prefixes, inspired by show_urls from django-extensions. --unsorted, -u¶ Lists URLs in the original ordering from urlpatterns. The default ordering is alphabetical by route. --prefix, -p [prefix ...]¶ Filters URLs by given prefixes. --format, -f {tabular,stacked,json}¶ Specifies the output format. Available values are tabular, stacked, and json. Default is tabular. loaddata¶ django-admin loaddata fixture [fixture ...]¶ Searches for and loads the contents of the named fixture into the database. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database into which the data will be loaded. Defaults to default. --ignorenonexistent, -i¶ Ignores fields and models that may have been removed since the fixture was originally generated. --app APP_LABEL¶ Specifies a single app to look for fixtures in rather than looking in all apps. --format FORMAT¶ Specifies the serialization format (e.g., json or xml) for fixtures read from stdin. --exclude EXCLUDE, -e EXCLUDE¶ Excludes loading the fixtures from the given applications and/or models (in the form of app_label or app_label.ModelName). Use the option multiple times to exclude more than one app or model. Loading fixtures from stdin¶ You can use a dash as the fixture name to load input from sys.stdin. For example: django-admin loaddata --format=json - When reading from stdin, the --format option is required to specify the serialization format of the input (e.g., json or xml). Loading from stdin is useful with standard input and output redirections. For example: django-admin dumpdata --format=json --database=test app_label.ModelName | django-admin loaddata --format=json --database=prod - The dumpdata command can be used to generate input for loaddata. See also For more detail about fixtures see the Fixtures topic. makemessages¶ django-admin makemessages¶ Runs over the entire source tree of the current directory and pulls out all strings marked for translation. It creates (or updates) a message file in the conf/locale (in the Django tree) or locale (for project and application) directory. After making changes to the messages files you need to compile them with compilemessages for use with the builtin gettext support. See the i18n documentation for details. This command doesn’t require configured settings. However, when settings aren’t configured, the command can’t ignore the MEDIA_ROOT and STATIC_ROOT directories or include LOCALE_PATHS. --all, -a¶ Updates the message files for all available languages. --extension EXTENSIONS, -e EXTENSIONS¶ Specifies a list of file extensions to examine (default: html, txt, py or js if --domain is djangojs). Example usage: django-admin makemessages --locale=de --extension xhtml Separate multiple extensions with commas or use -e or --extension multiple times: django-admin makemessages --locale=de --extension=html,txt --extension xml --locale LOCALE, -l LOCALE¶ Specifies the locale(s) to process. --exclude EXCLUDE, -x EXCLUDE¶ Specifies the locale(s) to exclude from processing. If not provided, no locales are excluded. Example usage: django-admin makemessages --locale=pt_BR django-admin makemessages --locale=pt_BR --locale=fr django-admin makemessages -l pt_BR django-admin makemessages -l pt_BR -l fr django-admin makemessages --exclude=pt_BR django-admin makemessages --exclude=pt_BR --exclude=fr django-admin makemessages -x pt_BR django-admin makemessages -x pt_BR -x fr --domain DOMAIN, -d DOMAIN¶ Specifies the domain of the messages files. Supported options are: django for all *.py, *.html and *.txt files (default) djangojs for *.js files --symlinks, -s¶ Follows symlinks to directories when looking for new translation strings. Example usage: django-admin makemessages --locale=de --symlinks --ignore PATTERN, -i PATTERN¶ Ignores files or directories matching the given glob-style pattern. Use multiple times to ignore more. These patterns are used by default: 'CVS', '.*', '*~', '*.pyc'. Example usage: django-admin makemessages --locale=en_US --ignore=apps/* --ignore=secret/*.html --no-default-ignore¶ Disables the default values of --ignore. --no-wrap¶ Disables breaking long message lines into several lines in language files. --no-location¶ Suppresses writing ‘#: filename:line’ comment lines in language files. Using this option makes it harder for technically skilled translators to understand each message’s context. --add-location [{full,file,never}]¶ Controls #: filename:line comment lines in language files. If the option is: full (the default if not given): the lines include both file name and line number. file: the line number is omitted. never: the lines are suppressed (same as --no-location). Requires gettext 0.19 or newer. --no-obsolete¶ Removes obsolete message strings from the .po files. --keep-pot¶ Prevents deleting the temporary .pot files generated before creating the .po file. This is useful for debugging errors which may prevent the final language files from being created. See also See Customizing the makemessages command for instructions on how to customize the keywords that makemessages passes to xgettext. makemigrations¶ django-admin makemigrations [app_label [app_label ...]]¶ Creates new migrations based on the changes detected to your models. Migrations, their relationship with apps and more are covered in depth in the migrations documentation. Providing one or more app names as arguments will limit the migrations created to the app(s) specified and any dependencies needed (the table at the other end of a ForeignKey, for example). To add migrations to an app that doesn’t have a migrations directory, run makemigrations with the app’s app_label. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. If a suppressed prompt cannot be resolved automatically, the command will exit with error code 3. --empty¶ Outputs an empty migration for the specified apps, for manual editing. This is for advanced users and should not be used unless you are familiar with the migration format, migration operations, and the dependencies between your migrations. --dry-run¶ Shows what migrations would be made without actually writing any migrations files to disk. Using this option along with --verbosity 3 will also show the complete migrations files that would be written. --merge¶ Enables fixing of migration conflicts. --name NAME, -n NAME¶ Allows naming the generated migration(s) instead of using a generated name. The name must be a valid Python identifier. --no-header¶ Generate migration files without Django version and timestamp header. --check¶ Makes makemigrations exit with a non-zero status when model changes without migrations are detected. Implies --dry-run. --scriptable¶ Diverts log output and input prompts to stderr, writing only paths of generated migration files to stdout. --update¶ Merges model changes into the latest migration and optimize the resulting operations. The updated migration will have a generated name. In order to preserve the previous name, set it using --name. migrate¶ django-admin migrate [app_label] [migration_name]¶ Synchronizes the database state with the current set of models and migrations. Migrations, their relationship with apps and more are covered in depth in the migrations documentation. The behavior of this command changes depending on the arguments provided: No arguments: All apps have all of their migrations run. <app_label>: The specified app has its migrations run, up to the most recent migration. This may involve running other apps’ migrations too, due to dependencies. <app_label> <migrationname>: Brings the database schema to a state where the named migration is applied, but no later migrations in the same app are applied. This may involve unapplying migrations if you have previously migrated past the named migration. You can use a prefix of the migration name, e.g. 0001, as long as it’s unique for the given app name. Use the name zero to migrate all the way back i.e. to revert all applied migrations for an app. Warning When unapplying migrations, all dependent migrations will also be unapplied, regardless of <app_label>. You can use --plan to check which migrations will be unapplied. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to migrate. Defaults to default. --fake¶ Marks the migrations up to the target one (following the rules above) as applied, but without actually running the SQL to change your database schema. This is intended for advanced users to manipulate the current migration state directly if they’re manually applying changes; be warned that using --fake runs the risk of putting the migration state table into a state where manual recovery will be needed to make migrations run correctly. --fake-initial¶ Allows Django to skip an app’s initial migration if all database tables with the names of all models created by all CreateModel operations in that migration already exist. This option is intended for use when first running migrations against a database that preexisted the use of migrations. This option does not, however, check for matching database schema beyond matching table names and so is only safe to use if you are confident that your existing schema matches what is recorded in your initial migration. --plan¶ Shows the migration operations that will be performed for the given migrate command. --run-syncdb¶ Allows creating tables for apps without migrations. While this isn’t recommended, the migrations framework is sometimes too slow on large projects with hundreds of models. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. An example prompt is asking about removing stale content types. --check¶ Makes migrate exit with a non-zero status when unapplied migrations are detected. --prune¶ Deletes nonexistent migrations from the django_migrations table. This is useful when migration files replaced by a squashed migration have been removed. See Squashing migrations for more details. optimizemigration¶ django-admin optimizemigration app_label migration_name¶ Optimizes the operations for the named migration and overrides the existing file. If the migration contains functions that must be manually copied, the command creates a new migration file suffixed with _optimized that is meant to replace the named migration. --check¶ Makes optimizemigration exit with a non-zero status when a migration can be optimized. runserver¶ django-admin runserver [addrport]¶ Starts a lightweight development web server on the local machine. By default, the server runs on port 8000 on the IP address 127.0.0.1. You can pass in an IP address and port number explicitly. If you run this script as a user with normal privileges (recommended), you might not have access to start a port on a low port number. Low port numbers are reserved for the superuser (root). This server uses the WSGI application object specified by the WSGI_APPLICATION setting. Warning DO NOT USE THIS SERVER IN A PRODUCTION SETTING. This lightweight development server has not gone through security audits or performance tests, hence is unsuitable for production. Making this server able to handle a production environment is outside the scope of Django. The development server automatically reloads Python code for each request, as needed. You don’t need to restart the server for code changes to take effect. However, some actions like adding files don’t trigger a restart, so you’ll have to restart the server in these cases. If you’re using Linux or MacOS and install both pywatchman and the Watchman service, kernel signals will be used to autoreload the server (rather than polling file modification timestamps each second). This offers better performance on large projects, reduced response time after code changes, more robust change detection, and a reduction in power usage. Django supports pywatchman 1.2.0 and higher. Large directories with many files may cause performance issues When using Watchman with a project that includes large non-Python directories like node_modules, it’s advisable to ignore this directory for optimal performance. See the watchman documentation for information on how to do this. Watchman timeout DJANGO_WATCHMAN_TIMEOUT¶ The default timeout of Watchman client is 5 seconds. You can change it by setting the DJANGO_WATCHMAN_TIMEOUT environment variable. When you start the server, and each time you change Python code while the server is running, the system check framework will check your entire Django project for some common errors (see the check command). If any errors are found, they will be printed to standard output. You can use the --skip-checks option to skip running system checks. You can run as many concurrent servers as you want, as long as they’re on separate ports by executing django-admin runserver more than once. Note that the default IP address, 127.0.0.1, is not accessible from other machines on your network. To make your development server viewable to other machines on the network, use its own IP address (e.g. 192.168.2.1), 0 (shortcut for 0.0.0.0), 0.0.0.0, or :: (with IPv6 enabled). You can provide an IPv6 address surrounded by brackets (e.g. [200a::1]:8000). This will automatically enable IPv6 support. A hostname containing ASCII-only characters can also be used. If the staticfiles contrib app is enabled (default in new projects) the runserver command will be overridden with its own runserver command. Logging of each request and response of the server is sent to the django.server logger. --noreload¶ Disables the auto-reloader. This means any Python code changes you make while the server is running will not take effect if the particular Python modules have already been loaded into memory. --nothreading¶ Disables use of threading in the development server. The server is multithreaded by default. --ipv6, -6¶ Uses IPv6 for the development server. This changes the default IP address from 127.0.0.1 to ::1. DJANGO_RUNSERVER_HIDE_WARNING¶ By default, a warning is printed to the console that runserver is not suitable for production: WARNING: This is a development server. Do not use it in a production setting. Use a production WSGI or ASGI server instead. For more information on production servers see: https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/|version|/howto/deployment/ Set this environment variable to \"true\" to hide this warning. Examples of using different ports and addresses¶ Port 8000 on IP address 127.0.0.1: django-admin runserver Port 8000 on IP address 1.2.3.4: django-admin runserver 1.2.3.4:8000 Port 7000 on IP address 127.0.0.1: django-admin runserver 7000 Port 7000 on IP address 1.2.3.4: django-admin runserver 1.2.3.4:7000 Port 8000 on IPv6 address ::1: django-admin runserver -6 Port 7000 on IPv6 address ::1: django-admin runserver -6 7000 Port 7000 on IPv6 address 2001:0db8:1234:5678::9: django-admin runserver [2001:0db8:1234:5678::9]:7000 Port 8000 on IPv4 address of host localhost: django-admin runserver localhost:8000 Port 8000 on IPv6 address of host localhost: django-admin runserver -6 localhost:8000 Serving static files with the development server¶ By default, the development server doesn’t serve any static files for your site (such as CSS files, images, things under MEDIA_URL and so forth). If you want to configure Django to serve static media, read How to manage static files (e.g. images, JavaScript, CSS). Serving with ASGI in development¶ Django’s runserver command provides a WSGI server. In order to run under ASGI you will need to use an ASGI server. The Django Daphne project provides Integration with runserver that you can use. sendtestemail¶ django-admin sendtestemail [email [email ...]]¶ Sends a test email (to confirm email sending through Django is working) to the recipient(s) specified. For example: django-admin sendtestemail foo@example.com bar@example.com The following options may be used in any combination with each other and with recipient email arguments. --using ALIAS¶ Added in version 6.1. Specifies the MAILERS alias to use for sending the test email to the recipients given as arguments. Defaults to the \"default\" mailer. --managers¶ Mails the email addresses specified in MANAGERS using mail_managers(). --admins¶ Mails the email addresses specified in ADMINS using mail_admins(). shell¶ django-admin shell¶ Starts the Python interactive interpreter. All models from installed apps are automatically imported into the shell environment. Models from apps listed earlier in INSTALLED_APPS take precedence. The following common utilities are also imported: from django.db import connection, reset_queries, models from django.conf import settings from django.utils import timezone For a --verbosity of 2 or higher, the automatically imported objects will be listed. To disable automatic importing entirely, use the --no-imports flag. See the guide on customizing this behavior to add or remove automatic imports. --interface {ipython,bpython,python}, -i {ipython,bpython,python}¶ Specifies the shell to use. By default, Django will use IPython or bpython if either is installed. If both are installed, specify which one you want like so: IPython: django-admin shell -i ipython bpython: django-admin shell -i bpython If you have a “rich” shell installed but want to force use of the “plain” Python interpreter, use python as the interface name, like so: django-admin shell -i python --no-startup¶ Disables reading the startup script for the “plain” Python interpreter. By default, the script pointed to by the PYTHONSTARTUP environment variable or the ~/.pythonrc.py script is read. --no-imports¶ Disables the automatic import of models from INSTALLED_APPS. --command COMMAND, -c COMMAND¶ Lets you pass a command as a string to execute it as Django, like so: django-admin shell --command=\"import django; print(django.__version__)\" You can also pass code in on standard input to execute it. For example: $ django-admin shell <<EOF > import django > print(django.__version__) > EOF On Windows, the REPL is output due to implementation limits of select.select() on that platform. showmigrations¶ django-admin showmigrations [app_label [app_label ...]]¶ Shows all migrations in a project. You can choose from one of two formats: --list, -l¶ Lists all of the apps Django knows about, the migrations available for each app, and whether or not each migration is applied (marked by an [X] next to the migration name). For a --verbosity of 2 and above, the applied datetimes are also shown. Apps without migrations are also listed, but have (no migrations) printed under them. This is the default output format. --plan, -p¶ Shows the migration plan Django will follow to apply migrations. Like --list, applied migrations are marked by an [X]. For a --verbosity of 2 and above, all dependencies of a migration will also be shown. app_labels arguments limit the output, however, dependencies of provided apps may also be included. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to examine. Defaults to default. sqlflush¶ django-admin sqlflush¶ Prints the SQL statements that would be executed for the flush command. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database for which to print the SQL. Defaults to default. sqlmigrate¶ django-admin sqlmigrate app_label migration_name¶ Prints the SQL for the named migration. This requires an active database connection, which it will use to resolve constraint names; this means you must generate the SQL against a copy of the database you wish to later apply it on. Note that sqlmigrate doesn’t colorize its output. --backwards¶ Generates the SQL for unapplying the migration. By default, the SQL created is for running the migration in the forwards direction. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database for which to generate the SQL. Defaults to default. sqlsequencereset¶ django-admin sqlsequencereset app_label [app_label ...]¶ Prints the SQL statements for resetting sequences for the given app name(s). Sequences are indexes used by some database engines to track the next available number for automatically incremented fields. Use this command to generate SQL which will fix cases where a sequence is out of sync with its automatically incremented field data. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database for which to print the SQL. Defaults to default. squashmigrations¶ django-admin squashmigrations app_label [start_migration_name] migration_name¶ Squashes the migrations for app_label up to and including migration_name down into fewer migrations, if possible. The resulting squashed migrations can live alongside the unsquashed ones safely. For more information, please read Squashing migrations. When start_migration_name is given, Django will only include migrations starting from and including this migration. This helps to mitigate the squashing limitation of RunPython and django.db.migrations.operations.RunSQL migration operations. --no-optimize¶ Disables the optimizer when generating a squashed migration. By default, Django will try to optimize the operations in your migrations to reduce the size of the resulting file. Use this option if this process is failing or creating incorrect migrations, though please also file a Django bug report about the behavior, as optimization is meant to be safe. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. --squashed-name SQUASHED_NAME¶ Sets the name of the squashed migration. When omitted, the name is based on the first and last migration, with _squashed_ in between. --no-header¶ Generate squashed migration file without Django version and timestamp header. startapp¶ django-admin startapp name [directory]¶ Creates a Django app directory structure for the given app name in the current directory or the given destination. By default, the new directory contains a models.py file and other app template files. If only the app name is given, the app directory will be created in the current working directory. If the optional destination is provided, Django will use that name instead. If the directory with the given name doesn’t exist, it will be created. You can use ‘.’ to denote the current working directory. For example: django-admin startapp myapp /Users/jezdez/Code/myapp --template TEMPLATE¶ Provides the path to a directory with a custom app template file, or a path to an uncompressed archive (.tar) or a compressed archive (.tar.gz, .tar.bz2, .tar.xz, .tar.lzma, .tgz, .tbz2, .txz, .tlz, .zip) containing the app template files. For example, this would look for an app template in the given directory when creating the myapp app: django-admin startapp --template=/Users/jezdez/Code/my_app_template myapp Django will also accept URLs (http, https, ftp) to compressed archives with the app template files, downloading and extracting them on the fly. For example, taking advantage of GitHub’s feature to expose repositories as zip files, you can use a URL like: django-admin startapp --template=https://github.com/githubuser/django-app-template/archive/main.zip myapp Warning Templates provided via --template are used as is. Malicious or poorly constructed templates may introduce security weaknesses or unintended behavior. Compressed archives may also consume excessive resources during extraction, potentially causing crashes or hangs. Contents of templates should be carefully inspected before use. --extension EXTENSIONS, -e EXTENSIONS¶ Specifies which file extensions in the app template should be rendered with the template engine. Defaults to py. --name FILES, -n FILES¶ Specifies which files in the app template (in addition to those matching --extension) should be rendered with the template engine. Defaults to an empty list. --exclude DIRECTORIES, -x DIRECTORIES¶ Specifies which directories in the app template should be excluded, in addition to .git and __pycache__. If this option is not provided, directories named __pycache__ or starting with . will be excluded. The template context (see Context) used for all matching files is: Any option passed to the startapp command (among the command’s supported options) app_name – the app name as passed to the command app_directory – the full path of the newly created app camel_case_app_name – the app name in camel case format docs_version – the version of the documentation: 'dev' or '1.x' django_version – the version of Django, e.g. '2.0.3' Warning When the app template files are rendered with the Django template engine (by default all *.py files), Django will also replace all stray template variables contained. For example, if one of the Python files contains a docstring explaining a particular feature related to template rendering, it might result in an incorrect example. To work around this problem, you can use the templatetag template tag to “escape” the various parts of the template syntax. In addition, to allow Python template files that contain Django template language syntax while also preventing packaging systems from trying to byte-compile invalid *.py files, template files ending with .py-tpl will be renamed to .py. Warning The contents of custom app (or project) templates should always be audited before use: Such templates define code that will become part of your project, and this means that such code will be trusted as much as any app you install, or code you write yourself. Further, even rendering the templates is, effectively, executing code that was provided as input to the management command. The Django template language may provide wide access into the system, so make sure any custom template you use is worthy of your trust. startproject¶ django-admin startproject name [directory]¶ Creates a Django project directory structure for the given project name in the current directory or the given destination. By default, the new directory contains manage.py and a project package (containing a settings.py and other files). If only the project name is given, both the project directory and project package will be named <projectname> and the project directory will be created in the current working directory. If the optional destination is provided, Django will use that name as the project directory, and create manage.py and the project package within it. If the directory with the given name doesn’t exist, it will be created. Use ‘.’ to denote the current working directory. For example: django-admin startproject myproject /Users/jezdez/Code/myproject_repo --template TEMPLATE¶ Specifies a directory, file path, or URL of a custom project template. See the startapp --template documentation for examples and usage. The same security considerations described for startapp templates apply here: malicious or poorly constructed templates may introduce weaknesses or consume excessive resources, and templates should be carefully inspected before use. --extension EXTENSIONS, -e EXTENSIONS¶ Specifies which file extensions in the project template should be rendered with the template engine. Defaults to py. --name FILES, -n FILES¶ Specifies which files in the project template (in addition to those matching --extension) should be rendered with the template engine. Defaults to an empty list. --exclude DIRECTORIES, -x DIRECTORIES¶ Specifies which directories in the project template should be excluded, in addition to .git and __pycache__. If this option is not provided, directories named __pycache__ or starting with . will be excluded. The template context (see Context) used is: Any option passed to the startproject command (among the command’s supported options) project_name – the project name as passed to the command project_directory – the full path of the newly created project secret_key – a random key for the SECRET_KEY setting docs_version – the version of the documentation: 'dev' or '1.x' django_version – the version of Django, e.g. '2.0.3' Please also see the rendering warning and trusted code warning as mentioned for startapp. test¶ django-admin test [test_label [test_label ...]]¶ Runs tests for all installed apps. See Testing in Django for more information. --failfast¶ Stops running tests and reports the failure immediately after a test fails. --testrunner TESTRUNNER¶ Controls the test runner class that is used to execute tests. This value overrides the value provided by the TEST_RUNNER setting. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. A typical prompt is a warning about deleting an existing test database. Test runner options¶ The test command receives options on behalf of the specified --testrunner. These are the options of the default test runner: DiscoverRunner. --keepdb¶ Preserves the test database between test runs. This has the advantage of skipping both the create and destroy actions which can greatly decrease the time to run tests, especially those in a large test suite. If the test database does not exist, it will be created on the first run and then preserved for each subsequent run. Unless the MIGRATE test setting is False, any unapplied migrations will also be applied to the test database before running the test suite. --shuffle [SEED]¶ Randomizes the order of tests before running them. This can help detect tests that aren’t properly isolated. The test order generated by this option is a deterministic function of the integer seed given. When no seed is passed, a seed is chosen randomly and printed to the console. To repeat a particular test order, pass a seed. The test orders generated by this option preserve Django’s guarantees on test order. They also keep tests grouped by test case class. The shuffled orderings also have a special consistency property useful when narrowing down isolation issues. Namely, for a given seed and when running a subset of tests, the new order will be the original shuffling restricted to the smaller set. Similarly, when adding tests while keeping the seed the same, the order of the original tests will be the same in the new order. --reverse, -r¶ Sorts test cases in the opposite execution order. This may help in debugging the side effects of tests that aren’t properly isolated. Grouping by test class is preserved when using this option. This can be used in conjunction with --shuffle to reverse the order for a particular seed. --debug-mode¶ Sets the DEBUG setting to True prior to running tests. This may help troubleshoot test failures. --debug-sql, -d¶ Enables SQL logging for failing tests. If --verbosity is 2, then queries in passing tests are also output. --parallel [N]¶ DJANGO_TEST_PROCESSES¶ Runs tests in separate parallel processes. Since modern processors have multiple cores, this allows running tests significantly faster. Using --parallel without a value, or with the value auto, runs one test process per core according to multiprocessing.cpu_count(). You can override this by passing the desired number of processes, e.g. --parallel 4, or by setting the DJANGO_TEST_PROCESSES environment variable. Django distributes test cases — unittest.TestCase subclasses — to subprocesses. If there are fewer test case classes than configured processes, Django will reduce the number of processes accordingly. Each process gets its own database. You must ensure that different test case classes don’t access the same resources. For instance, test case classes that touch the filesystem should create a temporary directory for their own use. Note If you have test classes that cannot be run in parallel, you can use SerializeMixin to run them sequentially. See Enforce running test classes sequentially. This option requires the third-party tblib package to display tracebacks correctly: $ python -m pip install tblib This feature isn’t available on Windows. It doesn’t work with the Oracle database backend either. If you want to use pdb while debugging tests, you must disable parallel execution (--parallel=1). You’ll see something like bdb.BdbQuit if you don’t. Warning When test parallelization is enabled and a test fails, Django may be unable to display the exception traceback. This can make debugging difficult. If you encounter this problem, run the affected test without parallelization to see the traceback of the failure. This is a known limitation. It arises from the need to serialize objects in order to exchange them between processes. See What can be pickled and unpickled? for details. --tag TAGS¶ Runs only tests marked with the specified tags. May be specified multiple times and combined with test --exclude-tag. Tests that fail to load are always considered matching. --exclude-tag EXCLUDE_TAGS¶ Excludes tests marked with the specified tags. May be specified multiple times and combined with test --tag. -k TEST_NAME_PATTERNS¶ Runs test methods and classes matching test name patterns, in the same way as unittest's -k option. Can be specified multiple times. --pdb¶ Spawns a pdb debugger at each test error or failure. If you have it installed, ipdb is used instead. --buffer, -b¶ Discards output (stdout and stderr) for passing tests, in the same way as unittest's --buffer option. --no-faulthandler¶ Django automatically calls faulthandler.enable() when starting the tests, which allows it to print a traceback if the interpreter crashes. Pass --no-faulthandler to disable this behavior. --timing¶ Outputs timings, including database setup and total run time. --durations N¶ Shows the N slowest test cases (N=0 for all). testserver¶ django-admin testserver [fixture [fixture ...]]¶ Runs a Django development server (as in runserver) using data from the given fixture(s). For example, this command: django-admin testserver mydata.json …would perform the following steps: Create a test database, as described in The test database. Populate the test database with fixture data from the given fixtures. (For more on fixtures, see the documentation for loaddata above.) Runs the Django development server (as in runserver), pointed at this newly created test database instead of your production database. This is useful in a number of ways: When you’re writing unit tests of how your views act with certain fixture data, you can use testserver to interact with the views in a web browser, manually. Let’s say you’re developing your Django application and have a “pristine” copy of a database that you’d like to interact with. You can dump your database to a fixture (using the dumpdata command, explained above), then use testserver to run your web application with that data. With this arrangement, you have the flexibility of messing up your data in any way, knowing that whatever data changes you’re making are only being made to a test database. Note that this server does not automatically detect changes to your Python source code (as runserver does). It does, however, detect changes to templates. --addrport ADDRPORT¶ Specifies a different port, or IP address and port, from the default of 127.0.0.1:8000. This value follows exactly the same format and serves exactly the same function as the argument to the runserver command. Examples: To run the test server on port 7000 with fixture1 and fixture2: django-admin testserver --addrport 7000 fixture1 fixture2 django-admin testserver fixture1 fixture2 --addrport 7000 (The above statements are equivalent. We include both of them to demonstrate that it doesn’t matter whether the options come before or after the fixture arguments.) To run on 1.2.3.4:7000 with a test fixture: django-admin testserver --addrport 1.2.3.4:7000 test --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. A typical prompt is a warning about deleting an existing test database. Commands provided by applications¶ Some commands are only available when the django.contrib application that implements them has been enabled. This section describes them grouped by their application. django.contrib.auth¶ changepassword¶ django-admin changepassword [<username>]¶ This command is only available if Django’s authentication system (django.contrib.auth) is installed. Allows changing a user’s password. It prompts you to enter a new password twice for the given user. If the entries are identical, this immediately becomes the new password. If you do not supply a user, the command will attempt to change the password whose username matches the current user. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to query for the user. Defaults to default. Example usage: django-admin changepassword ringo createsuperuser¶ django-admin createsuperuser¶ DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD¶ This command is only available if Django’s authentication system (django.contrib.auth) is installed. Creates a superuser account (a user who has all permissions). This is useful if you need to create an initial superuser account or if you need to programmatically generate superuser accounts for your site(s). When run interactively, this command will prompt for a password for the new superuser account. When run non-interactively, you can provide a password by setting the DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD environment variable. Otherwise, no password will be set, and the superuser account will not be able to log in until a password has been manually set for it. In non-interactive mode, the USERNAME_FIELD and required fields (listed in REQUIRED_FIELDS) fall back to DJANGO_SUPERUSER_<uppercase_field_name> environment variables, unless they are overridden by a command line argument. For example, to provide an email field, you can use DJANGO_SUPERUSER_EMAIL environment variable. --noinput, --no-input¶ Suppresses all user prompts. If a suppressed prompt cannot be resolved automatically, the command will exit with error code 1. --username USERNAME¶ --email EMAIL¶ The username and email address for the new account can be supplied by using the --username and --email arguments on the command line. If either of those is not supplied, createsuperuser will prompt for it when running interactively. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database into which the superuser object will be saved. You can subclass the management command and override get_input_data() if you want to customize data input and validation. Consult the source code for details on the existing implementation and the method’s parameters. For example, it could be useful if you have a ForeignKey in REQUIRED_FIELDS and want to allow creating an instance instead of entering the primary key of an existing instance. django.contrib.contenttypes¶ remove_stale_contenttypes¶ django-admin remove_stale_contenttypes¶ This command is only available if Django’s contenttypes app (django.contrib.contenttypes) is installed. Deletes stale content types (from deleted models) in your database. Any objects that depend on the deleted content types will also be deleted. A list of deleted objects will be displayed before you confirm it’s okay to proceed with the deletion. --database DATABASE¶ Specifies the database to use. Defaults to default. --include-stale-apps¶ Deletes stale content types including ones from previously installed apps that have been removed from INSTALLED_APPS. Defaults to False. django.contrib.gis¶ ogrinspect¶ This command is only available if GeoDjango (django.contrib.gis) is installed. Please refer to its description in the GeoDjango documentation. django.contrib.sessions¶ clearsessions¶ django-admin clearsessions¶ Can be run as a cron job or directly to clean out expired sessions. django.contrib.staticfiles¶ collectstatic¶ This command is only available if the static files application (django.contrib.staticfiles) is installed. Please refer to its description in the staticfiles documentation. findstatic¶ This command is only available if the static files application (django.contrib.staticfiles) is installed. Please refer to its description in the staticfiles documentation. Default options¶ Although some commands may allow their own custom options, every command allows for the following options by default: --pythonpath PYTHONPATH¶ Adds the given filesystem path to the Python sys.path module attribute. If this isn’t provided, django-admin will use the PYTHONPATH environment variable. This option is unnecessary in manage.py, because it takes care of setting the Python path for you. Example usage: django-admin migrate --pythonpath='/home/djangoprojects/myproject' --settings SETTINGS¶ Specifies the settings module to use. The settings module should be in Python package syntax, e.g. mysite.settings. If this isn’t provided, django-admin will use the DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE environment variable. This option is unnecessary in manage.py, because it uses settings.py from the current project by default. Example usage: django-admin migrate --settings=mysite.settings --traceback¶ Displays a full stack trace when a CommandError is raised. By default, django-admin will show an error message when a CommandError occurs and a full stack trace for any other exception. This option is ignored by runserver. Example usage: django-admin migrate --traceback --verbosity {0,1,2,3}, -v {0,1,2,3}¶ Specifies the amount of notification and debug information that a command should print to the console. 0 means no output. 1 means normal output (default). 2 means verbose output. 3 means very verbose output. This option is ignored by runserver. Example usage: django-admin migrate --verbosity 2 --no-color¶ Disables colorized command output. Some commands format their output to be colorized. For example, errors will be printed to the console in red and SQL statements will be syntax highlighted. Example usage: django-admin runserver --no-color --force-color¶ Forces colorization of the command output if it would otherwise be disabled as discussed in Syntax coloring. For example, you may want to pipe colored output to another command. --skip-checks¶ Skips running system checks prior to running the command. This option is only available if the requires_system_checks command attribute is not an empty list or tuple. Example usage: django-admin migrate --skip-checks Extra niceties¶ Syntax coloring¶ DJANGO_COLORS¶ The django-admin / manage.py commands will use pretty color-coded output if your terminal supports ANSI-colored output. It won’t use the color codes if you’re piping the command’s output to another program unless the --force-color option is used. Windows support¶ On Windows 10, the Windows Terminal application, VS Code, and PowerShell (where virtual terminal processing is enabled) allow colored output, and are supported by default. Under Windows, the legacy cmd.exe native console doesn’t support ANSI escape sequences so by default there is no color output. In this case either of two third-party libraries are needed: Install colorama, a Python package that translates ANSI color codes into Windows API calls. Django commands will detect its presence and will make use of its services to color output just like on Unix-based platforms. colorama can be installed via pip: ...\\> py -m pip install \"colorama >= 0.4.6\" Install ANSICON, a third-party tool that allows cmd.exe to process ANSI color codes. Django commands will detect its presence and will make use of its services to color output just like on Unix-based platforms. Other modern terminal environments on Windows, that support terminal colors, but which are not automatically detected as supported by Django, may “fake” the installation of ANSICON by setting the appropriate environmental variable, ANSICON=\"on\". Custom colors¶ The colors used for syntax highlighting can be customized. Django ships with three color palettes: dark, suited to terminals that show white text on a black background. This is the default palette. light, suited to terminals that show black text on a white background. nocolor, which disables syntax highlighting. You select a palette by setting a DJANGO_COLORS environment variable to specify the palette you want to use. For example, to specify the light palette under a Unix or OS/X BASH shell, you would run the following at a command prompt: export DJANGO_COLORS=\"light\" You can also customize the colors that are used. Django specifies a number of roles in which color is used: error - A major error. notice - A minor error. success - A success. warning - A warning. sql_field - The name of a model field in SQL. sql_coltype - The type of a model field in SQL. sql_keyword - An SQL keyword. sql_table - The name of a model in SQL. http_info - A 1XX HTTP Informational server response. http_success - A 2XX HTTP Success server response. http_not_modified - A 304 HTTP Not Modified server response. http_redirect - A 3XX HTTP Redirect server response other than 304. http_not_found - A 404 HTTP Not Found server response. http_bad_request - A 4XX HTTP Bad Request server response other than 404. http_server_error - A 5XX HTTP Server Error response. migrate_heading - A heading in a migrations management command. migrate_label - A migration name. Each of these roles can be assigned a specific foreground and background color, from the following list: black red green yellow blue magenta cyan white Each of these colors can then be modified by using the following display options: bold underscore blink reverse conceal A color specification follows one of the following patterns: role=fg role=fg/bg role=fg,option,option role=fg/bg,option,option where role is the name of a valid color role, fg is the foreground color, bg is the background color and each option is one of the color modifying options. Multiple color specifications are then separated by a semicolon. For example: export DJANGO_COLORS=\"error=yellow/blue,blink;notice=magenta\" would specify that errors be displayed using blinking yellow on blue, and notices displayed using magenta. All other color roles would be left uncolored. Colors can also be specified by extending a base palette. If you put a palette name in a color specification, all the colors implied by that palette will be loaded. So: export DJANGO_COLORS=\"light;error=yellow/blue,blink;notice=magenta\" would specify the use of all the colors in the light color palette, except for the colors for errors and notices which would be overridden as specified. Bash completion¶ If you use the Bash shell, consider installing the Django bash completion script, which lives in extras/django_bash_completion in the Django source distribution. It enables tab-completion of django-admin and manage.py commands, so you can, for instance… Type django-admin. Press [TAB] to see all available options. Type sql, then [TAB], to see all available options whose names start with sql. See How to create custom django-admin commands for how to add customized actions. Black formatting¶ The Python files created by startproject, startapp, optimizemigration, makemigrations, and squashmigrations are formatted using the black command if it is present on your PATH. If you have black globally installed, but do not wish it used for the current project, you can set the PATH explicitly: PATH=path/to/venv/bin django-admin makemigrations For commands using stdout you can pipe the output to black if needed: django-admin inspectdb | black - Running management commands from your code¶ django.core.management.call_command(name, *args, **options)¶ To call a management command from code use call_command(). namethe name of the command to call or a command object. Passing the name is preferred unless the object is required for testing. *argsa list of arguments accepted by the command. Arguments are passed to the argument parser, so you can use the same style as you would on the command line. For example, call_command('flush', '--verbosity=0'). **optionsnamed options accepted on the command-line. Options are passed to the command without triggering the argument parser, which means you’ll need to pass the correct type. For example, call_command('flush', verbosity=0) (zero must be an integer rather than a string). Examples: from django.core import management from django.core.management.commands import loaddata management.call_command(\"flush\", verbosity=0, interactive=False) management.call_command(\"loaddata\", \"test_data\", verbosity=0) management.call_command(loaddata.Command(), \"test_data\", verbosity=0) Note that command options that take no arguments are passed as keywords with True or False, as you can see with the interactive option above. Named arguments can be passed by using either one of the following syntaxes: # Similar to the command line management.call_command(\"dumpdata\", \"--natural-foreign\") # Named argument similar to the command line minus the initial dashes and # with internal dashes replaced by underscores management.call_command(\"dumpdata\", natural_foreign=True) # `use_natural_foreign_keys` is the option destination variable management.call_command(\"dumpdata\", use_natural_foreign_keys=True) Some command options have different names when using call_command() instead of django-admin or manage.py. For example, django-admin createsuperuser --no-input translates to call_command('createsuperuser', interactive=False). To find what keyword argument name to use for call_command(), check the command’s source code for the dest argument passed to parser.add_argument(). Command options which take multiple options are passed a list: management.call_command(\"dumpdata\", exclude=[\"contenttypes\", \"auth\"]) The return value of the call_command() function is the same as the return value of the handle() method of the command. Output redirection¶ Note that you can redirect standard output and error streams as all commands support the stdout and stderr options. For example, you could write: with open(\"/path/to/command_output\", \"w\") as f: management.call_command(\"dumpdata\", stdout=f)","title":"django-admin and manage.py¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/django-admin/","id":384},{"content_hash":"aed9240c033db0604274176354799b1d4eb02293c63e0b52d4b9ada45c0cce7f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:42.540590+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/deux-projets-de-la-commission-economique-du-conseil-de-l-europe-bull-creation-d-un-office-europeen-des-transports-bull-reduction-des-droits-de-douane_3140709_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Le premier préconise la création d'un Office européen des transports \" dans le but d'encourager l'exploitation efficace, économique et appropriée des moyens de transport existants et d'aider au développement rationnel des transports européens \". Le mandat de l'office s'étend à toutes les formes et moyens de transport intra-européen par route, chemin de fer et voie d'eau. Le nouvel organisme ne disposerait toutefois que de faibles pouvoirs : il procéderait uniquement à des \" études \" et formulerait des \" recommandations \". Il coordonnerait l'action des bureaux déjà existants. La proposition de M. Bonnefous tendant à créer une véritable autorité européenne des transporte n'est donc que partiellement retenue, du moins pour le moment. Le \" Low-Tariff Club \" Le second document concerne les échanges commerciaux. Le secrétariat général du Conseil de l'Europe avait demandé à un groupe de personnalités d'étudier dans quelles conditions les pays européens pourraient supprimer les barrières douanières et les restrictions commerciales. L'étude entreprise a permis de mettre en lumière certaines idées directrices : 1 Les pays européens devraient s'engager à ne pas maintenir de droit de douane dont l'incidence serait supérieure à 35 %. Aucun délai n'est fixé, mais les experts du Conseil de l'Europe considèrent qu'il pourrait être relativement court. Le taux proposé pour la limite maximum des tarifs n'est qu'indicatif. Il appartiendrait évidemment à l'Assemblée de Strasbourg de prendre une décision définitive. On sait que beaucoup de pays ont des droits particulièrement élevés parce qu'ils considèrent les tarifs douaniers comme une ressource fiscale. Pour leur faciliter l'application du principe ci-dessus, les experts suggèrent qu'ils transforment en taxes intérieures ces droits à caractère fiscal ; 2 Une fois fixé le plafond au-dessus duquel aucun tarif ne serait admis on procéderait à une distinction entre trois grandes catégories de marchandises : matières premières, produits semi-finis, produits finis et denrées alimentaires. Les maxima de tarifs autorisés varieraient selon les catégories ; Il vous reste 17.16% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"Deux projets de la commission économique du conseil de l'Europe : • Création d'un office européen des transports • Réduction des droits de douane","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/deux-projets-de-la-commission-economique-du-conseil-de-l-europe-bull-creation-d-un-office-europeen-des-transports-bull-reduction-des-droits-de-douane_3140709_1819218.html","id":385},{"content_hash":"ef6a603fdc6c2d918b494522756af6323cdb0d59c62f176d948ee0e974dfd0fc","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:42.501622+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/sagittarius-horoscope-today-october-09-2026-career-success-takes-the-spotlight-as-new-income-opportunities-emerge/articleshow/134792946.cms","language":"","text":"Sagittarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Career success takes the spotlight as new income opportunities emerge - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIAstrologyHoroscopeSun Sign PredictionsCompatibility CalculatorYearly Horoscope 2026Chinese HoroscopeNumerologyNumerology PredictionsPlanets TransitsGemstones Vastu & feng shuikundali dasha & RemediesReligionMonthly HoroscopeOthersToday's ePaperLiveNewsAstrology NewsHoroscope NewsSagittarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Career success takes the spotlight as new income opportunities emergeTrendingHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodaySagittarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Career success takes the spotlight as new income opportunities emergeAstroDevam, Dr Achary Kalki Krishnan & Anita Baranwal / TOI Astrology / Oct 09, 2026, 04:30 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint General:The spotlight falls on your public life today. Your attempt is more likely to be detected by your superiors, seniors, or by those in power, and this is a plus. The Moon is making your career angle stronger and Jupiter is adding dignity and a broader platform. For those waiting for a blessing from above, this is a good day.But things are not so idyllic in the home circle. You might feel disappointed if your child does something you don't want, or you are concerned that he or she may not be listening or eating properly or using their time well. Don't be too strict or judgmental because of concern. Tone conveys more to the children than words. They may also require a tactile assessment of their health or their normal dynamic.Love and Relationship:Your husband or wife stays happy and understanding and provides a stable backbone to the day. Your partner can be a soothing force in your life, even when you're working on a lot of things. A short evening chat about family plans or household needs will go well if you don't bring office impatience into it.It's a sure bet if you're in love, but it's not the most romantic day. Today Love's language is in simple forms. Has she eaten? Is it time to meet? A cab arrangement, waiting for her up after an evening meeting etc.Education and Career:This is your strongest area today. This is likely to receive respect from superiors as Saturn is known to reward discipline and Jupiter gives credibility. Present your ideas clearly. If there is a review, appraisal, client discussion or expansion talk set for the future, treat it as a real thing. Business expansion plans can begin to be developed now.Students may feel dull or slow, though. First day of the week, my mind may be dragging. Do not suffer from guilt over that! Divide study into small chunks, and edit, don't stuff what you don't know into an already overworked brain.Money and Finance:There are chances of earning from other sources, which is good news. This may come through freelance work, side income, incentives, commission, rental support, or a family-linked opportunity. Keep track of it. Small streams make a river.Consider costs thoroughly if you're thinking about expanding your business. The stars aid and do not rule blindly. Build now, but with a calculator and an unassuming attitude!Health and Well-being:Today, stress can be more in the shoulders and the head, as responsibilities are heavier. Don't brush off the signals when you're in the middle of meetings, on your way to work, or running errands, or making family calls. Tiredness is not weakness. It's information.Don't forget to monitor your child's health either, even if the problem appears to be small. Eat at regular times and don't use tea as an aperitif or a substitute for lunch for yourself. If your head starts to spin at night, take a walk and clear the cobwebs before you go to bed at night.Tip for the Day: Support your child with patience, not disappointment, and watch their routine.Get the latest astrology news and horoscope updates. Download the TOI App.Read Also: Sagittarius Daily Horoscope | Sagittarius Weekly Horoscope | Sagittarius Monthly HoroscopeAbout the AuthorAstroDevamAstroDevam is a premium organisation providing ancient and authentic knowledge of Astrology, Vastu, Numerology, and Innovative Corporate Solutions with a contemporary perspective. AstroDevam, having patrons in more than 100 countries, has been promoted by Achary Anita Baranwal and Achary Kalki Krishnan, who not only have Master's Degrees in Astrology, but are engaged in teaching Scientific Astrology, Vastu, and Numerology for more than three decades.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaHot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn AstrologyEntire WebsitePractise Peace Amid Problems: Dhammapada 202 urges calm now; small rests build steadinessDaily Scorpio Horoscope: Support boosts gains; put savings before pleasureLibra Horoscope Today: Low confidence and money strain; avoid junk food and impulse buysLeo Horoscope Today: Use your words gently to heal family strains; favour steady saving nowVirgo Horoscope Today: Moon brings clarity and calm focus; love and money look steadyCancer Horoscope Today: Extra effort lifts work and love; avoid big spendsDaily Gemini Horoscope: Family support steadies you; hidden money and property gains beckonLucky